window.VARC = {"topics":[["rc","Reading Comprehension"],["pj","Para Jumbles"],["ps","Para Summary"],["ooo","Odd Sentence Out"],["pc","Para Completion"],["vocab","Vocabulary"],["grammar","Grammar"]],"passages":{"rc-p1":{"title":"Free Will and Determinism","paras":["The question of whether human beings possess free will has occupied philosophers for millennia, but the terms of the debate shifted decisively with the rise of modern neuroscience. Beginning in the 1980s, experiments using brain imaging suggested that neural activity associated with a decision could be detected before subjects reported being consciously aware of having decided anything at all. To many observers, this seemed to settle a very old question in a very unflattering way: the feeling of choosing, on this reading, is not the cause of our actions but a kind of after-the-fact narration the brain supplies once the decision has already been made elsewhere.","It would be a mistake, however, to treat this finding as more conclusive than it is. The original experiments involved trivial, unplanned motor actions — pressing a button whenever the urge struck — and it remains deeply unclear whether the same mechanism governs deliberate, reasoned choices, the kind we actually care about when we ask whether someone is morally responsible for a decision. Critics have also pointed out that detecting neural activity slightly before conscious awareness does not prove that activity determined the outcome; it may simply reflect the brain assembling the conditions under which a conscious decision will shortly be made, much as a kitchen can be “getting ready” for a decision about what to cook without that preparation dictating the final choice.","What the debate has usefully clarified, even without resolving the underlying metaphysical question, is that “free will” was never a single, well-defined concept to begin with. Philosophers distinguish at minimum between a strong libertarian sense — the idea that our choices are genuinely uncaused by prior physical states, a notion increasingly difficult to reconcile with a physical universe governed by cause and effect — and a compatibilist sense, which holds that free will simply means acting according to one’s own reasons and desires, without external coercion, regardless of whether those reasons and desires were themselves caused by prior physical states. Under the compatibilist definition, the neuroscience findings are almost beside the point: of course your brain state causes your decision, because your brain state simply is you making the decision, not some external puppeteer pulling your strings.","This distinction matters enormously for how society ought to think about responsibility and punishment. If free will requires an impossible kind of uncaused causation, then strict moral responsibility becomes incoherent, and punishment can only be justified on forward-looking grounds — deterrence, rehabilitation, incapacitation — never on the backward-looking notion that someone “deserved” it. If, instead, the compatibilist definition is the one that actually matters for everyday moral life, then the neuroscience findings, however interesting, leave our ordinary practices of praise and blame largely undisturbed, since those practices were never premised on the libertarian notion in the first place. The persistence of this disagreement, more than a century after the philosophical groundwork was laid and decades after the neuroscience began, suggests that what looks like an empirical dispute is, underneath, a disagreement about which questions are even the right ones to be asking."]},"rc-p2":{"title":"The Nature of Consciousness","paras":["Among the many unresolved problems in philosophy of mind, none has proven more stubbornly resistant to progress than what is often called the “hard problem” of consciousness. The distinction, first articulated with particular clarity in the 1990s, separates the “easy” problems of consciousness — explaining how the brain processes information, integrates sensory data, or produces behavior — from the genuinely hard problem: explaining why any of that physical processing is accompanied by subjective experience at all. Why does the processing of red wavelengths of light feel like something — why is there a subjective quality to seeing red, rather than the information simply being processed in the dark, so to speak, with no one home to experience it?","The “easy” problems are not actually easy in any everyday sense; they represent some of the most sophisticated ongoing work in neuroscience. But they are easy in the specific philosophical sense that we can, in principle, imagine a complete answer: a full account of neural mechanisms that produces a full account of the behavior in question. The hard problem resists this kind of resolution because no matter how complete a mechanistic account becomes, a further question always seems to remain: why should this mechanism be accompanied by experience rather than occurring, as philosophers put it, “in the dark”? A being that processed all the same information, produced all the same behavior, but had no inner experience at all — a so-called philosophical zombie — is, at least conceivably, imaginable, which suggests that experience is not simply identical to or automatically entailed by the physical processing itself.","Not everyone accepts that the hard problem is genuinely hard, or even that it is a coherent problem at all. Some philosophers argue that the intuition driving it — the sense that a mechanistic explanation could never fully account for subjective experience — is itself a kind of illusion produced by the very cognitive architecture we’re trying to explain, and that as our mechanistic understanding deepens, the intuition that “something is left out” will simply fade away, the way many other apparent mysteries have dissolved under sufficiently detailed explanation. Others go further, arguing that talk of subjective experience “over and above” physical processes smuggles in a dualism that careful philosophical analysis should dissolve rather than accommodate.","What makes the debate genuinely difficult to adjudicate, rather than merely difficult to resolve, is that it may be impossible, even in principle, to gather evidence that would settle it either way. Behavioral and neural evidence can tell us a great deal about the structure and function of experience, but by the hard problem’s own definition, no amount of third-person, externally observable data can directly confirm or rule out the presence of first-person, subjective experience in a system other than oneself. This asymmetry — between what science observes from the outside and what consciousness, if it exists, is experienced from the inside — is precisely why the problem has outlasted so many other disputes in philosophy of mind that seemed, at one time, equally intractable."]},"rc-p3":{"title":"Moral Luck","paras":["Ordinary moral judgment assumes that we should be held responsible only for what is within our control. Yet a striking feature of how we actually judge one another, first systematically examined by philosophers in the late 1970s, is that outcomes entirely outside an agent’s control routinely affect how harshly we judge them — a phenomenon now known as “moral luck.” Consider two drivers, both momentarily distracted while checking a phone, both running equally significant red lights. One street is empty; nothing happens. The other has a child crossing at exactly the wrong moment. We judge the second driver far more harshly than the first, often holding them criminally liable in a way we never would the first — despite the fact that the two drivers behaved identically, made identical choices, and differed only in a matter of pure chance entirely outside either one’s control.","This asymmetry sits uneasily with the seemingly basic moral principle that we should only be held responsible for what we control. If two agents make the same choice under the same information, differing only in how the world happened to unfold afterward, consistency would seem to demand judging them identically. And yet almost no one, on reflection, actually wants to abandon the practice of judging the unlucky driver more harshly — the intuition that outcomes matter, that the child’s death is not morally irrelevant simply because it resulted from chance, is nearly as strong as the intuition that only choices should matter.","Philosophers have identified several distinct categories of moral luck, of which outcome luck — the kind illustrated above — is only one. There is also circumstantial luck: the fact that a person raised in relative comfort, never seriously tested, cannot know how they would have behaved under the pressures that led someone else, raised amid deprivation and desperation, into wrongdoing they themselves never faced the temptation to commit. And there is constitutive luck: the deeper, still more unsettling fact that our very characters — our temperaments, our capacities for patience or empathy or self-control — are themselves shaped by genetics and upbringing we did not choose, raising the uncomfortable question of how much of what we praise or blame in a person’s character was ever really “theirs” to begin with, in the sense that would justify calling it freely chosen.","No fully satisfying resolution to the puzzle has emerged, and this may be revealing in itself. One response bites the bullet and insists that, strictly speaking, only the distracted glance at the phone — the choice itself — is what either driver is truly responsible for, and that our harsher judgment of the unlucky driver is a kind of understandable but ultimately unjustified emotional reaction we should try to correct rather than endorse. A different response holds that this “correction” asks too much of moral psychology, effectively demanding that we stop caring about consequences altogether, which no workable system of ethics — let alone law — has ever actually managed to do or, arguably, should even want to."]},"rc-p4":{"title":"Behavioral Economics and Nudges","paras":["For much of the twentieth century, mainstream economics rested on a simplifying assumption: that people, given accurate information, make decisions that rationally maximize their own well-being. Behavioral economics, which emerged as a serious challenge to this framework over the past several decades, has assembled a substantial body of evidence that this assumption fails in specific, predictable ways. People are loss-averse, weighting potential losses roughly twice as heavily as equivalent gains. They are anchored by irrelevant reference points, so that an arbitrary starting number can shift subsequent judgments even when both parties know the number was arbitrary. And they are heavily influenced by how choices are framed, even when the underlying options are logically identical.","One practical response to these findings has been the rise of “nudge” policies: interventions that preserve an individual’s freedom to choose while structuring the choice environment to make certain outcomes more likely. The canonical example is retirement savings: switching a workplace pension scheme from opt-in, where employees must actively enroll, to opt-out, where employees are automatically enrolled unless they actively decline, has been shown repeatedly to dramatically increase participation rates, even though the actual menu of choices — enroll or don’t — remains exactly the same. No one is forced to save; the default is simply changed, exploiting the well-documented tendency for people to stick with whatever option requires no action.","Nudge advocates argue this represents a genuinely liberal form of policy: unlike a mandate or a ban, a nudge leaves the option to choose otherwise fully intact, respecting individual autonomy while still steering outcomes toward what most people, on reflection, say they actually want (higher savings rates, healthier diets, and so on). Critics, however, raise a harder question: who decides what counts as the outcome people “really” want, and is it appropriate for governments or employers to exploit known cognitive biases — even gently, even for ostensibly good ends — rather than working to correct or educate people out of those biases directly? A nudge, on this view, is a subtle form of manipulation that succeeds precisely because it bypasses conscious deliberation rather than engaging it, which sits uncomfortably with any political tradition that places a high value on individual rational agency.","A further complication, less discussed but increasingly important as nudge-based policy has scaled up worldwide, is the empirical durability of these effects outside the carefully controlled conditions of the original studies. Several widely cited nudge interventions have failed to replicate at the same magnitude when tested at larger scale or in different populations, and some researchers now suspect that early, striking results were disproportionately drawn from the more dramatic end of a wide range of actual effect sizes, a statistical pattern common enough across the social sciences to have earned its own name: the “decline effect.” This does not mean nudges don’t work — the retirement-savings finding, among others, has proven remarkably robust — but it does suggest that the field’s early enthusiasm outpaced, in some cases, the strength of its evidence, a caution equally applicable to any policy claiming to have found a costless, purely beneficial way to improve human decision-making."]},"rc-p5":{"title":"Meritocracy and Its Discontents","paras":["The idea that social position should be earned through talent and effort rather than inherited through birth has, for much of the modern era, been treated as an unambiguous moral advance over aristocratic systems that allocated status by bloodline. Yet a growing body of criticism, drawing on both empirical research and moral philosophy, has begun to question whether meritocracy, as actually practiced in contemporary societies, delivers on its own promise — and whether, even in its ideal form, it rests on premises that don’t hold up to scrutiny.","The empirical challenge is comparatively straightforward: access to the education, networks, and cultural capital that allow “merit” to be recognized and rewarded is itself highly unequally distributed, correlating strongly with the very family background meritocracy was supposed to render irrelevant. A child born to well-resourced parents typically receives, from early childhood, cumulative advantages — tutoring, enrichment activities, connections, the simple modeling of what an ambitious life trajectory looks like — that are difficult to distinguish, by the time that child reaches adulthood, from “natural” talent or “earned” effort. Studies of intergenerational income mobility in many wealthy countries have found remarkably persistent correlations between parental and child income, suggesting that formally open, merit-based systems reproduce existing hierarchies nearly as effectively as the openly hereditary systems they replaced, simply through less visible mechanisms.","The philosophical challenge cuts deeper still, questioning meritocracy’s ideal form rather than merely its flawed implementation. Even setting aside unequal access entirely, the traits meritocracy rewards — intelligence, diligence, particular talents — are not, on reflection, things individuals can be said to have earned in any deep sense. One does not choose one’s native cognitive endowments, nor the temperament that makes sustained effort feel bearable rather than exhausting, nor the specific talents that happen to be highly rewarded by one’s particular society at one’s particular moment in history. If none of these traits were chosen, then rewarding people in proportion to them starts to look less like justly rewarding earned merit and more like a different, more subtle form of the same moral arbitrariness meritocracy was supposed to correct.","None of this establishes that meritocratic ideals should be abandoned outright — critics of meritocracy are notably less unified on what should replace it than on what is wrong with it, and most acknowledge that some link between effort, competence, and reward remains both practically necessary for a functioning economy and intuitively important to most people’s sense of fairness. What the critique does suggest, at minimum, is that meritocratic rhetoric can do real social harm when it is taken too literally: societies that tell themselves success is purely earned tend, the evidence suggests, to show less sympathy toward those who don’t succeed, and more contempt toward the poor, than societies that retain some public acknowledgment of luck’s role — a finding that should give pause to anyone tempted to treat “meritocracy” as a simple, unqualified good rather than an ideal in permanent, unresolved tension with the way merit actually gets produced and recognized in the real world."]},"rc-p6":{"title":"Automation and the Future of Work","paras":["Predictions that automation will eliminate mass employment have accompanied nearly every major wave of technological change since industrialization began, and have, so far, proven wrong in a specific and instructive way: not because technology fails to automate tasks — it reliably does — but because economies have, historically, generated new categories of work fast enough to absorb workers displaced from old ones. Bank tellers did not disappear when ATMs became widespread; their numbers, initially, actually grew, as lower per-branch staffing costs allowed banks to open more branches, and tellers shifted toward relationship-based and advisory tasks machines could not easily replicate. This pattern — displacement of specific tasks followed by adaptation into a different and often initially unforeseeable role — has repeated across enough technological transitions to have become the default assumption among most labor economists.","The current wave of automation, driven substantially by advances in machine learning, is frequently described as different in kind rather than merely degree, and there is at least a plausible case for why. Previous waves of automation primarily displaced manual and routine tasks; the tasks now within reach of automation increasingly include cognitive and even creative work — drafting documents, writing code, generating images — that was, until recently, assumed to be comfortably outside automation’s reach for decades to come. If the boundary of what can be automated is expanding into domains previously considered safely human, the “adapt into a new role” pattern may hold less reliably, simply because there may be fewer domains left for displaced workers to adapt into.","Even those most concerned about this current wave, however, tend to stop short of predicting mass, permanent unemployment, for a reason worth taking seriously: human demand for goods and services is not fixed, and historically has expanded to absorb freed-up productive capacity in ways that are difficult to predict in advance. Few nineteenth-century observers could have anticipated that automating agricultural and manufacturing labor would eventually create demand for professions like social media management or user experience design — categories of work that didn’t exist, and would have made little sense, before the technological changes that created the need for them. This argues for genuine humility about confidently predicting which new forms of work automation might create, rather than certainty that none will emerge.","What does appear genuinely different this time, and worth taking seriously independent of the employment-level question, is the pace and distributional character of the transition. Even if aggregate employment eventually adjusts, the adjustment period can be long, and the workers displaced are rarely the same workers who benefit from newly created roles, which tend to require different skills, often concentrated in different geographic regions. A truck driver displaced by autonomous vehicles is not well positioned, without substantial retraining and relocation, to become a machine learning engineer. Policy debates have accordingly begun shifting away from the binary question of whether automation destroys jobs in aggregate, toward the more tractable and arguably more urgent question of how to manage the transition for those caught on the losing side of a shift whose aggregate benefits, however real, are unevenly and unpredictably distributed."]},"rc-p7":{"title":"The Attention Economy","paras":["Advertising has always competed for a scarce resource, but the resource in question has shifted in a way worth pausing over. Where twentieth-century advertising competed primarily for a slice of consumers’ spending — trying to convince someone to buy this brand of soap rather than that one — the dominant business model of the contemporary internet competes for something more fundamental: the finite hours of a person’s attention, which advertising-funded platforms then resell, in aggregate and at scale, to advertisers. This shift has consequences for how digital products are designed that are easy to overlook precisely because they operate beneath the level of any single conscious purchasing decision.","A platform funded by selling advertising space has a straightforward incentive: the more time users spend on the platform, and the more frequently they return, the more inventory the platform has to sell. This incentive does not require any single feature to be deliberately, consciously “addictive” in a moralized sense; it simply requires that, across thousands of design decisions — how notifications are timed, how feeds are ordered, how the boundary between content and advertisement is blurred — the aggregate effect of a competitive marketplace of attention-optimizing products is to select, over time, for whichever design choices are most effective at capturing and holding attention, regardless of whether that attention capture serves the user’s own considered interests.","Critics of this model point to a specific structural problem: unlike a market for physical goods, where a buyer’s dissatisfaction with a product typically shows up quickly and directly, the costs of attention capture are diffuse, delayed, and difficult for any individual user to directly perceive in the moment of use — degraded sleep, fragmented attention, a vague but hard-to-pin-down sense of having spent an evening on something that added little value. The user experiencing these costs and the “market” mechanism that might otherwise correct for them are, in an important sense, disconnected: platforms are optimized against engagement metrics that correlate with attention capture, not against the harder-to-measure outcomes users say, when surveyed, that they actually care about.","Defenders of the model raise a legitimate objection to this critique: users are not, in fact, powerless, and revealed preference — what people actually choose to spend time on, repeatedly, over years — is at least some evidence of genuine value, however uncomfortable that evidence might be for critics inclined to distrust it. Dismissing what billions of people freely choose to do with unstructured leisure time as mere manipulation risks a certain paternalism of its own, substituting the critic’s judgment about what people should want for what people, in practice, actually do want. The genuinely difficult question, left largely unresolved by either side, is how to take both halves of this picture seriously at once: that attention-capture business models are shaped by real incentive structures that don’t automatically align with user wellbeing, and that dismissing user choices as simply the product of manipulation, rather than engaging with what those choices might actually be revealing, risks a different kind of intellectual dishonesty of its own."]},"rc-p8":{"title":"Microfinance and Development","paras":["When microfinance — the practice of extending small loans to poor borrowers, often without traditional collateral, typically to fund small-scale entrepreneurial activity — first gained international prominence in the 1990s and 2000s, it was frequently described in strikingly utopian terms: a market-based mechanism that could lift millions out of poverty by unlocking the latent entrepreneurial potential that conventional banking, with its collateral requirements and minimum loan sizes, had systematically excluded. The core insight was genuinely clever: by lending in small groups whose members guaranteed each other’s loans, microfinance institutions could achieve high repayment rates without traditional collateral, since social pressure within the group substituted for the kind of financial security a conventional bank would otherwise require.","Rigorous evaluation of microfinance’s actual effects, however, has told a considerably more modest story than the early enthusiasm suggested. Randomized controlled trials conducted across several countries beginning in the late 2000s found that access to microloans did produce measurable effects — increased business investment and, in some contexts, expanded business scale among borrowers who already ran small enterprises — but found little to no evidence of the dramatic, broad-based poverty reduction or household income gains the earlier rhetoric had promised. Many borrowers used loans not to start ambitious new ventures but to smooth consumption during difficult periods, cover emergency expenses, or make modest investments in already-existing small businesses, none of which is without value, but none of which matches the transformative narrative that had driven the sector’s rapid global expansion and, for a period, its enormous popularity among development donors.","This gap between rhetoric and rigorous evidence has prompted a useful, broader recalibration in how development interventions are evaluated. Microfinance is not unique in having been oversold relative to what careful evidence eventually supported; it is, however, a particularly well-documented case, partly because its high early visibility attracted an unusually large volume of serious academic scrutiny that many other development interventions, launched with similarly confident rhetoric, have never received. The lesson many researchers draw is not that microfinance is worthless — the consumption-smoothing and modest business benefits it does provide are real and valuable to the people receiving them — but that even well-intentioned, theoretically sound interventions require rigorous, skeptical empirical testing before their effects can be confidently claimed at the scale early advocates asserted.","A further complication, increasingly discussed within the sector itself, concerns the risk of over-indebtedness in markets where microfinance has expanded rapidly and with limited regulatory oversight. Multiple microfinance institutions competing for the same limited pool of borrowers in a given region can lead, and in some documented cases has led, to individuals taking out loans from several institutions simultaneously to cover repayments on earlier ones — a dynamic uncomfortably similar to conventional predatory lending, despite microfinance’s founding intentions being close to the opposite. This does not invalidate the model, but it does illustrate a pattern worth remembering across many well-intentioned development interventions: a genuinely good idea, implemented at scale without sufficiently careful safeguards, can produce outcomes that diverge substantially, and sometimes harmfully, from what its original designers intended."]},"rc-p9":{"title":"Artificial Intelligence and the Question of Understanding","paras":["When a large language model produces a fluent, contextually appropriate response to a complex question, it is tempting to describe the system as “understanding” the question in something like the way a person would. Whether this description is accurate, or merely a convenient shorthand that obscures something importantly different happening underneath, has become one of the more consequential disputes in contemporary philosophy of mind and computer science alike, with implications well beyond academic interest as these systems are increasingly deployed in contexts that assume some degree of genuine comprehension.","Skeptics of the “understanding” framing point to a thought experiment first proposed decades before such systems existed: imagine a person, with no knowledge of Chinese, locked in a room with an exhaustive rulebook that specifies, for any sequence of Chinese characters received, exactly which sequence of characters to send back out. To someone outside the room, the responses might be indistinguishable from those of a fluent Chinese speaker. Yet the person inside, we’re meant to conclude, understands no Chinese at all — they are merely following syntactic rules, manipulating symbols according to their shape, without any access to what those symbols mean. Critics of large language models argue these systems are the room: extraordinarily sophisticated symbol manipulators, trained to produce statistically plausible continuations of text, without anything that deserves to be called comprehension of what the text refers to.","Defenders of a more expansive view raise a pointed rejoinder: what, precisely, would count as evidence of “real” understanding that current systems fail to provide, and is human understanding itself so categorically different from sophisticated pattern-completion, once we look closely at how human cognition actually works? Human brains, after all, are themselves systems that learned to produce contextually appropriate outputs through an enormous volume of exposure to patterned input, without any of us having direct, unmediated access to some separate realm of “true meaning” floating free of the neural processes that produce our own responses. If the objection to language models is that we can, in principle, describe their internal operations mechanistically, human cognition may eventually be susceptible to an equally mechanistic description without most people concluding that human understanding is therefore illusory.","The dispute may be less resolvable than either side would like, for a specific reason: “understanding” may not be a single, unified phenomenon that a system either fully possesses or entirely lacks, but rather a cluster of related capacities — tracking reference, maintaining consistency, updating beliefs in light of new evidence, applying a concept correctly to genuinely novel cases — that can, at least in principle, come apart from one another. A system might reliably succeed at some of these components while reliably failing at others, in which case asking whether it “really” understands, phrased as a single yes-or-no question, may be less useful than asking, with much greater precision, which specific components of understanding a given system does and doesn’t reliably exhibit, and in which specific contexts. This more granular framing is less satisfying as a headline than a clean verdict on the ultimate metaphysical question, but it may be considerably more useful for the practical work of deciding where and how much to actually trust these systems."]},"rc-p10":{"title":"Gene Editing and Its Ethical Boundaries","paras":["The development of CRISPR-based gene editing, a technique allowing scientists to make precise, targeted changes to DNA with a precision and low cost unimaginable under earlier genetic engineering methods, has been widely described as one of the most significant biotechnological advances of the century. Its therapeutic potential is genuinely substantial: clinical trials have already demonstrated the ability to correct the specific genetic mutations responsible for certain inherited blood disorders, offering the realistic prospect of a functional cure — not merely symptom management — for conditions that had, until recently, required lifelong treatment.","The ethical questions raised by the technology, however, extend well beyond its application to treating existing patients. A crucial distinction, insisted upon by nearly every serious ethical framework addressing the technology, separates somatic gene editing — changes made to the cells of an individual patient, which are not passed on to that patient’s offspring — from germline gene editing, changes made to eggs, sperm, or early embryos, which would be inherited by all future descendants of the edited individual. The therapeutic blood disorder treatments mentioned above are somatic; their effects, whatever they are, end with the treated patient. Germline editing raises a fundamentally different order of ethical concern, since it involves making permanent, hereditary changes to the human gene pool on the basis of decisions made by a single generation, with no realistic mechanism for the many future generations affected to have any say in a choice made on their behalf, generations before they exist.","This distinction became a matter of urgent, global controversy in 2018, when a scientist in China announced he had created the first gene-edited human babies, altering a gene associated with HIV resistance in embryos that were subsequently brought to term. The announcement was met with near-universal condemnation from the international scientific community — not primarily because the underlying editing technique was scientifically unsound, but because the intervention had bypassed the extensive ethical review, long-term safety verification, and broad societal deliberation that virtually every relevant scientific body had insisted must precede any germline application in humans. The scientist was subsequently prosecuted and imprisoned in China, and the episode significantly accelerated international efforts to establish clearer, more binding regulatory frameworks specifically governing germline editing.","Even among those who accept that germline editing should, for now, remain off-limits for human reproduction, a harder question looms on the horizon that the current moratorium does not resolve: assuming the technology’s safety profile continues to improve, as most experts studying it expect it will, where exactly should the line be drawn between editing to prevent serious, well-understood genetic disease — an application many ethicists find easier to defend — and editing for what might loosely be called “enhancement,” such as selecting for greater height, or specific cognitive traits, where no clear disease is being prevented and no clean line reliably separates prevention from enhancement in every conceivable case? Some geneticists argue the distinction, while intuitively appealing, will prove difficult to maintain with any philosophical rigor once implemented in practice, since many traits exist on a continuous spectrum rather than falling into a clean binary of “disease” versus “normal variation” — a concern that suggests today’s ethical debates, urgent as they already are, may look comparatively simple next to the ones the technology’s continued advancement will eventually force upon us."]},"rc-p11":{"title":"Geoengineering and the Politics of Climate Intervention","paras":["As global efforts to reduce greenhouse gas emissions have consistently fallen short of the pace required to meet international climate targets, a category of technologies once confined to the margins of climate policy discussion — collectively termed geoengineering — has moved steadily toward the mainstream of serious scientific and policy consideration. The most widely studied approach, solar radiation management, would involve deliberately injecting reflective aerosol particles into the upper atmosphere to reflect a portion of incoming sunlight back into space, mimicking the well-documented global cooling effect observed following major volcanic eruptions, which release similar particles naturally.","The appeal of the approach lies substantially in its apparent cost-effectiveness relative to the alternative: estimates suggest that a solar radiation management program capable of offsetting a meaningful fraction of global warming could be implemented for a fraction of the cost of the emissions reductions required to achieve a comparable temperature effect, and could, in principle, begin producing measurable cooling within a matter of months rather than the decades required for atmospheric carbon concentrations to respond meaningfully to reduced emissions. For a problem where the pace of action has consistently lagged the pace of scientific consensus about its urgency, this speed is not a trivial consideration.","The risks, however, are severe enough that a significant portion of the climate science community has approached the technology with something closer to alarm than enthusiasm, even as they acknowledge circumstances under which deploying it might become the least-bad option available. Modeling suggests that solar radiation management could significantly disrupt regional precipitation patterns, potentially triggering droughts in some agriculturally vital regions even while achieving its intended global average cooling effect — meaning the technology could create clear winners and losers among different countries and regions, a moral and geopolitical hazard distinct from anything posed by emissions reduction, which, whatever its other difficulties, does not by its nature redistribute climate harms from one region onto another. There is also the specific danger known as “termination shock”: were a large-scale solar radiation management program to be halted abruptly, for any reason — a funding crisis, a war, a political change of government — the full, un-mitigated warming corresponding to whatever greenhouse gas concentration had accumulated in the meantime would reassert itself over a period of just a few years, a far more violent and destabilizing pace of change than the gradual warming trajectory the world is currently on, since the underlying greenhouse gas buildup would not have paused merely because its warming effect was being artificially masked.","Perhaps the deepest concern is not technical but institutional: no existing international governance framework is remotely equipped to make decisions, on behalf of the entire planet, about deploying a technology whose effects would be global, largely irreversible on a meaningful timescale once emissions have continued accumulating beneath the mask, and unevenly distributed in ways certain to generate intense disagreement about whose interests should prevail when regional effects diverge. Whether such a framework can be developed with anything like the urgency the technology’s own proponents argue may soon be needed remains, at present, entirely unresolved — a governance gap that may prove, in the final accounting, a harder problem to solve than the atmospheric chemistry itself."]},"rc-p12":{"title":"Quantum Computing and the Limits of Classical Intuition","paras":["Quantum computers derive their potential power from exploiting phenomena — superposition and entanglement chief among them — that have no straightforward analogue in the classical computers most people interact with daily, and that resist, in ways worth taking seriously rather than glossing over, easy translation into everyday intuition. A classical bit holds a value of either 0 or 1 at any given moment. A quantum bit, or qubit, can exist in a superposition of both states simultaneously, and — crucially, since superposition alone does not explain quantum computing’s potential advantage — multiple qubits can become entangled, such that the state of one qubit is correlated with the state of another in a way that cannot be described by treating each qubit as having its own independent, well-defined state at all.","It is tempting, and extremely common in popular explanations, to describe this as the quantum computer “trying all possible answers simultaneously” and thereby achieving a kind of brute-force parallelism unavailable to classical machines. This description, while capturing something real about superposition, is misleading in a way that matters: simply placing qubits into superposition and then measuring them does not, by itself, yield a useful answer, because measurement collapses a superposition into a single, effectively random classical outcome, discarding all the other possibilities that had been “explored.” The genuine source of quantum computing’s power lies not in superposition alone but in carefully designed algorithms that manipulate the quantum state before measurement so that the probability of measuring a useful, correct answer is amplified, while the probability of measuring all the unhelpful alternatives is suppressed — a subtle process called interference, borrowed conceptually from wave physics, where quantum amplitudes can reinforce or cancel each other depending on the specific structure of the computation being performed.","This distinction matters enormously for understanding which problems quantum computers are actually expected to solve faster than classical ones, and it is a considerably shorter list than popular coverage often implies. For most everyday computational tasks — the kind of processing involved in word processors, video streaming, or standard business software — no quantum algorithm currently known offers any meaningful advantage over classical approaches, and there is no strong theoretical reason to expect one will be found for most such tasks. Quantum computers show genuine, well-established promise for a comparatively narrow but scientifically and commercially significant class of problems: certain cryptographic calculations, specific forms of chemical and materials simulation where quantum effects are themselves central to the underlying physics being simulated, and particular optimization problems with a specific mathematical structure that current quantum algorithms happen to exploit well.","The practical engineering challenges standing between today’s quantum computers and reliable, large-scale application to even these narrower use cases remain substantial. Qubits are notoriously fragile, prone to losing their delicate quantum properties through unwanted interaction with their environment — a problem known as decoherence — within timescales often measured in mere fractions of a second, and current approaches to correcting the resulting errors require a significant overhead of additional “helper” qubits for every qubit actually performing useful computation. Whether engineering can close this gap fast enough to deliver quantum computing’s most significant promised applications within the coming decade, as many optimistic industry roadmaps currently suggest, or whether the field will instead require a longer and more uncertain runway, remains one of the genuinely open empirical questions in a field that has, at various points over its history, been prone to both premature hype and unfairly premature dismissal in roughly equal measure."]},"rc-p13":{"title":"Memory and the Reconstructive Brain","paras":["For most of the twentieth century, both popular and much scientific thinking about memory relied, whether explicitly or implicitly, on a storage metaphor: memories as discrete files, recorded at the moment of an experience and retrieved, more or less intact, whenever later recalled — a kind of biological filing cabinet or video recording that could occasionally be misplaced or degraded but was not, on this view, actively altered each time it was accessed. Decades of subsequent research in cognitive psychology and neuroscience have overturned this picture almost entirely, replacing it with a considerably stranger and more consequential one: memory is not a recording but an active, reconstructive process, rebuilt anew, at least in part, every single time it is recalled.","The evidence for this reconstructive view is both extensive and, in some of its implications, genuinely unsettling. Laboratory studies have repeatedly demonstrated that misleading information introduced after an event — even something as subtle as the specific wording of a question asked during a later interview — can become incorporated into a person’s memory of the original event itself, such that the person subsequently, and with complete sincerity, recalls details that were never actually part of their original experience at all. Perhaps most strikingly, researchers have successfully implanted entirely fabricated memories of events that never happened — a specific, detailed childhood experience that had no basis in the subject’s actual history — with a meaningful percentage of study participants coming to genuinely believe, describe in vivid and confident detail, and even express real emotion about, an event that simply never occurred.","The mechanism underlying this reconstructive fragility appears to involve a process researchers call reconsolidation: each time a memory is retrieved, the specific neural circuits encoding it are thought to become temporarily labile — open to modification — before being “re-stored” in a process that, unlike the original encoding, is influenced by the current context of recall, including current emotional state, subsequently acquired information, and even the specific way a question happens to be phrased. This is not, evolutionarily speaking, best understood as a design flaw so much as a plausible trade-off: a memory system that updates itself with new, contextually relevant information each time it is accessed may generally serve an organism’s practical needs better than one that preserves perfect, unchanging fidelity to a single past moment at the cost of never being able to meaningfully integrate what has been learned since.","The practical stakes of this research extend well beyond academic psychology, most consequentially into legal contexts where eyewitness testimony has historically carried enormous evidentiary weight, precisely because witnesses so often report their memories with total, convincing sincerity and confidence — a confidence that, the research makes clear, bears no reliable relationship to the memory’s actual accuracy, since a confidently and vividly recalled false memory is, from the rememberer’s own first-person perspective, subjectively indistinguishable from an accurate one. This disjunction between subjective confidence and objective accuracy has driven significant, if still incomplete, reforms in how police interviews are conducted and how courts instruct juries to weigh eyewitness testimony, though implementation of these evidence-based reforms across different jurisdictions remains notably uneven, lagging well behind what the underlying science has established with reasonable confidence for decades."]},"rc-p14":{"title":"Revisiting the History of Colonial Trade","paras":["For much of the twentieth century, standard economic histories of colonial trade tended to present it in relatively benign terms: a natural, mutually beneficial exchange in which colonizing powers supplied capital, technology, and access to global markets in return for raw materials and agricultural goods that colonized regions were, in any case, well-suited to produce. This framing, while never entirely uncontested, has been substantially revised by several decades of subsequent economic history research that has taken more seriously a question the earlier framing tended to treat as secondary: under what specific political and coercive conditions did this “exchange” actually take place, and how much of its apparent structure reflected genuine comparative advantage as opposed to power asymmetries deliberately engineered and enforced by the colonizing power itself.","The evidence assembled by this revisionist scholarship points toward the latter far more heavily than earlier accounts acknowledged. Colonial administrations frequently used direct legal coercion — taxation structured specifically to force subsistence farmers into cash-crop production, prohibitions on local manufacturing that would have competed with goods imported from the colonizing country, and infrastructure investment (railways and ports, most visibly) designed overwhelmingly to move raw materials outward to imperial markets rather than to connect and develop internal regional economies. None of this fits comfortably within a model of voluntary exchange between consenting economic parties freely responding to market signals and comparative advantage; it describes, more accurately, an economic structure imposed and actively enforced through the colonizer’s political and military authority over the colonized population.","This is not to suggest, as some cruder versions of the revisionist critique risk implying, that colonial economies were static, that colonized populations were merely passive victims with no economic agency of their own, or that every commercial transaction under colonial rule was individually coerced at the point of a gun. Considerable, well-documented economic agency persisted throughout the colonial period: local merchants adapted deliberately to new market opportunities, some indigenous industries survived and occasionally thrived despite restrictive policies working actively against them, and colonized populations frequently found and exploited the specific gaps and inconsistencies in colonial economic control. But acknowledging this genuine agency does not, on careful examination, rescue the older, more benign framing, since the “opportunities” being adapted to were themselves substantially shaped, and often deliberately narrowed, by a coercive political structure that heavily constrained which economic paths were realistically available in the first place.","The revised understanding carries direct, ongoing consequences for how economists and policymakers think about persistent patterns of global economic inequality today. If the specialization of former colonies in raw material export and former colonizers in manufacturing reflects, at least in substantial part, a structure actively and deliberately imposed rather than one purely emerging from underlying, natural comparative advantage, then the resulting economic patterns deserve to be understood, and evaluated, quite differently than a model of neutral market outcomes would suggest — a reframing with direct and continuing relevance for contemporary debates about international trade rules, development assistance, and reparative justice, debates in which how one understands this specific economic history quietly but substantially shapes what one is prepared to accept as a legitimate response to it today."]},"rc-p15":{"title":"Oral Tradition and the Politics of Written History","paras":["Written records have long enjoyed a kind of default epistemic authority in historical scholarship, treated, often implicitly, as inherently more reliable than oral traditions transmitted across generations without the fixing power of the written word. This assumption, while understandable given writing’s genuine capacity to preserve specific details — exact dates, precise names, verbatim wording — with a fidelity oral transmission cannot straightforwardly replicate, has come under sustained and increasingly well-evidenced challenge from historians and anthropologists studying societies where oral tradition served, for centuries, as the primary vehicle of historical memory.","Part of the challenge is empirical: detailed studies of specific oral traditions, particularly in West African societies with dedicated hereditary classes of oral historians trained rigorously from childhood in precise, formalized methods of historical transmission, have found that certain categories of information — genealogies, the sequence and approximate dates of significant political events, the specific terms of important treaties — were preserved across many generations with an accuracy that, when later checked against independent written sources (colonial administrative records, archaeological evidence, and so on), proved considerably higher than casual assumptions about the inevitable, progressive “decay” of orally transmitted information would have predicted. These systems of oral transmission were not, as an earlier generation of historians sometimes assumed, informal or accidental, but employed structured, deliberately redundant methods — call-and-response verification during performance, multiple independent trained lineages transmitting the same core historical material in parallel, allowing for cross-checking — specifically designed to catch and correct exactly the kind of transmission errors a naive model of oral tradition would predict as inevitable and uncorrectable over time.","None of this suggests, and careful scholars in the field are notably careful not to suggest, that oral tradition and written record are simply interchangeable, equally reliable sources of historical information in every category and every case. Oral traditions are, by their nature as living, actively performed traditions, more susceptible than static written texts to a particular kind of alteration: gradual reshaping to serve present political and social needs, most visible when a tradition’s account of ancient succession or territorial claims happens to align suspiciously well with whichever political faction currently controls, sponsors, or otherwise has an active stake in that specific tradition’s ongoing performance and transmission — a vulnerability written records possess too, of course, but through a different mechanism (selective preservation, destruction, and deliberate falsification at the point of composition or archiving, rather than gradual reshaping through repeated oral performance over time).","The more useful methodological lesson emerging from this research is not that either written or oral sources deserve a blanket presumption of superior reliability, but that both categories of historical source require the same kind of specific, source-critical scrutiny historians have long applied, in principle if not always consistently in practice, to written documents: who transmitted this specific account, through what particular mechanism, serving whose interests, and checked, wherever independently possible, against what other, ideally independent lines of evidence. Treating “written” as a reliability category unto itself, superior by default to “oral,” turns out on closer examination to conflate a source’s specific medium of transmission with its actual evidentiary reliability — two genuinely distinct properties that happen to have been historically, and misleadingly, correlated in the specific societies where modern academic historical scholarship first developed its methodological habits and default assumptions."]},"rc-p16":{"title":"Museum Repatriation and Cultural Heritage","paras":["Major encyclopedic museums in Europe and North America hold, collectively, millions of cultural and historical artifacts acquired from formerly colonized regions during a period spanning roughly the eighteenth through mid-twentieth centuries — acquisitions whose circumstances range from straightforward looting during military conquest, through various forms of coerced or unequal “sale” conducted under conditions of colonial power imbalance, to smaller numbers of transactions that even by contemporary standards would likely be considered legitimate. Sorting any specific object’s actual provenance into one of these categories, decades or centuries after the fact and often with fragmentary documentation, has become one of the more contentious practical and methodological challenges facing the growing international movement calling for the repatriation of at least a significant portion of these holdings to their countries and communities of origin.","Museums resisting repatriation requests have historically offered several distinct lines of justification, not all of equal persuasive weight and not always clearly distinguished from one another in public debate. One argument holds that “universal” encyclopedic museums, gathering objects from across the globe under a single roof, serve an educational and cross-cultural mission unavailable to more geographically dispersed, national or regional collections — a mission, the argument continues, that would be substantially and irreversibly diminished by large-scale repatriation. A second, more practical argument raises legitimate questions about the receiving country’s capacity for climate-controlled storage and specialized conservation infrastructure adequate to the artifact’s long-term physical preservation, particularly for the more fragile categories of object. A third argument rests on straightforward legal claims to current ownership, however ethically questionable the original acquisition might have been by contemporary standards, given the passage of enough time and the absence of any specific claiming party.","Each of these justifications has drawn substantial and increasingly effective counterargument in recent years. The “universal museum” argument, critics point out, was itself substantially formulated well after most of the relevant original acquisitions had already occurred, functioning in practice more as a retroactive philosophical justification for retaining existing holdings than as a genuine, independently-motivated organizing principle that predated and shaped the collecting practice itself. The conservation-capacity argument, meanwhile, has grown markedly weaker as an increasing number of formerly colonized nations have built world-class museum and conservation infrastructure fully capable of housing repatriated objects to the same professional standard — a development some critics note was itself partly delayed, ironically, by the very extraction of expertise, cultural capital, and objects that colonization and subsequent retention entailed. And the legal-ownership argument, while descriptively accurate as a matter of current, existing law in most relevant jurisdictions, does not, in the eyes of critics, settle the separate and more fundamental question of what justice actually requires independent of what current law happens, in each specific jurisdiction, to currently permit or prohibit.","Several prominent museums and national governments have, in recent years, begun shifting policy in response to this sustained pressure, moving from blanket, near-automatic refusal toward negotiated, case-by-case return of specific objects, particularly in high-profile, well-documented cases where the circumstances of original acquisition are least ambiguous and most clearly involved coercion or outright looting. This piecemeal, case-by-case approach satisfies few observers on either side of the underlying debate completely — restitution advocates generally consider the current pace too slow and the selection criteria applied too conservative relative to the full scale of contested holdings, while museum defenders worry, with at least some legitimate institutional basis, about where a fully consistent application of the same underlying restitution principle might eventually, if pursued to its logical conclusion, ultimately lead for their broader collections."]},"rc-p17":{"title":"Language Extinction and Linguistic Diversity","paras":["Of the roughly seven thousand languages currently spoken worldwide, linguists estimate that a substantial proportion — by some widely cited estimates, as many as half — face a serious risk of falling out of active use within the current century, typically through a gradual, multi-generational process in which a language ceases to be transmitted to children as a first language, surviving for a time among an aging population of remaining fluent speakers before eventually falling entirely out of active use once that final generation of speakers has passed. This process, commonly termed language death, has almost always accelerated dramatically as smaller, often indigenous linguistic communities have come into sustained, asymmetric contact with larger, more economically and politically dominant language groups, whether through colonization, urbanization, state-mandated schooling conducted exclusively in a dominant national language, or the more diffuse economic and cultural pressures of globalization more broadly.","The case for actively caring about this loss, beyond a general and somewhat abstract regret at cultural diminishment, rests on several more specific and substantive grounds that linguists and anthropologists have worked to articulate with increasing precision. Each language encodes, often in ways not straightforwardly translatable into other languages without real loss of specificity or nuance, a particular community’s accumulated way of categorizing and making sense of the world — its specific system of kinship terms, its detailed vocabulary for local flora, fauna, and environmental phenomena often developed and refined over many centuries of close, sustained observation, and its particular grammatical structures, which linguists studying the diversity of the world’s languages have found to reveal genuinely different, empirically documented ways that human cognition can organize and represent fundamental concepts like time, space, and causation. When a language with no close surviving relatives disappears, this accumulated, often irreplaceable body of specific cultural and cognitive knowledge is generally lost along with it, in a manner not straightforwardly recoverable even through the most careful subsequent linguistic documentation and archiving efforts.","Documentation efforts — systematically recording vocabulary, grammar, and, crucially, extended natural speech and narrative from remaining fluent speakers before a language’s speaker community disappears entirely — have expanded considerably in recent decades, aided substantially by more affordable, higher-quality digital recording technology and, in some notable and encouraging cases, meaningful collaboration between academic linguists and the language communities themselves, correcting an older pattern where documentation was too often conducted by outside academic researchers with comparatively little direct benefit, and sometimes even active disregard for the interests and wishes, of the community whose language was actually being studied and recorded. Documentation, however, preserves a linguistic record for future scholarly and community reference; it does not, by itself, constitute genuine linguistic revitalization, and the two goals, while frequently complementary and often pursued together in practice, are conceptually and practically distinct.","Revitalization — the more ambitious and considerably more difficult project of restoring or substantially increasing a language’s number of active, everyday speakers, ideally including a meaningful new generation of children raised speaking it as a genuine first or co-first language — has achieved some well-documented, genuinely inspiring successes, most notably in the case of Hebrew’s large-scale revival as an everyday spoken vernacular after centuries of primarily liturgical and scholarly use, and in smaller but still meaningful degree with several indigenous languages in North America, New Zealand, and elsewhere that have specifically implemented sustained language-immersion education programs for children. These successes, however, required an unusually demanding and sustained combination of political will, dedicated and often substantial institutional resources, and a critical mass of committed community members willing to prioritize language transmission as an active, ongoing project across successive generations — a combination that, realistically, is unlikely to be replicable at anything approaching the scale that would meaningfully address the sheer number of currently endangered languages facing the same underlying pressures worldwide."]},"rc-p18":{"title":"Urban Planning and the Social Life of Public Space","paras":["The mid-twentieth-century wave of large-scale urban renewal projects across many Western cities was undertaken with genuine, if in retrospect badly miscalibrated, confidence that modernist planning principles — clear functional separation of residential, commercial, and industrial zones; wide, car-oriented thoroughfares favored over the dense, narrow street networks of older neighborhoods; large open plazas and green spaces designed primarily to be viewed rather than actively, informally used and inhabited — represented an unambiguous, empirically grounded improvement over the organic, often haphazard-seeming street patterns of older, pre-automobile urban neighborhoods that these projects frequently demolished wholesale in the name of progress and rational improvement.","A body of subsequent research and, more influentially in shaping public and professional opinion, sustained on-the-ground observation and critique by planning writers skeptical of these prevailing modernist assumptions found something notably different from what the confident planning theory had actually predicted: many of the newly built, deliberately rationalized spaces went conspicuously underused, functionally sterile despite their considerable design sophistication and investment, while precisely the older, denser, architecturally “disorderly” neighborhoods that planners had confidently targeted for wholesale demolition and replacement often supported markedly more vibrant, safer, and genuinely more socially functional public life than either the demolished neighborhoods’ outward appearance or prevailing planning theory had predicted or, in many documented cases, than the shiny new developments explicitly built to improve upon and replace them.","The explanation that emerged from this sustained observational critique centered on a specific factor modernist planning theory had significantly undervalued in its confident calculations: the quality and density of what came to be called “eyes on the street” — the informal, low-level, largely unplanned social surveillance and interaction generated naturally by mixed-use neighborhoods where shops, homes, and foot traffic coexist closely at close range throughout most hours of the day and evening, rather than being cleanly and deliberately separated by function and activated only at their own specific, narrowly scheduled times. A street lined with small shops, apartments with windows and stoops facing directly onto it, and steady pedestrian traffic throughout the day generates continuous, low-level informal social monitoring that formal, top-down security measures struggle to fully replicate; a monumental plaza surrounded by office towers that empty out completely after five in the evening, by stark contrast, however impressive and rationally planned it may appear in an architectural rendering, can become genuinely unsafe and functionally dead space for the majority of the day precisely because no one has any specific, ongoing reason to actually be there once the scheduled office activity that justified its planned design has ended for the day.","This critique reshaped mainstream professional urban planning practice substantially over subsequent decades, contributing directly to a broader and still ongoing revival of interest in mixed-use zoning, pedestrian-scaled street design, and the deliberate preservation, rather than wholesale demolition, of older neighborhood fabric specifically for the social functions it demonstrably continued to perform even where it had failed to match modernist aesthetic and rationalized functional ideals. It would, however, be an overstatement to describe this shift as total or fully complete even today: large-scale, single-use development driven by cost efficiency, straightforward zoning convenience, and simple institutional habit continues to be built in many contexts, suggesting that the underlying lesson about the social value of density, mixed use, and organic, unplanned street life, however well-documented and widely cited in professional literature, has proven considerably easier for planning professionals to articulate approvingly in principle than to consistently apply in the actual, ongoing practice of urban development."]},"rc-p19":{"title":"The Unreliable Narrator","paras":["The device of the unreliable narrator — a first-person narrative voice whose account of events the reader is meant to regard with some degree of skepticism, whether due to the narrator’s limited knowledge, psychological instability, self-interested deception, or simple naivety — occupies a curious position in literary craft: it asks the reader to do something most conventional narrative technique works hard to avoid asking, namely to actively distrust the very voice delivering the story, while nonetheless extracting meaning, and often considerable emotional investment, from that same distrusted account.","The technique’s effectiveness rests on a specific kind of productive tension the author must carefully calibrate throughout the entire length of the work. Signal the narrator’s unreliability too heavily, too early, or too bluntly, and the reader disengages from the narrative voice entirely, reading everything that follows as a kind of decoding exercise rather than as a story genuinely being told and experienced — checking each claim against an assumed “true” version of events the reader is meanwhile busy reconstructing on their own, at some real cost to actual immersion in the narrative itself. Signal it too subtly, or too late in the work, and the eventual reveal can feel less like a carefully earned recontextualization of everything that preceded it and more like an unfair authorial trick played on a reader who had no genuinely fair opportunity to notice, and correctly interpret, the accumulating clues along the way.","The most enduringly successful examples in the literary canon tend to thread this needle through the careful, deliberate use of what critics term “the gap” — small, specific inconsistencies between what the narrator explicitly claims and what other, more incidental details in the narrative inadvertently reveal, often in ways the narrator themselves does not consciously notice or intend. A narrator might, for instance, insist at length and with obvious sincerity on their own even-handedness and fairness while nonetheless describing every interaction with a particular other character in reflexively, tellingly uncharitable terms — a gap the attentive reader is meant to notice growing, incident by incident, well before the novel’s plot machinery makes it fully, explicitly clear. This technique rewards precisely the kind of close, attentive reading literary fiction more broadly tends to reward and actively cultivate, but it does so through a specific formal device rather than merely through generally elevated or “difficult” prose style for its own sake.","What makes the unreliable narrator more than simply a clever formal trick, when it’s genuinely well executed, is its capacity to formally enact one of fiction’s deeper, more persistent preoccupations: the fundamentally constructed, motivated, and inevitably partial nature of any single account of events, including, when we are being fully honest with ourselves, the internal narratives each of us constructs, moment to moment, about our own lives, motivations, and past actions. A skillfully unreliable narrator does not merely tell a story that happens to turn out to be false or importantly incomplete in some particular respect; the best examples of the form dramatize, through the specific and considered choice of narrative technique itself, the more general, unsettling proposition that all first-person narrative — quite possibly including, uncomfortably, the ones we each silently tell ourselves about our own lives — is, to some meaningful and unavoidable degree, unreliable in exactly this same underlying way, whether or not we happen to notice or acknowledge it as such in any given moment."]},"rc-p20":{"title":"Translation and the Problem of Untranslatability","paras":["Every act of literary translation involves a paradox familiar to anyone who has seriously attempted it: the translator’s explicit task is to produce, in a different language, a text that somehow reads as though it were originally written in that target language, while simultaneously remaining genuinely faithful to a source text whose specific meaning, rhythm, connotation, and cultural resonance are often, on close examination, inseparable from the precise linguistic form of the original — the exact words, in the exact order, carrying the exact set of cultural and historical associations the original author, whether consciously or not, drew on and relied upon in composing it. These two governing goals, fluency in the receiving language and fidelity to the source, pull with genuine force in different, often directly opposed directions, and no translator, however skilled, fully escapes having to make real trade-offs between them on essentially every meaningful sentence-level decision throughout an entire work.","The problem is frequently, and not entirely inaccurately, framed in terms of specific “untranslatable” words or concepts — a particular language’s single word capturing a nuanced emotional state, social relationship, or aesthetic quality that requires an entire explanatory clause, or sometimes a full paragraph of surrounding context, to even begin to approximate adequately in another language. But focusing the discussion primarily on such isolated, admittedly striking lexical gaps somewhat understates the deeper, more pervasive nature of the actual challenge, which operates continuously at every level of a text rather than merely at these occasional dramatic points of vocabulary. Syntax itself carries meaning: the specific rhythm produced by a language’s particular sentence structures, the ordering of information a specific grammar happens to make natural or, alternatively, notably difficult or strained, the connotative weight a given word or specific phrasing carries by virtue of its accumulated prior use and citation within that language’s own literary tradition — none of this transfers cleanly or automatically across languages even when a serviceable, technically accurate dictionary-level equivalent for each individual word can, in fact, be readily found.","Given the genuine, structural depth of this challenge, translation theorists have largely abandoned, as both practically unworkable and theoretically confused, the older, more naive goal of achieving full linguistic “equivalence” between a source text and its translation, replacing it with more explicitly interpretive, less strictly definitional frameworks. One particularly influential approach distinguishes between “domesticating” translations, which prioritize smooth, natural readability in the target language even where this genuinely requires altering culturally specific references or idioms the target-language reader might otherwise find opaque or confusing, and “foreignizing” translations, which deliberately preserve a degree of linguistic and cultural strangeness, actively resisting full assimilation into the target language’s existing literary norms and expectations, on the theoretical grounds that this preserved strangeness itself communicates something real and valuable about the source text’s actual cultural distance and difference from the reader’s own frame of reference — information a fully smoothed-over, domesticated translation would, by its very success at reading naturally, tend to actively erase from the reader’s experience.","Neither of these two general approaches, translation theorists broadly agree, represents a straightforwardly and universally “correct” method applicable in every case; the appropriate choice, most contemporary theorists argue, depends substantially on a given text’s specific purpose, its intended readership, and, perhaps most importantly and most easily overlooked in these methodological debates, the specific power relationship between the source and target languages and cultures in the particular translation context at hand — since a domesticating approach applied when translating from a dominant global language into a marginalized one can function very differently, both practically and politically, than the same general translation strategy applied in the reverse direction, a genuinely political dimension of translation theory that purely technical, linguistics-focused discussions of “accuracy” or “fidelity” alone can too easily and too conveniently overlook."]},"rc-p21":{"title":"The Role and Authority of the Literary Critic","paras":["The professional literary critic occupies an increasingly contested position within contemporary culture, caught between two long-standing but genuinely conflicting conceptions of what critical authority is actually for and where, if anywhere, it legitimately derives from. The first, older conception treats the critic as a kind of trained, credentialed arbiter of quality: someone whose extensive reading, formal literary education, and sustained, disciplined engagement with a given tradition equips them to make comparative aesthetic judgments — this work is more accomplished, more formally innovative, more successful on its own terms than that one — that carry genuine, earned authority precisely because they rest on a breadth and depth of comparative literary knowledge most individual general readers, however genuinely thoughtful and well-read on their own personal terms, typically lack the specific occasion, formal training, or systematic exposure to develop for themselves.","The second, increasingly influential conception treats this entire framing with considerable and growing suspicion, arguing that claims to “expert” aesthetic judgment have historically functioned, whether consciously intended by individual critics or not, to systematically privilege certain narrow, culturally specific tastes and forms — generally those of a comparatively narrow, historically dominant demographic largely responsible for constituting and effectively gatekeeping the critical establishment across a long historical period — while dismissing or marginalizing other genuinely legitimate aesthetic traditions and reading practices, including forms of popular and genre fiction, oral storytelling traditions, and reading practices oriented primarily toward emotional and communal engagement with a text rather than the more detached, analytically distanced formal evaluation traditional criticism has generally, if often only implicitly, prized and rewarded.","Both conceptions, taken to their logical extreme without any moderating qualification, run into genuine and serious difficulty. A purely credentialist view of critical authority, pushed to its limit, risks calcifying into a closed, self-referential system where critics primarily validate and reward works that closely resemble other works critics have already, on separate occasions, decided to validate and reward — a kind of self-perpetuating aesthetic conservatism that has, at various historical moments and in various critical traditions, demonstrably failed to recognize significant, ultimately influential innovation precisely because that innovation did not initially resemble the existing, already-established forms critics had been comparatively trained, both formally and through professional acculturation, to recognize, value, and reward. But a purely relativist view — that all aesthetic judgments are, without genuine remainder, merely expressions of arbitrary individual or cultural preference, with no meaningful, defensible basis for preferring any one to any other — struggles to account for something that persists remarkably robustly even among the view’s own most committed and sophisticated proponents: the strong, widely and cross-culturally shared intuition that at least some aesthetic judgments genuinely are better reasoned, more perceptive, and more genuinely responsive to specific, identifiable features of the actual work under discussion than other, comparatively weaker judgments of the very same work.","A more defensible middle position, increasingly though far from universally influential within contemporary critical practice and theory, treats critical authority as neither an unquestionable, self-justifying credential nor as straightforwardly and entirely illusory, but as something closer to earned, situated expertise of a kind more familiar from other, less contested domains: a critic’s judgment deserves genuine, serious consideration not because of formal institutional position or credential alone, but because of demonstrated, repeatedly exhibited skill at close, careful noticing and at offering genuinely illuminating, well-reasoned articulation of a text’s specific features and effects — an expertise that, crucially, remains itself always open to challenge, productive revision, and even eventual rejection by other readers offering better, more genuinely perceptive reasons for a different, competing account of the very same work, rather than being treated, on this more defensible view, as simply final and beyond further legitimate question."]},"rc-p22":{"title":"Genre Fiction and the Literary Hierarchy","paras":["The distinction between “literary fiction” and “genre fiction” — the latter category encompassing science fiction, fantasy, mystery, romance, and related forms typically organized, both in publishing and in critical discussion, around relatively established generic conventions, reader expectations, and often fairly recognizable plot structures — has functioned for much of the twentieth and early twenty-first centuries as something considerably more consequential than a simple, neutral descriptive category for organizing bookstore shelves or library catalog systems. It has operated, more significantly and more contentiously, as an implicit but remarkably persistent hierarchy, with literary fiction generally positioned, in most institutional contexts (major prize committees, university syllabi, prestige review outlets), as inherently more serious, more artistically ambitious, and more worthy of sustained critical attention than genre fiction, which has often been treated, again mostly implicitly rather than through any single, clearly articulated explicit argument, as comparatively formulaic, commercially rather than artistically motivated, and largely unworthy of the same kind of close critical engagement literary fiction routinely receives as a matter of course.","This hierarchy has faced sustained, increasingly influential challenge from several distinct directions simultaneously. One line of criticism, more historical and empirical in its approach, points out that the category “literary fiction” is itself considerably less stable and less neutrally descriptive than it is generally treated as being — many works now confidently and unproblematically canonized as serious, unambiguously literary classics were, at the time of their original publication, marketed, received, and read as straightforward genre fiction (early gothic novels, much of what we now call detective fiction, and a substantial portion of what would today be readily classified as science fiction), suggesting that the boundary between the two categories has arguably always been more a matter of subsequent critical reception, canonization, and retrospective institutional sorting than of any stable, intrinsic quality reliably distinguishing “literary” works from “genre” ones at the actual moment of their original composition and publication.","A second, more theoretically oriented line of criticism argues that the entire hierarchy rests, when examined closely, on a questionable and largely unexamined assumption: that formal innovation and stylistic ambition — generally the specific, valorized hallmarks of literary fiction as a category — represent an inherently and self-evidently higher, more valuable artistic achievement than the kind of deep, sustained structural and generic mastery, along with the different, no less demanding kind of emotional and thematic sophistication, genre fiction at its considerable best can and regularly does achieve within its own more established conventions and structures. A genuinely well-executed mystery novel, on this view, demonstrates its own considerable and specific craft — in the careful, deliberate management and pacing of suspense, in the precise, calibrated fairness of exactly what information is withheld from and eventually revealed to the reader, in the ultimately satisfying construction of narrative and logical resolution — that is neither straightforwardly inferior nor superior to the different, differently demanding kind of craft a formally innovative literary novel demonstrates in its own comparatively different terms, but simply differently oriented and differently, if no less rigorously, structured.","The practical consequences of this ongoing critical reassessment remain genuinely unevenly distributed across the literary landscape. Major literary prizes have, in recent years, begun somewhat more frequently to recognize works that consciously and deliberately blend or straddle literary and genre conventions, and a small but growing and increasingly vocal number of academic literature departments have begun offering serious, credit-bearing courses specifically devoted to genre fiction analyzed with the same close, sustained critical attention traditionally reserved for canonical literary works. Yet the underlying hierarchy persists robustly enough, in enough institutional contexts and critical venues, that “transcends the genre” remains a genuinely common and only rarely challenged critical compliment applied to particularly well-regarded genre works — a small but genuinely telling phrase that reveals, in its very structure and continued casual use, how much of the old hierarchical assumption it implicitly presupposes and quietly perpetuates even in the specific act of offering what is clearly intended, by the critic using it, as sincere praise."]},"rc-p23":{"title":"The Psychology of Overconfidence","paras":["Among the more robustly replicated findings in cognitive psychology is the observation that people, across an unusually wide range of domains and tested populations, systematically overestimate their own abilities, knowledge, and the accuracy of their own judgments relative to objective, independently verifiable performance measures. This tendency, generally studied under the broad heading of overconfidence, manifests across several distinguishable forms that researchers have worked carefully to separate analytically, since conflating them has historically led to some genuine confusion in both popular and even some earlier academic discussion of the underlying phenomenon.","Overestimation refers to believing one’s actual performance, ability, or degree of control over outcomes is better than it objectively, independently is. Overplacement refers to the closely related but analytically distinct belief that one is better than other people specifically — the well-documented finding, for instance, that a clear majority of drivers in survey after survey rate their own driving skill as distinctly above the median for drivers generally, an arithmetic and statistical impossibility if survey respondents are being even reasonably accurate in their collective self-assessment. And overprecision, arguably the most consequential and the most difficult of the three to correct once entrenched, refers to excessive, unwarranted confidence in the specific accuracy of one’s own beliefs and estimates — expressing near-certainty about a particular conclusion or predicted outcome when the actual, objectively appropriate evidentiary basis would rationally justify only much greater tentativeness and acknowledged uncertainty.","These three closely related biases do not always co-occur consistently within the same individual or the same specific judgment context, an important and sometimes counterintuitive finding in its own right that complicates any simple, unified account of “overconfidence” as though it were a single unified trait or tendency rather than several related but genuinely separable ones. A person can, for example, correctly and even excessively humbly acknowledge that their driving skill is merely average — resisting overplacement in this specific domain — while nonetheless remaining highly and unwarrantedly overprecise about their own specific ability to judge, quickly and accurately, exactly how much following distance is genuinely safe under a given set of road and traffic conditions. This domain-specific variability, robustly documented across numerous independent studies, suggests overconfidence is less a single fixed personality trait some people simply “have” more of than others in some general, across-the-board sense, and more a systematic, predictable cognitive tendency that particular contexts, particular kinds of tasks, and particular available forms of feedback either reliably trigger and reinforce, or alternatively and just as reliably, help meaningfully to suppress and correct.","The single most consistent and well-replicated moderating factor researchers have identified across this varied body of research is the specific quality, timing, and directness of feedback available within a given domain. Domains that reliably provide clear, immediate, and unambiguous feedback on the actual accuracy of one’s judgments — competitive chess, professional weather forecasting, several other similarly structured and closely tracked fields — tend, other things being equal, to show meaningfully lower rates of overconfidence among their most experienced practitioners than domains where feedback is comparatively delayed, ambiguous in its interpretation, or easily and often unconsciously rationalized away after the fact, such as many forms of long-range political, economic, or broadly strategic business forecasting, where a confident, high-profile prediction that later fails to materialize as forecast can often be attributed, with at least superficial plausibility and little immediate personal cost to the forecaster’s ongoing credibility, to some unforeseeable, external, and conveniently unpredictable intervening factor rather than to any genuine, underlying deficiency in the original judgment or forecasting process itself."]},"rc-p24":{"title":"Social Trust and Institutional Decline","paras":["Cross-national survey data collected over several decades reveals a pattern many social scientists consider one of the more consequential, if comparatively under-discussed in mainstream public discourse, long-term social trends of the contemporary era: a sustained, measurable decline in reported trust toward major institutions — national government, mainstream news media, organized religion, and large private corporations among the most consistently and heavily tracked — across a majority of the wealthy democracies for which comparable, methodologically consistent long-term survey data happens to be available. This decline is not, on close and careful examination of the actual underlying survey data, uniform either across different types of institutions or across different demographic groups within a given society, a specific pattern that itself carries real, if easily overlooked, interpretive significance for understanding what is actually driving the broader trend.","Trust in national government has, in most tracked countries, declined more sharply and more consistently than trust in most other institutional categories, though the specific pace, timing, and severity of this particular decline vary considerably by country and appear closely correlated, in the available data, with specific, identifiable national events — significant political scandals, periods of sustained and visible economic hardship, high-profile and well-publicized policy failures — rather than reflecting a single smooth, uniform, and universal downward trend playing out identically and independently across every wealthy democracy studied. Trust in scientific and medical institutions, by contrast, has generally held up somewhat better on average across the same tracked period, though even here recent years have seen new and more visible fracture lines emerge, often correlating closely with specific, high-salience political controversies rather than reflecting any general, across-the-board decline in trust toward scientific expertise or institutions as such.","Several competing, not fully mutually exclusive explanations have been offered for the broader decline, and most serious researchers working in this specific area now favor some considered combination of several factors working together rather than any single, monocausal explanation for such a broad and geographically dispersed social trend. Increased information access through digital media has made institutional failures, hypocrisy, and internal misconduct considerably more visible than in earlier eras when institutional gatekeepers had comparatively greater practical ability to control and shape their own public image and the information the public could readily access about their internal conduct and decision-making. Rising economic inequality has plausibly eroded a specific, historically important sense of shared stake in, and shared benefit from, institutions widely perceived, rightly or wrongly, as now serving increasingly narrow, elite interests rather than a genuinely broad and inclusive public one. And a more diffuse, harder to precisely measure cultural shift toward individualism in many wealthy societies may have gradually weakened the specific kind of default, less-examined deference toward established institutional authority that earlier generations, on average, more readily extended to such institutions.","The consequences of sustained low institutional trust extend well beyond survey researchers’ comparatively narrow professional interest in accurately measuring and tracking public sentiment. Political scientists studying democratic institutions specifically have documented measurable, real-world associations between low institutional trust and several outcomes of direct practical concern: reduced voluntary compliance with public health guidance during emergencies, increased receptivity to conspiratorial explanations for genuinely complex social and political phenomena, and, in the more severe cases studied, measurably reduced willingness among citizens to accept unfavorable democratic election outcomes as ultimately legitimate. Whether declining institutional trust should be understood primarily as a rational, evidence-based public response to genuine institutional failures that these same institutions have, in important respects, actually earned through their own documented conduct, or instead as a broader, less rational cultural drift that, left unaddressed, risks becoming corrosively self-reinforcing regardless of institutions’ actual, measurable performance and conduct going forward, remains a genuinely and seriously contested question among researchers actively working in this specific area, without any clear scholarly consensus yet in sight."]},"rc-p25":{"title":"The Placebo Effect and the Limits of Belief","paras":["The placebo effect — the well-documented phenomenon whereby a treatment with no active therapeutic ingredient nonetheless produces genuine, measurable improvement in a patient’s condition, apparently through mechanisms related to expectation, belief, and the broader context and ritual of receiving what is perceived to be genuine medical care — has moved, over recent decades of sustained research, from a mere methodological nuisance that clinical trials needed to statistically control for and subtract out, toward a legitimate and increasingly well-studied object of serious scientific inquiry in its own right, with its own specific, increasingly well-characterized underlying neurobiological mechanisms.","Early explanations of the phenomenon tended to attribute it, often somewhat dismissively, to patient suggestibility, or in less charitable framings, to a kind of self-deception on the part of the person experiencing improvement — an interpretation that treated the effect as a subjective quirk of individual patient reporting rather than a genuine, physiologically real phenomenon with measurable biological correlates. Subsequent, more rigorous research employing brain imaging and other quantifiable physiological measures has substantially complicated this dismissive picture, demonstrating that placebo treatments can, in specific and well-documented cases, trigger measurable, genuinely physiological changes: the release of the body’s own natural pain-relieving compounds in response to a placebo painkiller, for instance, and detectable changes in specific, relevant brain activity patterns that correlate closely and predictably with subjectively reported symptom improvement, changes that are considerably more difficult to dismiss as merely a matter of patients simply reporting what they believe a researcher wants or expects them to report.","The effect’s magnitude and reliability, however, vary dramatically and in theoretically informative ways across different types of medical conditions, a pattern that has itself become an important active area of ongoing research. Placebo treatments show comparatively strong, well-replicated effects for conditions substantially mediated by subjective, self-reported experience — pain perception, subjectively reported symptoms of clinical depression and anxiety, and self-assessed quality-of-life measures more generally — but show correspondingly minimal to no measurable effect on more objective, directly and independently verifiable physiological measures, such as tumor size in cancer, or specific measured viral load in an active infection. This carefully documented pattern strongly suggests the effect operates predominantly, though evidently not exclusively, through the brain’s own capacity to modulate subjective perception and certain specific physiological processes it directly regulates, rather than through some more general, all-purpose “mind over matter” healing mechanism capable of curing any and all disease processes regardless of their specific underlying biological nature — an important and clinically consequential distinction that unfortunately gets lost, with some real potential for harm, in the more sweeping and less carefully qualified popular claims occasionally made about the placebo effect’s supposed broader healing power.","Perhaps most genuinely surprising among recent findings in this area is evidence that placebo effects can occur, to a real if generally somewhat reduced degree, even when patients are explicitly and honestly informed they are receiving a placebo rather than an active treatment — so-called “open-label” placebos — a finding that meaningfully complicates the earlier, seemingly intuitive assumption that deception, or at minimum some degree of continued patient uncertainty about which specific treatment they are actually receiving, was strictly necessary for the effect to occur at all. This specific finding has opened a genuinely promising and ethically important new avenue for clinical application: the real possibility of harnessing at least part of the placebo effect’s demonstrated therapeutic benefit without requiring the ethically fraught practice of deceiving patients about their actual treatment, a practice standard clinical ethics has long and rightly regarded, on independent ethical grounds, as seriously and legitimately problematic in ordinary clinical care contexts."]},"rc-np1":{"title":"The Economics of Attention in Museums","paras":["Walk through any major art museum and time how long visitors spend in front of each painting. Studies that have done precisely this find the median to be somewhere between fifteen and thirty seconds — less time, often, than it takes to read the wall label. Museum professionals tend to treat this figure as an indictment, either of a distracted public or of institutions that have failed to make their collections engaging. But the statistic may tell us less about attention than about the structure of the museum visit itself.","Consider the problem from the visitor's side. A large museum may display several thousand works across dozens of rooms. A visitor with two hours, a finite supply of energy and an uncertain sense of what is worth seeing faces a classic allocation problem. Spending ten minutes with each painting would allow them to see perhaps a dozen; spending fifteen seconds allows them to survey hundreds. Under these conditions, rapid browsing is not a failure of attention but a rational search strategy — a way of sampling widely in order to identify the few works that merit closer engagement.","The evidence supports this reading. When researchers look beyond the median, they find that dwell times are highly unequal: most works receive a glance, but a small number receive several minutes from a substantial share of visitors. The pattern resembles foraging more than neglect. Visitors skim until something catches them, then stop. What matters, on this view, is not the average time per painting but whether each visitor finds at least a few works that hold them.","This reframing has practical implications. Museums that respond to short dwell times by adding more text, more audio guides and more interactive screens may be making the search problem harder rather than easier, adding to the volume of material visitors must process. Some curators have experimented with the opposite approach: showing fewer works, with more space between them, and encouraging visitors to choose a handful of objects to study closely. Early results suggest that visitors in such displays report greater satisfaction, even though they see less.","None of this means that the fifteen-second glance is ideal. A glance at a Rembrandt is not an experience of a Rembrandt. But the remedy may lie less in exhorting visitors to slow down than in designing spaces that make slowing down a natural consequence of finding what one came, often without knowing it, to find."]},"rc-np2":{"title":"Urban Trees and the Inequality of Shade","paras":["Few urban amenities seem as innocent as a street tree. Trees cool the air, absorb stormwater, reduce noise and, by most accounts, make neighbourhoods more pleasant places to live. Yet the distribution of urban tree cover in many cities is strikingly unequal. Satellite analyses of American cities have found that wealthier neighbourhoods often have far more canopy than poorer ones, and that the difference can translate into surface temperatures several degrees higher in the least-shaded districts during summer heat waves.","Part of the explanation is historical. In many cities, neighbourhoods that were denied mortgage lending in the early twentieth century — a practice known as redlining — remain less green today. Lacking investment, these areas saw fewer trees planted and maintained, and were more likely to be cleared for highways, industry and dense housing. The legacy persists because trees take decades to mature: a planting decision made in the 1930s shapes the canopy of the 2020s.","Planting more trees in under-shaded neighbourhoods would seem to be the obvious remedy, and many cities have launched ambitious programmes to do just that. But these programmes have encountered unexpected resistance. Some residents worry about the costs of maintenance, falling branches, or roots that damage pavements. Others fear, with some justification, that greening a neighbourhood will raise property values and rents, displacing the very residents the trees were meant to benefit — a phenomenon researchers have begun to call 'green gentrification'.","These concerns suggest that the problem cannot be solved by planting alone. Successful programmes tend to involve residents in choosing where trees go, commit the city to long-term maintenance rather than leaving it to homeowners, and pair greening with protections against displacement, such as rent stabilisation or community land trusts. Trees, in other words, are not merely environmental infrastructure; they are embedded in the same economic and political forces that shape everything else in the city.","As heat waves grow more frequent, the stakes of this question will only rise. Shade may come to be seen not as an amenity but as a public health necessity — and its unequal distribution as a matter of justice rather than aesthetics."]},"rc-np3":{"title":"The Myth of the Learning Style","paras":["Ask a room of teachers whether students learn better when instruction matches their preferred 'learning style' — visual, auditory or kinaesthetic — and most will say yes. The idea is intuitive, widely taught in teacher-training programmes and enthusiastically endorsed by students themselves, many of whom can tell you confidently which type of learner they are. It is also, according to a substantial body of research, almost entirely unsupported.","The key test of the learning-styles hypothesis is straightforward. Identify students' preferred styles, randomly assign them to instruction that either matches or does not match those preferences, and measure what they learn. If the hypothesis is correct, students should do better when instruction matches their style. Reviews of the research have found very few studies that use this design at all, and those that do generally find no such benefit. Visual learners do not learn more from diagrams than auditory learners do; both benefit from diagrams when the content is spatial.","This last point hints at what actually matters. The best way to present material depends far more on the material than on the learner. Geography is best taught with maps, music with sound, and a tennis serve with practice, regardless of anyone's stated preference. Moreover, students who are taught in multiple modes — seeing, hearing and doing — tend to learn more than those taught in any single mode, because each representation reinforces the others.","Why, then, does the myth persist? Part of the answer is that people do have genuine preferences, and it is easy to mistake a preference for an aptitude. A student who enjoys diagrams may engage more with them, but that is not the same as learning more from them. Another part is that the idea flatters both teachers and students: it promises a personalised key to every mind, and it offers a ready explanation for failure that does not implicate effort.","Debunking the myth is not merely an academic exercise. Time and money spent assessing learning styles and tailoring lessons to them is time and money not spent on practices with strong evidence behind them, such as spaced repetition and retrieval practice. The harm of the learning-styles myth lies less in what it does than in what it displaces."]},"rc-np4":{"title":"Why Ships Got Bigger","paras":["In 1956, an American trucking entrepreneur named Malcolm McLean loaded fifty-eight aluminium boxes onto a converted tanker in New Jersey and sent them to Texas. The shipping container was not, strictly speaking, a new idea — various forms of standardised cargo boxes had been tried before — but McLean's insistence on a system in which the same box moved seamlessly between ship, truck and train transformed global trade. Before containerisation, loading a ship was a labour-intensive process that could take days, with cargo handled piece by piece by teams of dockworkers. Afterwards, a crane could lift a box from a truck onto a ship in minutes.","The economic effects were profound but not immediate. For more than a decade, ports, unions, shipping lines and railways resisted the new system or failed to adapt to it. Containers required massive investments in specialised cranes, ships and port facilities, and they rendered entire categories of dock labour obsolete. It was only when standards for container sizes and fittings were agreed internationally in the late 1960s that the system's full advantages could be realised.","Once those standards were in place, the logic of scale took over. A ship carrying twice as many containers costs far less than twice as much to build and operate, so shipping lines competed by ordering ever-larger vessels. The largest container ships today carry more than twenty thousand boxes. Ports that could not dredge deep enough channels or build large enough cranes found themselves bypassed, while a handful of mega-ports grew into global hubs.","Economists still debate how much containerisation contributed to the explosion of world trade in the late twentieth century. Some studies suggest that it did more to boost trade than all the tariff reductions of the period combined. Others caution that it is difficult to separate its effects from those of other changes, such as cheaper communications and the opening of China to the world economy.","What is clear is that the humble box changed the geography of production. When shipping goods across oceans became cheap and predictable, manufacturers could split production across continents, sourcing components wherever they were cheapest. The global supply chain, for better and worse, is in large part the container's creation."]},"rc-np5":{"title":"The Return of the Repair Shop","paras":["For much of the twentieth century, repairing broken goods was an ordinary part of life. Radios, shoes, toasters and clothing were mended at home or by local tradespeople, and products were often designed to be opened and fixed. Over recent decades, that culture has largely disappeared in wealthy countries. Goods have become cheaper relative to labour, so that replacing an item often costs less than paying someone to repair it. At the same time, many products have become harder to fix: components are glued rather than screwed together, spare parts are unavailable, and repair manuals are kept confidential.","Manufacturers offer several justifications for designs that resist repair. Sealed devices can be thinner and more water-resistant. Restricting repairs to authorised technicians, they argue, protects consumers from shoddy work and protects the safety of products such as batteries. Critics counter that these justifications often mask a commercial interest: a product that cannot be repaired must be replaced, and a repair monopoly allows manufacturers to charge high prices for their own services.","A growing 'right to repair' movement has challenged these practices. Advocates have pushed for laws requiring manufacturers to make spare parts, tools and repair information available to consumers and independent repair shops. The European Union and several American states have passed such laws in recent years, covering products from smartphones to farm tractors. Farmers, in fact, have been among the movement's most effective advocates, since a tractor that cannot be repaired during harvest because of software locks can mean a ruined crop.","The environmental argument for repair is strong. Manufacturing a new smartphone, for example, accounts for the large majority of the device's lifetime carbon emissions, so extending its life by even a year or two yields significant savings. Electronic waste is among the fastest-growing waste streams in the world, and much of it contains toxic materials.","Yet laws alone may not revive repair culture. The skills once common among ordinary people and local tradespeople have faded, and repair remains expensive where labour costs are high. Some cities have responded with 'repair cafés', volunteer-run events where people bring broken items and learn to fix them together. Whether such initiatives can scale beyond enthusiasts remains to be seen."]},"rc-np6":{"title":"The Authority of the Archive","paras":["There is a peculiar innocence in the way we speak of 'primary sources', as though the documents that survive from the past were simply deposits left by time, waiting to be read. The historian goes to the archive as the geologist goes to the rock face: the material is there, and the task is to interpret it. But archives are not sediment. They are the outcome of a long series of decisions — about what to record, what to keep, how to classify and whom to admit — and these decisions were made, for the most part, by institutions with interests of their own.","The point is not merely that archives are incomplete; every historian knows that. It is that their incompleteness is patterned. A colonial administration recorded what it needed in order to govern: tax assessments, criminal proceedings, reports on unrest. It did not, as a rule, record the internal life of the communities it governed, except where that life became a problem for the state. The colonised therefore appear in the colonial archive chiefly as objects of administration — as taxpayers, defendants, rebels — and the historian who reads that archive at face value will reproduce, without intending to, the administrator's angle of vision.","One response has been to read 'against the grain': to attend to the moments when the archive's categories strain or break, when a court transcript records an unexpected answer, or when a report's anxious tone betrays what its author could not control. Such reading can recover traces of lives the archive was not designed to preserve. But it has its own hazards. Silence is not evidence, and a historian determined to find resistance in every gap risks substituting her own desires for the voices she claims to recover. The archive's distortions do not license the historian's inventions.","A more radical response holds that some pasts are simply unrecoverable from the official record, and that the historian must turn to other sources — oral traditions, material culture, the built landscape — that were produced outside the administrative gaze. This expands the historian's toolkit considerably. Yet it does not escape the underlying problem, for these sources too were shaped by the conditions of their survival. An oral tradition is preserved by those with the standing to transmit it; a building survives because someone found it worth maintaining.","What emerges is not a counsel of despair but a demand for reflexivity. Every source carries the imprint of the forces that made and kept it, and the historian's first question must be not only 'what does this tell me?' but 'why does this exist, and what did its existence require?' The archive does not speak; it is spoken through. The historian's task is to hear both the voice and the throat."]},"rc-np7":{"title":"Rules, Discretion and the Problem of the Particular","paras":["Every legal system must decide how much to govern by rules and how much by discretion. A rule — 'the speed limit is 60 km/h' — is announced in advance, applied mechanically, and indifferent to the circumstances of the particular case. A standard — 'drive at a reasonable speed' — leaves the decision to an official who can weigh circumstances as they arise. Neither is self-evidently superior, and the choice between them recurs in contexts far removed from law: in corporate policy, in parenting, in the design of algorithms.","The virtues of rules are those of predictability. A rule tells citizens in advance what is required of them, constrains officials who might otherwise act arbitrarily or with bias, and is cheap to apply, since it requires no deliberation. Its characteristic vice is that it will sometimes produce results that are plainly wrong. Any rule is a generalisation, and generalisations have exceptions: the driver who exceeds the limit while rushing a dying patient to hospital is caught by the letter of the rule but not by its purpose.","Standards avoid this vice by allowing the decision-maker to respond to the particular. But they purchase that sensitivity at a price. A standard tells citizens less in advance; it invites inconsistency, since different officials will weigh the same circumstances differently; and it opens the door to precisely the bias and arbitrariness that rules were designed to exclude. The discretion that allows a judge to show mercy also allows a judge to show prejudice.","It is tempting to imagine a system that combines the strengths of both — rules for the ordinary case, discretion for the exceptional one. Many legal systems attempt something like this. Yet the hybrid inherits a problem of its own: someone must decide which cases are exceptional, and that decision is itself an exercise of discretion, unconstrained by rule. The exceptions clause becomes the channel through which all the vices of discretion re-enter.","There is, then, no formula that dissolves the dilemma. The choice between rules and standards is ultimately a judgement about which errors a society is more willing to tolerate: the occasional injustice of rigid application, or the diffuse injustices of inconsistency and bias. That judgement will rightly differ across domains. Where the stakes of arbitrariness are high and the officials least trusted, rules may be preferable even at the cost of hard cases; where circumstances vary wildly and officials are accountable, discretion may serve better. What cannot be avoided is the choice itself."]},"rc-np8":{"title":"Authenticity and the Copy","paras":["In 1936, the critic Walter Benjamin argued that mechanical reproduction — photography, film, the printing press — was stripping works of art of their 'aura', the sense of unique presence that attaches to an original object in a particular place and time. A photograph of a cathedral, however faithful, lacks the cathedral's history, its weathered stone, its embeddedness in a specific tradition of worship. As reproductions multiplied, Benjamin suggested, the cult value of art would give way to its exhibition value: art would become something to be seen everywhere rather than venerated somewhere.","Benjamin's prediction appears, at first glance, to have been confounded. Never have originals been more fetishised. Millions travel to Paris each year to stand, often for a few seconds and at a considerable distance, before the Mona Lisa — a painting whose image they have seen countless times and could examine in far greater detail on their phones. Auction prices for original works have reached sums that would have astonished Benjamin's contemporaries, and a painting revealed to be a forgery can lose nearly all of its value overnight, even though nothing about its appearance has changed.","Yet the paradox may be more apparent than real. It is arguably reproduction itself that has manufactured this new aura. The Mona Lisa is famous because it has been reproduced, and the pilgrimage to the original is motivated precisely by the ubiquity of the copies: the original becomes the source of all those images, the point at which the endlessly circulating representation is anchored in a single object. The aura, on this view, has not survived reproduction; it has been produced by it.","If so, the value we assign to originals may rest less on their intrinsic properties than on a kind of social and historical narrative. The forgery that loses its value overnight has not changed; what has changed is the story we can tell about it — whose hand made it, when, and in what relation to the history of art. To value the original is to value a relation to that history, not merely a configuration of pigment.","This conclusion is unsettling for those who believe that aesthetic experience is a direct response to what is before our eyes. It suggests that what we see is always already shaped by what we know, and that the 'pure' encounter with a work of art may be a fiction. But it need not lead to cynicism. A relation to history is not nothing. To stand before an object that was touched by Leonardo is to stand in a particular relation to the past, and that relation can be a genuine, if not purely visual, source of meaning."]},"rc-np9":{"title":"The Measurement of Merit","paras":["Meritocracy is usually defended as the alternative to inherited privilege: positions should go to those who earn them, not to those born into the right families. Few would dispute the principle in the abstract. The difficulties begin when we ask how merit is to be identified. Any practical meritocracy must rely on measures — examinations, credentials, performance reviews — and every measure captures only some of the qualities that might count as merit, while being sensitive to factors that have little to do with it.","Consider the competitive examination. It has the great virtue of impersonality: the examiner does not know the candidate's family or connections, and the same paper is set for all. Yet success in such examinations correlates strongly with the resources available to candidates in the years before — private tuition, quiet places to study, parents with the knowledge to guide preparation. The examination is impartial at the moment of testing, but the competition it adjudicates began long before.","Defenders of examinations reply, with some force, that the alternatives are worse. Interviews, portfolios and personal recommendations are more sensitive to the qualities of character and creativity that examinations miss, but they are also more susceptible to precisely the advantages of class and connection that meritocracy was meant to neutralise. The candidate who knows how to present herself, who has had internships arranged through family friends, who speaks in the accent of the selectors, will tend to do better in a holistic assessment than in a written test. The examination may be a crude instrument, but it is a relatively incorruptible one.","There is a deeper difficulty, however, which no choice of measure can solve. Even a perfect measure of present ability would reward qualities that are themselves partly the product of luck — of genetic endowment, of upbringing, of the accidents that cultivate one talent rather than another. If the earlier advantages of the privileged are unearned, so, in a sense, are the abilities of the gifted. A meritocracy that rewarded only 'pure' effort would need to distinguish effort from the capacity for effort, which is itself unevenly distributed.","None of this implies that positions should be allocated by lottery, or that skill should be disregarded. A surgeon should be chosen for her ability to operate, whatever its origins. The implication is rather that the rewards attached to merit — the income, status and esteem — cannot be justified simply by appeal to desert. Those who succeed under even the fairest meritocracy owe their success, in part, to fortune. A society that forgets this risks breeding, among its winners, a self-satisfaction that meritocracy's original egalitarian impulse would have found troubling."]},"rc-np10":{"title":"Translation and the Untranslatable","paras":["Every few years an article appears listing 'untranslatable words': the Portuguese saudade, the German Schadenfreude, the Japanese wabi-sabi. The lists are charming, and they reinforce a widely held belief that each language carves the world in its own way, leaving residues of meaning that no other language can capture. Yet the very existence of the lists is curious. Each entry is followed by an explanation — in English — of what the untranslatable word means. If the word could not be translated, how did the list-maker convey its meaning?","The answer is that 'untranslatable' usually means 'not translatable by a single word'. Saudade can be rendered as 'a melancholy longing for something absent, which may never return', and while that phrase is clumsier than the original, it is not obviously less precise. What is lost is not meaning so much as economy: the fact that Portuguese has lexicalised this concept, packing it into a single word, while English must assemble it from parts. Whether that loss is trivial or significant depends on what one thinks words do.","If words are merely labels for concepts that exist independently of them, the loss is trivial: the concept survives, merely wearing a longer name. But if words shape how readily concepts come to mind — if having a single word for a feeling makes that feeling easier to notice, name and share — then lexicalisation matters. A culture with a word for saudade may attend to that feeling more often, discuss it more readily, and build more of its art around it. The difference would lie not in what can be thought, but in what tends to be thought.","This more modest claim is easier to defend than the strong version of linguistic relativity, which held that language determines the limits of thought. There is little evidence that speakers are unable to grasp concepts their language lacks words for; people learn new words, and new concepts, throughout their lives. But there is growing evidence that language shapes habits of attention, memory and categorisation in subtle ways. The untranslatable word is less a locked door than a well-worn path.","For the literary translator, this distinction has practical consequences. A translator who renders saudade with a lengthy paraphrase has preserved its sense but altered its weight; what was an everyday word becomes an elaborate construction, and the reader's experience of the text changes accordingly. Translation, on this view, is never a question of whether meaning can be carried across, but of which of its many dimensions — sense, sound, weight, familiarity — the translator chooses to preserve, and which to let go."]}},"questions":[{"id":"vocab-1","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Speech & Expression","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"taciturn. Taciturn means habitually silent or reluctant to speak — fits “answering only in single words.” Garrulous and verbose both mean excessively talkative, the opposite. Eloquent means fluent and persuasive in speech, not about quantity of speech.","stem":"Despite being surrounded by chatty relatives, Arjun remained his usual _______ self, answering only in single words.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"garrulous"},{"k":"B","t":"taciturn"},{"k":"C","t":"eloquent"},{"k":"D","t":"verbose"}]},{"id":"vocab-2","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Speech & Expression","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"garrulous. Garrulous means excessively talkative. Laconic and succinct both mean using very few words — opposite of the three-hour lecture. Reticent means reserved in speech.","stem":"The professor’s _______ lecturing style — three hours without a single pause — exhausted even his most dedicated students.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"laconic"},{"k":"B","t":"succinct"},{"k":"C","t":"garrulous"},{"k":"D","t":"reticent"}]},{"id":"vocab-3","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Speech & Expression","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"laconic. Laconic means using very few words, terse. The other three options all mean talkative.","stem":"Her _______ reply — just “no” — left no room for further discussion.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"verbose"},{"k":"B","t":"laconic"},{"k":"C","t":"loquacious"},{"k":"D","t":"voluble"}]},{"id":"vocab-4","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Speech & Expression","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"equivocal. Equivocal means deliberately ambiguous or vague to avoid commitment. Candid, forthright, and unambiguous all mean the opposite — direct and clear.","stem":"The politician’s _______ answer avoided directly confirming or denying the allegation.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"candid"},{"k":"B","t":"forthright"},{"k":"C","t":"equivocal"},{"k":"D","t":"unambiguous"}]},{"id":"vocab-5","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Speech & Expression","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"candid. Candid means open and honest, straightforward. The contrast clue (“unlike his brother’s guarded answers”) signals the opposite of evasive/circumspect/equivocal.","stem":"Unlike his brother’s guarded answers, Rohan was refreshingly _______, admitting his mistake without hesitation.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"evasive"},{"k":"B","t":"candid"},{"k":"C","t":"equivocal"},{"k":"D","t":"circumspect"}]},{"id":"vocab-6","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Speech & Expression","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"A","explanation":"circumlocution. Circumlocution means using many words where fewer would do, especially to avoid saying something directly. Fits “never quite saying what everyone knew.”","stem":"The ambassador’s speech was full of _______, never quite saying what everyone knew he meant.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"circumlocution"},{"k":"B","t":"brevity"},{"k":"C","t":"candor"},{"k":"D","t":"bluntness"}]},{"id":"vocab-7","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Speech & Expression","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"eloquence. Eloquence means fluent, persuasive speaking ability — matches “gifted orator” and “persuaded.”","stem":"A gifted orator, she could speak with such _______ that even hostile audiences found themselves persuaded.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"ineloquence"},{"k":"B","t":"eloquence"},{"k":"C","t":"taciturnity"},{"k":"D","t":"reticence"}]},{"id":"vocab-8","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Speech & Expression","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"A","explanation":"prevaricating. To prevaricate is to speak evasively or misleadingly, often to avoid the truth or delay stating one’s real point.","stem":"He has a habit of _______ — talking around a topic for ten minutes before ever stating his actual point.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"prevaricating"},{"k":"B","t":"elaborating"},{"k":"C","t":"clarifying"},{"k":"D","t":"summarizing"}]},{"id":"vocab-9","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Speech & Expression","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"voluble. Voluble means speaking rapidly and fluently, talkative — fits needing her to “slow down.”","stem":"The witness’s testimony was so _______ that the lawyer had to ask her to slow down and repeat herself.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"terse"},{"k":"B","t":"voluble"},{"k":"C","t":"reticent"},{"k":"D","t":"laconic"}]},{"id":"vocab-10","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Speech & Expression","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"vague. Vague means lacking clarity or precision — fits “satisfied no one” as a non-answer. The other three all mean clear and direct.","stem":"Rather than answer directly, the CEO gave a _______ statement that satisfied no one.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"forthright"},{"k":"B","t":"vague"},{"k":"C","t":"explicit"},{"k":"D","t":"transparent"}]},{"id":"vocab-11","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Criticism, Praise & Judgment","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"scathing. Scathing means harshly critical — matches the devastating emotional effect described.","stem":"The critic’s _______ review left the young author in tears, questioning whether she should ever write again.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"laudatory"},{"k":"B","t":"scathing"},{"k":"C","t":"tepid"},{"k":"D","t":"glowing"}]},{"id":"vocab-12","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Criticism, Praise & Judgment","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"A","explanation":"laudatory. Laudatory means expressing praise. Fits “praising its ambition.”","stem":"Despite the film’s flaws, most reviewers were _______, praising its ambition and visual style.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"laudatory"},{"k":"B","t":"scathing"},{"k":"C","t":"derisive"},{"k":"D","t":"contemptuous"}]},{"id":"vocab-13","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Criticism, Praise & Judgment","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"tepid. Tepid means lukewarm, showing little enthusiasm — fits “neither strongly positive nor negative.”","stem":"The teacher’s feedback was _______ — neither strongly positive nor negative, just a list of minor observations.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"scathing"},{"k":"B","t":"laudatory"},{"k":"C","t":"tepid"},{"k":"D","t":"glowing"}]},{"id":"vocab-14","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Criticism, Praise & Judgment","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"A","explanation":"disdainful. Disdainful means showing contempt or scorn — fits “refusing to dignify it,” which implies looking down on the criticism.","stem":"He responded to the criticism with _______ silence, refusing to dignify it with a reply.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"disdainful"},{"k":"B","t":"admiring"},{"k":"C","t":"grateful"},{"k":"D","t":"appreciative"}]},{"id":"vocab-15","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Criticism, Praise & Judgment","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"grudging. Grudging means given reluctantly or unwillingly — fits “reluctant but unwilling to reject.”","stem":"The board gave the proposal only _______ approval, clearly reluctant but unwilling to reject it outright.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"enthusiastic"},{"k":"B","t":"grudging"},{"k":"C","t":"wholehearted"},{"k":"D","t":"unqualified"}]},{"id":"vocab-16","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Criticism, Praise & Judgment","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"disparage. Disparage means to belittle or criticize dismissively — fits the contrast with privately thinking it was excellent.","stem":"She was quick to _______ her colleague’s work in meetings, but privately admitted it was excellent.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"commend"},{"k":"B","t":"extol"},{"k":"C","t":"disparage"},{"k":"D","t":"applaud"}]},{"id":"vocab-17","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Criticism, Praise & Judgment","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"exasperation. Exasperation means a feeling of intense irritation mixed with frustration/resignation — closest match to “resigned disappointment.”","stem":"The reviewer’s tone was one of _______ — a kind of resigned disappointment rather than outright anger.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"fury"},{"k":"B","t":"exuberance"},{"k":"C","t":"exasperation"},{"k":"D","t":"elation"}]},{"id":"vocab-18","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Criticism, Praise & Judgment","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"perfunctory. Perfunctory means done as a routine or minimum obligation, without real interest — fits “polite, but not enthusiastic.”","stem":"His speech was met with _______ applause — polite, but clearly not enthusiastic.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"rapturous"},{"k":"B","t":"perfunctory"},{"k":"C","t":"thunderous"},{"k":"D","t":"unanimous"}]},{"id":"vocab-19","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Criticism, Praise & Judgment","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"censure. Censure means strong formal or public criticism — fits criticism of crisis “handling,” implied to be poor.","stem":"The critics were unanimous in their _______ of the government’s handling of the crisis.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"commendation"},{"k":"B","t":"censure"},{"k":"C","t":"endorsement"},{"k":"D","t":"approval"}]},{"id":"vocab-20","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Criticism, Praise & Judgment","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"sycophantic. Sycophantic means excessively flattering toward someone in order to gain advantage — fits “aimed at winning favor.”","stem":"Rather than genuine admiration, his compliments seemed like _______ flattery aimed at winning favor.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"sincere"},{"k":"B","t":"heartfelt"},{"k":"C","t":"sycophantic"},{"k":"D","t":"candid"}]},{"id":"vocab-21","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Temperament & Personality","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"mercurial. Mercurial means subject to sudden, unpredictable changes of mood — fits “cheerful one moment, sullen the next” exactly.","stem":"His _______ moods made him unpredictable — cheerful one moment, sullen the next.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"stable"},{"k":"B","t":"mercurial"},{"k":"C","t":"placid"},{"k":"D","t":"staid"}]},{"id":"vocab-22","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Temperament & Personality","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"placid. Placid means calm and unruffled — fits “unmoved by the panic.”","stem":"Despite the chaos around her, she remained _______, unmoved by the panic of others.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"volatile"},{"k":"B","t":"placid"},{"k":"C","t":"mercurial"},{"k":"D","t":"excitable"}]},{"id":"vocab-23","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Temperament & Personality","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"tenacious. Tenacious means persistent and determined — fits continuing “despite repeated setbacks.”","stem":"His _______ pursuit of the goal, despite repeated setbacks, eventually paid off.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"fickle"},{"k":"B","t":"tenacious"},{"k":"C","t":"capricious"},{"k":"D","t":"desultory"}]},{"id":"vocab-24","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Temperament & Personality","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"capricious. Capricious means given to sudden, unpredictable changes of mind — fits “changing plans on a whim.”","stem":"Her _______ decisions — changing plans on a whim almost daily — frustrated the whole team.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"resolute"},{"k":"B","t":"steadfast"},{"k":"C","t":"capricious"},{"k":"D","t":"tenacious"}]},{"id":"vocab-25","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Temperament & Personality","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"A","explanation":"temerity. Temerity means excessive confidence or boldness, often reckless daring — closest fit here in a positive military-courage sense (though it can carry a negative “reckless” connotation too, it’s the only option meaning boldness).","stem":"Even as a young officer, he displayed remarkable _______ in the face of enemy fire.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"temerity"},{"k":"B","t":"timidity"},{"k":"C","t":"diffidence"},{"k":"D","t":"trepidation"}]},{"id":"vocab-26","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Temperament & Personality","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"diffident. Diffident means shy, lacking self-confidence — fits “never volunteering an opinion, always deferring.”","stem":"His _______ manner in front of his superiors — never volunteering an opinion, always deferring — held back his career.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"audacious"},{"k":"B","t":"diffident"},{"k":"C","t":"brazen"},{"k":"D","t":"assertive"}]},{"id":"vocab-27","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Temperament & Personality","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"brazen. Brazen means bold and shameless — fits shocking behavior on the first day.","stem":"The new intern’s _______ attitude — questioning senior managers openly on his first day — shocked the office.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"diffident"},{"k":"B","t":"reticent"},{"k":"C","t":"brazen"},{"k":"D","t":"demure"}]},{"id":"vocab-28","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Temperament & Personality","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"magnanimous. Magnanimous means generous, especially toward a rival, or noble in spirit — fits generous giving without seeking credit.","stem":"A naturally _______ person, she gave generously to charity without ever seeking recognition.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"miserly"},{"k":"B","t":"magnanimous"},{"k":"C","t":"parsimonious"},{"k":"D","t":"frugal"}]},{"id":"vocab-29","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Temperament & Personality","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"vindictive. Vindictive means having a strong desire for revenge, unforgiving — fits “refusal to forgive even minor slights.”","stem":"His _______ refusal to forgive even minor slights made him difficult to work with.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"magnanimous"},{"k":"B","t":"vindictive"},{"k":"C","t":"benevolent"},{"k":"D","t":"altruistic"}]},{"id":"vocab-30","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Temperament & Personality","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"pragmatic. Pragmatic means dealing with things sensibly and realistically — fits “cool reasoning,” “rarely swayed by emotion.”","stem":"She approached every decision with cool, _______ reasoning, rarely swayed by emotion.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"impetuous"},{"k":"B","t":"pragmatic"},{"k":"C","t":"mercurial"},{"k":"D","t":"capricious"}]},{"id":"vocab-31","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Effort, Diligence & Laziness","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"meticulous. Meticulous means showing great attention to detail, very careful and precise — fits checking “every footnote.”","stem":"Her _______ attention to every footnote and citation made her the most reliable editor on staff.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"cursory"},{"k":"B","t":"meticulous"},{"k":"C","t":"perfunctory"},{"k":"D","t":"superficial"}]},{"id":"vocab-32","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Effort, Diligence & Laziness","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"cursory. Cursory means hasty and not thorough — fits a quick “glance.”","stem":"He gave the report only a _______ glance before signing off on it.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"meticulous"},{"k":"B","t":"thorough"},{"k":"C","t":"cursory"},{"k":"D","t":"exhaustive"}]},{"id":"vocab-33","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Effort, Diligence & Laziness","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"assiduous. Assiduous means showing great care, perseverance, and hard work — fits sustained effort leading to success.","stem":"Years of _______ effort finally allowed the small startup to overtake its larger competitors.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"desultory"},{"k":"B","t":"assiduous"},{"k":"C","t":"indolent"},{"k":"D","t":"languid"}]},{"id":"vocab-34","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Effort, Diligence & Laziness","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"desultory. Desultory means lacking a plan, sporadic, disconnected — fits the disjointed description given.","stem":"The committee’s _______ approach to the investigation — a meeting here, a memo there, with months of inactivity between — drew public criticism.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"rigorous"},{"k":"B","t":"diligent"},{"k":"C","t":"desultory"},{"k":"D","t":"assiduous"}]},{"id":"vocab-35","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Effort, Diligence & Laziness","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"lethargic. Lethargic means sluggish, lacking energy — fits staying in bed with no motivation.","stem":"After the diagnosis, he grew increasingly _______, spending entire days in bed with no motivation to move.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"industrious"},{"k":"B","t":"lethargic"},{"k":"C","t":"energetic"},{"k":"D","t":"vigorous"}]},{"id":"vocab-36","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Effort, Diligence & Laziness","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"A","explanation":"perfunctory. Perfunctory means done with minimum effort, as a mere formality — fits “barely engaged.”","stem":"The professor dismissed the student’s essay as _______, noting it barely engaged with the assigned reading.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"perfunctory"},{"k":"B","t":"exhaustive"},{"k":"C","t":"rigorous"},{"k":"D","t":"comprehensive"}]},{"id":"vocab-37","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Effort, Diligence & Laziness","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"token. Token means done as a symbolic gesture rather than a genuine effort — fits closing the case without real investigation.","stem":"Rather than genuinely investigating the complaint, the manager made only a _______ effort before closing the case.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"concerted"},{"k":"B","t":"token"},{"k":"C","t":"sustained"},{"k":"D","t":"exhaustive"}]},{"id":"vocab-38","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Effort, Diligence & Laziness","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"indolent. Indolent means habitually lazy, avoiding activity — fits preferring to nap over helping.","stem":"His _______ nature meant he would rather nap through the afternoon than help with chores.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"industrious"},{"k":"B","t":"indolent"},{"k":"C","t":"diligent"},{"k":"D","t":"assiduous"}]},{"id":"vocab-39","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Effort, Diligence & Laziness","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"rigorous. Rigorous means thorough and careful — fits “every scenario rehearsed.”","stem":"The team’s _______ preparation — every scenario rehearsed, every contingency planned — paid off during the actual crisis.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"haphazard"},{"k":"B","t":"rigorous"},{"k":"C","t":"cursory"},{"k":"D","t":"desultory"}]},{"id":"vocab-40","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Effort, Diligence & Laziness","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"complacent. Complacent means self-satisfied and unconcerned about further effort — fits “content to let opportunities pass.”","stem":"Once ambitious, he had grown _______ in middle age, content to let opportunities pass him by.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"enterprising"},{"k":"B","t":"complacent"},{"k":"C","t":"zealous"},{"k":"D","t":"driven"}]},{"id":"vocab-41","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Scale, Frequency & Spread","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"ubiquitous. Ubiquitous means present or found everywhere — fits the global smartphone example.","stem":"Smartphones have become so _______ that it is difficult to find anyone, in any country, without one.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"scarce"},{"k":"B","t":"ubiquitous"},{"k":"C","t":"rare"},{"k":"D","t":"isolated"}]},{"id":"vocab-42","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Scale, Frequency & Spread","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"A","explanation":"endemic. Endemic means regularly found in a particular area or population — fits spreading across “the entire region” as its new normal state (note: this can also connote “native to” a region — here it signals a settled, widespread presence).","stem":"The disease, once confined to a single village, has now become _______ across the entire region.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"endemic"},{"k":"B","t":"sporadic"},{"k":"C","t":"isolated"},{"k":"D","t":"contained"}]},{"id":"vocab-43","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Scale, Frequency & Spread","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"sporadic. Sporadic means occurring occasionally, at irregular intervals — fits “a few times a month, unpredictably.”","stem":"Power cuts in the area were _______ rather than constant — a few times a month, unpredictably.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"perpetual"},{"k":"B","t":"incessant"},{"k":"C","t":"sporadic"},{"k":"D","t":"continuous"}]},{"id":"vocab-44","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Scale, Frequency & Spread","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"incessant. Incessant means continuing without pause — fits “never stopping even for a moment.”","stem":"The noise from the construction site was _______, never stopping even for a moment throughout the day.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"intermittent"},{"k":"B","t":"sporadic"},{"k":"C","t":"incessant"},{"k":"D","t":"occasional"}]},{"id":"vocab-45","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Scale, Frequency & Spread","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"fleeting. Fleeting means lasting for a very short time — fits “faded within a year.”","stem":"His fame was _______ — a brief spell of popularity that faded within a year.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"perennial"},{"k":"B","t":"enduring"},{"k":"C","t":"fleeting"},{"k":"D","t":"permanent"}]},{"id":"vocab-46","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Scale, Frequency & Spread","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"perennial. Perennial means recurring or persisting for a long time, enduring — fits recurring across generations.","stem":"Some questions about the nature of consciousness seem _______, resurfacing in every generation of philosophy.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"transient"},{"k":"B","t":"perennial"},{"k":"C","t":"fleeting"},{"k":"D","t":"momentary"}]},{"id":"vocab-47","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Scale, Frequency & Spread","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"intermittent. Intermittent means occurring at irregular intervals, stopping and starting — fits “cutting in and out.”","stem":"The signal was _______, cutting in and out every few minutes rather than staying connected.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"constant"},{"k":"B","t":"intermittent"},{"k":"C","t":"uninterrupted"},{"k":"D","t":"continuous"}]},{"id":"vocab-48","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Scale, Frequency & Spread","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"A","explanation":"rampant. Rampant means flourishing or spreading unchecked, widespread — fits corruption becoming routine and expected.","stem":"Corruption had become so _______ in the department that even junior clerks expected bribes as routine.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"rampant"},{"k":"B","t":"rare"},{"k":"C","t":"contained"},{"k":"D","t":"isolated"}]},{"id":"vocab-49","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Scale, Frequency & Spread","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"erratic. Erratic means irregular and unpredictable — fits “nobody could predict.”","stem":"The volatile currency made prices _______ — nobody could predict what anything would cost a week ahead.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"stable"},{"k":"B","t":"erratic"},{"k":"C","t":"fixed"},{"k":"D","t":"predictable"}]},{"id":"vocab-50","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Scale, Frequency & Spread","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"persistent. Persistent means continuing to exist over a prolonged period — fits remaining a feature after the treaty.","stem":"Even after the treaty, low-level border skirmishes remained a _______ feature of relations between the two countries.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"transient"},{"k":"B","t":"fleeting"},{"k":"C","t":"persistent"},{"k":"D","t":"momentary"}]},{"id":"vocab-51","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Change, Impact & Consequence","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"alleviate. Alleviate means to make (a problem) less severe — fits the known benefit of exercise.","stem":"Regular exercise is known to _______ the symptoms of chronic stress.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"exacerbate"},{"k":"B","t":"alleviate"},{"k":"C","t":"aggravate"},{"k":"D","t":"intensify"}]},{"id":"vocab-52","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Change, Impact & Consequence","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"exacerbate. Exacerbate means to make a problem worse — fits ignoring a leak leading to worse damage.","stem":"Ignoring the leak will only _______ the structural damage to the building.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"mitigate"},{"k":"B","t":"alleviate"},{"k":"C","t":"exacerbate"},{"k":"D","t":"diminish"}]},{"id":"vocab-53","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Change, Impact & Consequence","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"mitigate. Mitigate means to make less severe or intense — fits a policy designed to reduce impact.","stem":"The new policy was designed to _______ the impact of rising fuel prices on low-income households.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"exacerbate"},{"k":"B","t":"mitigate"},{"k":"C","t":"intensify"},{"k":"D","t":"aggravate"}]},{"id":"vocab-54","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Change, Impact & Consequence","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"A","explanation":"catalyst. Catalyst means something that precipitates an event or change — fits triggering delayed reforms.","stem":"The scandal acted as a _______ for reforms that had been delayed for years.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"catalyst"},{"k":"B","t":"impediment"},{"k":"C","t":"deterrent"},{"k":"D","t":"obstacle"}]},{"id":"vocab-55","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Change, Impact & Consequence","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"impede. Impede means to delay or obstruct — fits red tape slowing a rollout.","stem":"Bureaucratic red tape continues to _______ the rollout of the new healthcare scheme.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"facilitate"},{"k":"B","t":"expedite"},{"k":"C","t":"impede"},{"k":"D","t":"accelerate"}]},{"id":"vocab-56","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Change, Impact & Consequence","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"facilitate. Facilitate means to make an action or process easier — fits tools enabling collaboration.","stem":"Digital tools have done much to _______ collaboration between researchers across countries.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"impede"},{"k":"B","t":"hinder"},{"k":"C","t":"facilitate"},{"k":"D","t":"obstruct"}]},{"id":"vocab-57","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Change, Impact & Consequence","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"static. Static means not moving or changing, stationary — fits “neither growth nor decline.”","stem":"The economy remained largely _______ for the decade, showing neither growth nor decline.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"volatile"},{"k":"B","t":"dynamic"},{"k":"C","t":"static"},{"k":"D","t":"turbulent"}]},{"id":"vocab-58","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Change, Impact & Consequence","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"dynamic. Dynamic means characterized by constant change or activity — fits new companies emerging monthly.","stem":"After years of stagnation, the sector suddenly became _______, with new companies and ideas emerging monthly.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"static"},{"k":"B","t":"dynamic"},{"k":"C","t":"inert"},{"k":"D","t":"dormant"}]},{"id":"vocab-59","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Change, Impact & Consequence","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"A","explanation":"impetus. Impetus means the force or energy that drives something forward, a stimulus — fits driving “sweeping changes.”","stem":"The committee’s report was the _______ for the sweeping changes that followed in the industry.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"impetus"},{"k":"B","t":"hindrance"},{"k":"C","t":"deterrent"},{"k":"D","t":"obstruction"}]},{"id":"vocab-60","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Change, Impact & Consequence","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"deleterious. Deleterious means causing harm or damage — fits negative health effects from chemical exposure.","stem":"Prolonged exposure to the chemical is known to have a _______ effect on the nervous system.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"salubrious"},{"k":"B","t":"beneficial"},{"k":"C","t":"deleterious"},{"k":"D","t":"salutary"}]},{"id":"vocab-61","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Honesty, Deception & Motive","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"duplicitous. Duplicitous means deceitful, engaging in double-dealing — fits a friendly appearance hiding a hidden decision.","stem":"His _______ smile hid the fact that he had already decided to fire half the department.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"genuine"},{"k":"B","t":"duplicitous"},{"k":"C","t":"candid"},{"k":"D","t":"guileless"}]},{"id":"vocab-62","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Honesty, Deception & Motive","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"forthright. Forthright means direct and outspoken — fits “always saying exactly what she meant.”","stem":"She was praised for her _______ approach to negotiations — always saying exactly what she meant.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"evasive"},{"k":"B","t":"forthright"},{"k":"C","t":"equivocal"},{"k":"D","t":"devious"}]},{"id":"vocab-63","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Honesty, Deception & Motive","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"unconvincing. Unconvincing fits “fueled further suspicion” — the denial failed to persuade. (Note: this tests reading the causal logic rather than a rare word — a reminder that not every blank needs an exotic word to test precision.)","stem":"The minister’s _______ denial only fueled further suspicion that he was hiding something.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"convincing"},{"k":"B","t":"transparent"},{"k":"C","t":"unconvincing"},{"k":"D","t":"candid"}]},{"id":"vocab-64","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Honesty, Deception & Motive","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"sycophant. A sycophant is someone who acts obsequiously toward people in power to gain advantage — fits “always agreeing eagerly with whoever held power.”","stem":"He was known around the office as something of a _______, always agreeing eagerly with whoever held power.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"skeptic"},{"k":"B","t":"sycophant"},{"k":"C","t":"critic"},{"k":"D","t":"contrarian"}]},{"id":"vocab-65","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Honesty, Deception & Motive","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"A","explanation":"obsequious. Obsequious means excessively eager to please or obey, servile — fits the described flattering behavior.","stem":"Her _______ manner toward her boss — constant compliments, exaggerated laughter at his jokes — embarrassed her colleagues.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"obsequious"},{"k":"B","t":"candid"},{"k":"C","t":"forthright"},{"k":"D","t":"blunt"}]},{"id":"vocab-66","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Honesty, Deception & Motive","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"altruistic. Altruistic means showing selfless concern for others’ wellbeing — fits “gained nothing personally.”","stem":"The whistleblower’s motives were entirely _______ — she gained nothing personally from the disclosure.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"mercenary"},{"k":"B","t":"altruistic"},{"k":"C","t":"self-serving"},{"k":"D","t":"opportunistic"}]},{"id":"vocab-67","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Honesty, Deception & Motive","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"opportunistic. Opportunistic means exploiting circumstances for personal advantage with little regard for principle — fits switching parties purely for personal gain.","stem":"His decision to switch parties just before the election, purely to secure a ministerial post, was widely seen as _______.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"principled"},{"k":"B","t":"altruistic"},{"k":"C","t":"opportunistic"},{"k":"D","t":"selfless"}]},{"id":"vocab-68","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Honesty, Deception & Motive","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"calculated. Calculated means done deliberately with a specific outcome in mind — fits suspicious timing before a PR crisis.","stem":"Rather than a spontaneous gesture, the donation appeared entirely _______, timed just before the company’s public image crisis.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"genuine"},{"k":"B","t":"calculated"},{"k":"C","t":"sincere"},{"k":"D","t":"unplanned"}]},{"id":"vocab-69","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Honesty, Deception & Motive","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"blatant. Blatant means done openly and unashamedly, glaringly obvious — fits contrasting sharply with witness statements as an obvious lie.","stem":"His account of the accident, when compared with witness statements, appeared to be a _______ fabrication.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"plausible"},{"k":"B","t":"blatant"},{"k":"C","t":"subtle"},{"k":"D","t":"credible"}]},{"id":"vocab-70","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Honesty, Deception & Motive","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"tokenism. Tokenism means making only a symbolic effort, especially to comply with expectations, without genuine change — fits “no real change in practices.”","stem":"The company’s environmental claims turned out to be little more than _______, with no real change in its practices.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"substance"},{"k":"B","t":"tokenism"},{"k":"C","t":"commitment"},{"k":"D","t":"reform"}]},{"id":"vocab-71","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Scarcity, Excess & Measure","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"parsimonious. Parsimonious means unwilling to spend money, extremely frugal — fits reusing tea bags despite wealth.","stem":"Even as a millionaire, he remained _______, reusing tea bags and turning off every unused light.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"lavish"},{"k":"B","t":"parsimonious"},{"k":"C","t":"profligate"},{"k":"D","t":"extravagant"}]},{"id":"vocab-72","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Scarcity, Excess & Measure","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"profligate. Profligate means recklessly extravagant, wasteful with money — fits three yachts emptying a fortune.","stem":"The heir’s _______ spending — three yachts in one year — quickly emptied the family fortune.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"frugal"},{"k":"B","t":"parsimonious"},{"k":"C","t":"profligate"},{"k":"D","t":"austere"}]},{"id":"vocab-73","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Scarcity, Excess & Measure","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"austerity. Austerity means difficult economic conditions created by government measures to reduce spending — fits slashing public spending. (Grammatically the sentence wants a noun here — austerity fits as “austerity measures.”)","stem":"After the crash, the government imposed _______ measures, slashing public spending across every ministry.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"lavish"},{"k":"B","t":"austerity"},{"k":"C","t":"extravagant"},{"k":"D","t":"generous"}]},{"id":"vocab-74","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Scarcity, Excess & Measure","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"ostentatious. Ostentatious means designed to impress, showy — fits the extravagant wedding details.","stem":"The wedding was famously _______, with imported flowers, a live orchestra, and a guest list of a thousand.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"austere"},{"k":"B","t":"frugal"},{"k":"C","t":"ostentatious"},{"k":"D","t":"modest"}]},{"id":"vocab-75","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Scarcity, Excess & Measure","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"austere. Austere means severe or plain in appearance, unadorned — fits bare walls and minimal furniture.","stem":"Their apartment was strikingly _______ — bare white walls, a single chair, nothing more.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"ostentatious"},{"k":"B","t":"austere"},{"k":"C","t":"lavish"},{"k":"D","t":"opulent"}]},{"id":"vocab-76","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Scarcity, Excess & Measure","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"opulent. Opulent means lavishly wealthy or luxurious — fits gold leaf and marble.","stem":"The palace’s _______ interiors, covered in gold leaf and imported marble, left visitors speechless.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"austere"},{"k":"B","t":"spartan"},{"k":"C","t":"opulent"},{"k":"D","t":"minimalist"}]},{"id":"vocab-77","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Scarcity, Excess & Measure","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"judicious. Judicious means having or showing good sense and judgment, especially in careful use of resources — fits careful resource management.","stem":"Given the tight budget, the team had to be _______ with resources, using every rupee carefully.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"profligate"},{"k":"B","t":"judicious"},{"k":"C","t":"extravagant"},{"k":"D","t":"wasteful"}]},{"id":"vocab-78","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Scarcity, Excess & Measure","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"exaggerated. Exaggerated means represented as larger or better than is actually the case — fits claims beyond what trials supported.","stem":"His claims about the drug’s benefits were wildly _______, far beyond what the clinical trials supported.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"modest"},{"k":"B","t":"exaggerated"},{"k":"C","t":"understated"},{"k":"D","t":"conservative"}]},{"id":"vocab-79","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Scarcity, Excess & Measure","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"measured. Measured means carefully considered and restrained — fits “never claim more than the data could support.”","stem":"The report’s conclusions were notably _______, careful never to claim more than the data could support.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"overstated"},{"k":"B","t":"measured"},{"k":"C","t":"exaggerated"},{"k":"D","t":"hyperbolic"}]},{"id":"vocab-80","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Scarcity, Excess & Measure","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"excessive. Excessive means more than is necessary or desirable, immoderate — fits overwhelming the story.","stem":"Critics called the film’s special effects _______, so over-the-top that they overwhelmed the story.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"understated"},{"k":"B","t":"subtle"},{"k":"C","t":"excessive"},{"k":"D","t":"restrained"}]},{"id":"vocab-81","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Clarity, Complexity & Understanding","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"lucid. Lucid means expressed clearly, easy to understand — fits first-time users having no trouble.","stem":"The instructions were so _______ that even first-time users had no trouble following them.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"convoluted"},{"k":"B","t":"lucid"},{"k":"C","t":"obscure"},{"k":"D","t":"cryptic"}]},{"id":"vocab-82","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Clarity, Complexity & Understanding","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"convoluted. Convoluted means extremely complex and difficult to follow — fits needing three lawyers for one clause.","stem":"The contract’s _______ language required three lawyers to interpret a single clause.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"lucid"},{"k":"B","t":"convoluted"},{"k":"C","t":"transparent"},{"k":"D","t":"plain"}]},{"id":"vocab-83","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Clarity, Complexity & Understanding","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"esoteric. Esoteric means intended for or understood by only a small, specialized group — fits fewer than a dozen qualified evaluators.","stem":"His area of research was so _______ that fewer than a dozen people worldwide could evaluate his papers.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"mainstream"},{"k":"B","t":"accessible"},{"k":"C","t":"esoteric"},{"k":"D","t":"popular"}]},{"id":"vocab-84","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Clarity, Complexity & Understanding","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"arcane. Arcane means understood by few, mysterious or secret — fits contrasting with “simple and intuitive” for beginners.","stem":"The professor had a gift for making _______ concepts feel simple and intuitive to beginners.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"elementary"},{"k":"B","t":"arcane"},{"k":"C","t":"basic"},{"k":"D","t":"simple"}]},{"id":"vocab-85","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Clarity, Complexity & Understanding","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"lucid. Lucid fits “no jargon, no digressions” as a description of clear explanation.","stem":"Despite the topic’s complexity, her explanation was refreshingly _______ — no jargon, no digressions.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"obscure"},{"k":"B","t":"opaque"},{"k":"C","t":"lucid"},{"k":"D","t":"convoluted"}]},{"id":"vocab-86","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Clarity, Complexity & Understanding","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"opaque. Opaque means hard to understand, unclear — fits resisting translation for centuries.","stem":"The ancient manuscript’s meaning remained _______ to scholars for centuries, resisting every attempted translation.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"transparent"},{"k":"B","t":"opaque"},{"k":"C","t":"evident"},{"k":"D","t":"obvious"}]},{"id":"vocab-87","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Clarity, Complexity & Understanding","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"nebulous. Nebulous means vague or ill-defined, unclear — fits phrasing that obscures meaning.","stem":"Rather than tackle the issue directly, the memo was full of _______ phrasing that obscured its actual message.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"unambiguous"},{"k":"B","t":"explicit"},{"k":"C","t":"nebulous"},{"k":"D","t":"precise"}]},{"id":"vocab-88","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Clarity, Complexity & Understanding","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"profound. Profound means very great or intense, showing deep insight or understanding — fits “genuinely” deep insight rewarding careful readers.","stem":"The philosopher’s writing, while dense, rewards careful readers with genuinely _______ insight.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"shallow"},{"k":"B","t":"superficial"},{"k":"C","t":"profound"},{"k":"D","t":"trivial"}]},{"id":"vocab-89","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Clarity, Complexity & Understanding","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"banal. Banal means lacking originality, so overused it has become uninteresting — fits repeated arguments without new substance.","stem":"Much of the discussion at the conference felt _______ — the same arguments repeated without new substance.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"novel"},{"k":"B","t":"banal"},{"k":"C","t":"original"},{"k":"D","t":"groundbreaking"}]},{"id":"vocab-90","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Clarity, Complexity & Understanding","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"novel. Novel means new, original, not previously known or used — fits “borrowing nothing from previous solutions.”","stem":"The startup’s approach to the old problem was refreshingly _______, borrowing nothing from previous solutions.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"derivative"},{"k":"B","t":"conventional"},{"k":"C","t":"novel"},{"k":"D","t":"banal"}]},{"id":"vocab-91","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Agreement, Conflict & Relationship","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"harmony. Harmony means agreement or concord — fits rarely needing to escalate disagreements.","stem":"The two departments worked in surprising _______, rarely needing to escalate disagreements to management.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"discord"},{"k":"B","t":"harmony"},{"k":"C","t":"friction"},{"k":"D","t":"conflict"}]},{"id":"vocab-92","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Agreement, Conflict & Relationship","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"animosity. Animosity means strong hostility, ill will — fits a shocking wedding announcement between hostile families.","stem":"Years of _______ between the two families made the wedding announcement all the more shocking.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"amity"},{"k":"B","t":"accord"},{"k":"C","t":"animosity"},{"k":"D","t":"camaraderie"}]},{"id":"vocab-93","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Agreement, Conflict & Relationship","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"rapport. Rapport means a close, harmonious relationship characterized by mutual understanding — fits exchanging ideas by letter despite rivalry.","stem":"Despite their public rivalry, the two scientists shared a private _______, exchanging ideas by letter for decades.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"enmity"},{"k":"B","t":"rapport"},{"k":"C","t":"hostility"},{"k":"D","t":"antagonism"}]},{"id":"vocab-94","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Agreement, Conflict & Relationship","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"friction. Friction means conflict or disagreement caused by differing views — fits clashing corporate cultures.","stem":"The merger created immediate _______ between the two corporate cultures, one hierarchical and one famously informal.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"synergy"},{"k":"B","t":"friction"},{"k":"C","t":"alignment"},{"k":"D","t":"cohesion"}]},{"id":"vocab-95","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Agreement, Conflict & Relationship","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"reconciliation. Reconciliation means the restoration of friendly relations, or reaching agreement between differing views — fits the goal of peace talks.","stem":"The peace talks aimed to find a lasting _______ between two positions that had seemed irreconcilable.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"rupture"},{"k":"B","t":"schism"},{"k":"C","t":"reconciliation"},{"k":"D","t":"estrangement"}]},{"id":"vocab-96","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Agreement, Conflict & Relationship","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"rift. Rift means a serious break or estrangement between people, previously friendly — fits “permanent” break between former partners.","stem":"The scandal caused a permanent _______ between the two former business partners.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"rapport"},{"k":"B","t":"rift"},{"k":"C","t":"alliance"},{"k":"D","t":"accord"}]},{"id":"vocab-97","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Agreement, Conflict & Relationship","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"disparate. Disparate means essentially different in kind, not allowing comparison — fits “opposite directions with no overlap.”","stem":"The two proposals were entirely _______, addressing the problem from opposite directions with no overlap.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"congruent"},{"k":"B","t":"compatible"},{"k":"C","t":"disparate"},{"k":"D","t":"aligned"}]},{"id":"vocab-98","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Agreement, Conflict & Relationship","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"congruent. Congruent means in agreement or harmony, matching — fits opinions matching “right down to the smallest detail.”","stem":"Surprisingly, the twins’ opinions on the matter were entirely _______, right down to the smallest detail.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"disparate"},{"k":"B","t":"divergent"},{"k":"C","t":"congruent"},{"k":"D","t":"conflicting"}]},{"id":"vocab-99","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Agreement, Conflict & Relationship","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"dichotomy. Dichotomy means a division or contrast between two things that are represented as opposed — fits the split between two shareholder camps.","stem":"The board’s decision created a clear _______ between shareholders who wanted growth and those who wanted stability.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"consensus"},{"k":"B","t":"dichotomy"},{"k":"C","t":"unity"},{"k":"D","t":"accord"}]},{"id":"vocab-100","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Agreement, Conflict & Relationship","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"consensus. Consensus means general agreement — fits “every member agreeing to the final wording.”","stem":"After weeks of debate, the committee finally reached _______, with every member agreeing to the final wording.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"discord"},{"k":"B","t":"consensus"},{"k":"C","t":"impasse"},{"k":"D","t":"stalemate"}]},{"id":"grammar-1","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Subject-Verb Agreement","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"The subject is “collection” (singular), not “manuscripts.” Should be “was damaged.”","segments":["The collection of rare manuscripts","housed in the university library","were damaged","during the recent flood."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"collection of rare manuscripts"},{"k":"B","t":"housed in the university library"},{"k":"C","t":"were damaged"},{"k":"D","t":"during the recent flood."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-2","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Subject-Verb Agreement","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"With “neither…nor,” the verb agrees with the nearer subject — “employees” (plural) — so it should be “were aware.”","segments":["Neither the manager nor the employees","was aware","that the merger","had already been finalized."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Neither the manager nor the employees"},{"k":"B","t":"was aware"},{"k":"C","t":"that the merger"},{"k":"D","t":"had already been finalized."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-3","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Subject-Verb Agreement","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"“The number of” takes a singular verb (“the number… has increased”); it’s “a number of students have” that takes plural. Here “the number” is the subject, so singular is correct.","segments":["The number of students","opting for online courses","have increased","significantly this year."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The number of students"},{"k":"B","t":"opting for online courses"},{"k":"C","t":"have increased"},{"k":"D","t":"significantly this year."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-4","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Subject-Verb Agreement","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"“Each” is singular regardless of the plural noun that follows it in the prepositional phrase. Should be “has submitted.”","segments":["Each of the applicants","in the final round","have submitted","an impressive portfolio."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Each of the applicants"},{"k":"B","t":"in the final round"},{"k":"C","t":"have submitted"},{"k":"D","t":"an impressive portfolio."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-5","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Subject-Verb Agreement","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"D","explanation":"Should be “serves” — “policy” (singular) is the subject of this subordinate clause.","segments":["The committee","is divided","on whether the new policy","serve the public interest."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The committee"},{"k":"B","t":"is divided"},{"k":"C","t":"on whether the new policy"},{"k":"D","t":"serve the public interest."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-6","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Subject-Verb Agreement","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"“A majority of the voters” takes a plural verb here since it refers to individual voters, not a single collective decision — should be “believe.”","segments":["A vast majority of the voters","in the survey","believes","the reform is necessary."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"A vast majority of the voters"},{"k":"B","t":"in the survey"},{"k":"C","t":"believes"},{"k":"D","t":"the reform is necessary."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-7","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Subject-Verb Agreement","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"“Along with” is a prepositional phrase, not a conjunction — it doesn’t make the subject plural. “Economics” (as a subject name) is singular here, so it should be “is considered.”","segments":["Economics, along with statistics and finance,","are considered","essential subjects","for this degree."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Economics, along with statistics and finance,"},{"k":"B","t":"are considered"},{"k":"C","t":"essential subjects"},{"k":"D","t":"for this degree."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-8","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Subject-Verb Agreement","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"E","explanation":"No Error. “Jury” acting with a unified opinion can correctly take “were” in this context where the emphasis is on the individual jurors reaching unanimity together — many style guides accept collective nouns with plural verbs when individual members’ actions are emphasized; this sentence, emphasizing “unanimous… in their verdict,” is idiomatically acceptable as written.","segments":["The jury","were unanimous","in their verdict","after six hours of deliberation."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The jury"},{"k":"B","t":"were unanimous"},{"k":"C","t":"in their verdict"},{"k":"D","t":"after six hours of deliberation."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-9","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Subject-Verb Agreement","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"With “either…or,” the verb agrees with the nearer subject, “supervisor” (singular) — should be “is responsible.”","segments":["Either the workers or the supervisor","are responsible","for reporting","the safety violation."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Either the workers or the supervisor"},{"k":"B","t":"are responsible"},{"k":"C","t":"for reporting"},{"k":"D","t":"the safety violation."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-10","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Subject-Verb Agreement","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"E","explanation":"No Error. “Data,” though technically the plural of “datum,” is now standard in modern usage as a singular mass noun (“the data suggests”) in most style guides including CAT-level convention — acceptable as written.","segments":["The data","collected over five years","suggests","a clear seasonal pattern."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The data"},{"k":"B","t":"collected over five years"},{"k":"C","t":"suggests"},{"k":"D","t":"a clear seasonal pattern."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-11","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Tenses","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"An action completed before another past action needs past perfect: “had already lost.”","segments":["By the time the ambulance arrived,","the patient","already lost","consciousness."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"By the time the ambulance arrived,"},{"k":"B","t":"the patient"},{"k":"C","t":"already lost"},{"k":"D","t":"consciousness."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-12","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Tenses","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"D","explanation":"“Since” is used with a point in time (“since 2019”); “for” is used with a duration (“for five years”).","segments":["She has worked","at the firm","since","five years."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"She has worked"},{"k":"B","t":"at the firm"},{"k":"C","t":"since"},{"k":"D","t":"five years."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-13","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Tenses","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"E","explanation":"No Error. A general scientific truth stays in present tense even inside a past-tense reporting clause — correct as written.","segments":["Scientists confirmed","that light","travels faster","than sound."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Scientists confirmed"},{"k":"B","t":"that light"},{"k":"C","t":"travels faster"},{"k":"D","t":"than sound."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-14","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Tenses","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"Time clauses starting with “as soon as,” “when,” “before,” “after,” “until” use present tense even for future meaning: “as soon as I reach.”","segments":["I will call you","as soon as","I will reach","the airport."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"I will call you"},{"k":"B","t":"as soon as"},{"k":"C","t":"I will reach"},{"k":"D","t":"the airport."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-15","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Tenses","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"A","explanation":"“Since” with a past starting point requires present perfect continuous: “He has been living in Mumbai.”","segments":["He is living in Mumbai","since","he got","his first job there."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"He is living in Mumbai"},{"k":"B","t":"since"},{"k":"C","t":"he got"},{"k":"D","t":"his first job there."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-16","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Tenses","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"A","explanation":"Third conditional uses simple past perfect in the “if” clause, not “would have”: “If she had studied.”","segments":["If she would have studied","harder,","she would have passed","the exam."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"If she would have studied"},{"k":"B","t":"harder,"},{"k":"C","t":"she would have passed"},{"k":"D","t":"the exam."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-17","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Tenses","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"D","explanation":"Should be simple past to match the sequence: “by the time we reached the station.”","segments":["The train","had already left","by the time","we reach the station."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The train"},{"k":"B","t":"had already left"},{"k":"C","t":"by the time"},{"k":"D","t":"we reach the station."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-18","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Tenses","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"Tense inconsistency — “having finished” (past participle) pairs naturally with a past-tense main verb: “she watched television.”","segments":["Having finished","her homework,","she watches","television for an hour."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Having finished"},{"k":"B","t":"her homework,"},{"k":"C","t":"she watches"},{"k":"D","t":"television for an hour."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-19","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Tenses","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"Simple past fits a specific past time reference (“last quarter”), not past perfect: “unemployment rose sharply.”","segments":["The report states","that unemployment","had risen","sharply last quarter."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The report states"},{"k":"B","t":"that unemployment"},{"k":"C","t":"had risen"},{"k":"D","t":"sharply last quarter."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-20","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Tenses","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"E","explanation":"No Error. Future perfect (“will have overtaken”) correctly describes an action expected to be completed before a future point in time (“by 2030”) — correct as written.","segments":["By 2030,","most experts believe","renewable energy will have overtaken","fossil fuels in most economies."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"By 2030,"},{"k":"B","t":"most experts believe"},{"k":"C","t":"renewable energy will have overtaken"},{"k":"D","t":"fossil fuels in most economies."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-21","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Articles","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"“One” starts with a consonant sound (/w/), so it takes “a,” not “an”: “a one-year-old.”","segments":["She is","an one-year-old","child prodigy","in classical music."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"She is"},{"k":"B","t":"an one-year-old"},{"k":"C","t":"child prodigy"},{"k":"D","t":"in classical music."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-22","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Articles","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"“Honest” starts with a silent H, so the sound is a vowel — should be “an honest.”","segments":["He wants to become","a honest","and respected","journalist."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"He wants to become"},{"k":"B","t":"a honest"},{"k":"C","t":"and respected"},{"k":"D","t":"journalist."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-23","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Articles","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Superlatives require “the”: “the longest river.”","segments":["The Ganga is","longest river","that flows entirely","within India."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The Ganga is"},{"k":"B","t":"longest river"},{"k":"C","t":"that flows entirely"},{"k":"D","t":"within India."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-24","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Articles","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"“Unicorn” starts with the /y/ sound, a consonant sound, so it needs “a”: “a unicorn.”","segments":["I saw","an unicorn","in my dream","last night."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"I saw"},{"k":"B","t":"an unicorn"},{"k":"C","t":"in my dream"},{"k":"D","t":"last night."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-25","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Articles","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"D","explanation":"Unique, specific named landmarks take “the”: “the Sydney Opera House.”","segments":["She plays","the violin","and has performed","at a Sydney Opera House."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"She plays"},{"k":"B","t":"the violin"},{"k":"C","t":"and has performed"},{"k":"D","t":"at a Sydney Opera House."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-26","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Articles","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"D","explanation":"Double article error — should simply be “the damage.”","segments":["A UNESCO official","visited the site","to assess","the a damage."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"A UNESCO official"},{"k":"B","t":"visited the site"},{"k":"C","t":"to assess"},{"k":"D","t":"the a damage."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-27","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Articles","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"“Chartered” begins with the consonant sound /ʧ/, so it takes “a”: “a chartered accountant.”","segments":["He is studying","to become","an chartered accountant","after graduation."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"He is studying"},{"k":"B","t":"to become"},{"k":"C","t":"an chartered accountant"},{"k":"D","t":"after graduation."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-28","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Articles","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"Unique celestial body, should be “the earth.”","segments":["The moon","orbits","an earth","roughly every 27 days."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The moon"},{"k":"B","t":"orbits"},{"k":"C","t":"an earth"},{"k":"D","t":"roughly every 27 days."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-29","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Articles","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"Double article — should be simply “the skeptics” or “even skeptics”: “even the skeptics.”","segments":["It was","such a historic moment","that even the a skeptics","were moved to applause."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"It was"},{"k":"B","t":"such a historic moment"},{"k":"C","t":"that even the a skeptics"},{"k":"D","t":"were moved to applause."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-30","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Articles","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Missing article before a countable singular noun starting with a vowel sound (/em-bee-ay/): “an MBA graduate.”","segments":["She is","MBA graduate","from a well-known","business school."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"She is"},{"k":"B","t":"MBA graduate"},{"k":"C","t":"from a well-known"},{"k":"D","t":"business school."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-31","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Prepositions","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"Formal usage prefers “different from,” not “different than.”","segments":["The final results","are quite different","than","what we expected."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The final results"},{"k":"B","t":"are quite different"},{"k":"C","t":"than"},{"k":"D","t":"what we expected."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-32","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Prepositions","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Correct idiom is “married to.”","segments":["She is","married with","a doctor","she met in college."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"She is"},{"k":"B","t":"married with"},{"k":"C","t":"a doctor"},{"k":"D","t":"she met in college."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-33","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Prepositions","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Correct idiom is “convicted of” a crime.","segments":["He was convicted","for fraud","after a trial","that lasted three months."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"He was convicted"},{"k":"B","t":"for fraud"},{"k":"C","t":"after a trial"},{"k":"D","t":"that lasted three months."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-34","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Prepositions","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Correct idiom is “capable of handling,” not “capable to handle.”","segments":["The company","is capable to","handle orders","far larger than this."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The company"},{"k":"B","t":"is capable to"},{"k":"C","t":"handle orders"},{"k":"D","t":"far larger than this."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-35","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Prepositions","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"E","explanation":"No Error. “Depend upon” (or “depend on”) is correct as written.","segments":["The final decision","will depend","upon","how the board votes tomorrow."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The final decision"},{"k":"B","t":"will depend"},{"k":"C","t":"upon"},{"k":"D","t":"how the board votes tomorrow."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-36","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Prepositions","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"Correct idiom here is “capable of handling.”","segments":["Students who excel academically","are not necessarily","capable in","handling real-world problems."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Students who excel academically"},{"k":"B","t":"are not necessarily"},{"k":"C","t":"capable in"},{"k":"D","t":"handling real-world problems."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-37","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Prepositions","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"D","explanation":"“It’s” is a contraction of “it is”; the possessive should be “its final submission” — this is a word-choice/apostrophe error, not a preposition error, but it’s the flaw in the sentence.","segments":["The professor insisted","on","reviewing the thesis","before it’s final submission."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The professor insisted"},{"k":"B","t":"on"},{"k":"C","t":"reviewing the thesis"},{"k":"D","t":"before it’s final submission."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-38","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Prepositions","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"Correct idiom is “consistent with,” not “consistent to.”","segments":["The findings","are consistent","to","earlier studies on the topic."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The findings"},{"k":"B","t":"are consistent"},{"k":"C","t":"to"},{"k":"D","t":"earlier studies on the topic."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-39","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Prepositions","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"D","explanation":"Redundant/incorrect preposition pairing — should simply be “differ in temperament.”","segments":["He is often compared","to his father,","though the two differ","from in temperament."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"He is often compared"},{"k":"B","t":"to his father,"},{"k":"C","t":"though the two differ"},{"k":"D","t":"from in temperament."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-40","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Prepositions","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"Correct idiom is “prohibit companies from dumping.”","segments":["The new regulations","prohibit companies","of dumping","waste into the river."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The new regulations"},{"k":"B","t":"prohibit companies"},{"k":"C","t":"of dumping"},{"k":"D","t":"waste into the river."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-41","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Modifiers (Dangling & Misplaced)","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"A","explanation":"Dangling modifier — the cathedral cannot be “walking.” Needs a human subject: “As we walked through the old town, the ancient cathedral came into view.”","segments":["Walking through the old town,","the ancient cathedral","came into view","rather suddenly."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Walking through the old town,"},{"k":"B","t":"the ancient cathedral"},{"k":"C","t":"came into view"},{"k":"D","t":"rather suddenly."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-42","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Modifiers (Dangling & Misplaced)","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"A","explanation":"Dangling modifier — the exam did not study. Should be “Having studied all night, Reema still found the exam impossibly difficult.”","segments":["Having studied all night,","the exam","still felt","impossibly difficult to Reema."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Having studied all night,"},{"k":"B","t":"the exam"},{"k":"C","t":"still felt"},{"k":"D","t":"impossibly difficult to Reema."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-43","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Modifiers (Dangling & Misplaced)","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Misplaced modifier — “only” should sit closer to what it limits: “hired only five new engineers,” emphasizing the small number, not the act of hiring itself.","segments":["The company","only hired","five new engineers","last quarter, far below its hiring target."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The company"},{"k":"B","t":"only hired"},{"k":"C","t":"five new engineers"},{"k":"D","t":"last quarter, far below its hiring target."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-44","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Modifiers (Dangling & Misplaced)","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"A","explanation":"Ambiguous/dangling — as written it suggests the collector was covered in dust, when it’s the painting that was. Should be “The antique collector found the painting, covered in dust and cobwebs, in the attic.”","segments":["Covered in dust and cobwebs,","the antique collector","found the painting","in the attic."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Covered in dust and cobwebs,"},{"k":"B","t":"the antique collector"},{"k":"C","t":"found the painting"},{"k":"D","t":"in the attic."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-45","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Modifiers (Dangling & Misplaced)","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"A","explanation":"Dangling infinitive phrase — a “record” cannot qualify. Should be restructured: “To qualify for the scholarship, every applicant needs a strong academic record and two recommendation letters.”","segments":["To qualify for the scholarship,","a strong academic record","and two recommendation letters","are required of every applicant."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"To qualify for the scholarship,"},{"k":"B","t":"a strong academic record"},{"k":"C","t":"and two recommendation letters"},{"k":"D","t":"are required of every applicant."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-46","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Modifiers (Dangling & Misplaced)","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"A","explanation":"Dangling participle — “concerns” cannot review reports. Should be “After reviewing the quarterly reports, the board raised several concerns.”","segments":["After reviewing","the quarterly reports,","several concerns","were raised by the board."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"After reviewing"},{"k":"B","t":"the quarterly reports,"},{"k":"C","t":"several concerns"},{"k":"D","t":"were raised by the board."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-47","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Modifiers (Dangling & Misplaced)","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"A","explanation":"Misplaced “almost” changes meaning — as written it implies she nearly drove them (but didn’t); if the intent is “most weekends, though not quite every one,” “almost” should modify “every”: “she drove her family to the coast almost every weekend.”","segments":["She almost drove","her entire family","to the coast","every single weekend."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"She almost drove"},{"k":"B","t":"her entire family"},{"k":"C","t":"to the coast"},{"k":"D","t":"every single weekend."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-48","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Modifiers (Dangling & Misplaced)","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"A","explanation":"Dangling modifier — tourists were not built in the 14th century. Should be “Built in the 14th century, the fortress attracts tourists throughout the year.”","segments":["Built in the 14th century,","tourists visit","the fortress","throughout the year."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Built in the 14th century,"},{"k":"B","t":"tourists visit"},{"k":"C","t":"the fortress"},{"k":"D","t":"throughout the year."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-49","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Modifiers (Dangling & Misplaced)","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"A","explanation":"Dangling — the bed was not exhausted. Should be “Exhausted after the long flight, Aditi found the hotel bed unbelievably comfortable.”","segments":["Exhausted after the long flight,","the hotel bed","felt","unbelievably comfortable to Aditi."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Exhausted after the long flight,"},{"k":"B","t":"the hotel bed"},{"k":"C","t":"felt"},{"k":"D","t":"unbelievably comfortable to Aditi."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-50","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Modifiers (Dangling & Misplaced)","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"E","explanation":"No Error. The final participial phrase clearly modifies “the manager” (the one doing the summarizing), and there’s no ambiguity — correct as written.","segments":["The manager","explained the new policy","to the staff","briefly summarizing its key points on a single slide."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The manager"},{"k":"B","t":"explained the new policy"},{"k":"C","t":"to the staff"},{"k":"D","t":"briefly summarizing its key points on a single slide."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-51","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Parallelism","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"D","explanation":"Breaks parallel structure — should be “communicating results” to match “write” and “analyze” in form (infinitive-style verbs after “how to”).","segments":["The course teaches students","how to write code,","analyze data,","and communication of results."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The course teaches students"},{"k":"B","t":"how to write code,"},{"k":"C","t":"analyze data,"},{"k":"D","t":"and communication of results."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-52","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Parallelism","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"D","explanation":"Correlative conjunction “not only…but also” requires matching grammatical form — should be “but also hardworking” to match the adjective “talented.”","segments":["She is","not only talented","but also","has a lot of hard work."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"She is"},{"k":"B","t":"not only talented"},{"k":"C","t":"but also"},{"k":"D","t":"has a lot of hard work."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-53","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Parallelism","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"Should be parallel with the noun phrases before it: “and its conciseness,” not “because it was concise.”","segments":["The report was praised","for its clarity, its thoroughness,","and because it was concise.","No further errors in this sentence."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The report was praised"},{"k":"B","t":"for its clarity, its thoroughness,"},{"k":"C","t":"and because it was concise."},{"k":"D","t":"No further errors in this sentence."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-54","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Parallelism","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"Should match the verb-adverb pattern: “and decide decisively/quickly,” not a shift to “they are decisive.”","segments":["Good leaders listen carefully,","communicate clearly,","and they are decisive quickly.","No further errors."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Good leaders listen carefully,"},{"k":"B","t":"communicate clearly,"},{"k":"C","t":"and they are decisive quickly."},{"k":"D","t":"No further errors."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-55","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Parallelism","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"Should be “to increase efficiency” to match the infinitive structure of the other two items.","segments":["The policy aims","to reduce emissions,","increasing efficiency,","and to promote clean energy."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The policy aims"},{"k":"B","t":"to reduce emissions,"},{"k":"C","t":"increasing efficiency,"},{"k":"D","t":"and to promote clean energy."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-56","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Parallelism","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"D","explanation":"“Either…or” should connect parallel elements cleanly — as written the sentence structure shifts awkwardly; the corrected form is “Either you submit the report today or it will be marked late tomorrow,” keeping both clauses parallel in structure.","segments":["Either you","submit the report today","or tomorrow","it will be marked late."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Either you"},{"k":"B","t":"submit the report today"},{"k":"C","t":"or tomorrow"},{"k":"D","t":"it will be marked late."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-57","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Parallelism","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"D","explanation":"Should be “experienced with SQL” to match “fluent” and “proficient” (all adjectives).","segments":["The applicant should be","fluent in English,","proficient in Excel,","and having experience with SQL."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The applicant should be"},{"k":"B","t":"fluent in English,"},{"k":"C","t":"proficient in Excel,"},{"k":"D","t":"and having experience with SQL."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-58","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Parallelism","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"“Whether…or” needs matching structure: “or to relocate it entirely,” not “or that we should relocate.”","segments":["We debated","whether to expand the factory","or that we should relocate it","entirely."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"We debated"},{"k":"B","t":"whether to expand the factory"},{"k":"C","t":"or that we should relocate it"},{"k":"D","t":"entirely."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-59","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Parallelism","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"D","explanation":"Should remain a noun phrase to match the list: “and his generous encouragement of students.”","segments":["The professor is known","for his sharp wit,","his rigorous standards,","and encourages students generously."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The professor is known"},{"k":"B","t":"for his sharp wit,"},{"k":"C","t":"his rigorous standards,"},{"k":"D","t":"and encourages students generously."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-60","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Parallelism","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"D","explanation":"Should be “and trust their teams” to match the simple present tense verbs “delegate” and “communicate.”","segments":["Effective managers","delegate wisely,","communicate transparently,","and are trusting their teams."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Effective managers"},{"k":"B","t":"delegate wisely,"},{"k":"C","t":"communicate transparently,"},{"k":"D","t":"and are trusting their teams."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-61","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Pronoun Reference & Agreement","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Ambiguous pronoun — “she” could refer to either the manager or the intern; needs a specific noun instead of “she.”","segments":["When the manager spoke to the intern,","she mentioned","that the deadline","had been moved up."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"When the manager spoke to the intern,"},{"k":"B","t":"she mentioned"},{"k":"C","t":"that the deadline"},{"k":"D","t":"had been moved up."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-62","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Pronoun Reference & Agreement","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"“Every employee” is singular; strict formal usage requires “his or her timesheet” (though singular “their” is increasingly accepted in casual usage, CAT-level formal grammar tests the traditional agreement rule).","segments":["Every employee","must submit","their timesheet","by Friday evening."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Every employee"},{"k":"B","t":"must submit"},{"k":"C","t":"their timesheet"},{"k":"D","t":"by Friday evening."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-63","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Pronoun Reference & Agreement","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"E","explanation":"No Error. “It” (implicit, via “its”), “which,” and “they” all have clear antecedents (company, results, investors) — correct as written.","segments":["The company","announced its results,","which impressed investors","more than they had anticipated."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The company"},{"k":"B","t":"announced its results,"},{"k":"C","t":"which impressed investors"},{"k":"D","t":"more than they had anticipated."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-64","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Pronoun Reference & Agreement","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"E","explanation":"No Error. “Their” correctly agrees with “brothers” (the nearer subject in “neither…nor,” which is also plural) — correct as written.","segments":["Neither Ravi nor his brothers","have collected","their share","of the inheritance yet."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Neither Ravi nor his brothers"},{"k":"B","t":"have collected"},{"k":"C","t":"their share"},{"k":"D","t":"of the inheritance yet."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-65","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Pronoun Reference & Agreement","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"“A student” is singular — strict formal usage prefers “he or she should,” matching singular “a student.”","segments":["If a student wants to succeed,","they should","attend every lecture","and submit all assignments on time."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"If a student wants to succeed,"},{"k":"B","t":"they should"},{"k":"C","t":"attend every lecture"},{"k":"D","t":"and submit all assignments on time."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-66","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Pronoun Reference & Agreement","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"D","explanation":"Ambiguous “which” — unclear whether it refers to “roads,” “the empire,” or the entire preceding idea; needs a clearer restatement.","segments":["The book describes","how ancient Romans built roads","that connected the empire,","which was their greatest engineering feat."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The book describes"},{"k":"B","t":"how ancient Romans built roads"},{"k":"C","t":"that connected the empire,"},{"k":"D","t":"which was their greatest engineering feat."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-67","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Pronoun Reference & Agreement","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Ambiguous “he” — unclear which of the two CEOs is meant; needs a specific name.","segments":["After the two CEOs met,","he announced","that the merger","was finally complete."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"After the two CEOs met,"},{"k":"B","t":"he announced"},{"k":"C","t":"that the merger"},{"k":"D","t":"was finally complete."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-68","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Pronoun Reference & Agreement","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"E","explanation":"No Error. “Each” (singular) correctly takes “has” and “her” — correct as written, assuming both twins are female as context implies.","segments":["Each of the twins","has","her own bedroom","in the new house."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Each of the twins"},{"k":"B","t":"has"},{"k":"C","t":"her own bedroom"},{"k":"D","t":"in the new house."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-69","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Pronoun Reference & Agreement","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"D","explanation":"Pronoun mismatch — “organization” is singular (“it”), but “they” is plural; should be “which it has maintained.”","segments":["The organization","prides itself","on transparency,","which they have maintained for decades."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The organization"},{"k":"B","t":"prides itself"},{"k":"C","t":"on transparency,"},{"k":"D","t":"which they have maintained for decades."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-70","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Pronoun Reference & Agreement","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"“It” has no clear singular antecedent (both “report” and “summary” were compared); should be reworded, e.g., “the comparison revealed.”","segments":["When the report and the summary were compared,","it revealed","several inconsistencies","that needed correction."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"When the report and the summary were compared,"},{"k":"B","t":"it revealed"},{"k":"C","t":"several inconsistencies"},{"k":"D","t":"that needed correction."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-71","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Redundancy & Wordiness","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"“Enclosed” and “herewith” are redundant together — one should be dropped.","segments":["Please find enclosed","herewith","the completed application form","for your review."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Please find enclosed"},{"k":"B","t":"herewith"},{"k":"C","t":"the completed application form"},{"k":"D","t":"for your review."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-72","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Redundancy & Wordiness","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"“Collaborate” already implies working together — “collaborate together” is redundant.","segments":["The two companies","decided to collaborate together","on a new product line","launching next year."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The two companies"},{"k":"B","t":"decided to collaborate together"},{"k":"C","t":"on a new product line"},{"k":"D","t":"launching next year."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-73","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Redundancy & Wordiness","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"“Final” and “concluding” are redundant together.","segments":["She gave","her final","and concluding remarks","at the end of the ceremony."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"She gave"},{"k":"B","t":"her final"},{"k":"C","t":"and concluding remarks"},{"k":"D","t":"at the end of the ceremony."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-74","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Redundancy & Wordiness","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"A","explanation":"“Returned back” is redundant — “returned” alone implies going back.","segments":["He returned back","to his hometown","after fifteen years","abroad."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"He returned back"},{"k":"B","t":"to his hometown"},{"k":"C","t":"after fifteen years"},{"k":"D","t":"abroad."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-75","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Redundancy & Wordiness","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"A","explanation":"“Unexpected surprise” is redundant — a surprise is by definition unexpected.","segments":["The unexpected surprise","announcement","caught","everyone off guard."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The unexpected surprise"},{"k":"B","t":"announcement"},{"k":"C","t":"caught"},{"k":"D","t":"everyone off guard."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-76","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Redundancy & Wordiness","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"A","explanation":"“Each and every” is a redundant doubling — either “each” or “every” alone suffices.","segments":["Each and every","member of the team","contributed","to the final report."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Each and every"},{"k":"B","t":"member of the team"},{"k":"C","t":"contributed"},{"k":"D","t":"to the final report."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-77","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Redundancy & Wordiness","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"A","explanation":"“Past history” is redundant — history is inherently about the past.","segments":["The past history","of the region","reveals","centuries of conflict."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The past history"},{"k":"B","t":"of the region"},{"k":"C","t":"reveals"},{"k":"D","t":"centuries of conflict."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-78","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Redundancy & Wordiness","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"“Plan in advance” is redundant — planning inherently happens before the event.","segments":["We need to plan","in advance","for the upcoming","product launch."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"We need to plan"},{"k":"B","t":"in advance"},{"k":"C","t":"for the upcoming"},{"k":"D","t":"product launch."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-79","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Redundancy & Wordiness","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"A","explanation":"“Final outcome” is redundant — an outcome is, by definition, a final result.","segments":["The final outcome","of the negotiations","surprised","both parties involved."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The final outcome"},{"k":"B","t":"of the negotiations"},{"k":"C","t":"surprised"},{"k":"D","t":"both parties involved."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-80","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Redundancy & Wordiness","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"“Repeated again” is redundant — “repeated” already means done again.","segments":["She repeated","the instructions","again","for the new recruits."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"She repeated"},{"k":"B","t":"the instructions"},{"k":"C","t":"again"},{"k":"D","t":"for the new recruits."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-81","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Conjunctions & Correlatives","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"“Although” and “but” cannot both be used — one connector is enough; remove “but.”","segments":["Although the weather was terrible,","but the match","went ahead","as scheduled."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Although the weather was terrible,"},{"k":"B","t":"but the match"},{"k":"C","t":"went ahead"},{"k":"D","t":"as scheduled."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-82","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Conjunctions & Correlatives","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"The correct correlative here is “so weak that he cannot,” not “and cannot.”","segments":["He is","so weak","and cannot","walk without support."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"He is"},{"k":"B","t":"so weak"},{"k":"C","t":"and cannot"},{"k":"D","t":"walk without support."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-83","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Conjunctions & Correlatives","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"D","explanation":"After “but also,” the structure should mirror “did she” — should be “but also did she break” or restructure the whole pair.","segments":["Not only did she","finish the marathon,","but also","she broke the course record."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Not only did she"},{"k":"B","t":"finish the marathon,"},{"k":"C","t":"but also"},{"k":"D","t":"she broke the course record."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-84","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Conjunctions & Correlatives","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"A","explanation":"“Despite” is never followed by “of” — should be simply “Despite heavy rain” (or “in spite of”).","segments":["Despite of","heavy rain,","the flight departed","on schedule."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Despite of"},{"k":"B","t":"heavy rain,"},{"k":"C","t":"the flight departed"},{"k":"D","t":"on schedule."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-85","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Conjunctions & Correlatives","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"“Scarcely…when” is the correct pairing, not “scarcely…than” — should be “when the fire alarm.”","segments":["Scarcely had the meeting begun","than","the fire alarm","went off."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Scarcely had the meeting begun"},{"k":"B","t":"than"},{"k":"C","t":"the fire alarm"},{"k":"D","t":"went off."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-86","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Conjunctions & Correlatives","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"E","explanation":"No Error. “No sooner…than” is the correct idiomatic pairing — correct as written.","segments":["No sooner had he","sat down","than","his phone was ringing."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"No sooner had he"},{"k":"B","t":"sat down"},{"k":"C","t":"than"},{"k":"D","t":"his phone was ringing."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-87","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Conjunctions & Correlatives","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"For grammatical parallelism with “not because of,” the second half should also use “because of”: “but because of insufficient funding.”","segments":["The project failed","not because of poor planning","but due to","insufficient funding."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The project failed"},{"k":"B","t":"not because of poor planning"},{"k":"C","t":"but due to"},{"k":"D","t":"insufficient funding."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-88","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Conjunctions & Correlatives","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"Mixed construction — “as tall as” and “taller than” require different prepositions; the correct form is “as tall as, if not taller than, her older sister” (both comparative structures need to be completed).","segments":["She is","as tall, if not taller,","than","her older sister."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"She is"},{"k":"B","t":"as tall, if not taller,"},{"k":"C","t":"than"},{"k":"D","t":"her older sister."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-89","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Conjunctions & Correlatives","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"E","explanation":"No Error. “Hardly…when” is a correct idiomatic inverted-conditional pairing — correct as written.","segments":["Hardly had the flight landed","when","passengers began","rushing for the exits."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Hardly had the flight landed"},{"k":"B","t":"when"},{"k":"C","t":"passengers began"},{"k":"D","t":"rushing for the exits."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-90","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Conjunctions & Correlatives","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"“Whether…or” needs parallel structure: “or downsizes,” not “or it will downsize.”","segments":["Whether the company expands","or it will downsize,","layoffs seem","likely either way."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Whether the company expands"},{"k":"B","t":"or it will downsize,"},{"k":"C","t":"layoffs seem"},{"k":"D","t":"likely either way."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-91","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Commonly Confused Words","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"“Effect” as a verb means to bring about — this is correct usage actually if intended as “cause,” but the common test is when a writer means “influence”: if the intended meaning is “impact,” it should be “affect.” Given the sentence intends “bring about,” “effect” (verb) is correct here — this item tests whether you can distinguish the rare correct use of “effect” as a verb from the more common error. Correct answer: (E) No Error.","segments":["The new regulation","will effect","major changes","to the tax code."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The new regulation"},{"k":"B","t":"will effect"},{"k":"C","t":"major changes"},{"k":"D","t":"to the tax code."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-92","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Commonly Confused Words","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"“Students” is countable — should be “fewer students,” not “less.”","segments":["There are","less","students","in this batch than last year."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"There are"},{"k":"B","t":"less"},{"k":"C","t":"students"},{"k":"D","t":"in this batch than last year."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-93","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Commonly Confused Words","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"A","explanation":"“Lay” (intransitive, to recline) is needed, not “laid” (past tense of “lay,” transitive, to place something down) — should be “She lay on the couch.”","segments":["She laid","on the couch","for an hour","after the long flight."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"She laid"},{"k":"B","t":"on the couch"},{"k":"C","t":"for an hour"},{"k":"D","t":"after the long flight."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-94","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Commonly Confused Words","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"A","explanation":"Data (the source) “suggests” or “indicates”; a person “infers” from data. Strictly, “implies” is acceptable for data suggesting something indirectly, but the more precise, commonly tested distinction is that a reader/analyst infers from data, while data itself may be said to “indicate” — this item is often flagged as an error in favor of “indicates.”","segments":["The data clearly implies","that consumer confidence","is falling","faster than expected."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The data clearly implies"},{"k":"B","t":"that consumer confidence"},{"k":"C","t":"is falling"},{"k":"D","t":"faster than expected."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-95","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Commonly Confused Words","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"“Between” is for two entities; for three or more, use “among.”","segments":["Please keep","this information","between","you, me, and the accountant."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Please keep"},{"k":"B","t":"this information"},{"k":"C","t":"between"},{"k":"D","t":"you, me, and the accountant."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-96","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Commonly Confused Words","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"A","explanation":"“Its” (possessive) is used where the contraction “It’s” (it is) is needed.","segments":["Its","a shame","the museum","closes so early."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Its"},{"k":"B","t":"a shame"},{"k":"C","t":"the museum"},{"k":"D","t":"closes so early."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-97","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Commonly Confused Words","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"“Farther” is for literal physical distance; for figurative/abstract extent, use “further.”","segments":["He ran","farther","into the debate","than anyone expected."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"He ran"},{"k":"B","t":"farther"},{"k":"C","t":"into the debate"},{"k":"D","t":"than anyone expected."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-98","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Commonly Confused Words","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"A","explanation":"The pronoun is the object of “give… to,” so it should be “Whom did you give the keys to?”","segments":["Who","did you","give the keys","to?"],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Who"},{"k":"B","t":"did you"},{"k":"C","t":"give the keys"},{"k":"D","t":"to?"},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-99","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Commonly Confused Words","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"“Who’s” (contraction of “who is”) is wrong here — the possessive “whose recommendation” is needed.","segments":["The board","will decide","who’s","recommendation to accept."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The board"},{"k":"B","t":"will decide"},{"k":"C","t":"who’s"},{"k":"D","t":"recommendation to accept."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"grammar-100","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Commonly Confused Words","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"A","explanation":"With three finalists, “among” is correct, not “between” — should be “Among the three finalists.”","segments":["Between the three finalists,","the judges","found it","impossible to choose."],"stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Between the three finalists,"},{"k":"B","t":"the judges"},{"k":"C","t":"found it"},{"k":"D","t":"impossible to choose."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}]},{"id":"pj-1","topic":"pj","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Mandatory Pairs via Pronouns","src":"doc","sentences":["This shift, though gradual, has already reshaped how universities design their curricula.","For decades, employers valued formal degrees above almost any other credential.","Increasingly, however, they are placing more weight on demonstrated skills and project portfolios.","That change reflects a broader disillusionment with credentials that don’t translate into workplace competence."],"type":"tita","answer":"2341","explanation":"Sentence 2 introduces the topic (employers valuing degrees) with no backward reference — the opener. Sentence 3’s “they” refers to “employers” in 2, and “however” signals a contrast to 2, so 3 follows 2. Sentence 4’s “that change” refers to the shift described in 3. Sentence 1’s “this shift” restates 4’s point and draws the concluding implication.","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-2","topic":"pj","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Mandatory Pairs via Pronouns","src":"doc","sentences":["It was later revealed that the meteor had disintegrated in the upper atmosphere before impact.","Residents across three states reported a blinding flash of light early Tuesday morning.","This explained why no crater or debris field was ever found at the reported location.","Initial reports speculated that a meteor had struck a rural area near the state border."],"type":"tita","answer":"2413","explanation":"Sentence 2 opens with the observed event. Sentence 4’s “initial reports” naturally follows the flash being reported. Sentence 1’s “it was later revealed” continues the timeline after the initial speculation in 4. Sentence 3’s “this explained” refers directly to the disintegration explanation in 1.","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-3","topic":"pj","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Mandatory Pairs via Pronouns","src":"doc","sentences":["They argued that whales evolved from a group of hoofed, deer-like land mammals.","This claim, radical at the time, was later strongly supported by fossil and genetic evidence.","In the early 1990s, paleontologists proposed a surprising theory about whale ancestry.","Genetic studies in particular showed whales sharing a close common ancestor with hippopotamuses."],"type":"tita","answer":"3124","explanation":"Sentence 3 opens, naming the topic (a theory about whale ancestry) with no backward references. Sentence 1’s “they” refers to the paleontologists in 3. Sentence 2’s “this claim” refers to the theory just stated in 1. Sentence 4 elaborates on the “genetic evidence” mentioned in 2.","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-4","topic":"pj","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Mandatory Pairs via Pronouns","src":"doc","sentences":["Its bright red flowers attract a specific species of moth found nowhere else on the island.","Without that moth, botanists believe, the plant would likely go extinct within a generation.","The relationship illustrates how fragile single-species dependencies can be within isolated ecosystems.","A rare orchid native to the island depends entirely on one pollinator for reproduction."],"type":"tita","answer":"4123","explanation":"Sentence 4 opens, naming “a rare orchid” for the first time. Sentence 1’s “its” refers to the orchid, describing its flowers. Sentence 2’s “that moth” refers to the moth introduced in 1. Sentence 3 draws the general conclusion (“the relationship illustrates”).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-5","topic":"pj","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Mandatory Pairs via Pronouns","src":"doc","sentences":["He refused, insisting the discovery should be shared freely with the entire scientific community.","This decision cost him a fortune but earned him a lasting place in scientific history.","Colleagues urged the young researcher to patent his groundbreaking formula immediately.","Years later, that same formula would form the basis of a multi-billion-dollar industry."],"type":"tita","answer":"3124","explanation":"Sentence 3 opens, naming “the young researcher” and the situation. Sentence 1’s “he refused” responds directly to the urging in 3. Sentence 2’s “this decision” refers to the refusal in 1. Sentence 4’s “that same formula” continues the story forward in time.","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-6","topic":"pj","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Mandatory Pairs via Pronouns","src":"doc","sentences":["She spent the next decade cataloguing every specimen by hand, without any institutional support.","That painstaking record eventually became the most cited botanical archive in the region.","A young botanist first noticed the unusual pattern in leaf structure during a routine field trip.","Recognizing its significance, she decided to document every variation she could find."],"type":"tita","answer":"3412","explanation":"Sentence 3 opens with “a young botanist,” introduced for the first time. Sentence 4’s “its significance” and “she” refer to the pattern and botanist from 3. Sentence 1 continues the action described in 4 (“decided to document” → “spent the next decade cataloguing”). Sentence 2’s “that painstaking record” refers to the cataloguing work in 1.","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-7","topic":"pj","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Mandatory Pairs via Pronouns","src":"doc","sentences":["This gap in the treaty allowed several nations to continue similar practices under a different name.","The original 1987 agreement banned a specific list of ozone-depleting chemicals by name.","Amendments over the following decades gradually closed this loophole one substance at a time.","It did not, however, anticipate the development of chemically similar substitute compounds."],"type":"tita","answer":"2413","explanation":"Sentence 2 opens, naming “the original 1987 agreement.” Sentence 4’s “it” refers to the agreement in 2, and “however” signals what the agreement failed to anticipate. Sentence 1’s “this gap” refers to the anticipation failure described in 4. Sentence 3’s “this loophole” refers to the gap named in 1.","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-8","topic":"pj","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Mandatory Pairs via Pronouns","src":"doc","sentences":["That instinct, researchers found, is triggered by a specific chemical released when the plant is under attack.","Certain species of acacia trees appear to “warn” nearby trees of grazing animals.","Nearby trees detecting the chemical respond by producing bitter-tasting compounds in their leaves.","Within minutes, this makes the entire grove less appealing to browsing herbivores."],"type":"tita","answer":"2134","explanation":"Sentence 2 opens, naming “acacia trees” and the behavior. Sentence 1’s “that instinct” refers to the warning behavior in 2. Sentence 3 continues by describing what “nearby trees” (mentioned via the chemical in 1) do in response. Sentence 4’s “this” refers to the compound production in 3, concluding with the effect.","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-9","topic":"pj","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Mandatory Pairs via Pronouns","src":"doc","sentences":["This meant traders had to rely on hand signals and shouted numbers to close deals in seconds.","Digital trading systems have since replaced nearly all of this frantic, in-person negotiation.","Trading floors in the twentieth century were famously loud, chaotic, and fast-moving environments.","Ironically, many veteran traders say they miss the energy of that old system."],"type":"tita","answer":"3124","explanation":"Sentence 3 opens, setting the scene of “trading floors.” Sentence 1’s “this meant” follows directly from the chaotic description in 3. Sentence 2 marks the shift away from what was described in 1 (“this frantic, in-person negotiation”). Sentence 4’s “that old system” refers back, closing with a reflective note.","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-10","topic":"pj","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Mandatory Pairs via Pronouns","src":"doc","sentences":["It took nearly forty years for the wrongly convicted man to be formally exonerated.","New DNA evidence, unavailable at the time of the original trial, finally proved his innocence.","His case is now taught in law schools as a cautionary example of forensic overreach.","A single hair sample, misidentified by an overconfident analyst, had sent an innocent man to prison."],"type":"tita","answer":"4123","explanation":"Sentence 4 opens, introducing the case for the first time. Sentence 1’s “it took nearly forty years” continues the story of the wrongful conviction from 4. Sentence 2 explains what finally resolved the situation described in 1. Sentence 3 draws the concluding, present-day implication.","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-11","topic":"pj","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Chronological / Sequential Order","src":"doc","sentences":["By the third week, the seedlings had outgrown every container the students had prepared.","On the first day of the experiment, students planted identical seeds in twelve different soil types.","Only two of the twelve soil types produced seedlings that survived past week five.","Within a week, visible differences in growth rate had already emerged between soil types."],"type":"tita","answer":"2413","explanation":"Clear timeline: “first day” (2) → “within a week” (4) → “by the third week” (1) → “past week five” (3).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-12","topic":"pj","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Chronological / Sequential Order","src":"doc","sentences":["By the second half, the champions had completely reversed the deficit.","The underdog team stunned the crowd by scoring twice within the opening ten minutes.","In the final minute, a controversial penalty decided the match in the champions’ favor.","The defending champions, rattled, struggled to find any rhythm for the rest of the first half."],"type":"tita","answer":"2413","explanation":"Timeline: “opening ten minutes” (2) → “rest of the first half” (4) → “second half” (1) → “final minute” (3).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-13","topic":"pj","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Chronological / Sequential Order","src":"doc","sentences":["Within five years, the same district was exporting surplus grain to neighboring regions.","A severe drought in 1972 pushed the district to the brink of famine.","By the following decade, its irrigation model was being studied and replicated nationwide.","Emergency wells and community-managed canals were dug in response over the next two years."],"type":"tita","answer":"2413","explanation":"Timeline: “1972” drought (2) → “next two years” wells dug (4) → “within five years” exporting (1) → “following decade” studied nationwide (3).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-14","topic":"pj","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Chronological / Sequential Order","src":"doc","sentences":["Ten years later, the same design would go on to win international recognition.","The architect’s first submission was rejected by the city council as impractical.","A revised version, submitted two years afterward, was quietly approved with little fanfare.","It sat largely unnoticed until a renewed public interest in sustainable design brought it attention."],"type":"tita","answer":"2341","explanation":"Timeline: first submission rejected (2) → “two years afterward” revised version (3) → sat unnoticed until renewed interest (4) → “ten years later” recognition (1).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-15","topic":"pj","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Chronological / Sequential Order","src":"doc","sentences":["Only after the third failed attempt did the team consider a completely different approach.","Their first prototype failed within seconds of the initial test.","That new approach, born of frustration, would eventually become their most successful product.","A modified second version lasted longer but still fell well short of the target."],"type":"tita","answer":"2413","explanation":"Timeline: first prototype fails (2) → modified second version (4) → “third failed attempt” (1) → eventual success (3).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-16","topic":"pj","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Chronological / Sequential Order","src":"doc","sentences":["It would take another three decades for a woman to hold the same position again.","In 1917, she became the first woman elected to the national legislature.","Long before her election, she had already spent years campaigning for suffrage.","Her single term was marked by fierce opposition from nearly all her male colleagues."],"type":"tita","answer":"3241","explanation":"Timeline: “long before her election” campaigning (3) → “1917” elected (2) → her term with opposition (4) → “another three decades” later (1).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-17","topic":"pj","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Chronological / Sequential Order","src":"doc","sentences":["Within hours, hospitals across the city reported an unusual spike in patient admissions.","Investigators eventually traced the spike to a contaminated batch at a single bottling plant.","A popular soft drink was recalled nationwide following the investigation’s conclusion.","City health officials began collecting samples from local restaurants and grocery stores by evening."],"type":"tita","answer":"1423","explanation":"Timeline: “within hours” spike reported (1) → “by evening” sample collection (4) → investigators traced (eventually) (2) → nationwide recall following conclusion (3).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-18","topic":"pj","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Chronological / Sequential Order","src":"doc","sentences":["It wasn’t until the fourth expedition, a decade later, that the summit was finally reached.","Two more attempts in subsequent years ended in retreat due to worsening weather.","The mountain’s first documented attempt ended in failure just below the tree line.","Since then, the route has been climbed by thousands, though it remains dangerous."],"type":"tita","answer":"3214","explanation":"Timeline: “first documented attempt” (3) → “two more attempts” (2) → “fourth expedition, a decade later” (1) → “since then” (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-19","topic":"pj","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Chronological / Sequential Order","src":"doc","sentences":["A generation later, the same technology had become standard in nearly every household.","Early versions of the appliance were bulky, expensive, and prone to frequent breakdowns.","Manufacturers spent the following years steadily reducing both size and cost.","Eventually, a single design breakthrough made mass production genuinely affordable."],"type":"tita","answer":"2341","explanation":"Timeline: “early versions” (2) → “following years” reducing (3) → “eventually” breakthrough (4) → “a generation later” standard (1).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-20","topic":"pj","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Chronological / Sequential Order","src":"doc","sentences":["Restoration work, begun almost immediately, took over fifteen years to complete.","A devastating fire in 1834 destroyed most of the original structure.","Today, few visitors realize how much of what they see was rebuilt rather than original.","The rebuilt sections were deliberately designed to be indistinguishable from the surviving originals."],"type":"tita","answer":"2143","explanation":"Timeline: “fire in 1834” (2) → restoration “begun almost immediately” (1) → rebuilt sections designed (4) → “today” (3).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-21","topic":"pj","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"General Statement → Specific Example","src":"doc","sentences":["Octopuses, for instance, can alter both color and skin texture within a fraction of a second.","Many marine creatures have evolved extraordinarily fast methods of camouflage.","Cuttlefish achieve a similar effect using specialized cells that respond directly to their surroundings.","Such abilities far outpace anything achievable by land animals relying on fur or skin pigment changes."],"type":"tita","answer":"2134","explanation":"Sentence 2 makes the general claim; “for instance” in 1 signals the first example; 3 gives a second, related example (“a similar effect”); 4 draws the broader comparison as a conclusion.","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-22","topic":"pj","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"General Statement → Specific Example","src":"doc","sentences":["Frugal engineering, for example, produced a sub-$2,000 automobile aimed at first-time car buyers.","Resource-constrained markets have historically driven some of the most creative product innovation.","Similarly, mobile payment systems flourished in regions where formal banking infrastructure was weakest.","In both cases, necessity — not abundance — proved to be the real driver of invention."],"type":"tita","answer":"2134","explanation":"General claim in 2; first example (“for example”) in 1; second, parallel example (“similarly”) in 3; concluding synthesis in 4.","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-23","topic":"pj","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"General Statement → Specific Example","src":"doc","sentences":["Beethoven, for one, continued composing major symphonic works after becoming almost completely deaf.","History offers several examples of artists whose greatest work followed profound personal hardship.","Frida Kahlo, similarly, produced some of her most searing paintings while bedridden and in constant pain.","Their experiences suggest that adversity, while never desirable, does not have to be creatively disabling."],"type":"tita","answer":"2134","explanation":"General claim (2), first example (1), second example (3), synthesizing conclusion (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-24","topic":"pj","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"General Statement → Specific Example","src":"doc","sentences":["In humans, this shows up as a preference for symmetrical faces, widely linked to perceived health.","Symmetry appears to be an evolutionarily favored trait across many species.","Peacocks with more symmetrical tail feathers, for example, are consistently chosen by female peahens.","The pattern suggests symmetry may function as a reliable, hard-to-fake signal of genetic fitness."],"type":"tita","answer":"2314","explanation":"General claim (2), first example, peacocks (3), second example, humans (1), concluding interpretation (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-25","topic":"pj","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"General Statement → Specific Example","src":"doc","sentences":["Government subsidies, for instance, briefly propped up an industry that private investors had already abandoned.","Markets do not always correct inefficient allocations of capital on their own.","Similarly, decades of favorable tax treatment sustained certain declining sectors well past their natural lifespan.","Both cases illustrate how policy can distort, as much as correct, market signals."],"type":"tita","answer":"2134","explanation":"General claim (2), first example (1), second example (3), conclusion (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-26","topic":"pj","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"General Statement → Specific Example","src":"doc","sentences":["Coral reefs, for example, can recover from moderate bleaching events if water temperatures stabilize in time.","Some ecosystems display a surprising capacity for resilience after severe disturbance.","Boreal forests, similarly, have been shown to regenerate fully within decades of a major wildfire.","This resilience, however, has clear limits when disturbances become too frequent or too severe."],"type":"tita","answer":"2134","explanation":"General claim (2), first example (1), second example (3), qualifying conclusion (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-27","topic":"pj","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"General Statement → Specific Example","src":"doc","sentences":["A small Nordic country, for instance, cut its overall energy consumption by a fifth within a decade.","Several nations have shown that ambitious climate targets can be met without sacrificing economic growth.","Its GDP, meanwhile, continued to grow steadily throughout the same period.","Such examples complicate the common assumption that decarbonization must come at an economic cost."],"type":"tita","answer":"2134","explanation":"General claim (2), example introduced (1), elaboration on that same example (3), conclusion (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-28","topic":"pj","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"General Statement → Specific Example","src":"doc","sentences":["One firm, for example, redesigned its entire supply chain around a single unproven assumption about consumer behavior.","Corporate overconfidence has led to some spectacularly expensive strategic failures.","The assumption proved wrong, and the redesign cost the company nearly a billion dollars.","Such failures are rarely due to a lack of data, but to an unwillingness to question it."],"type":"tita","answer":"2134","explanation":"General claim (2), example (1), elaboration/outcome of that example (3), broader conclusion (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-29","topic":"pj","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"General Statement → Specific Example","src":"doc","sentences":["Certain ant species, for instance, farm fungus in dedicated underground chambers for food.","Several insect species display behavior that closely resembles agriculture.","Termites, similarly, cultivate fungus gardens that they carefully tend and protect from pests.","These parallels suggest that agriculture is not a uniquely human invention after all."],"type":"tita","answer":"2134","explanation":"General claim (2), example (1), parallel example (3), conclusion (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-30","topic":"pj","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"General Statement → Specific Example","src":"doc","sentences":["One island nation, for instance, banned single-use plastic bags nearly a decade before most of the world.","Small nations have occasionally led on environmental policy well ahead of larger economies.","Its early ban is now cited as a model by policymakers elsewhere.","Such cases show that policy ambition is not always proportional to a country’s size or wealth."],"type":"tita","answer":"2134","explanation":"General claim (2), example (1), elaboration (3), conclusion (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-31","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Contrast & Concession","src":"doc","sentences":["Critics, however, point out that automation has historically created more jobs than it destroyed.","Many commentators warn that automation will eliminate millions of jobs within a decade.","Still, few dispute that the transition period could be painful for displaced workers.","Whether history repeats itself this time remains a genuinely open question."],"type":"tita","answer":"2134","explanation":"Opening claim (2); “however” signals the contrasting view (1); “still” signals a concession back toward the original concern (3); open-ended conclusion (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-32","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Contrast & Concession","src":"doc","sentences":["Skeptics counter that correlation in observational studies rarely proves such a strong causal claim.","Even the study’s authors admit that a controlled trial would be needed to settle the question.","A widely cited study claimed a direct causal link between the two variables.","Their caution has done little to slow the study’s uncritical citation in popular media."],"type":"tita","answer":"3124","explanation":"Opening claim (3); “skeptics counter” contrast (1); admission by the authors (2); consequence/irony (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-33","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Contrast & Concession","src":"doc","sentences":["Traditionalists, however, argue the changes strip the game of its historical character.","Reformers insist the new rules make the sport faster and more accessible to new fans.","Broadcasters, caught in the middle, have largely sided with whichever view sells more advertising.","The debate shows no sign of resolving before the rules take permanent effect next season."],"type":"tita","answer":"2134","explanation":"Opening view (2), contrasting view (1), a third party’s position (3), forward-looking conclusion (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-34","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Contrast & Concession","src":"doc","sentences":["Supporters, however, insist that short-term pain is unavoidable in any serious structural reform.","Opponents argue the reforms will disproportionately harm the poorest households in the near term.","Both sides agree, at least, that the current system is unsustainable.","The disagreement, then, is really about the pace of change rather than its necessity."],"type":"tita","answer":"3214","explanation":"Point of agreement first (3), one side’s concern (2), contrasting response (1), synthesizing conclusion (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-35","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Contrast & Concession","src":"doc","sentences":["Detractors, on the other hand, call it an overpriced product chasing a niche market.","Early reviewers described the device as a genuine breakthrough in wearable technology.","Sales figures released this week suggest the detractors may be closer to correct.","The company insists it remains committed to the product despite disappointing early numbers."],"type":"tita","answer":"2134","explanation":"Positive view (2), contrasting critical view (1), evidence favoring the critics (3), company’s response (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-36","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Contrast & Concession","src":"doc","sentences":["Nevertheless, the treaty succeeded where three previous attempts had failed.","Diplomats spent nearly two years negotiating terms that satisfied no single party completely.","Each side privately considered the final agreement to be a disappointing compromise.","That modest, unglamorous success may be the most realistic model for future negotiations."],"type":"tita","answer":"2314","explanation":"Process described (2), reaction to outcome (3), “nevertheless” contrast — success despite disappointment (1), broader lesson (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-37","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Contrast & Concession","src":"doc","sentences":["Yet independent audits later found no evidence to support either the praise or the alarm.","The new curriculum was praised by some educators as a long-overdue modernization.","Others warned it would leave students unprepared for standardized testing.","The muted, inconclusive result has done little to end the debate either way."],"type":"tita","answer":"2314","explanation":"Positive view (2), contrasting negative view (3), “yet” — neutral evidence undercutting both (1), aftermath (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-38","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Contrast & Concession","src":"doc","sentences":["However, later excavations revealed tools and settlements far older than the theory allowed.","For decades, a single migration theory dominated explanations of the continent’s earliest inhabitants.","That evidence has forced a substantial revision of the accepted timeline.","Researchers now debate multiple, possibly overlapping, waves of early settlement."],"type":"tita","answer":"2134","explanation":"Established view (2), contrasting new evidence (1), consequence of that evidence (3), current state of debate (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-39","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Contrast & Concession","src":"doc","sentences":["Even so, several holdout regions continued using the old system for years afterward.","The metric system was formally adopted nationwide following a single legislative act.","Officials attributed the delay to the high cost of replacing existing equipment and signage.","Complete, universal adoption was not achieved until nearly two decades after the original law."],"type":"tita","answer":"2134","explanation":"Formal adoption (2), “even so” contrast — holdouts persisted (1), reason for the holdouts (3), eventual resolution (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-40","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Contrast & Concession","src":"doc","sentences":["Even its defenders concede the policy has had unintended and costly side effects.","The subsidy program was originally designed to protect small farmers from price volatility.","In practice, however, the bulk of its benefits have flowed to large agribusinesses.","Reform proposals have repeatedly stalled amid disagreement over how to fix the imbalance."],"type":"tita","answer":"2314","explanation":"Original intent (2), “in practice, however” contrast (3), concession by defenders (1), current stalemate (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-41","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Cause and Effect","src":"doc","sentences":["As a result, insurance premiums in the affected region rose sharply the following year.","A series of unusually severe storms caused record property damage across the coastline.","Many homeowners, unable to afford the new rates, chose to sell and relocate inland.","That migration has already begun to reshape the economic profile of several coastal towns."],"type":"tita","answer":"2134","explanation":"Cause (2) → effect (“as a result”) (1) → consequence of that effect (3) → longer-term consequence (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-42","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Cause and Effect","src":"doc","sentences":["Consequently, the central bank raised interest rates for the third consecutive quarter.","Inflation climbed well above the target range for six months running.","Higher borrowing costs, in turn, began to cool an overheated housing market.","Economists remain divided on whether the slowdown will be temporary or prolonged."],"type":"tita","answer":"2134","explanation":"Cause (2) → response (“consequently”) (1) → effect of that response (3) → open question (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-43","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Cause and Effect","src":"doc","sentences":["This forced several major airlines to ground a significant portion of their fleets.","A design flaw was traced to a single sensor prone to erratic readings.","The grounding, though costly, likely prevented further incidents from occurring.","Investigators had begun examining the aircraft after two unrelated incidents within a single month."],"type":"tita","answer":"4213","explanation":"Trigger event (4) → what was found (“traced to”) (2) → consequence (“this forced”) (1) → assessment of that consequence (3).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-44","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Cause and Effect","src":"doc","sentences":["Consequently, several key witnesses became reluctant to testify at all.","The trial’s postponement stretched what should have been a six-month process into three years.","Memories faded, and evidence once considered solid grew increasingly disputed.","Prosecutors have since called for stricter limits on how long a case can be delayed."],"type":"tita","answer":"2134","explanation":"Cause (2) → effect (1) → further effect (3) → resulting policy call (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-45","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Cause and Effect","src":"doc","sentences":["As a direct consequence, local fish populations collapsed within a few short years.","A dam built upstream altered the river’s natural flow and temperature patterns.","Fishing communities downstream lost their primary source of income almost overnight.","Many families were forced to migrate to cities in search of alternative work."],"type":"tita","answer":"2134","explanation":"Cause (2) → effect (1) → consequence for communities (3) → further consequence (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-46","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Cause and Effect","src":"doc","sentences":["This, in turn, triggered a wave of loan defaults across the sector.","A sudden currency devaluation made imported raw materials significantly more expensive overnight.","Manufacturers who had borrowed heavily in foreign currency saw their debts effectively double.","Regulators are still assessing how much of the financial system was exposed to that risk."],"type":"tita","answer":"2314","explanation":"Cause (2) → immediate effect on borrowers (3) → resulting crisis (“this, in turn”) (1) → ongoing assessment (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-47","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Cause and Effect","src":"doc","sentences":["As a result, the once-thriving fishing village saw its population shrink by half within a decade.","Overfishing by large commercial trawlers depleted stocks that local fishers depended on.","Younger residents left first, seeking work in industries less tied to the sea.","Those who remained have increasingly turned to tourism as an alternative livelihood."],"type":"tita","answer":"2134","explanation":"Cause (2) → effect (1) → elaboration on who left first (3) → what remained did (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-48","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Cause and Effect","src":"doc","sentences":["This delay, ultimately, cost the company its first-mover advantage in the market.","Regulatory approval for the new drug took nearly twice as long as anticipated.","A rival firm launched a competing product just months before final clearance arrived.","Analysts now cite the case as a lesson in planning for regulatory uncertainty."],"type":"tita","answer":"2314","explanation":"Delay occurs (2) → rival launches during the delay (3) → “this delay,” as a result, cost the first-mover advantage (1) → lesson drawn (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-49","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Cause and Effect","src":"doc","sentences":["Consequently, the region’s groundwater levels have dropped to historic lows.","Decades of water-intensive crop subsidies encouraged farmers to over-extract from local aquifers.","Several wells that once supplied entire villages have now run completely dry.","Policymakers are only now beginning to reconsider the subsidies that started the cycle."],"type":"tita","answer":"2134","explanation":"Cause (2) → effect (1) → specific consequence (3) → policy response, decades later (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-50","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Cause and Effect","src":"doc","sentences":["As a result, small local publishers found themselves unable to compete on price.","A large retailer began aggressively discounting bestsellers well below their own cost.","Many closed within a few years, unable to sustain the losses.","The retailer’s long-term strategy, critics argue, was never really about the discounts themselves."],"type":"tita","answer":"2134","explanation":"Cause (2) → effect (1) → consequence (3) → critical reflection (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-51","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Definition / Concept-First Openers","src":"doc","sentences":["It occurs when a system, disturbed slightly, tends to return to its original state rather than spiral away from it.","Negative feedback is a foundational concept in both engineering and ecology.","Household thermostats and predator-prey population cycles are both everyday examples of the same underlying logic.","Without such stabilizing mechanisms, many natural and artificial systems would be dangerously unpredictable."],"type":"tita","answer":"2134","explanation":"Concept named (2), definition (1), examples (3), significance (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-52","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Definition / Concept-First Openers","src":"doc","sentences":["It refers to the tendency of new information to be interpreted in ways that confirm existing beliefs.","Confirmation bias is one of the most well-documented distortions in human reasoning.","Even trained scientists, studies show, are not immune to this pattern.","Awareness of the bias, unfortunately, does little on its own to eliminate its effects."],"type":"tita","answer":"2134","explanation":"Concept named (2), definition (1), scope of the problem (3), caveat/conclusion (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-53","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Definition / Concept-First Openers","src":"doc","sentences":["It describes a situation in which individually rational choices lead to a collectively harmful outcome.","The tragedy of the commons is a recurring theme in environmental economics.","Overfishing in unregulated waters is perhaps its most frequently cited real-world example.","Solutions typically require either regulation or a shift in how the resource is owned."],"type":"tita","answer":"2134","explanation":"Concept named (2), definition (1), example (3), solutions (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-54","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Definition / Concept-First Openers","src":"doc","sentences":["Such asymmetries can lead healthy markets to collapse entirely, as buyers assume the worst.","Information asymmetry occurs when one party in a transaction knows significantly more than the other.","The market for used cars is the textbook example, since sellers know more about a car’s condition than buyers.","Warranties and third-party inspections are two common ways markets try to correct the imbalance."],"type":"tita","answer":"2314","explanation":"Concept named (2), example (3), consequence (1), solutions (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-55","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Definition / Concept-First Openers","src":"doc","sentences":["It happens when a decision-maker continues investing in a failing course of action because of resources already spent.","The sunk cost fallacy affects decisions ranging from personal relationships to national infrastructure projects.","Economists argue that only future costs and benefits should factor into rational decisions.","Yet emotionally, the pull of “not wanting it to have been for nothing” is remarkably hard to resist."],"type":"tita","answer":"2134","explanation":"Concept named (2), definition (1), rational prescription (3), emotional counterpoint (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-56","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Definition / Concept-First Openers","src":"doc","sentences":["It is typically defined as a sustained decline in general price levels across an economy.","Deflation is often assumed to be the opposite, and therefore the cure, for inflation.","In practice, however, deflation can be just as economically damaging as its counterpart.","Falling prices discourage spending today in anticipation of even lower prices tomorrow."],"type":"tita","answer":"2134","explanation":"Concept named with common assumption (2), definition (1), contrasting reality (3), mechanism explaining why (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-57","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Definition / Concept-First Openers","src":"doc","sentences":["It refers to organisms so essential to their ecosystem that its removal triggers a cascade of collapse.","Wolves in Yellowstone are frequently cited as a real-world example of a keystone species.","A keystone species plays a role disproportionate to its actual population size.","Their reintroduction in the 1990s dramatically altered the park’s rivers, vegetation, and animal populations."],"type":"tita","answer":"3124","explanation":"Concept named (3), definition (1), example (2), elaboration on the example (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-58","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Definition / Concept-First Openers","src":"doc","sentences":["It occurs when a currency’s value is tied directly to a fixed quantity of another asset.","A gold standard is one of the oldest forms of monetary policy still occasionally debated today.","Most major economies abandoned the practice by the mid-twentieth century.","Critics argue it offers stability at the cost of flexibility during economic crises."],"type":"tita","answer":"2134","explanation":"Concept named (2), definition (1), historical fact (3), critique (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-59","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Definition / Concept-First Openers","src":"doc","sentences":["It describes the gradual loss of biodiversity as habitats are broken into smaller, isolated patches.","Habitat fragmentation is a leading, if underappreciated, driver of species extinction.","Species requiring large territories are often the first to disappear from fragmented landscapes.","Wildlife corridors have emerged as one practical attempt to counteract the effect."],"type":"tita","answer":"2134","explanation":"Concept named (2), definition (1), consequence (3), solution (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-60","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Definition / Concept-First Openers","src":"doc","sentences":["It refers to the psychological tendency to overweight losses relative to equivalent gains.","Loss aversion is a central finding of behavioral economics research.","People often reject a fair coin-flip gamble even when the potential gain exceeds the potential loss.","This asymmetry has significant implications for how insurance and pension products are designed."],"type":"tita","answer":"2134","explanation":"Concept named (2), definition (1), example (3), implication (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-61","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Narrative / Anecdotal Openers","src":"doc","sentences":["What began as a hobby soon consumed nearly all of her free time and savings.","She had first started restoring old furniture simply to fill weekends during a long illness.","Within two years, that hobby had become a full-fledged business with a waiting list of clients.","Today, she describes the illness as the accidental beginning of her real career."],"type":"tita","answer":"2134","explanation":"Origin story (2), immediate consequence (1), development (3), present-day reflection (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-62","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Narrative / Anecdotal Openers","src":"doc","sentences":["He had packed only a single change of clothes, certain the trip would last no more than a week.","A missed connection stranded him in a city he had never planned to visit.","Three months later, he was still there, having taken a job at a small local café.","The unplanned detour, he now says, taught him more than any itinerary could have."],"type":"tita","answer":"2134","explanation":"Inciting event (2), detail about his preparation (1), consequence over time (3), reflection (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-63","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Narrative / Anecdotal Openers","src":"doc","sentences":["The letter, undelivered for over sixty years, had been misfiled in a regional sorting office.","It finally reached its intended recipient, now in his nineties, last spring.","A postal worker clearing out old storage stumbled upon a stack of forgotten mail.","He wept reading words his late mother had written to him as a boy."],"type":"tita","answer":"3124","explanation":"Discovery (3), backstory of the letter (1), delivery (2), emotional reaction (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-64","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Narrative / Anecdotal Openers","src":"doc","sentences":["It would take a chance meeting with a stranger on a train to change her mind.","Certain her novel would never be published, she had all but given up on writing.","That stranger, as it turned out, was a literary agent looking for exactly her kind of story.","Within a year, the manuscript she had almost abandoned was a national bestseller."],"type":"tita","answer":"2134","explanation":"Setup (2), turning point (1), revelation about the stranger (3), outcome (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-65","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Narrative / Anecdotal Openers","src":"doc","sentences":["The village had gone nearly a century without a single case of the disease.","Doctors initially dismissed the first patient’s symptoms as an unrelated seasonal illness.","Within weeks, that misdiagnosis had allowed the outbreak to spread to dozens more.","Investigators later traced the source to a contaminated well the village had used for generations."],"type":"tita","answer":"1234","explanation":"Background (1), initial event (2), consequence (3), later explanation (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-66","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Narrative / Anecdotal Openers","src":"doc","sentences":["He had never intended for the sketch to be seen by anyone outside his notebook.","A friend, without asking, submitted it to a local art competition on his behalf.","It won first prize, launching a career the young artist had never seriously considered.","He still keeps that notebook, he says, as a reminder of how little he once expected of himself."],"type":"tita","answer":"1234","explanation":"Setup (1), inciting action (2), consequence (3), present-day reflection (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-67","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Narrative / Anecdotal Openers","src":"doc","sentences":["Locals had long dismissed the ruins as nothing more than an old, unremarkable farmhouse.","A university survey team, conducting an unrelated soil study, noticed unusual patterns beneath the topsoil.","Excavation revealed the foundations of a settlement nearly two thousand years older than anyone expected.","The farmhouse, it turned out, had been built directly atop a forgotten piece of history."],"type":"tita","answer":"1234","explanation":"Background belief (1), discovery trigger (2), consequence of investigation (3), reframing conclusion (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-68","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Narrative / Anecdotal Openers","src":"doc","sentences":["It was only after his death that the full scope of his quiet generosity became known.","Neighbors described him as reclusive, frugal, and almost invisible in the small community.","His will revealed decades of anonymous donations to the local school and hospital.","The town has since renamed its library in honor of a man few had truly known."],"type":"tita","answer":"2134","explanation":"Background characterization (2), reveal (1), specifics of the reveal (3), consequence (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-69","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Narrative / Anecdotal Openers","src":"doc","sentences":["The recipe, passed down through four generations, had never once been written down.","Her grandmother finally agreed to record it, insisting nothing be measured “properly.”","What resulted was a set of instructions built entirely around handfuls, pinches, and “until it feels right.”","It remains, unusually, one of the family’s most treasured possessions despite containing no exact numbers."],"type":"tita","answer":"1234","explanation":"Background (1), turning point (2), outcome (3), reflection (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-70","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Narrative / Anecdotal Openers","src":"doc","sentences":["No one on the expedition had expected the cave system to extend nearly as far as it did.","What started as a two-hour survey turned into a three-day journey underground.","Their equipment, packed for a short trip, ran dangerously low well before they found an exit.","The team now credits careful rationing, not luck, for getting them out safely."],"type":"tita","answer":"1234","explanation":"Setup/surprise (1), consequence (2), further consequence (3), retrospective assessment (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-71","topic":"pj","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Enumeration / Listing Structure","src":"doc","sentences":["Second, it must be resistant to at least three common strains of the pathogen.","Any candidate vaccine had to satisfy three strict conditions before advancing to trials.","First, it needed to remain stable without refrigeration for at least a month.","Third, and most challenging, it had to be producible at a cost under one dollar per dose."],"type":"tita","answer":"2314","explanation":"Introduces the criteria (2), then lists them in order: “first” (3), “second” (1), “third” (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-72","topic":"pj","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Enumeration / Listing Structure","src":"doc","sentences":["The second involves gradually raising the retirement age in line with life expectancy.","Economists have proposed three broad approaches to closing the pension shortfall.","The first would raise mandatory contribution rates across the workforce.","The third, and most politically fraught, would reduce guaranteed benefits for future retirees."],"type":"tita","answer":"2314","explanation":"Introduces the approaches (2), then in order: “the first” (3), “the second” (1), “the third” (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-73","topic":"pj","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Enumeration / Listing Structure","src":"doc","sentences":["The second phase focuses on identifying the specific gene responsible for the trait.","The research program is structured around three distinct phases.","Its final phase tests whether the finding holds across different populations.","An initial phase involves screening thousands of samples for a shared characteristic."],"type":"tita","answer":"2413","explanation":"Introduces the structure (2), then: “an initial phase” (4), “the second phase” (1), “final phase” (3).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-74","topic":"pj","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Enumeration / Listing Structure","src":"doc","sentences":["A third demands that manufacturers disclose full ingredient lists on packaging.","New consumer protection rules impose several fresh obligations on retailers.","One requires clear, itemized pricing at the point of sale.","Another mandates a minimum thirty-day return window for all online purchases."],"type":"tita","answer":"2341","explanation":"Introduces the obligations (2), then: “one” (3), “another” (4), “a third” (1).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-75","topic":"pj","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Enumeration / Listing Structure","src":"doc","sentences":["A second reason lies in currency fluctuations that eroded profit margins on exports.","Analysts point to three factors behind the company’s disappointing quarterly results.","The first is a sharp slowdown in demand from its largest overseas market.","Rising input costs, the third factor, compounded both of the earlier pressures."],"type":"tita","answer":"2314","explanation":"Introduces the factors (2), then: “the first” (3), “a second reason” (1), “the third factor” (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-76","topic":"pj","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Enumeration / Listing Structure","src":"doc","sentences":["The second criterion evaluates the originality of the proposed solution.","Judges scored each entry against four separate criteria.","Feasibility, assessed first, ruled out nearly a third of all submissions.","The final two criteria measured potential impact and quality of presentation."],"type":"tita","answer":"2314","explanation":"Introduces criteria (2), then: “assessed first” (3), “second criterion” (1), “final two” (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-77","topic":"pj","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Enumeration / Listing Structure","src":"doc","sentences":["The second stage tests the material’s performance under extreme temperature variation.","Before certification, every component must pass through four separate testing stages.","An initial stage checks for basic structural integrity under standard conditions.","The final two stages simulate, respectively, prolonged use and sudden impact."],"type":"tita","answer":"2314","explanation":"Introduces the stages (2), then: “an initial stage” (3), “second stage” (1), “final two stages” (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-78","topic":"pj","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Enumeration / Listing Structure","src":"doc","sentences":["A second wave targeted financial institutions with more sophisticated phishing techniques.","Cybersecurity experts identified three distinct waves in the recent attack campaign.","The first wave consisted of relatively unsophisticated attempts against small businesses.","The third and most alarming wave directly targeted critical infrastructure systems."],"type":"tita","answer":"2314","explanation":"Introduces the waves (2), then: “the first wave” (3), “a second wave” (1), “the third” (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-79","topic":"pj","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Enumeration / Listing Structure","src":"doc","sentences":["The second obstacle involved securing land rights from several competing local claimants.","Completing the highway required overcoming three major logistical obstacles.","Difficult mountain terrain presented the first, and most costly, engineering challenge.","Environmental clearances, the third obstacle, delayed the project by nearly two years."],"type":"tita","answer":"2314","explanation":"Introduces obstacles (2), then: “the first” (3), “the second obstacle” (1), “the third obstacle” (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-80","topic":"pj","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Enumeration / Listing Structure","src":"doc","sentences":["A second explanation points to a temporary supply disruption in a key growing region.","Economists offer three competing explanations for the sudden spike in coffee prices.","Rising global demand accounts for part of the increase, most agree.","Currency speculation in futures markets, the third factor, may explain the rest."],"type":"tita","answer":"2314","explanation":"Introduces explanations (2), then: “rising global demand” first explanation (3), “a second explanation” (1), “third factor” (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-81","topic":"pj","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Mixed Technique (Harder Sets)","src":"doc","sentences":["That single decision, in hindsight, may have saved the company from bankruptcy.","Facing a cash crunch, the founders considered laying off nearly half the staff.","Instead, they chose to cut their own salaries to zero for six months.","Employee loyalty during that period became, unexpectedly, the company’s strongest asset."],"type":"tita","answer":"2314","explanation":"Situation (2), the alternative chosen (“instead”) (3), assessment of that choice (1), resulting benefit (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-82","topic":"pj","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Mixed Technique (Harder Sets)","src":"doc","sentences":["It relies on bacteria naturally present in the soil to break down the contaminant.","Bioremediation offers a cheaper alternative to physically excavating contaminated land.","The process, though slower than excavation, avoids disturbing the surrounding ecosystem.","Its main limitation is that not every contaminant can be broken down this way."],"type":"tita","answer":"2134","explanation":"Concept and comparison (2), mechanism (1), advantage (3), limitation (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-83","topic":"pj","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Mixed Technique (Harder Sets)","src":"doc","sentences":["Yet within the company, the decision was framed as a long-overdue correction.","Outside observers called the layoffs an admission that the expansion had failed.","Investors, largely, sided with the internal framing and the stock rose on the news.","The company had announced an aggressive hiring spree only eighteen months earlier."],"type":"tita","answer":"4213","explanation":"Background (4), external interpretation (2), contrasting internal interpretation (“yet”) (1), market reaction favoring one side (3).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-84","topic":"pj","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Mixed Technique (Harder Sets)","src":"doc","sentences":["Its exact cause remained a mystery for nearly a decade after the first cases appeared.","A rare autoimmune condition was first documented in a small mountain community.","Genetic sequencing eventually revealed a shared mutation among nearly all affected families.","That finding has since opened new avenues for early, targeted diagnosis."],"type":"tita","answer":"2134","explanation":"First identification (2), unresolved mystery (1), breakthrough (3), consequence (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-85","topic":"pj","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Mixed Technique (Harder Sets)","src":"doc","sentences":["Regulators have so far declined to comment on whether new rules are being considered.","Several major banks quietly changed their overdraft fee structures within the same month.","Consumer groups suspect the timing was no coincidence.","They argue the changes were designed to preempt anticipated regulation."],"type":"tita","answer":"2341","explanation":"Event (2), suspicion (3), reasoning behind the suspicion (4), regulators’ current silence (1).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-86","topic":"pj","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Mixed Technique (Harder Sets)","src":"doc","sentences":["Only later did researchers realize the correlation had a far simpler explanation.","An early study linked the two variables and speculated about a possible biological mechanism.","The media coverage that followed rarely mentioned how tentative the original claim had been.","Both variables, it turned out, were independently caused by a third, unmeasured factor."],"type":"tita","answer":"2314","explanation":"Original claim (2), media reaction (3), later correction (1), explanation of the correction (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-87","topic":"pj","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Mixed Technique (Harder Sets)","src":"doc","sentences":["That gap has narrowed significantly over the past two decades, though it has not closed.","Researchers attribute part of the remaining gap to differences in career interruptions.","Historically, a persistent wage gap existed between men and women in comparable roles.","The remainder, they note, is harder to explain through measurable factors alone."],"type":"tita","answer":"3124","explanation":"Historical background (3), trend (1), partial explanation (2), the unexplained remainder (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-88","topic":"pj","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Mixed Technique (Harder Sets)","src":"doc","sentences":["Its success depended entirely on convincing farmers to abandon a centuries-old practice.","The reform’s architects underestimated just how deeply that practice was embedded in local identity.","A new agricultural policy aimed to modernize irrigation across the region.","Resistance, as a result, was far fiercer than any economic model had predicted."],"type":"tita","answer":"3124","explanation":"Policy introduced (3), what it depended on (1), the miscalculation (2), consequence (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-89","topic":"pj","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Mixed Technique (Harder Sets)","src":"doc","sentences":["Few realized, at the time, how thoroughly it would reshape the entire industry.","What seemed like a minor technical update was released with little fanfare.","Competitors scrambled for years afterward to catch up with what they had dismissed.","The update, in hindsight, is now considered a turning point in the field."],"type":"tita","answer":"2134","explanation":"Event (2), lack of foresight (1), consequence (3), retrospective judgment (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-90","topic":"pj","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Mixed Technique (Harder Sets)","src":"doc","sentences":["The city had spent millions preparing for a flood of a very different kind.","When the disaster finally struck, it came not as water but as fire.","Years of drought had made the surrounding forest far more flammable than anyone anticipated.","Existing flood defenses, ironically, did nothing to slow the blaze’s advance."],"type":"tita","answer":"1234","explanation":"Setup (1), the twist (“not as water but as fire”) (2), explanation (3), irony (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-91","topic":"pj","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Full-Length 5-Sentence Sets","src":"doc","sentences":["That gap, economists warned, would only widen as automation accelerated.","Manufacturing employment in the region had been declining steadily since the early 2000s.","A retraining program was launched to help displaced workers transition to service-sector jobs.","Early results, however, showed most participants struggling to find comparably paid work.","Wages in the new roles they did secure lagged significantly behind their previous manufacturing pay."],"type":"tita","answer":"21345","explanation":"Background trend (2), the warned consequence (1), the intervention (3), early results (4), elaboration on the shortfall (5).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-92","topic":"pj","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Full-Length 5-Sentence Sets","src":"doc","sentences":["Word of the discovery spread quickly through the small mining town.","A miner clearing rubble after a minor collapse noticed an unusual glint in the rock face.","Within days, hundreds of prospectors had arrived hoping to stake a claim nearby.","Closer inspection revealed a vein of a mineral no one had expected to find in the region.","The resulting boom transformed the sleepy town into a bustling settlement within a single year."],"type":"tita","answer":"24135","explanation":"Discovery event (2), what closer inspection showed (4), spreading word (1), the rush that followed (3), long-term consequence (5).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-93","topic":"pj","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Full-Length 5-Sentence Sets","src":"doc","sentences":["That single design choice would go on to influence nearly every competitor that followed.","The original team had almost abandoned the feature during early testing, judging it unnecessary.","A junior engineer’s late addition to the design was initially met with skepticism from senior staff.","User feedback after launch, however, singled it out as the product’s most valued feature.","Its unexpected popularity forced the team to reconsider its own assumptions about what users wanted."],"type":"tita","answer":"32451","explanation":"Introduction (3), context on internal doubt (2), contrasting user reaction (4), consequence for the team (5), lasting industry impact (1).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-94","topic":"pj","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Full-Length 5-Sentence Sets","src":"doc","sentences":["This forced hospitals in the region to activate emergency staffing protocols overnight.","A sudden spike in cases overwhelmed the capacity of local clinics within days.","Public health officials had initially downplayed the outbreak as a minor, contained cluster.","Investigators later concluded that earlier, more aggressive intervention could have limited its spread.","The revised response eventually brought the outbreak under control within three weeks."],"type":"tita","answer":"32154","explanation":"Initial downplaying (3), the spike that followed (2), the forced response (1), the eventual result (5), the later assessment (4).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-95","topic":"pj","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Full-Length 5-Sentence Sets","src":"doc","sentences":["Contracts had already been signed with three international suppliers before construction even began.","The bridge project was approved despite warnings from independent engineers about the terrain.","Six months in, cost overruns had already exceeded the original budget by half.","Officials, unwilling to abandon sunk investments, pushed ahead despite mounting evidence of trouble.","The bridge finally opened, four years late and at nearly triple its original projected cost."],"type":"tita","answer":"21345","explanation":"Approval despite warnings (2), early commitments (1), cost overruns (3), the decision to push ahead (4), final outcome (5).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-96","topic":"pj","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Full-Length 5-Sentence Sets","src":"doc","sentences":["That decision proved controversial among longtime employees who valued the company’s original culture.","A rapidly growing startup found itself struggling to maintain consistency across its expanding teams.","Several senior leaders left within the following year, citing the change as a key factor.","Management responded by introducing a detailed, standardized set of internal procedures.","The company now cites that turbulent transition as a necessary, if painful, growing phase."],"type":"tita","answer":"24135","explanation":"Problem (2), management’s response (4), reaction to that response (1), consequence of the reaction (3), retrospective framing (5).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-97","topic":"pj","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Full-Length 5-Sentence Sets","src":"doc","sentences":["Sales of the reissued edition, unexpectedly, outperformed even the original release.","A novel that had gone entirely out of print resurfaced decades later in an online forum.","Renewed interest online eventually caught the attention of a small independent publisher.","A single enthusiastic recommendation there set off a wave of secondhand searches for copies.","The publisher decided to reissue the forgotten book with a modest new print run."],"type":"tita","answer":"24351","explanation":"Resurfacing (2), the trigger (4), the resulting attention (3), publisher’s decision (5), outcome (1).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-98","topic":"pj","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Full-Length 5-Sentence Sets","src":"doc","sentences":["Independent labs, however, could not replicate the results under identical conditions.","A small pilot study reported a surprisingly large effect from the new supplement.","The company behind the supplement pointed to differences in trial protocol to explain the discrepancy.","Media coverage of the original study had already reached millions before the failed replications were published.","Regulators are now reviewing whether the marketing claims outpaced the actual evidence."],"type":"tita","answer":"24135","explanation":"Original claim (2), its wide reach (4), the failed replication (“however”) (1), company’s defense (3), regulatory response (5).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-99","topic":"pj","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Full-Length 5-Sentence Sets","src":"doc","sentences":["That gap in coverage left an entire region without reliable emergency communication for days.","A single point of failure in the network’s backbone infrastructure triggered a cascading outage.","Engineers traced the root cause to a routine software update pushed the previous night.","The outage disabled cell service across several states almost simultaneously.","The company has since committed to a slower, more staged rollout process for future updates."],"type":"tita","answer":"32415","explanation":"Root cause (3), the triggered failure (2), immediate effect (4), consequence for the region (1), company’s response (5).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pj-100","topic":"pj","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Full-Length 5-Sentence Sets","src":"doc","sentences":["Within a year, three more countries in the region had adopted nearly identical legislation.","A small nation’s parliament passed what was, at the time, considered a radical environmental law.","Businesses initially warned the law would cripple economic competitiveness.","Those predicted losses largely failed to materialize once the law took effect.","The law’s apparent success is now credited with shifting the broader regional conversation on regulation."],"type":"tita","answer":"23415","explanation":"Original event (2), initial objection (3), outcome contradicting the objection (4), regional ripple effect (1), broader significance (5).","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers."},{"id":"pc-1","topic":"pc","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Maintaining Logical Direction","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Directly follows from the induced-demand point just made — expanding capacity doesn’t solve the underlying problem. (A) overreaches into an absolute the paragraph never supports. (C) introduces an unrelated new claim about driver behavior. (D) pivots to cost comparison, which the paragraph never raised.","stem":"Urban planners have long assumed that adding lanes to a congested highway reduces traffic. But data from dozens of cities shows the opposite: new lanes fill up within a few years, and congestion returns to previous levels. This phenomenon, known as induced demand, suggests that _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"highways should never be built in any city, regardless of population."},{"k":"B","t":"simply expanding road capacity is unlikely to solve congestion on its own."},{"k":"C","t":"traffic congestion is primarily caused by poor driver behavior."},{"k":"D","t":"public transit is always cheaper to build than new highways."}]},{"id":"pc-2","topic":"pc","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Maintaining Logical Direction","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Ties directly to the specific failure described — investors not verifying numbers because they were dazzled. (A) contradicts the implied criticism. (C) and (D) overgeneralize far beyond what this single case supports.","stem":"The startup had raised three rounds of funding on the strength of a single charismatic pitch, with revenue numbers that were, in hindsight, wildly optimistic. Investors, dazzled by the founder’s confidence, rarely asked for independent verification of the figures. When the company finally collapsed, the postmortem made clear that _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"the founder had committed no wrongdoing whatsoever."},{"k":"B","t":"due diligence had been sacrificed for excitement about the founder’s vision."},{"k":"C","t":"all startups with charismatic founders are destined to fail."},{"k":"D","t":"revenue projections are impossible to verify in any industry."}]},{"id":"pc-3","topic":"pc","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Maintaining Logical Direction","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Follows the paragraph’s setup — a bet on tourism as a replacement — to its logical payoff. (A) contradicts the premise that stocks had collapsed and the town needed an alternative. (C) contradicts investing in coastline tourism. (D) introduces an irrelevant claim about other towns.","stem":"For years, the coastal town relied on a single fishing fleet for most of its income. When stocks collapsed after decades of overfishing, the town faced a choice: diversify or decline. Local leaders invested heavily in tourism infrastructure, betting that the same coastline that once fed the fishing industry could now attract visitors instead. A decade later, _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"the fishing industry had fully recovered to its former size."},{"k":"B","t":"tourism revenue had come to exceed what the fishing industry once provided."},{"k":"C","t":"the town had abandoned the coastline entirely for inland development."},{"k":"D","t":"no other coastal towns had attempted a similar transition."}]},{"id":"pc-4","topic":"pc","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Maintaining Logical Direction","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Follows directly from the procedural change described — breaking the proposal into clauses reducing friction. (A), (C), and (D) all contradict the constructive tone the paragraph has built toward.","stem":"The committee had debated the proposal for nearly two years without resolution, each side convinced the other was arguing in bad faith. When a new chairperson took over, she made a small but crucial change: rather than voting on the entire proposal, members would vote on each clause separately. Within a single meeting, _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"the committee was permanently disbanded."},{"k":"B","t":"most clauses were approved with far less friction than expected."},{"k":"C","t":"the original proposal was rejected without any amendments."},{"k":"D","t":"the new chairperson resigned over the dispute."}]},{"id":"pc-5","topic":"pc","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Maintaining Logical Direction","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Matches the described middle-ground fix exactly — keep new layout, restore missed shortcuts. (A) and (D) contradict the compromise described. (C) contradicts the team’s stated responsiveness to feedback.","stem":"Early users of the app complained loudly about a redesigned interface that removed several familiar shortcuts. The development team, initially defensive, eventually sat down to review months of user feedback in detail. Rather than reverting the design entirely, they restored the specific shortcuts users had missed most while keeping the new visual layout. The result was _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"a near-total abandonment of the app by its user base."},{"k":"B","t":"a version that retained the redesign’s benefits while addressing the core complaints."},{"k":"C","t":"a decision to ignore all future user feedback."},{"k":"D","t":"a complete return to the original, pre-redesign interface."}]},{"id":"pc-6","topic":"pc","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Maintaining Logical Direction","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"“Something curators hadn’t anticipated” signals a surprising, specific finding — this option gives a concrete, unexpected behavior. (A) is plausible but contradicts the “hadn’t anticipated” framing pointing toward something more specific. (B) is too vague to be “something” specific. (D) introduces an unrelated, unsupported claim.","stem":"The museum’s new policy allowed visitors to photograph any exhibit except a small number of light-sensitive manuscripts. Initially, curators worried this would encourage crowding and reduce the quality of the viewing experience for others. Attendance data from the first year, however, showed something curators hadn’t anticipated: _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"visitors spent less time overall in the museum than before."},{"k":"B","t":"photography privileges had no measurable effect on visitor behavior."},{"k":"C","t":"many visitors lingered longer, using photographs to study details more closely."},{"k":"D","t":"the manuscripts had to be removed from display entirely."}]},{"id":"pc-7","topic":"pc","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Maintaining Logical Direction","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Directly follows the setup — backchannel drafting designed to avoid either side “formally proposing” a compromise, which naturally resolves into a face-saving deal. (A), (C), and (D) all contradict the constructive purpose described for the backchannel effort.","stem":"The negotiations had stalled for months over a single disputed clause about intellectual property rights. Both delegations publicly insisted they would not compromise. Behind closed doors, however, junior staff on both sides had quietly begun drafting alternative language that neither side had to formally propose first. This backchannel work eventually _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"had no impact on the final outcome of the talks."},{"k":"B","t":"provided the face-saving compromise that broke the public deadlock."},{"k":"C","t":"was rejected by both delegations as irrelevant."},{"k":"D","t":"led to the complete collapse of the negotiations."}]},{"id":"pc-8","topic":"pc","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Maintaining Logical Direction","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Follows directly from the diagnosis — the issue was the rubric, not the content, so fixing the rubric should improve outcomes without changing difficulty. (A) contradicts the fix having any effect. (C) and (D) are unsupported and contradict the corrective direction of the paragraph.","stem":"The professor’s course had a reputation for being nearly impossible to pass, and enrollment had dwindled for years as a result. When a new dean reviewed the syllabus, she found the material wasn’t actually harder than comparable courses — the grading rubric was simply unusually punitive for minor errors. After the rubric was revised, _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"enrollment continued to decline at the same rate as before."},{"k":"B","t":"pass rates rose without any change to course content or difficulty."},{"k":"C","t":"the course was removed from the curriculum entirely."},{"k":"D","t":"the professor was asked to make the material more difficult."}]},{"id":"pc-9","topic":"pc","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Maintaining Logical Direction","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Matches the paragraph’s setup of skepticism giving way to a successful, resource-saving adaptation. (A) contradicts a positive outcome the paragraph is building toward. (C) contradicts adopting the new method successfully. (D) is an unsupported new claim.","stem":"The vineyard had used the same irrigation method for three generations, drawing water from a nearby river regardless of seasonal rainfall. A prolonged drought forced the owners to install sensors that adjusted watering based on real-time soil moisture. Skeptical at first, the family found that within two seasons, _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"grape yields collapsed due to insufficient water."},{"k":"B","t":"water usage dropped substantially while yields held steady or improved."},{"k":"C","t":"they abandoned the sensors and returned to the old method."},{"k":"D","t":"the vineyard shifted entirely to a different crop."}]},{"id":"pc-10","topic":"pc","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Maintaining Logical Direction","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Directly follows the described improvement — reliable information addressing the original complaint about unpredictability. (A), (C), and (D) all contradict the positive, problem-solving direction the paragraph has been building.","stem":"The city’s bus system had long been criticized for unpredictable arrival times, with riders often waiting without any real information. A pilot program installed GPS trackers on a quarter of the fleet and published real-time arrival estimates through a simple app. Ridership on those specific routes _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"dropped noticeably as riders switched to other forms of transport."},{"k":"B","t":"rose, as riders reported feeling more confident planning their trips."},{"k":"C","t":"remained completely unaffected by the change."},{"k":"D","t":"fell because the app was too complicated to use."}]},{"id":"pc-11","topic":"pc","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Avoiding New, Unrelated Information","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Stays tightly within the paragraph’s established theme — ambiguity as a strength because it reflects how memory actually works. (A) contradicts the deliberate ambiguity being praised. (C) and (D) introduce unrelated new information about sales and future work.","stem":"The novel’s central theme is the unreliability of memory — every character remembers the same pivotal event differently, and the author deliberately never resolves whose version is “true.” Critics have praised this ambiguity as the book’s greatest strength, arguing that _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"the author should have included a clear timeline of events."},{"k":"B","t":"it mirrors how memory itself functions in real life."},{"k":"C","t":"the book’s sales figures exceeded the publisher’s expectations."},{"k":"D","t":"the author’s next novel will explore a similar theme."}]},{"id":"pc-12","topic":"pc","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Avoiding New, Unrelated Information","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"A","explanation":"Directly follows the “rather than limiting creativity” setup — the constraint became a source of inventiveness. (B) directly contradicts the local-sourcing premise. (C) and (D) introduce unrelated new facts not set up by the paragraph.","stem":"The chef’s tasting menu is built entirely around ingredients sourced within a fifty-kilometer radius of the restaurant, changing weekly based on what local farms have available. This constraint, rather than limiting creativity, has _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"forced the kitchen to develop unusually inventive combinations."},{"k":"B","t":"led the restaurant to import ingredients from overseas instead."},{"k":"C","t":"resulted in the restaurant closing during the winter months."},{"k":"D","t":"prompted a rival restaurant to adopt a similar sourcing policy."}]},{"id":"pc-13","topic":"pc","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Avoiding New, Unrelated Information","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Follows directly from the successful restoration and reinforcement described. (A) contradicts a successful restoration outcome. (C) and (D) introduce new, unsupported claims not implied by the paragraph.","stem":"The city’s oldest bridge, built in 1889, was scheduled for demolition after engineers deemed it structurally unsound. A local preservation group raised funds not to demolish it, but to fully restore its original ironwork while reinforcing the foundation to modern safety standards. Today, the bridge _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"remains closed to all traffic pending further review."},{"k":"B","t":"carries both pedestrian and vehicle traffic safely."},{"k":"C","t":"has been relocated to a museum for public display."},{"k":"D","t":"is being considered for a second, unrelated renovation project."}]},{"id":"pc-14","topic":"pc","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Avoiding New, Unrelated Information","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Reasonable, paragraph-consistent continuation — a “controversial decision” plausibly produces divided opinion, which surveys would capture. (A) is an extreme overreach not indicated. (C) and (D) introduce unrelated new information about pricing and other orchestras.","stem":"The orchestra’s new music director made a controversial decision in her first season: eliminating the traditional intermission from every performance. She argued that unbroken performances preserved the emotional continuity that composers originally intended. Season ticket holder surveys later showed _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"most patrons had stopped attending performances altogether."},{"k":"B","t":"audiences were fairly evenly split, with strong opinions on both sides."},{"k":"C","t":"the orchestra’s ticket prices had increased significantly."},{"k":"D","t":"a rival orchestra had adopted the opposite policy."}]},{"id":"pc-15","topic":"pc","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Avoiding New, Unrelated Information","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Stays tied to the paragraph’s core thread — the delay caused by slow trial recruitment, and its consequence within the same competitive space (treatments for the same condition). (A), (C), and (D) all pivot to information the paragraph gave no basis to expect.","stem":"The biotech firm’s flagship drug had shown remarkable results in early trials, curing a rare condition in nearly ninety percent of patients. But the condition was so rare that recruiting enough patients for a full-scale trial took years longer than expected. By the time the drug finally reached regulators, _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"a competitor’s drug for an unrelated condition had already launched."},{"k":"B","t":"two other companies had developed treatments for the same condition."},{"k":"C","t":"the original research team had moved on to a different project entirely."},{"k":"D","t":"the company’s stock price had become entirely disconnected from the drug’s prospects."}]},{"id":"pc-16","topic":"pc","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Avoiding New, Unrelated Information","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"A","explanation":"A natural, paragraph-consistent consequence of a small publisher having a surprise bestseller — larger publishers taking notice. (B) contradicts the implied credit the small house’s translation choices deserve. (C) contradicts the pattern the house is known for. (D) introduces an unsupported, unrelated legal claim.","stem":"The small publishing house built its reputation on translating obscure foreign-language novels that larger publishers considered too risky. One such gamble, a debut novel from a little-known Icelandic author, unexpectedly became an international bestseller. The unexpected success _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"prompted several larger publishers to bid for the house itself."},{"k":"B","t":"had nothing to do with the quality of the translation."},{"k":"C","t":"discouraged the house from continuing to take similar risks."},{"k":"D","t":"led the original Icelandic publisher to file a lawsuit."}]},{"id":"pc-17","topic":"pc","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Avoiding New, Unrelated Information","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Follows directly from the described relocation and its intended purpose. (A) and (C) contradict a successful correction. (D) introduces an unsupported claim — the paragraph never suggests wealthy areas lost stations, only that new ones were added elsewhere.","stem":"The city’s new bike-share program was initially criticized for placing docking stations disproportionately in wealthier neighborhoods. After a public outcry, the operator committed to relocating a quarter of all stations to underserved areas within a year. By the following summer, ridership data showed _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"overall program usage had declined sharply."},{"k":"B","t":"meaningful growth in ridership specifically from the newly served neighborhoods."},{"k":"C","t":"the program had been shut down due to funding issues."},{"k":"D","t":"wealthier neighborhoods had lost access to the program entirely."}]},{"id":"pc-18","topic":"pc","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Avoiding New, Unrelated Information","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Directly follows the narrative tension the paragraph builds — near-abandonment followed by a last-chance payoff. (A), (C), and (D) all break from the story’s established trajectory with unrelated pivots.","stem":"The documentary crew had planned to spend two weeks filming a single elusive species of owl. After nearly ten days without a single sighting, they were prepared to abandon the project. On their final scheduled night, _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"they decided to focus the documentary on a different species instead."},{"k":"B","t":"they finally captured rare footage of the owl hunting."},{"k":"C","t":"the production company canceled their funding."},{"k":"D","t":"a separate research team announced the species was extinct."}]},{"id":"pc-19","topic":"pc","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Avoiding New, Unrelated Information","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Follows the “instead” signal directly — using its established strength (route familiarity) to compete effectively, contradicting the analysts’ prediction. (A) and (C) match the analysts’ prediction rather than contradicting it as “instead” requires. (D) introduces an unrelated new business direction.","stem":"The regional airline had built its entire business model around routes too small for major carriers to bother with. When a larger competitor unexpectedly entered one of its core markets, most analysts predicted the smaller airline would struggle to survive. Instead, its deep familiarity with the route’s specific demand patterns allowed it to _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"exit the market entirely within six months."},{"k":"B","t":"undercut the larger competitor on the routes that mattered most."},{"k":"C","t":"merge with the larger competitor soon after."},{"k":"D","t":"expand into international long-haul routes for the first time."}]},{"id":"pc-20","topic":"pc","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Avoiding New, Unrelated Information","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Fits the buildup toward a significant, specific discovery within the same site. (A) overreaches — a chamber doesn’t necessarily mean total misidentification. (C) and (D) introduce unrelated new information about funding or a separate site.","stem":"The archaeological team had spent three seasons excavating a site they believed to be a minor trading post. A chance discovery of a sealed underground chamber in the final week of the third season revealed _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"that the site had been misidentified as a trading post entirely."},{"k":"B","t":"a burial complex far more elaborate than anything expected at the site."},{"k":"C","t":"that the team’s funding would be renewed for a fourth season."},{"k":"D","t":"an unrelated site nearby that had never been surveyed."}]},{"id":"pc-21","topic":"pc","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Matching Tone and Register","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Matches the established measured, balanced tone. (A) and (C)/(D) are all extreme, one-sided judgments the paragraph’s tone explicitly rules out.","stem":"The critic’s essay on the film was measured throughout, acknowledging both its technical brilliance and its narrative shortcomings without ever tipping fully into praise or condemnation. Its final line captured this balance precisely: _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"“a masterpiece that redefines the genre for a new generation.”"},{"k":"B","t":"“a flawed but genuinely worthwhile two hours at the cinema.”"},{"k":"C","t":"“a complete waste of the audience’s time and money.”"},{"k":"D","t":"“the single worst film released this decade.”"}]},{"id":"pc-22","topic":"pc","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Matching Tone and Register","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Matches the described restraint — probabilistic, non-catastrophizing, and still emphasizing agency (“with immediate global action”). (A) and (D) are catastrophizing extremes the paragraph explicitly says she avoids. (C) contradicts her position as a climate scientist entirely.","stem":"The scientist’s popular book on climate modeling is notable for its restraint — she resists the temptation to catastrophize, presenting probabilities and ranges rather than certainties. Even in her concluding chapter, she writes only that _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"civilization as we know it will certainly collapse within decades."},{"k":"B","t":"the coming changes are likely to be manageable with immediate global action."},{"k":"C","t":"climate change is a hoax perpetuated by uninformed activists."},{"k":"D","t":"nothing meaningful can be done to change the outcome now."}]},{"id":"pc-23","topic":"pc","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Matching Tone and Register","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Matches the modest, understated tone described. (A) is grandiose, contradicting “understated.” (C) and (D) are dramatically negative, also contradicting the calm, reflective tone.","stem":"The retiring judge’s farewell speech was notably understated for someone with four decades on the bench — no dramatic pronouncements, just a quiet reflection on the weight of ordinary decisions. She closed simply by saying that _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"her career had been the greatest achievement in the history of the judiciary."},{"k":"B","t":"she hoped she had, in most cases, gotten it right."},{"k":"C","t":"the legal system was fundamentally broken beyond repair."},{"k":"D","t":"she regretted every decision she had made on the bench."}]},{"id":"pc-24","topic":"pc","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Matching Tone and Register","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Matches the described plain, unadorned, unromantic style. (A), (C), and (D) all use exactly the exoticizing, mystical language the paragraph says the writer deliberately avoided.","stem":"The travel writer’s account of the remote village was deliberately unromantic, avoiding the exoticizing language common to the genre and instead describing daily life with plain, unadorned observation. Of the village’s famous festival, she wrote only that _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"it was the most magical, otherworldly spectacle imaginable."},{"k":"B","t":"it was well-attended and lasted most of the afternoon."},{"k":"C","t":"no outsider could ever truly understand its ancient mysteries."},{"k":"D","t":"it transported visitors to another realm entirely."}]},{"id":"pc-25","topic":"pc","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Matching Tone and Register","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Matches the described directness and rejection of corporate euphemism. (A) and (C) are exactly the euphemistic corporate language the paragraph says she avoided. (D) contradicts the fact that layoffs were happening at all.","stem":"The startup founder’s internal memo announcing layoffs was unusually direct, avoiding corporate euphemism in favor of plain acknowledgment of the human cost. Rather than describing it as “rightsizing” or “restructuring,” she wrote that the company _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"was undergoing an exciting strategic realignment for the future."},{"k":"B","t":"was letting people go, and that this decision would hurt real families."},{"k":"C","t":"was implementing synergistic efficiencies across all departments."},{"k":"D","t":"remained fully committed to its existing headcount targets."}]},{"id":"pc-26","topic":"pc","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Matching Tone and Register","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Matches the “admiring but not uncritical” balance described. (A) is uncritical celebration, (D) is pure condemnation — both contradict the stated balance. (C) contradicts “undeniable political skill,” which implies real significance.","stem":"The historian’s tone throughout the biography is admiring but not uncritical — she credits her subject’s undeniable political skill while refusing to excuse his documented cruelties. Her final assessment describes him as _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"an unblemished hero whose legacy deserves nothing but celebration."},{"k":"B","t":"a man whose considerable talents were inseparable from his considerable flaws."},{"k":"C","t":"an unremarkable figure whose importance has been vastly overstated."},{"k":"D","t":"a villain with no redeeming qualities whatsoever."}]},{"id":"pc-27","topic":"pc","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Matching Tone and Register","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Matches “dry, understated humor” exactly — a mildly deflating comparison rather than outright mockery. (A) is outright mockery/exaggeration, contradicting “understated.” (C) and (D) contradict “clearly dislikes.”","stem":"The tech reviewer’s writing style is famous for dry, understated humor rather than outright mockery, even when reviewing a product she clearly dislikes. Summing up the disappointing new phone, she wrote that it was _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"an unmitigated, catastrophic disaster unfit for human use."},{"k":"B","t":"fine, in the way a slightly stale sandwich is fine."},{"k":"C","t":"the greatest technological achievement of the decade."},{"k":"D","t":"something she would recommend to absolutely everyone."}]},{"id":"pc-28","topic":"pc","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Matching Tone and Register","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Matches the described shift to “stark, minimal language.” (A) and (D) contradict this shift by describing increased complexity. (C) introduces an unrelated, unsupported genre change.","stem":"The poet’s later work abandoned the ornate, elaborate style of his early career in favor of stark, minimal language. Reviewers noted that even his poems about grief, once dense with metaphor, had become _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"even more elaborately metaphorical than before."},{"k":"B","t":"spare, direct, and almost unbearably plain."},{"k":"C","t":"entirely abandoned in favor of prose fiction."},{"k":"D","t":"longer and more structurally complex than his early work."}]},{"id":"pc-29","topic":"pc","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Matching Tone and Register","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Matches the described hedging and acknowledgment of uncertainty. (A), (C), and (D) all state unwarranted certainty, contradicting the entire premise of the paragraph.","stem":"The economist’s testimony before the committee was carefully hedged throughout, qualifying nearly every claim with acknowledgment of uncertainty. Asked directly whether a recession was coming, she would say only that _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"a recession was absolutely certain within the next two quarters."},{"k":"B","t":"the risk had risen, though the timing remained genuinely uncertain."},{"k":"C","t":"no recession would ever occur again in the modern economy."},{"k":"D","t":"she personally guaranteed the economy would avoid any downturn."}]},{"id":"pc-30","topic":"pc","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Matching Tone and Register","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Matches the described “flat, clinical detail” avoiding melodrama. (A) is melodramatic, contradicting the stated style. (C) imposes the “redemption-narrative” framing the paragraph says the book avoids. (D) contradicts the fact that she is, per the paragraph, describing it.","stem":"Unlike most memoirs about addiction, this one avoids both melodrama and redemption-narrative clichés, presenting the author’s relapses with the same flat, clinical detail as her recoveries. She describes her lowest point not with anguish, but simply as _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"the most devastating, soul-crushing tragedy imaginable."},{"k":"B","t":"a period that, factually, lasted about four months."},{"k":"C","t":"a triumphant turning point on her path to healing."},{"k":"D","t":"something she refuses to discuss even now."}]},{"id":"pc-31","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Avoiding Contradiction","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Consistent with the explicit warning against inferring causation from correlation. (A) and (C) directly contradict that warning. (D) makes no sense given the report is precisely about these findings.","stem":"The report’s authors were careful to note that correlation between the two economic indicators, while statistically strong, does not by itself establish causation. Any policy recommendation based solely on this data, they warned, would be _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"fully justified by the strength of the statistical relationship."},{"k":"B","t":"premature without further investigation into the underlying mechanism."},{"k":"C","t":"guaranteed to succeed regardless of the causal relationship."},{"k":"D","t":"unrelated to the report’s own findings."}]},{"id":"pc-32","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Avoiding Contradiction","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"The word “however” signals a contradiction to the official statement — engineers already knew about it as a safety risk well before the “precautionary” recall. (A) doesn’t contradict the official line at all. (C) undercuts rather than supports the implied contradiction (a safety story needs a safety flag, not a cosmetic one). (D) is irrelevant to the specific defect in question.","stem":"The company’s official statement insisted that the product recall was purely precautionary, with no confirmed reports of the defect causing harm. Internal emails later revealed, however, that engineers had flagged the exact same defect _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"only after the recall had already been announced."},{"k":"B","t":"as a known safety risk more than a year earlier."},{"k":"C","t":"as a minor cosmetic issue unrelated to safety."},{"k":"D","t":"in a completely different, unrelated product line."}]},{"id":"pc-33","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Avoiding Contradiction","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"“Ignoring this caveat” signals the media did exactly what the authors warned against — overgeneralizing. (A) and (D) would mean the media respected the caveat, contradicting “ignoring.” (C) is an overcorrection not indicated by the sentence.","stem":"The study’s authors explicitly stated that their sample, drawn entirely from one urban university, could not be assumed to represent the broader population. Subsequent media coverage, ignoring this caveat, described the findings as _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"applicable only to the specific group studied, as intended."},{"k":"B","t":"universally true of all young adults everywhere."},{"k":"C","t":"too limited to draw any conclusions from at all."},{"k":"D","t":"consistent with the authors’ own stated caveats."}]},{"id":"pc-34","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Avoiding Contradiction","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Consistent with the setup — if physical evidence was inconclusive, a verdict would logically lean more on testimony. (A) contradicts evidence being considered at all in reaching any verdict. (C) contradicts the premise that physical evidence alone was insufficient. (D) directly contradicts “eleven hours of deliberation” already stated.","stem":"The defense argued throughout the trial that the physical evidence was inconclusive and could not, on its own, establish guilt. The jury’s verdict, delivered after eleven hours of deliberation, ultimately _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"fully agreed that no evidence had been considered."},{"k":"B","t":"rested heavily on testimony rather than the physical evidence alone."},{"k":"C","t":"ignored testimony entirely in favor of only physical evidence."},{"k":"D","t":"was reached in under thirty minutes of discussion."}]},{"id":"pc-35","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Avoiding Contradiction","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"“However” signals a contradiction to the sweeping claim — a partial, conditional performance directly undercuts “completely waterproof under any conditions” without being an absolute reversal (D would be too extreme given “waterproof” fabrics are rarely entirely non-resistant). (A) and (C) don’t contradict the claim at all.","stem":"The manufacturer claimed its new fabric was completely waterproof under any conditions. Independent lab tests, however, found that the material _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"performed exactly as the manufacturer had claimed in every scenario."},{"k":"B","t":"resisted light rain but failed under sustained heavy exposure."},{"k":"C","t":"was even more waterproof than advertised."},{"k":"D","t":"had no water-resistant properties detected in testing."}]},{"id":"pc-36","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Avoiding Contradiction","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Fits the setup of an audit revealing a broken promise — public funds involved despite the “no burden on taxpayers” claim. (A) confirms rather than contradicts, missing the point of an audit “finding” something. (C) and (D) are positive, unrelated claims not indicated by the contradiction being set up.","stem":"The city council had promised that the new stadium would be funded entirely through private investment, with no burden on taxpayers. An independent financial audit released this week found that _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"private investment had, in fact, covered the entire project cost."},{"k":"B","t":"public funds had quietly covered nearly a third of construction costs."},{"k":"C","t":"the stadium project had been completed under budget."},{"k":"D","t":"taxpayers had received a partial refund from the project’s profits."}]},{"id":"pc-37","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Avoiding Contradiction","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"The structure (“marketing emphasized X… data showed”) signals a contradiction is coming. (A) confirms rather than contradicts the marketing claim. (C) sidesteps the comparison the sentence sets up. (D) is a positive, unrelated claim not indicated by the contrast being built.","stem":"The airline’s marketing emphasized an “on-time guarantee” as a key selling point over its competitors. Government transportation data for the same period showed that the airline’s on-time performance was _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"the best in the industry, exactly as advertised."},{"k":"B","t":"actually below the industry average."},{"k":"C","t":"impossible to measure using available data."},{"k":"D","t":"improving steadily every single month."}]},{"id":"pc-38","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Avoiding Contradiction","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"“Later revealed” signals a contradiction to the company’s stated justification — spending more on marketing than R&D undercuts the R&D-cost justification specifically. (A) supports rather than contradicts the company’s claim. (C) is an extreme overreach not indicated. (D) is unrelated to the R&D justification being questioned.","stem":"The pharmaceutical company insisted its pricing reflected the enormous cost of research and development. Financial disclosures later revealed that _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"R&D spending was, in fact, the company’s single largest expense."},{"k":"B","t":"marketing expenditures exceeded R&D spending by a significant margin."},{"k":"C","t":"the company had never invested in research of any kind."},{"k":"D","t":"pricing had remained flat over the disclosed period."}]},{"id":"pc-39","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Avoiding Contradiction","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Fits the “watchdog review found” structure signaling a contradiction — reclassifying spending undercuts the headline figure. (A) confirms rather than contradicts. (C) and (D) are unrelated positive claims not indicated by a critical watchdog review.","stem":"The nonprofit’s annual report proudly stated that over ninety percent of donations went directly to program services, with minimal administrative overhead. An independent watchdog’s review of the same financials found that _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"the ninety percent figure was accurately reported."},{"k":"B","t":"a significant portion of “program services” spending was actually administrative in nature."},{"k":"C","t":"the nonprofit had exceeded its own stated targets."},{"k":"D","t":"donations had increased significantly that year."}]},{"id":"pc-40","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Avoiding Contradiction","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Fits the “investigation revealed” structure signaling a debunking of the menu’s claim. (A) confirms rather than contradicts, undermining the point of an investigative reveal. (C) and (D) are unrelated claims not indicated by the setup.","stem":"The restaurant’s menu described the fish as “locally caught that morning.” A journalist’s investigation into the supply chain revealed that _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"the fish was, in fact, caught locally as described."},{"k":"B","t":"the fish was frozen and shipped from several states away."},{"k":"C","t":"the restaurant had stopped serving fish entirely."},{"k":"D","t":"local fishing had increased significantly in recent years."}]},{"id":"pc-41","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Cause-and-Effect Completion","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"A","explanation":"“On balance” signals a weighing of the described trade-off (savings vs. maintenance risk), and a net-positive conclusion fits a company that presumably kept the automation. (B) and (D) are extreme overreactions the paragraph gives no basis for. (C) contradicts the “forty percent” reduction already stated.","stem":"The factory’s new automated packing line reduced labor costs by nearly forty percent within its first year. But the same automation required specialized technicians to maintain equipment that occasional breakdowns could halt production for days. On balance, management concluded that _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"the savings clearly outweighed the new maintenance risks."},{"k":"B","t":"automation should be reversed immediately at all costs."},{"k":"C","t":"labor costs were actually unaffected by the change."},{"k":"D","t":"the factory should close permanently."}]},{"id":"pc-42","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Cause-and-Effect Completion","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Follows logically from “saturated soil… weakened” plus a final storm — a landslide is the direct, foreseeable consequence. (A) and (D) contradict the described rainy, wet conditions. (C) is unrelated to the disaster being set up.","stem":"Heavy rains upstream saturated soil that had already been weakened by months of unrelated construction work. When a final storm hit, the combination proved catastrophic: _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"the drought conditions worsened significantly."},{"k":"B","t":"a major landslide blocked the only road into the valley."},{"k":"C","t":"construction work was completed ahead of schedule."},{"k":"D","t":"the region experienced an unexpected heat wave."}]},{"id":"pc-43","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Cause-and-Effect Completion","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"A","explanation":"Directly follows the stated intent of the policy succeeding as designed — the straightforward, expected outcome given no contradicting signal (“however,” “but”) appears in the sentence. (B) would require a “however” to be a coherent point about unintended consequences, which isn’t set up. (C) and (D) contradict a policy working as intended with no signal of failure.","stem":"The new tax incentive was designed to encourage small businesses to hire additional staff. Within eighteen months of its introduction, economic data showed that _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"small business hiring had increased measurably in eligible sectors."},{"k":"B","t":"large corporations had captured the majority of the incentive’s benefits."},{"k":"C","t":"small business hiring had declined sharply during the same period."},{"k":"D","t":"the incentive had no measurable relationship to business decisions."}]},{"id":"pc-44","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Cause-and-Effect Completion","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Direct, sensible consequence of severe water scarcity — a forced shift to efficiency. (A) contradicts the scarcity described. (C) contradicts “forced to” respond. (D) is an illogical, unsupported extreme.","stem":"The river’s water level dropped to its lowest point in recorded history after three consecutive years of below-average rainfall. Farmers who had long relied on flood irrigation were forced to _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"increase their reliance on flood irrigation methods."},{"k":"B","t":"adopt more water-efficient irrigation techniques."},{"k":"C","t":"ignore the change in water levels entirely."},{"k":"D","t":"relocate to a region with even less rainfall."}]},{"id":"pc-45","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Cause-and-Effect Completion","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Direct, logical consequence for companies dependent on precisely-timed, low-buffer inventory when shipping becomes unreliable and expensive. (A), (C), and (D) all contradict the described disruption’s direct relevance to such companies.","stem":"A sudden drop in global shipping capacity, caused by a shortage of available containers, sent freight costs soaring across every major trade route. Companies dependent on just-in-time inventory models found that _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"their supply chains were completely unaffected by the shortage."},{"k":"B","t":"delays and cost increases forced a rethink of lean inventory strategies."},{"k":"C","t":"freight costs actually fell as a result of the shortage."},{"k":"D","t":"the shortage had no bearing on inventory planning."}]},{"id":"pc-46","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Cause-and-Effect Completion","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Direct, sensible next step given the described breakthrough and immediate industry interest. (A) contradicts the enzyme’s described speed advantage. (C) and (D) are unsupported pivots the paragraph gives no basis for.","stem":"A newly discovered enzyme was found to break down a common plastic polymer at room temperature, far faster than any previously known method. Waste management companies immediately began exploring whether the enzyme could be _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"used to slow down existing recycling processes."},{"k":"B","t":"scaled up for industrial-level plastic recycling."},{"k":"C","t":"applied to materials entirely unrelated to plastic."},{"k":"D","t":"banned due to safety concerns about its use."}]},{"id":"pc-47","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Cause-and-Effect Completion","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"“However” plus “speculative asset purchases” points directly to asset-price inflation as the unintended consequence. (A) contradicts “however,” which signals the outcome diverged from intention. (C) contradicts the described flow of new lending having an effect. (D) contradicts a rate cut, which typically increases, not reduces, money supply.","stem":"The central bank’s decision to cut interest rates was intended to stimulate borrowing and investment during a period of sluggish growth. Within months, however, most of the new lending had flowed not into productive investment but into speculative asset purchases, _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"exactly fulfilling the policy’s original intention."},{"k":"B","t":"inflating prices in property and financial markets instead."},{"k":"C","t":"having no measurable effect on any market."},{"k":"D","t":"reducing the money supply significantly."}]},{"id":"pc-48","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Cause-and-Effect Completion","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Direct, logical consequence of aging infrastructure facing unprecedented sustained demand. (A), (C), and (D) all contradict the described strain and the phrase “never designed for such sustained peak load.”","stem":"A prolonged heatwave pushed electricity demand for air conditioning to record levels across the grid. Several aging power plants, never designed for such sustained peak load, _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"operated at their lowest capacity in years."},{"k":"B","t":"experienced unexpected outages during the peak demand period."},{"k":"C","t":"were entirely unaffected by the increased demand."},{"k":"D","t":"reduced their output deliberately to save costs."}]},{"id":"pc-49","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Cause-and-Effect Completion","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Directly follows from unbundling’s known consumer friction — the gap between advertised base fare and actual final cost. (A) contradicts the stated purpose of a lower advertised fare. (C) and (D) are unrelated to the complaint pattern the paragraph sets up.","stem":"The airline’s decision to unbundle ticket prices — charging separately for baggage, seat selection, and meals — was designed to advertise a lower base fare than competitors. Consumer complaints in the following year focused overwhelmingly on _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"the airline’s base fares being too expensive."},{"k":"B","t":"the final cost, after add-ons, being higher than expected."},{"k":"C","t":"the airline’s meals being of unusually high quality."},{"k":"D","t":"baggage fees being eliminated entirely."}]},{"id":"pc-50","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Cause-and-Effect Completion","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Direct, foreseeable consequence of brittle, poorly-maintained pipes meeting an unusually cold winter. (A), (C), and (D) all contradict the setup emphasizing vulnerability and lack of preparation.","stem":"Years of reduced funding for basic infrastructure maintenance left the city’s water pipes far more brittle than official records indicated. When an unusually cold winter arrived, dozens of pipes across the city _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"performed better than expected despite their age."},{"k":"B","t":"burst within the same forty-eight-hour period."},{"k":"C","t":"were replaced proactively before any damage occurred."},{"k":"D","t":"remained undamaged due to new insulation standards."}]},{"id":"pc-51","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Specificity Over Vagueness","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"The sentence explicitly promises “a single policy change” as the specific cause — this option names one concretely. (A), (C), and (D) are all vague, generic statements that could apply to almost any environmental program, failing to deliver the promised specific detail.","stem":"The city’s new composting initiative diverted an unusually large volume of waste from landfills in its first year — not through public participation alone, but because a single policy change made the real difference: _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"residents were generally supportive of environmental initiatives."},{"k":"B","t":"commercial restaurants, not households, were made subject to mandatory composting."},{"k":"C","t":"the program was well-designed and effective overall."},{"k":"D","t":"composting is an important part of waste management."}]},{"id":"pc-52","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Specificity Over Vagueness","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"The sentence promises “one overlooked factor” specifically — this option delivers a concrete, traceable cause. (A), (C), and (D) are vague and non-specific, failing to name the promised factor.","stem":"The turnaround in the struggling company’s fortunes wasn’t due to a single dramatic decision, but analysts eventually traced most of the recovery to one overlooked factor: _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"the company had simply gotten lucky with market conditions."},{"k":"B","t":"a renegotiated supplier contract that cut input costs by a fifth."},{"k":"C","t":"the leadership team worked very hard during this period."},{"k":"D","t":"the overall business strategy was fundamentally sound."}]},{"id":"pc-53","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Specificity Over Vagueness","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"“Sediment core samples revealed” promises a specific, evidence-based finding — a drought is a concrete, testable cause matching what sediment cores can actually reveal (climate data). (A), (C), and (D) are all vague and don’t connect to what sediment analysis would specifically show.","stem":"Historians long debated why the ancient city was suddenly abandoned, until sediment core samples from a nearby lakebed revealed the specific cause: _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"the city’s population had simply grown too large to sustain."},{"k":"B","t":"a decade-long drought had made agriculture there impossible."},{"k":"C","t":"the region experienced significant historical change."},{"k":"D","t":"ancient cities were often abandoned for various reasons."}]},{"id":"pc-54","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Specificity Over Vagueness","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"The sentence promises “one specific marketing decision” — this option names a concrete, identifiable action. (A), (C), and (D) are all generic statements that fail to specify any decision at all.","stem":"The film’s unexpected box-office success surprised even its own studio, which later attributed it to one specific marketing decision made almost as an afterthought: _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"the film had a talented cast and crew."},{"k":"B","t":"releasing a single, low-budget trailer on social media two weeks before launch."},{"k":"C","t":"the story resonated with audiences in general."},{"k":"D","t":"the studio had a strong marketing department."}]},{"id":"pc-55","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Specificity Over Vagueness","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Delivers the promised “specific change” concretely. (A), (C), and (D) remain vague, generic explanations that don’t name any specific tactical change.","stem":"The team’s late-season improvement puzzled commentators until the coach revealed the specific change behind it: _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"the players had worked hard to improve as a team."},{"k":"B","t":"a shift from a three-defender to a four-defender formation."},{"k":"C","t":"the team had a new sense of belief and confidence."},{"k":"D","t":"the coaching staff made several small adjustments."}]},{"id":"pc-56","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Specificity Over Vagueness","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"“One decision” is delivered specifically and ties to the writing quality reviewers would credit for narrative improvement — the most plausible specific cause for improvement “in nearly every way” being tied to story. (A), (C), and (D) are specific but relate to scale/reach rather than the substantive creative improvement being described.","stem":"Reviewers agreed the sequel improved on the original in nearly every way, crediting the change most directly to one decision the studio made early in production: _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"hiring a much larger visual effects team."},{"k":"B","t":"bringing back the original screenwriter, who had been replaced for the first film."},{"k":"C","t":"spending significantly more on the overall production budget."},{"k":"D","t":"choosing to release the film in more theaters worldwide."}]},{"id":"pc-57","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Specificity Over Vagueness","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"“Specific, almost mundane cause” is delivered concretely and fits the “mundane” description well. (A), (C), and (D) are vague generalities, not a specific, identifiable cause.","stem":"The sudden drop in the neighborhood’s crime rate baffled residents until police data pointed to a specific, almost mundane cause: _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"the community had generally become safer over time."},{"k":"B","t":"the installation of brighter streetlighting along three main corridors."},{"k":"C","t":"crime rates fluctuate naturally from year to year."},{"k":"D","t":"residents had become more cautious in general."}]},{"id":"pc-58","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Specificity Over Vagueness","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"“A change nobody would have predicted” promises something specific and counter-intuitively small — this option delivers exactly that. (A), (C), and (D) are all plausible but unsurprising, generic operational improvements, contradicting “nobody would have predicted.”","stem":"The airline’s on-time performance improved dramatically after a change nobody outside the industry would have predicted: _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"hiring more staff across all departments."},{"k":"B","t":"rescheduling a single hub airport’s peak departure window by twenty minutes."},{"k":"C","t":"investing heavily in newer aircraft."},{"k":"D","t":"improving communication with passengers overall."}]},{"id":"pc-59","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Specificity Over Vagueness","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Delivers the promised “one internal insight” specifically and concretely, matching a very common real-world pivot story. (A), (C), and (D) are all generic observations, not a specific insight “almost dismissed.”","stem":"The startup’s pivot from a failing consumer app to a business-to-business tool succeeded largely because of one internal insight the founders almost dismissed: _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"that businesses generally have more money to spend than consumers."},{"k":"B","t":"that a minor internal tool they’d built for themselves solved a widespread problem."},{"k":"C","t":"that the market for consumer apps was very competitive."},{"k":"D","t":"that pivoting is sometimes necessary for startups to survive."}]},{"id":"pc-60","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Specificity Over Vagueness","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Names a specific translation decision, directly contrasting with “fidelity to the original text” as the sentence sets up. (A) and (D) are generic process/publishing facts, not a translation choice. (C) directly contradicts “less to fidelity.”","stem":"The novel’s translator later explained that the book’s success in English owed less to fidelity to the original text than to one specific choice: _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"working closely with a skilled editor throughout the process."},{"k":"B","t":"deliberately rewriting the protagonist’s internal monologue in a more colloquial register."},{"k":"C","t":"staying as faithful as possible to the source language."},{"k":"D","t":"publishing the book with a major international publisher."}]},{"id":"pc-61","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Qualified vs. Absolute Claims","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Matches the explicitly qualified finding (most improved, a subset did not). (A) and (D) overstate into universal claims the paragraph explicitly contradicts. (C) understates, ignoring the “meaningful reduction” for most participants.","stem":"The clinical trial showed a meaningful reduction in symptoms for most participants, though researchers were careful to note that a small subset saw no improvement at all. The overall conclusion, they emphasized, was that the treatment _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"works for absolutely everyone who takes it."},{"k":"B","t":"appears effective for many, though not all, patients."},{"k":"C","t":"has been proven to have no effect whatsoever."},{"k":"D","t":"should be immediately mandated for all patients with the condition."}]},{"id":"pc-62","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Qualified vs. Absolute Claims","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Matches “not universal” and “varying” — a nuanced, non-unanimous split, consistent with the paragraph’s qualified framing. (A) and (D) both assert unanimity, contradicting “not universal” and “varying.” (C) is unrelated and implausible for survey respondents.","stem":"The survey found broad, though not universal, support for the proposed reform, with opinions varying significantly by age group. Younger respondents were considerably more favorable, while older respondents remained _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"unanimously and enthusiastically supportive."},{"k":"B","t":"more divided, with a slight majority opposed."},{"k":"C","t":"completely unaware the survey existed."},{"k":"D","t":"unanimously opposed without exception."}]},{"id":"pc-63","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Qualified vs. Absolute Claims","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Matches “modest” effect and the explicit caution against overstatement. (A) and (D) overstate into revolutionary/definitive claims the paragraph explicitly warns against. (C) understates, ignoring the real, if modest, benefit found.","stem":"The report concluded that while the new drug reduced average recovery time, the effect size was modest and the researchers cautioned against overstating its clinical significance. Doctors, they suggested, should view it as _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"a revolutionary breakthrough that changes standard treatment entirely."},{"k":"B","t":"one modestly useful option among several, not a definitive solution."},{"k":"C","t":"something with no practical value in clinical settings."},{"k":"D","t":"a treatment that should replace all existing alternatives immediately."}]},{"id":"pc-64","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Qualified vs. Absolute Claims","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"A","explanation":"Matches “promising but far from guaranteed” precisely. (B) and (C) both assert unwarranted certainty in opposite directions. (D) contradicts analysts actively assessing risks, which implies real interest in the outcome.","stem":"Analysts described the merger’s prospects as promising but far from guaranteed, citing regulatory hurdles and cultural differences between the two companies as real, if not necessarily fatal, risks. Their forecast, in short, was _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"cautious optimism rather than confident certainty."},{"k":"B","t":"complete confidence that the merger would fail."},{"k":"C","t":"absolute certainty that the merger would succeed without issue."},{"k":"D","t":"total indifference to whether the merger happened at all."}]},{"id":"pc-65","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Qualified vs. Absolute Claims","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Matches “confidence in the near-term trend” specifically, while respecting the stated long-term uncertainty by not extending that confidence too far. (A) contradicts “confidence in the near-term.” (C) overextends confidence into the long term, which the paragraph explicitly separates out as uncertain. (D) contradicts “confidence” with an unsupported reversal claim.","stem":"The panel’s report acknowledged genuine uncertainty in long-term projections while still expressing confidence in the near-term trend, which it described as _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"completely unpredictable in every respect."},{"k":"B","t":"reasonably clear for the next two to three years."},{"k":"C","t":"certain to continue unchanged for the next fifty years."},{"k":"D","t":"already reversing based on preliminary, unconfirmed data."}]},{"id":"pc-66","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Qualified vs. Absolute Claims","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Matches the explicit “nuanced” framing — sound overall, with specific weak spots. (A) ignores the acknowledged weaknesses. (C) and (D) overstate into sweeping negative claims the paragraph explicitly contradicts by calling the overall controls “sound.”","stem":"The auditor’s findings were nuanced: significant weaknesses existed in three specific departments, but the organization’s overall financial controls were sound. The report’s summary line reflected this balance, describing the organization as _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"entirely free of any financial control issues."},{"k":"B","t":"fundamentally sound but with targeted areas needing improvement."},{"k":"C","t":"riddled with financial mismanagement throughout every department."},{"k":"D","t":"beyond any hope of meaningful reform."}]},{"id":"pc-67","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Qualified vs. Absolute Claims","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Matches the qualified stance — slowing decline, not guaranteeing reversal, given limited resources. (A) overstates into guaranteed revival, contradicting her explicit caution. (C) and (D) understate, contradicting her belief that decline “could be slowed.”","stem":"The linguist’s research suggested the endangered language’s decline could be slowed, though she stopped short of claiming it could be fully reversed given how few fluent speakers remained. Her recommendations focused on _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"guaranteeing the language’s complete revival within a decade."},{"k":"B","t":"realistic, incremental steps to preserve what remained."},{"k":"C","t":"abandoning any preservation effort as futile."},{"k":"D","t":"declaring the language already extinct."}]},{"id":"pc-68","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Qualified vs. Absolute Claims","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Matches the balanced assessment — safe now, but proactive action needed before the decade-long window closes. (A) and (D) are alarmist responses the paragraph explicitly rules out. (C) is dismissive, also explicitly ruled out.","stem":"The engineer’s assessment of the aging bridge was neither alarmist nor dismissive: it remained safe for current traffic loads but would require significant reinforcement within the next decade to remain so. Local officials were advised to _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"close the bridge to all traffic immediately."},{"k":"B","t":"begin budgeting now for reinforcement work over the coming years."},{"k":"C","t":"ignore the assessment entirely, since it was not urgent."},{"k":"D","t":"declare the bridge permanently unsafe for any use."}]},{"id":"pc-69","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Qualified vs. Absolute Claims","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Matches “neither full approval nor full rejection,” pointing to the specific, differentiated middle path the sentence sets up. (A) and (C) are exactly the two extremes explicitly ruled out. (D) avoids any decision, which doesn’t match a “recommending” committee.","stem":"The committee’s review found the proposed budget cuts were justifiable in some departments but excessive in others, recommending neither full approval nor full rejection, but rather _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"an unconditional approval of the entire budget as originally proposed."},{"k":"B","t":"a department-by-department revision based on the review’s specific findings."},{"k":"C","t":"a complete rejection of any cuts whatsoever."},{"k":"D","t":"an indefinite postponement of the decision entirely."}]},{"id":"pc-70","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Qualified vs. Absolute Claims","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Matches “likely, but not conclusively” and “fell short of definitive proof” precisely. (A) and (D) overstate into certainty the paragraph explicitly denies. (C) understates, ignoring the “likely” and “stylistic similarities” already noted.","stem":"The panel’s linguistic analysis found the disputed text likely, but not conclusively, written by the same author as the earlier known works, based on stylistic similarities that fell short of definitive proof. Their published finding was carefully worded to state that the attribution was _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"proven beyond any reasonable doubt."},{"k":"B","t":"plausible, though not fully certain."},{"k":"C","t":"entirely without merit or evidence."},{"k":"D","t":"confirmed by definitive physical evidence."}]},{"id":"pc-71","topic":"pc","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Argument-Building Paragraphs","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Directly follows from the “selection bias” critique — the specific, logical limitation this creates for generalizability. (A) overstates into a conclusion of failure the paragraph doesn’t support. (C) is an unsupported absolute claim. (D) is an extreme, unwarranted policy recommendation.","stem":"Proponents of the four-day workweek point to several pilot programs showing no drop in productivity despite the reduced hours. Critics counter that these pilots were mostly conducted at companies that volunteered for the trial — firms already inclined toward flexible, well-managed work practices. This selection bias suggests that _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"the four-day workweek definitely fails in most companies."},{"k":"B","t":"the results may not generalize to less flexible or less well-managed organizations."},{"k":"C","t":"productivity is entirely unrelated to the number of hours worked."},{"k":"D","t":"all future pilot programs should be immediately abandoned."}]},{"id":"pc-72","topic":"pc","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Argument-Building Paragraphs","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Precisely captures the described disagreement — both sides agree scores can be measured; they disagree about what those numbers mean. (A) misstates the dispute as being about measurement itself. (C) and (D) introduce unrelated issues not raised in the paragraph.","stem":"Defenders of standardized testing argue it provides an objective, comparable measure of student achievement across vastly different schools. Critics respond that “objective” scores can still reflect unequal access to test preparation resources rather than underlying ability. The disagreement, at its core, is really about _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"whether test scores can be measured accurately at all."},{"k":"B","t":"what the scores actually represent, not whether they can be measured."},{"k":"C","t":"whether schools should exist in their current form."},{"k":"D","t":"which specific test format is easiest to administer."}]},{"id":"pc-73","topic":"pc","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Argument-Building Paragraphs","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"A","explanation":"Directly follows from the historical pattern of overpromising cited by opponents — reasonable skepticism, not certainty either way. (B) contradicts the cited historical underperformance. (C) contradicts the paragraph’s entire focus on cost savings as central to the debate. (D) overstates into certainty the historical pattern alone doesn’t guarantee.","stem":"Supporters of the merger emphasize the cost savings from combined operations, projecting significant efficiency gains within two years. Opponents point out that similar mergers in the same industry have historically underperformed their own efficiency projections by a wide margin. This history suggests that _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"the projected savings should be treated with some skepticism."},{"k":"B","t":"mergers always succeed regardless of industry track record."},{"k":"C","t":"cost savings are irrelevant to merger decisions."},{"k":"D","t":"this particular merger is certain to fail."}]},{"id":"pc-74","topic":"pc","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Argument-Building Paragraphs","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Follows logically — since the curriculum targets skills (problem-solving) that standardized tests may not capture, fair evaluation needs broader measures. (A) and (C) rely solely on the measure the paragraph suggests may be incomplete for this purpose. (D) dismisses a legitimate point raised, which a “fair” evaluation wouldn’t do.","stem":"Advocates for the new curriculum argue that project-based learning better prepares students for real-world problem-solving than traditional lecture formats. Skeptics note that standardized test scores, the most common measure of academic achievement, have shown no significant change under the new approach. Evaluating the curriculum fairly, then, requires _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"relying exclusively on standardized test scores as the only valid measure."},{"k":"B","t":"considering outcomes beyond test scores that the new approach specifically targets."},{"k":"C","t":"abandoning the new curriculum immediately based on test results alone."},{"k":"D","t":"ignoring the skeptics’ concerns entirely."}]},{"id":"pc-75","topic":"pc","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Argument-Building Paragraphs","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Directly addresses the critics’ specific objection (temporary, small-scale) by proposing exactly the kind of evidence that would resolve it. (A) and (D) dismiss the debate prematurely, contradicting the paragraph’s framing of genuine, unresolved disagreement. (C) is an extreme overreaction not warranted by a valid methodological limitation.","stem":"Supporters of universal basic income cite pilot studies showing recipients spent the money primarily on necessities and job training, not on reduced work effort as critics feared. Critics respond that pilot programs, being temporary and small-scale, may not predict behavior under a permanent, nationwide policy. Settling the debate conclusively would likely require _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"no further data of any kind, since the question is already settled."},{"k":"B","t":"evidence from a longer-term, larger-scale implementation."},{"k":"C","t":"abandoning pilot studies as a research method entirely."},{"k":"D","t":"accepting the critics’ view without any further evidence."}]},{"id":"pc-76","topic":"pc","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Argument-Building Paragraphs","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Matches “balanced assessment” — weighing genuine, context-dependent trade-offs already outlined on both sides. (A) and (D) ignore half the trade-offs just described. (C) is a false binary the paragraph’s nuanced framing explicitly rejects.","stem":"Proponents of nuclear energy point to its low carbon emissions and high energy density compared to renewables requiring far more land per unit of power generated. Opponents raise concerns about long-term waste storage and the high upfront capital costs of new plants. A balanced assessment would need to weigh _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"only the emissions data, since that is the most important factor."},{"k":"B","t":"the specific trade-offs relevant to each region’s energy needs and constraints."},{"k":"C","t":"nothing, since nuclear energy is either entirely good or entirely bad."},{"k":"D","t":"exclusively the opinions of nuclear energy’s most vocal supporters."}]},{"id":"pc-77","topic":"pc","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Argument-Building Paragraphs","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Directly addresses the specific equity concern raised, proposing a solution that preserves the benefits while mitigating the harm. (A) and (C) ignore or overreact to the critics’ point rather than engaging with it. (D) contradicts the paragraph’s acknowledgment that different cities have different needs.","stem":"Advocates for congestion pricing argue it reduces traffic and funds public transit improvements, pointing to successful implementations in several major cities. Critics counter that it disproportionately burdens lower-income commuters who have fewer alternatives to driving. Effective policy design, many suggest, would need to _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"ignore the concerns of lower-income commuters entirely."},{"k":"B","t":"pair congestion pricing with targeted relief for those most affected."},{"k":"C","t":"abandon congestion pricing as a concept altogether."},{"k":"D","t":"apply the exact same policy in every city regardless of local conditions."}]},{"id":"pc-78","topic":"pc","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Argument-Building Paragraphs","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Directly follows the “not whether… but on” framing — the real, specific question the paragraph is steering toward. (A) is an extreme not indicated by the nuanced debate described. (C) and (D) introduce narrow, unrelated tangents.","stem":"Supporters of remote work point to studies showing improved employee satisfaction and reduced office overhead costs. Skeptics note that spontaneous, informal collaboration — often credited with sparking innovation — is harder to replicate virtually. The debate increasingly centers not on whether remote work is inherently good or bad, but on _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"whether offices should be abolished entirely and immediately."},{"k":"B","t":"how organizations can deliberately recreate the benefits of informal collaboration."},{"k":"C","t":"which specific software tool is best for video calls."},{"k":"D","t":"whether employees should be paid less for working remotely."}]},{"id":"pc-79","topic":"pc","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Argument-Building Paragraphs","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"Directly addresses both sides’ legitimate points — using the promising trial data while addressing the specific gap (short duration) that opponents raise. (A) and (B) are extremes that ignore one side’s legitimate concern entirely. (D) dismisses the trial evidence the paragraph treats as real and “measurable.”","stem":"Proponents of gene-edited crops argue they can increase yields and reduce pesticide use, citing field trials with measurable results. Opponents raise concerns about long-term ecological effects that trials, by their short duration, cannot fully capture. Reasonable policy, in this light, might favor _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"an outright, permanent ban on all gene-edited crops."},{"k":"B","t":"unrestricted approval with no monitoring requirements at all."},{"k":"C","t":"phased approval paired with long-term ecological monitoring."},{"k":"D","t":"ignoring the trial data entirely in favor of speculation."}]},{"id":"pc-80","topic":"pc","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Argument-Building Paragraphs","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"A","explanation":"Draws a reasonable, evidence-based synthesis from real-world precedent, addressing the pacing concern specifically raised by opponents. (B) and (C) are absolute claims contradicting the premises already established in the paragraph. (D) is a dismissive overreach not supported by genuinely balancing both sides.","stem":"Supporters of the new zoning law argue it will increase housing supply and moderate rising rents over time. Opponents worry it will alter neighborhood character faster than existing communities can reasonably adapt. Cities that have implemented similar reforms elsewhere suggest that _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"both concerns can be partially addressed through phased, gradual implementation."},{"k":"B","t":"neighborhood character never actually changes as a result of zoning laws."},{"k":"C","t":"housing supply is completely unrelated to zoning regulations."},{"k":"D","t":"opponents of zoning reform are always factually incorrect."}]},{"id":"pc-81","topic":"pc","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Narrative Closings","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"A","explanation":"Directly fulfills “prove him right” — a concrete instance where human observation succeeded where automation elsewhere failed. (B) and (D) contradict “prove him right.” (C) makes his vindication pointless and contradicts the setup.","stem":"The old lighthouse keeper had refused every offer to automate his post, insisting that a human eye caught things sensors missed. When the storm that would prove him right finally came, _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"the automated system elsewhere failed to detect the same danger in time."},{"k":"B","t":"he decided automation was the better option after all."},{"k":"C","t":"the lighthouse was destroyed regardless of his efforts."},{"k":"D","t":"he retired the following week, unrelated to the storm."}]},{"id":"pc-82","topic":"pc","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Narrative Closings","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Follows the narrative logic directly — the specific obstacle (her father’s illness) has now been removed, making acceptance the natural next beat. (A) and (D) contradict the buildup toward this decision. (C) undercuts the entire setup of “offered a third time.”","stem":"She had turned down the promotion twice before, unwilling to relocate while her father was ill. When he passed and the position was offered a third time, _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"she declined once more, for entirely unrelated reasons."},{"k":"B","t":"she finally accepted, ready for the change she had long postponed."},{"k":"C","t":"the company had already filled the position with someone else."},{"k":"D","t":"she resigned from the company entirely."}]},{"id":"pc-83","topic":"pc","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Narrative Closings","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"The setup (“until”) clearly signals his streak is about to be broken by this specific opponent. (A) contradicts the clear signal that this match is different. (C) and (D) sidestep the decisive outcome the “until” framing sets up.","stem":"The chess prodigy had never lost a match against an opponent his own age, until a quiet, unassuming twelve-year-old sat across the board from him at the regional finals. Three hours later, _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"he had extended his unbeaten streak once again, as expected."},{"k":"B","t":"he shook hands, having just lost his first match to a peer."},{"k":"C","t":"the match was declared a draw by the officials."},{"k":"D","t":"both players withdrew from the tournament."}]},{"id":"pc-84","topic":"pc","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Narrative Closings","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Matches the established pattern of resilience (“survived two economic downturns”) — the bakery’s track record suggests it would weather this threat too. (A) contradicts the resilience theme built up. (C) is an unrelated event not set up by the paragraph. (D) contradicts the bakery’s identity as being built on that specific recipe.","stem":"The small bakery had survived two economic downturns on the strength of a single recipe passed down for three generations. When a large chain opened directly across the street, longtime customers worried the bakery’s days were numbered. A year later, _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"the bakery had closed, unable to compete with the chain’s prices."},{"k":"B","t":"the bakery was thriving, its loyal customer base largely unchanged."},{"k":"C","t":"the large chain had also closed due to unrelated issues."},{"k":"D","t":"the bakery had abandoned its original recipe entirely."}]},{"id":"pc-85","topic":"pc","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Narrative Closings","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Fulfills the narrative setup of an unconventional path leading to unexpected success. (A) undercuts the entire narrative arc being built. (C) and (D) are irrelevant tangents that don’t resolve the story.","stem":"The young violinist had been rejected by the conservatory twice, each time citing “insufficient technical polish.” Rather than reapplying a third time, she spent a year busking in the city’s subway stations instead. A talent scout who happened to pass through _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"dismissed her performance as unremarkable."},{"k":"B","t":"offered her a spot in a professional touring orchestra."},{"k":"C","t":"was actually looking for a completely different kind of musician."},{"k":"D","t":"had never heard of the conservatory that rejected her."}]},{"id":"pc-86","topic":"pc","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Narrative Closings","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Matches the setup that the case was never really “solved” — new technology naturally reveals what old methods missed. (A) contradicts the detective’s stated belief that it wasn’t truly solved. (C) and (D) sidestep the resolution the paragraph is building toward.","stem":"The retired detective had always maintained that one case, closed decades earlier, had never truly been solved — just abandoned. When new forensic technology reopened the file, _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"it confirmed the original, decades-old conclusion exactly."},{"k":"B","t":"it revealed a suspect no one had previously considered."},{"k":"C","t":"the case file had been lost entirely and could not be reopened."},{"k":"D","t":"the detective refused to look at the new evidence."}]},{"id":"pc-87","topic":"pc","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Narrative Closings","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Follows the natural, hopeful arc of the story — a small beginning growing into something larger. (A) and (D) contradict the constructive trajectory the story sets up. (C) is irrelevant to the village’s own outcome, which is what the sentence is tracking.","stem":"The remote village had no access to electricity until a group of engineering students, on a class project, installed a small solar microgrid over a single summer. Five years later, _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"the village had reverted entirely to its previous way of life."},{"k":"B","t":"that small grid had expanded into the region’s main power source."},{"k":"C","t":"the students had forgotten about the project entirely."},{"k":"D","t":"the village had been abandoned by its residents."}]},{"id":"pc-88","topic":"pc","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Narrative Closings","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"A","explanation":"The narrative setup (eleven years of nothing, framed as leading somewhere, plus the ironic “unremarkable Tuesday”) signals a payoff is coming. (B), (C), and (D) all deflate the story with anticlimax, contradicting the clear narrative buildup toward a discovery.","stem":"The amateur astronomer had scanned the same patch of night sky for eleven years without ever finding anything worth reporting. On what he later called an unremarkable Tuesday, _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"he finally spotted a comet that would later bear his name."},{"k":"B","t":"he decided to give up stargazing altogether."},{"k":"C","t":"his telescope broke beyond repair."},{"k":"D","t":"nothing unusual happened, as on every other night."}]},{"id":"pc-89","topic":"pc","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Narrative Closings","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"A classic, well-signaled narrative turn — shared crisis prompting unlikely cooperation between rivals. (A) and (C) contradict the constructive turn the “threatened to shut down both” setup suggests is coming. (D) contradicts the stated threat to both kitchens.","stem":"The two rival restaurants had competed for the same customers for twenty years, each convinced the other would eventually fail. When a citywide gas shortage threatened to shut down both kitchens, _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"the owners refused to speak to each other, as always."},{"k":"B","t":"they pooled resources and shared a single working kitchen for weeks."},{"k":"C","t":"both restaurants closed permanently within the same month."},{"k":"D","t":"neither restaurant was affected by the shortage at all."}]},{"id":"pc-90","topic":"pc","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Narrative Closings","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Fulfills the narrative setup of an unexpected spark reawakening a dormant passion — a common, well-signaled emotional payoff. (A), (C), and (D) all contradict the warm, reawakening trajectory the anecdote is clearly building toward.","stem":"The elderly composer had not written a new piece in over a decade, convinced his best work was behind him. When his granddaughter, learning piano for the first time, played him a simple melody she had made up herself, _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"he dismissed it as childish and unremarkable."},{"k":"B","t":"he found himself humming it for days, and began composing again."},{"k":"C","t":"he decided to give up music entirely."},{"k":"D","t":"he was too busy to listen to her at all."}]},{"id":"pc-91","topic":"pc","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Mixed / Hardest Set","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Directly follows from the stated fragile assumption being violated almost immediately. (A) contradicts the model’s own stated dependency on an assumption that just failed. (C) contradicts “trade tensions escalated.” (D) is an irrelevant, nonsensical pivot.","stem":"The economist’s model predicted the policy would boost employment, but only under an assumption of stable global trade conditions that, in practice, rarely held for more than a year or two at a time. When trade tensions escalated shortly after implementation, _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"the policy’s effects tracked the model’s predictions closely regardless."},{"k":"B","t":"the real-world outcome diverged sharply from the model’s forecast."},{"k":"C","t":"global trade conditions became more stable than ever before."},{"k":"D","t":"the policy was implemented in a completely different country instead."}]},{"id":"pc-92","topic":"pc","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Mixed / Hardest Set","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Directly resolves the specific dilemma described (untranslatable pun) with the most creative, commonly used solution translators actually use. (A) doesn’t solve the described problem at all — it’s not really a translation “solution.” (C) is a wild overreaction to a single passage. (D) explicitly contradicts “her eventual solution,” implying she found a way to preserve rather than lose it.","stem":"The translator faced a specific dilemma: the original text relied on a pun with no equivalent in the target language, and any literal translation would lose the joke entirely. Her eventual solution was to _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"leave the passage completely untranslated in the final text."},{"k":"B","t":"invent a different pun in the target language that preserved the comic effect, if not the exact words."},{"k":"C","t":"remove humor from the entire book to maintain consistency."},{"k":"D","t":"translate the passage literally, accepting the lost joke."}]},{"id":"pc-93","topic":"pc","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Mixed / Hardest Set","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"A","explanation":"The clustering of supposedly rare events logically points to the underlying data/assumptions being outdated — a standard, sensible engineering conclusion (often tied to climate change, though the paragraph doesn’t need to say so explicitly). (B) dismisses a pattern the paragraph treats as significant enough to prompt a conclusion. (C) and (D) contradict the implied inadequacy suggested by the defenses being repeatedly overwhelmed.","stem":"The city’s flood defenses had been designed for a “hundred-year storm,” a statistical benchmark based on historical rainfall data collected decades earlier. When three such storms struck within a single decade, engineers were forced to conclude that _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"the original historical data likely no longer reflected current conditions."},{"k":"B","t":"the storms were simply an extraordinarily unlikely coincidence with no broader implication."},{"k":"C","t":"the flood defenses had performed exactly as intended."},{"k":"D","t":"no further investment in flood defense was necessary."}]},{"id":"pc-94","topic":"pc","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Mixed / Hardest Set","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Fits the described entrenched impasse — a simple fix is unlikely to be immediately embraced when the real sticking point (as with many stalled negotiations) is about principle or precedent, not just the number itself; “initially” signals this reaction may later change. (A) contradicts “initially,” which implies some resistance before eventual movement. (C) contradicts the associate’s suggestion being framed as new. (D) is an excessive overreaction to a reasonable proposal.","stem":"The negotiation had reached an impasse over a single number: each side’s final offer differed by less than two percent of the total contract value, yet neither would move. A junior associate suggested splitting the difference exactly in half, a proposal both sides initially _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"accepted immediately without any hesitation."},{"k":"B","t":"dismissed as too simple to address the real, underlying tension."},{"k":"C","t":"had already proposed themselves earlier in the talks."},{"k":"D","t":"found offensive and refused to discuss further."}]},{"id":"pc-95","topic":"pc","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Mixed / Hardest Set","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"The discovery directly removes the estate’s core objection (not knowing her true intent) — a natural, logical consequence. (A) contradicts the significance the paragraph places on this discovery. (C) contradicts “outlining exactly how she intended to end the story.” (D) is an unsupported new claim the paragraph gives no basis for.","stem":"The novelist’s estate had long refused to authorize a sequel to her unfinished final manuscript, arguing any completion by another writer would betray her original vision. Decades later, the discovery of her personal notes, outlining exactly how she intended to end the story, _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"changed nothing about the estate’s position."},{"k":"B","t":"led the estate to finally authorize a completion based on those notes."},{"k":"C","t":"revealed she had never intended to finish the story at all."},{"k":"D","t":"was dismissed by scholars as an obvious forgery."}]},{"id":"pc-96","topic":"pc","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Mixed / Hardest Set","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Matches “unexpected secondary finding” precisely — an unrelated benefit is a classic, real-world pattern (drug repurposing) and fits the surprise framing. (A) isn’t really a “finding” worth highlighting as unexpected. (C) contradicts “modest effect,” which implies some improvement, not worsening. (D) doesn’t constitute a meaningful secondary finding at all.","stem":"The pharmaceutical trial’s most striking result wasn’t the drug’s effect on the target condition, which was modest, but an unexpected secondary finding: patients also reported _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"no side effects of any kind throughout the trial."},{"k":"B","t":"significant improvement in an entirely unrelated chronic condition."},{"k":"C","t":"a worsening of the original target condition."},{"k":"D","t":"complete indifference to having taken the drug at all."}]},{"id":"pc-97","topic":"pc","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Mixed / Hardest Set","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"“However” signals a reversal of the initially feared outcome — a well-documented real-world pattern with urban park conversions (the “High Line effect”). (A) confirms rather than reverses the fear, contradicting “however.” (C) and (D) sidestep the clear contrast the sentence sets up.","stem":"The city’s plan to convert an abandoned rail line into a public park faced fierce initial opposition from nearby property owners concerned about foot traffic and noise. Once construction was complete, however, property values along the new park _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"fell sharply, confirming the owners’ original concerns."},{"k":"B","t":"rose significantly, reversing most owners’ initial opposition."},{"k":"C","t":"remained completely unchanged in every respect."},{"k":"D","t":"were never tracked or measured by any official body."}]},{"id":"pc-98","topic":"pc","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Mixed / Hardest Set","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"“Upon closer examination” plus the emphasis on stumbling onto something during a “routine” audit signals a significant, escalating discovery, not a trivial one. (A) deflates the buildup the sentence has constructed. (C) contradicts the framing of this as a new “discrepancy” found during the audit. (D) makes no sense given it’s described as a discrepancy “in a single subsidiary’s accounts.”","stem":"The company’s internal audit was meant to be routine, but auditors stumbled onto a discrepancy in a single subsidiary’s accounts that, upon closer examination, _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"turned out to be a minor, easily explained clerical error."},{"k":"B","t":"revealed a pattern of misreporting stretching back several years."},{"k":"C","t":"had already been fully disclosed in previous public filings."},{"k":"D","t":"was unrelated to any of the subsidiary’s actual finances."}]},{"id":"pc-99","topic":"pc","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Mixed / Hardest Set","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"“Something the team had never documented before” signals a genuinely surprising finding — a dramatic range expansion delivers on that promise specifically. (A) is exactly what was expected, contradicting “never documented before.” (C) and (D) don’t constitute a meaningful scientific finding about migration at all.","stem":"The marine biologists had tagged the whale to study its typical migratory route along the coast. Signal data over the following year showed something the team had never documented before: _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"the whale following the exact route previously assumed."},{"k":"B","t":"the whale crossing an entire ocean basin, far beyond its expected range."},{"k":"C","t":"the tagging device malfunctioning within the first week."},{"k":"D","t":"the whale remaining in the exact same location for the entire year."}]},{"id":"pc-100","topic":"pc","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Mixed / Hardest Set","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"The setup explicitly contrasts with “territory or resources” (what was expected), so the answer must name something different and specific — a symbolic, non-material issue fits precisely and is a well-documented real pattern in actual peace negotiations. (A) and (C) directly contradict the “not about territory or resources” framing. (D) contradicts “most difficult clause,” which implies high, not low, importance.","stem":"The peace accord’s most difficult clause wasn’t about territory or resources, as both sides had expected going into negotiations, but about _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"territorial boundaries, which took the longest to resolve."},{"k":"B","t":"the wording of a formal apology, a symbolic issue neither side would compromise on."},{"k":"C","t":"resource-sharing agreements that were settled within the first week."},{"k":"D","t":"a technical matter that no one considered particularly important."}]},{"id":"ps-1","topic":"ps","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Too Narrow vs. Correctly Scoped","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"Captures the paragraph’s actual point — the failure of the rationality assumption as a general finding. (A) and (B) are each just one supporting example, too narrow to represent the whole point. (D) overstates into “entirely wrong about everything,” which the paragraph never claims.","stem":"For decades, economists assumed rational actors would always choose the option that maximized their financial return. Behavioral economics has since shown this assumption breaks down repeatedly in practice: people routinely choose smaller, immediate rewards over larger, delayed ones, and often let emotion override calculation entirely. This isn’t a minor exception — it’s now considered central to understanding real-world economic behavior.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"People often prefer smaller rewards now over larger rewards later."},{"k":"B","t":"Emotion frequently overrides careful financial calculation in individual decisions."},{"k":"C","t":"Behavioral economics has shown that the classical assumption of purely rational choice fails to explain real economic behavior."},{"k":"D","t":"Classical economics was entirely wrong about every aspect of human decision-making."}]},{"id":"ps-2","topic":"ps","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Too Narrow vs. Correctly Scoped","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Captures the overall claim the data point supports — the program’s connection to retention. (A) and (C) are single specific features, not the point of citing them. (D) introduces a claim about satisfaction the paragraph never actually makes.","stem":"The company’s new onboarding program pairs every new hire with a mentor from a different department, requires a structured thirty-day check-in schedule, and includes a formal skills assessment at the ninety-day mark. Internal data shows employees who complete the full program are significantly more likely to still be with the company after two years than those hired before the program existed.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The company assigns every new hire a mentor from a different department."},{"k":"B","t":"The new onboarding program appears linked to improved long-term employee retention."},{"k":"C","t":"Ninety-day skills assessments are the most important part of onboarding."},{"k":"D","t":"Employees at the company are generally satisfied with their jobs."}]},{"id":"ps-3","topic":"ps","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Too Narrow vs. Correctly Scoped","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"Captures the paragraph’s full causal chain and its actual conclusion. (A) and (B) are supporting facts, not the overall point. (D) overstates with a specific, dramatic prediction the paragraph never makes.","stem":"Coral bleaching events, once considered rare, have become measurably more frequent over the past three decades as ocean temperatures have risen. While healthy reefs can sometimes recover from a single bleaching event, repeated events in quick succession give reefs no time to regenerate, leading to permanent degradation in an increasing number of reef systems worldwide.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Ocean temperatures have risen over the past thirty years."},{"k":"B","t":"A single bleaching event is not usually fatal to a coral reef."},{"k":"C","t":"Increasingly frequent bleaching events are causing permanent, worldwide degradation of coral reefs."},{"k":"D","t":"All coral reefs will be destroyed within the next decade."}]},{"id":"ps-4","topic":"ps","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Too Narrow vs. Correctly Scoped","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"Captures the paragraph’s actual point — why the effort mattered, not just how long it took. (A) and (B) are process details, not the payoff being emphasized. (D) is an unsupported comparative claim the paragraph never makes.","stem":"The museum’s decision to digitize its entire archive took nearly six years and required scanning over two million individual documents by hand, since many were too fragile for automated scanners. The result is a searchable database that has already been used by researchers in over forty countries — far more than could have ever visited the physical archive in the same period.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The digitization project took six years to complete."},{"k":"B","t":"Automated scanners could not be used on many of the documents."},{"k":"C","t":"The digitization effort has dramatically expanded global access to the archive’s contents."},{"k":"D","t":"The museum’s archive is now more valuable than any other in the world."}]},{"id":"ps-5","topic":"ps","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Too Narrow vs. Correctly Scoped","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"Captures the full arc — the initial concern and the actual, contrary outcome. (A) and (B) are only the setup, not the paragraph’s actual finding. (D) overstates into a citywide consensus the paragraph never claims.","stem":"The city’s bike-lane expansion faced significant early criticism from business owners who feared reduced parking would hurt sales. A year after completion, surveys of businesses along the new lanes found sales had, on average, increased slightly, likely due to increased foot and bike traffic in the area.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Business owners initially opposed the bike-lane expansion."},{"k":"B","t":"Parking availability decreased significantly along the new bike lanes."},{"k":"C","t":"Despite initial business concerns, the bike-lane expansion did not harm — and may have modestly helped — nearby sales."},{"k":"D","t":"All businesses in the city now strongly support future bike-lane expansions."}]},{"id":"ps-6","topic":"ps","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Too Narrow vs. Correctly Scoped","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Captures both parts of the finding — the general success and the specific, disproportionate exception. (A) introduces an unsupported claim about fraud never mentioned. (C) overstates “limited access” into total absence. (D) is an extreme, unsupported recommendation.","stem":"A recent audit of the state’s disaster relief fund found that while the vast majority of claims were processed within the mandated thirty-day window, a small subset — fewer than five percent — took over six months to resolve, disproportionately affecting rural applicants with limited access to in-person support offices.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The disaster relief fund’s audit found significant fraud in claims processing."},{"k":"B","t":"Most claims were processed within the required time, but a small share of rural applicants faced major delays."},{"k":"C","t":"Rural applicants do not have access to in-person support offices."},{"k":"D","t":"The disaster relief fund should be shut down immediately."}]},{"id":"ps-7","topic":"ps","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Too Narrow vs. Correctly Scoped","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"Captures the full narrative — the discovery and the resulting strategic response, which is the paragraph’s real point. (A) and (B) are supporting facts that set up the point but aren’t themselves the point. (D) overstates — the paragraph describes an adaptation, not a failure.","stem":"The startup’s founders initially built their product for professional photographers, but usage data revealed that over sixty percent of active users were hobbyists using the tool for simple personal projects. Recognizing this mismatch, the company redesigned its pricing and onboarding specifically around this larger, previously unaddressed user base.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The product was originally designed for professional photographers."},{"k":"B","t":"Over sixty percent of users were hobbyists rather than professionals."},{"k":"C","t":"The company adapted its product strategy after discovering its actual user base differed from its intended one."},{"k":"D","t":"The company’s original strategy was a complete failure from the start."}]},{"id":"ps-8","topic":"ps","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Too Narrow vs. Correctly Scoped","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"Captures the full scope of findings and, crucially, the “even after controlling for” point that these effects are independent of background. (A) captures only one of the listed benefits. (B) misstates the point — income was controlled for, not shown to be irrelevant to outcomes generally. (D) overstates into an absolute guarantee the study doesn’t claim.","stem":"Long-term studies tracking children who attended high-quality early childhood education programs show measurable benefits persisting into adulthood, including higher rates of high school graduation and lower rates of involvement with the criminal justice system, even after controlling for family income and other background factors.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"High-quality early childhood education is associated with better adult graduation rates."},{"k":"B","t":"Family income does not affect a child’s educational outcomes."},{"k":"C","t":"High-quality early childhood education shows lasting positive effects on life outcomes that persist independent of family background."},{"k":"D","t":"Every child who attends preschool will graduate from high school."}]},{"id":"ps-9","topic":"ps","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Too Narrow vs. Correctly Scoped","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"Captures the full cause-and-effect arc — early failure, response, and successful turnaround. (A) and (B) are steps within the story, not its overall point. (D) is an extreme, unsupported conclusion contradicted by the actual turnaround described.","stem":"The restaurant chain’s expansion into a new country initially struggled, with several locations closing within the first year due to menu items that failed to resonate with local tastes. After significant menu localization — replacing nearly forty percent of the standard offerings with region-specific dishes — the remaining locations saw a marked turnaround in performance.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Several of the chain’s locations closed within the first year of expansion."},{"k":"B","t":"Forty percent of the original menu items were unpopular with local customers."},{"k":"C","t":"Localizing the menu to fit regional tastes reversed the chain’s early struggles in the new market."},{"k":"D","t":"The restaurant chain should never expand into new countries again."}]},{"id":"ps-10","topic":"ps","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Too Narrow vs. Correctly Scoped","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Captures the full, specific finding — the safety benefit alongside the important qualifier that traffic wasn’t reduced. (A) is only the qualifier, not the main finding. (C) overstates into a universal policy prescription the paragraph doesn’t make. (D) introduces an unsupported claim about causes of death never mentioned.","stem":"Analysis of traffic accident data across several cities found that intersections redesigned with raised pedestrian crossings saw a substantial drop in pedestrian injuries, even though overall vehicle traffic volume at those intersections remained largely unchanged.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Vehicle traffic volume did not change at the redesigned intersections."},{"k":"B","t":"Raised pedestrian crossings appear to reduce pedestrian injuries without reducing traffic flow."},{"k":"C","t":"All intersections in every city should be redesigned with raised crossings."},{"k":"D","t":"Pedestrian injuries are the leading cause of traffic-related deaths."}]},{"id":"ps-11","topic":"ps","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Too Broad vs. Correctly Scoped","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Correctly scopes the finding to the specific study while including the researchers’ own caveat about generalizability. (A) overstates “reduced by a third” into “eliminates all,” and ignores the explicit generalizability caution. (C) is a sweeping policy conclusion the paragraph doesn’t support. (D) overstates the caveat about “less robust” support into “no technical support of any kind.”","stem":"A study of one large hospital network found that switching to electronic prescribing reduced medication errors by nearly a third, primarily by eliminating errors caused by illegible handwritten prescriptions. Researchers cautioned, however, that the results might not generalize to smaller clinics with less robust technical support.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Electronic prescribing eliminates all medication errors in every healthcare setting."},{"k":"B","t":"Electronic prescribing reduced medication errors substantially at one hospital network, though its broader applicability remains uncertain."},{"k":"C","t":"Handwritten prescriptions should be banned nationwide immediately."},{"k":"D","t":"Smaller clinics have no technical support of any kind."}]},{"id":"ps-12","topic":"ps","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Too Broad vs. Correctly Scoped","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Stays scoped to the specific population studied and includes the stated open question. (A) overgeneralizes from one fox population to “all urban wildlife.” (C) is an unsupported inference not stated in the paragraph. (D) misreads the finding entirely — waste schedules are an opportunity the foxes exploit, not a “threat” described in the text.","stem":"Researchers studying a specific population of urban foxes found they had adapted to nocturnal scavenging patterns closely tied to local restaurant waste collection schedules, timing their activity within a narrow window each night. It remains unclear whether this behavior is learned individually or passed between generations.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"All urban wildlife has adapted its behavior to human schedules."},{"k":"B","t":"This specific fox population has adapted its activity patterns to local waste schedules, though how this behavior spreads remains unknown."},{"k":"C","t":"Foxes are more intelligent than previously believed."},{"k":"D","t":"Restaurant waste collection schedules are the primary threat to urban wildlife."}]},{"id":"ps-13","topic":"ps","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Too Broad vs. Correctly Scoped","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Correctly scopes to one firm’s trial and reflects the explicit caveat about representativeness. (A) and (D) overgeneralize into universal prescriptions and claims. (C) overstates “decreased significantly” into total elimination.","stem":"A trial of a four-day workweek at one mid-sized technology firm found productivity per employee remained stable while reported burnout decreased significantly over six months. The company has not yet decided whether to make the policy permanent, and researchers noted the firm’s flexible, project-based work style may not be representative of most workplaces.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Every company should immediately switch to a four-day workweek."},{"k":"B","t":"One firm’s four-day workweek trial showed promising but not necessarily generalizable results."},{"k":"C","t":"Burnout has been eliminated entirely at the firm."},{"k":"D","t":"Productivity always improves when work hours are reduced."}]},{"id":"ps-14","topic":"ps","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Too Broad vs. Correctly Scoped","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Correctly scoped to the specific city and the specific trend described. (A) generalizes beyond the one city studied. (C) and (D) both overstate a “difficulty finding” into an absolute absence.","stem":"A regional survey found that among small business owners in one mid-sized city, nearly seventy percent reported difficulty finding qualified staff for entry-level positions over the past year, a notably higher rate than the same survey recorded five years earlier.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Small businesses everywhere are struggling to find qualified staff."},{"k":"B","t":"In this city, small business owners are reporting a growing difficulty in hiring for entry-level roles compared to five years ago."},{"k":"C","t":"There are no qualified workers left in the region’s labor market."},{"k":"D","t":"Entry-level positions no longer exist in this city."}]},{"id":"ps-15","topic":"ps","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Too Broad vs. Correctly Scoped","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Matches the paragraph’s own explicit framing — encouraging but preliminary, requiring confirmation. (A), (C), and (D) all overstate “may reduce inflammation markers” into proof, universal applicability, or cure, all explicitly contradicted by “conclusions can be drawn” not yet being possible.","stem":"Preliminary results from a small clinical trial suggest the experimental compound may reduce inflammation markers in patients with a specific autoimmune condition, though the trial included only forty participants and researchers emphasized that larger trials are needed before any conclusions can be drawn.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The compound has been proven to cure the autoimmune condition."},{"k":"B","t":"A small preliminary trial found encouraging but inconclusive results that require larger studies to confirm."},{"k":"C","t":"All autoimmune conditions can now be treated with this compound."},{"k":"D","t":"The trial’s forty participants were fully cured of their condition."}]},{"id":"ps-16","topic":"ps","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Too Broad vs. Correctly Scoped","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Reflects both the observed improvement and the explicit causal uncertainty the economists raised. (A) overstates certainty the paragraph explicitly denies. (C) and (D) are unsupported absolutes in opposite directions.","stem":"An analysis of one country’s tax reform found that, in its first two years, revenue collection improved modestly among mid-sized businesses, though economists cautioned that the initial period coincided with unusually strong overall economic growth, making it difficult to isolate the reform’s specific effect.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The tax reform is definitively responsible for the entire increase in revenue."},{"k":"B","t":"Revenue improved after the reform, but economists caution the reform’s specific causal role is unclear amid strong overall growth."},{"k":"C","t":"Tax reforms never have any measurable effect on revenue."},{"k":"D","t":"All countries should immediately adopt this exact tax reform."}]},{"id":"ps-17","topic":"ps","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Too Broad vs. Correctly Scoped","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Reflects the actual scope (one neighborhood’s pilot) and the officials’ specific caveat about civic engagement. (A) and (D) are unsupported, sweeping negative claims. (C) overgeneralizes the neighborhood-level result to the entire city.","stem":"A pilot recycling program in one neighborhood succeeded in diverting nearly half of household waste from landfills within its first year, though city officials noted the neighborhood had unusually high participation in past civic initiatives, which may have contributed to the program’s success.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Recycling programs never work in neighborhoods with low civic engagement."},{"k":"B","t":"This neighborhood’s pilot program was successful, though its result may be linked to the area’s unusually high civic engagement."},{"k":"C","t":"The entire city’s household waste has been diverted from landfills."},{"k":"D","t":"All future recycling programs are guaranteed to fail."}]},{"id":"ps-18","topic":"ps","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Too Broad vs. Correctly Scoped","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Correctly captures the specific, notable comparison the paragraph highlights. (A) and (C) overgeneralize into universal claims about testing the paragraph never makes. (D) misstates “comparable rates” as “much higher.”","stem":"A university’s internal review of its need-based financial aid program found that recipients graduated at rates comparable to non-recipients, a notable finding given that recipients, on average, entered with somewhat lower standardized test scores than the general student population.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Standardized test scores have no relationship to graduation rates for any student."},{"k":"B","t":"Financial aid recipients at this university graduated at comparable rates to others despite entering with lower average test scores."},{"k":"C","t":"All universities should eliminate standardized testing entirely."},{"k":"D","t":"Financial aid recipients graduate at a much higher rate than other students."}]},{"id":"ps-19","topic":"ps","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Too Broad vs. Correctly Scoped","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Correctly scoped to the specific finding at this firm and the specific, notable independence from attendance. (A) overgeneralizes to all remote work and all employees. (C) misreads the point — attendance was shown to be a poor predictor, not “the only factor.” (D) is an unsupported policy leap.","stem":"A survey of remote employees at one multinational firm found that those working across more than three time zones from their direct manager reported lower feelings of inclusion in team decisions, even when objective measures like meeting attendance were similar to their more geographically aligned colleagues.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Remote work always reduces feelings of inclusion for every employee."},{"k":"B","t":"At this firm, greater time-zone distance from a manager was linked to lower reported inclusion, independent of meeting attendance."},{"k":"C","t":"Meeting attendance is the only factor that affects employee inclusion."},{"k":"D","t":"This company should eliminate remote work entirely."}]},{"id":"ps-20","topic":"ps","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Too Broad vs. Correctly Scoped","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Correctly scoped to the specific experiment and its notable finding about first-time donors. (A) and (C) overgeneralize a single experiment’s result into a universal prescription against statistics. (D) introduces an unsupported historical comparison never made in the paragraph.","stem":"A field experiment testing two different email subject lines for a nonprofit’s fundraising campaign found the version emphasizing a specific beneficiary’s story generated nearly twice the donation rate of a version citing broad statistics about the cause, though the effect was strongest among first-time donors specifically.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Statistics are never effective in any kind of fundraising appeal."},{"k":"B","t":"A story-focused subject line outperformed a statistics-focused one in this experiment, especially among first-time donors."},{"k":"C","t":"All nonprofits should immediately stop using statistics in their communications."},{"k":"D","t":"This particular email campaign raised more money than any previous campaign in the nonprofit’s history."}]},{"id":"ps-21","topic":"ps","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Distorted / Reversed Relationships","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately reflects the paragraph’s actual finding — a weak, not absent or reversed, correlation. (A) directly reverses the point, restating the “popular belief” the paragraph explicitly contradicts. (C) overstates “weak correlation” into “no effect whatsoever.” (D) reverses the claim into an unsupported opposite extreme.","stem":"Contrary to popular belief, higher-priced wines do not consistently receive higher ratings from expert tasters in blind tests; several studies have found only a weak correlation between price and perceived quality once tasters cannot see the label.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Expert tasters consistently rate more expensive wines higher, confirming popular belief."},{"k":"B","t":"Blind taste tests show only a weak link between a wine’s price and how experts actually rate its quality."},{"k":"C","t":"Price has no effect whatsoever on how wine tastes."},{"k":"D","t":"Cheaper wines are objectively superior to expensive wines."}]},{"id":"ps-22","topic":"ps","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Distorted / Reversed Relationships","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"Accurately reflects the nuanced finding — net job loss alongside a real, if smaller, shift toward new roles. (A) reverses the relative scale described (“significant” losses vs. “smaller” gains). (B) understates the significant job losses described. (D) overstates “significant number” into “every.”","stem":"The report found that while automation eliminated a significant number of manufacturing jobs over the decade, it simultaneously created a smaller but real number of new roles in machine maintenance and systems oversight — a shift in the type of work available, not simply a net loss.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Automation created far more jobs than it eliminated during the decade."},{"k":"B","t":"Automation had no real impact on the manufacturing job market at all."},{"k":"C","t":"While automation reduced manufacturing jobs overall, it also shifted some work toward new, related roles rather than eliminating labor entirely."},{"k":"D","t":"Automation eliminated every manufacturing job that existed before it."}]},{"id":"ps-23","topic":"ps","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Distorted / Reversed Relationships","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately reflects the paragraph’s reframing — internal weakness as a critical, prior factor complicating the invasion-only narrative. (A) restates the very narrative the research is shown to complicate. (C) is an unsupported extreme not claimed by the paragraph. (D) directly reverses the described relationship between dysfunction and response capacity.","stem":"The historian’s research complicates the traditional narrative that the empire’s decline was caused primarily by external invasion; internal administrative dysfunction, her evidence suggests, had already severely weakened the empire’s capacity to respond effectively well before major invasions began.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"External invasion was the sole and primary cause of the empire’s decline."},{"k":"B","t":"The historian’s research suggests internal dysfunction, not invasion alone, played a critical and prior role in the empire’s decline."},{"k":"C","t":"The empire never faced any external invasions at all."},{"k":"D","t":"Internal administrative dysfunction had no relationship to the empire’s ability to respond to threats."}]},{"id":"ps-24","topic":"ps","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Distorted / Reversed Relationships","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately reflects the paragraph’s specific reframing of relative causal weight. (A) restates the assumption the new evidence is shown to challenge. (C) understates the specificity of the finding, which does identify a cause (droughts). (D) is an unsupported absolute the paragraph doesn’t claim.","stem":"New evidence suggests the ancient trade route’s decline was driven less by the rise of sea trade, as long assumed, and more by a series of regional droughts that made overland travel increasingly impractical for merchants.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The rise of sea trade was definitively the primary cause of the trade route’s decline."},{"k":"B","t":"New evidence points to regional droughts, rather than the rise of sea trade, as the more significant cause of the route’s decline."},{"k":"C","t":"The trade route declined due to a combination of factors that researchers cannot identify."},{"k":"D","t":"Sea trade never had any impact on overland trade routes throughout history."}]},{"id":"ps-25","topic":"ps","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Distorted / Reversed Relationships","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately reflects the specific reversed direction of causality the study suggests. (A) restates the commonly assumed direction the study explicitly contradicts. (C) understates the finding — a relationship was found, just not in the commonly assumed direction. (D) overstates into a universal, deterministic claim.","stem":"The study did not find that social media use causes depression in teenagers; rather, it found that teenagers already experiencing depressive symptoms were more likely to increase their social media use, suggesting the relationship may run in the opposite direction from what is commonly assumed.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Social media use is the primary cause of depression among teenagers."},{"k":"B","t":"The study found that depressive symptoms may lead to increased social media use, rather than the reverse."},{"k":"C","t":"Social media use and depression have no relationship of any kind."},{"k":"D","t":"All teenagers who use social media frequently will become depressed."}]},{"id":"ps-26","topic":"ps","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Distorted / Reversed Relationships","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately reflects the reveal — the true cause contradicting media coverage. (A) restates the media’s incorrect attribution as fact. (C) directly contradicts “sales had actually been stable.” (D) contradicts the specific strategic cause identified.","stem":"The company’s layoffs were widely attributed in media coverage to declining sales, but internal documents later showed that sales had actually been stable; the layoffs had instead been driven by a strategic decision to reallocate spending toward an unrelated, unproven new product line.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Declining sales were the true and confirmed cause of the layoffs, as media coverage suggested."},{"k":"B","t":"Internal documents reveal that a strategic reallocation of spending, not declining sales, actually drove the layoffs."},{"k":"C","t":"The company’s sales had been declining sharply for several years before the layoffs."},{"k":"D","t":"The layoffs had no connection to any business decision made by the company."}]},{"id":"ps-27","topic":"ps","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Distorted / Reversed Relationships","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately reflects the paragraph’s reframing based on patent evidence. (A) restates the popular account the paragraph is shown to complicate. (C) misstates the situation — records do exist and are cited as evidence. (D) reverses “independent teams” into a false claim of collaboration.","stem":"Popular accounts often credit a single inventor with the breakthrough, but patent records show that at least three independent teams were working on nearly identical solutions within the same eighteen-month period, suggesting the underlying technology’s time had simply come rather than resulting from one person’s singular genius.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"A single inventor was solely and uniquely responsible for the breakthrough."},{"k":"B","t":"Patent records suggest the breakthrough emerged from parallel, independent efforts rather than one individual’s unique genius."},{"k":"C","t":"No credible records exist regarding who actually invented the technology."},{"k":"D","t":"The three competing teams worked together collaboratively on the same solution."}]},{"id":"ps-28","topic":"ps","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Distorted / Reversed Relationships","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately reflects the study’s actual finding, which explicitly contradicts the stated assumption. (A) restates the very assumption the study contradicts. (C) and (D) are unsupported extremes contradicting the “statistically indistinguishable” finding.","stem":"Contrary to the assumption that older workers are less adaptable to new technology, a workplace study found employees over fifty adopted a new software system at rates statistically indistinguishable from younger colleagues, though they were more likely to seek formal training beforehand.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Older workers adapt to new technology more slowly than younger workers, confirming common assumptions."},{"k":"B","t":"The study found no meaningful age-related difference in technology adoption rates, challenging a common assumption."},{"k":"C","t":"Only younger workers were able to successfully use the new software system."},{"k":"D","t":"Older workers refused to use the new technology under any circumstances."}]},{"id":"ps-29","topic":"ps","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Distorted / Reversed Relationships","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately reflects the finding of “no consistent relationship,” directly addressing and complicating the stated assumption. (A) restates the assumption the finding contradicts. (C) is an unsupported policy conclusion. (D) is a specific, unsupported claim the “no consistent relationship” finding directly rules out.","stem":"It is often assumed that stricter environmental regulations necessarily slow economic growth, but a comparison of regions with varying regulatory strictness found no consistent relationship between regulation levels and GDP growth rates over the studied period.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Stricter environmental regulations are proven to consistently slow economic growth."},{"k":"B","t":"The comparison found no consistent link between regulatory strictness and GDP growth, challenging the common assumption."},{"k":"C","t":"All environmental regulations should be eliminated to maximize growth."},{"k":"D","t":"Regions with no environmental regulations grew fastest during the studied period."}]},{"id":"ps-30","topic":"ps","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Distorted / Reversed Relationships","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately reflects the employees’ account, which directly contradicts the “common narrative.” (A) restates the very narrative being contradicted. (C) directly contradicts the described “aggressive and well-funded marketing campaign.” (D) contradicts “inferior to competitors.”","stem":"The common narrative holds that the software’s early success was due to superior engineering, but former employees describe a product that was, by most technical measures, inferior to competitors — its success, they argue, came almost entirely from an aggressive and well-funded marketing campaign.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The product’s superior engineering was the true and confirmed source of its early success."},{"k":"B","t":"Former employees suggest the product’s success stemmed more from marketing than from technical superiority, contradicting the common narrative."},{"k":"C","t":"The product had no marketing campaign of any kind."},{"k":"D","t":"The product was universally regarded as the best in its category on every measure."}]},{"id":"ps-31","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"True-But-Off-Focus vs. Main Point","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"Correctly identifies what the paragraph explicitly states is the novel’s “true subject.” (A) and (B) are setting details the paragraph explicitly frames as backdrop, not the actual focus. (D) overstates into an argumentative claim the paragraph doesn’t make — the novel isn’t described as arguing anything about decline “always” destroying relationships.","stem":"The novel is set in a fictional coastal town during a decades-spanning economic decline, but its true subject is not the town’s economy — it’s the way three generations of a single family cope, in strikingly different ways, with watching a place they love slowly disappear.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The novel is set in a fictional coastal town."},{"k":"B","t":"The town in the novel experiences a decades-long economic decline."},{"k":"C","t":"The novel primarily explores how one family emotionally navigates the slow loss of a beloved place, using economic decline as its backdrop."},{"k":"D","t":"The novel argues that economic decline always destroys family relationships."}]},{"id":"ps-32","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"True-But-Off-Focus vs. Main Point","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"Correctly identifies the paragraph’s explicit statement of where the “real focus” lies. (A) describes only the opening section, not the documentary’s actual focus. (B) is a detail not even confirmed as the paragraph’s point — the paragraph doesn’t explain why the project failed. (D) overstates into a sweeping argument not attributed to the documentary.","stem":"The documentary spends its first thirty minutes establishing the mechanics of the failed engineering project in exhaustive technical detail, but its real focus emerges in the second half: the human cost borne by the small town that had staked its economic future on the project’s success.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The documentary provides exhaustive technical detail about a failed engineering project."},{"k":"B","t":"The engineering project ultimately failed due to a design flaw."},{"k":"C","t":"The documentary’s central focus is the human impact on a town that depended on a project which failed, despite its technical opening."},{"k":"D","t":"The documentary argues that engineering projects should never be attempted in small towns."}]},{"id":"ps-33","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"True-But-Off-Focus vs. Main Point","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"Correctly identifies the author’s stated “clear intent,” not just the events catalogued. (A) and (B) are individual facts, not the author’s actual purpose in including them. (D) overstates “illustrate an argument” into a claim of universal proof.","stem":"The biography spends considerable time cataloguing its subject’s numerous professional failures before his eventual success, but the author’s clear intent is to use those failures to illustrate a broader argument about resilience and the nonlinear nature of achievement.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The subject of the biography experienced numerous professional failures."},{"k":"B","t":"The biography eventually describes its subject achieving success."},{"k":"C","t":"The biography uses its subject’s failures to build a broader argument about resilience and non-linear paths to achievement."},{"k":"D","t":"The biography proves that failure is a necessary requirement for anyone’s success."}]},{"id":"ps-34","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"True-But-Off-Focus vs. Main Point","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Correctly identifies the “actual argument” the paragraph explicitly names, distinguishing it from the “technical groundwork.” (A) is only the setup, not the argument it exists to support. (C) is an unsupported claim never made. (D) overstates the argument into a blanket exoneration of engineers.","stem":"The lecture opens with a lengthy discussion of the physics of bridge construction, but this technical groundwork exists only to set up the lecture’s actual argument: that engineering failures are, more often than not, failures of communication between specialists rather than failures of calculation.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The lecture provides a detailed explanation of bridge construction physics."},{"k":"B","t":"The lecture’s central argument is that engineering failures more often stem from poor communication than from calculation errors."},{"k":"C","t":"Bridges are the most complex structures to engineer."},{"k":"D","t":"Engineers should not be blamed for structural failures of any kind."}]},{"id":"ps-35","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"True-But-Off-Focus vs. Main Point","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Correctly identifies the “more significant point” the article’s own conclusion emphasizes. (A) is the technical setup, not the point emphasized. (C) is an unsupported, invented claim. (D) is a claim about energy storage capacity never actually made in the paragraph, which focuses on materials, not capacity.","stem":"The article describes in detail the chemical process by which the new battery technology stores energy, but its concluding paragraphs make clear the more significant point: that the technology’s real advantage lies in using only abundant, non-toxic materials, unlike current alternatives.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The article explains the chemical process behind the new battery’s energy storage."},{"k":"B","t":"The new battery technology’s most significant advantage, per the article, is its use of abundant, non-toxic materials rather than the alternatives’ materials."},{"k":"C","t":"Current battery alternatives are illegal in most countries due to their toxicity."},{"k":"D","t":"The new battery stores more energy than any other technology available."}]},{"id":"ps-36","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"True-But-Off-Focus vs. Main Point","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Correctly identifies what the executive summary explicitly frames as “the real story.” (A) is only the supporting data, not the point. (C) directly contradicts “revenue grows.” (D) overstates into a specific recommendation the paragraph never makes.","stem":"The report includes extensive charts on quarterly revenue figures across every division, but its executive summary makes clear the real story is not the revenue numbers themselves — it’s the sharp rise in customer acquisition cost, which is quietly eroding profit margins even as revenue grows.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The report includes detailed quarterly revenue charts for every division."},{"k":"B","t":"The report’s executive summary identifies rising customer acquisition costs, not revenue growth, as the real story eroding profit margins."},{"k":"C","t":"Revenue has declined significantly across every division this quarter."},{"k":"D","t":"The company should stop acquiring new customers immediately."}]},{"id":"ps-37","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"True-But-Off-Focus vs. Main Point","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Correctly identifies what the paragraph explicitly calls “notable” and “reframing,” rather than the more extensively covered but less significant training details. (A) is the extensively covered but explicitly secondary content. (C) directly contradicts the disclosure described. (D) is an unsupported claim never made about the regimen.","stem":"The interview covers the athlete’s training regimen in considerable depth, but what makes it notable is a brief, almost offhand comment revealing she nearly quit the sport entirely three years before her breakthrough — a detail that reframes everything discussed before it.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The interview describes the athlete’s training regimen in detail."},{"k":"B","t":"The interview’s most notable and reframing content is the athlete’s disclosure that she nearly quit the sport before her eventual breakthrough."},{"k":"C","t":"The athlete has never faced any doubts about her career."},{"k":"D","t":"The athlete’s training regimen is considered unusual by industry standards."}]},{"id":"ps-38","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"True-But-Off-Focus vs. Main Point","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Correctly identifies what actually “generated the most public attention,” which is the paragraph’s real emphasis, not the majority-time technical content. (A) and (C) describe only the time-majority content, not what mattered most per the paragraph. (D) is an unsupported, overstated claim about unanimous agreement and universal unfairness.","stem":"The panel discussion spends most of its ninety minutes on technical questions about the new policy’s implementation timeline, but the moment that generated the most public attention afterward was a single, unscripted exchange about the policy’s fairness to a specific vulnerable group.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The panel discussion primarily addressed the policy’s implementation timeline."},{"k":"B","t":"The exchange that drew the most public attention concerned the policy’s fairness to a specific vulnerable group, despite the discussion’s mostly technical focus."},{"k":"C","t":"The policy’s implementation timeline was the most controversial aspect discussed."},{"k":"D","t":"The panel unanimously agreed the policy was unfair to all groups involved."}]},{"id":"ps-39","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"True-But-Off-Focus vs. Main Point","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Correctly identifies what reviewers explicitly single out as the “emotional core.” (A) is the majority content but explicitly not the core per the paragraph. (C) conflates two unrelated things — career and the father relationship. (D) contradicts “devotes several chapters to… career achievements.”","stem":"The memoir devotes several chapters to the author’s career achievements, but its emotional core, as several reviewers have noted, is a much shorter passage describing a single conversation with her estranged father shortly before his death.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The memoir describes the author’s career achievements across several chapters."},{"k":"B","t":"Reviewers have identified a brief passage about a final conversation with the author’s estranged father as the memoir’s emotional core, despite the career-focused chapters."},{"k":"C","t":"The author’s relationship with her father was the primary subject of her career."},{"k":"D","t":"The memoir focuses exclusively on the author’s family relationships."}]},{"id":"ps-40","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"True-But-Off-Focus vs. Main Point","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Correctly identifies what analysts’ reports “focused almost entirely on,” distinguishing it from what the call itself spent the most time on. (A) is the call’s time-majority content, not what analysts emphasized. (C) overstates “hinting at” into a formal announcement. (D) is an unsupported claim about stock price never mentioned.","stem":"The quarterly earnings call spent considerable time addressing a minor product recall, but analysts’ subsequent reports focused almost entirely on a single line in the CEO’s prepared remarks hinting at an upcoming entry into an entirely new market.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The earnings call addressed a minor product recall in detail."},{"k":"B","t":"Analysts’ subsequent reports centered on the CEO’s hint about entering a new market, rather than the recall discussed at length on the call."},{"k":"C","t":"The company has already formally announced its entry into a new market."},{"k":"D","t":"The product recall significantly damaged the company’s stock price."}]},{"id":"ps-41","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Argumentative Paragraphs (Author’s Stance)","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures the author’s stated position and their reasoning. (A) contradicts “remained flat.” (C) contradicts the entire premise that grade inflation is real and being explained. (D) is an unsupported policy leap never suggested.","stem":"Some argue that grade inflation reflects genuinely improving student performance. But the more plausible explanation, given that standardized test scores have remained flat over the same period, is that grading standards themselves have simply loosened over time.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Standardized test scores have improved alongside rising grades."},{"k":"B","t":"The author considers loosened grading standards a more plausible explanation for rising grades than genuine improvement in student performance."},{"k":"C","t":"Grade inflation does not exist and is a myth invented by critics of education."},{"k":"D","t":"All standardized tests should be abolished immediately."}]},{"id":"ps-42","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Argumentative Paragraphs (Author’s Stance)","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures the author’s skeptical, qualifying stance. (A) contradicts the author’s skepticism entirely. (C) is an unsupported extreme never claimed. (D) is a policy conclusion the author never draws — the paragraph questions a claim, it doesn’t argue for a specific mandate.","stem":"Advocates of remote work often cite reduced commuting stress as a clear benefit. Yet this argument overlooks the significant number of workers who report new, different stressors — blurred boundaries between work and home life chief among them — suggesting the net effect on wellbeing may be far less positive than commonly claimed.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Remote work definitively improves employee wellbeing more than office work."},{"k":"B","t":"The author argues that remote work’s wellbeing benefits are less clear-cut than commonly claimed, given new stressors like blurred work-life boundaries."},{"k":"C","t":"No workers experience any stress related to commuting."},{"k":"D","t":"All employees should be required to return to the office immediately."}]},{"id":"ps-43","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Argumentative Paragraphs (Author’s Stance)","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures the author’s specific, reasoned skepticism. (A) directly contradicts the skeptical stance. (C) overstates into a blanket policy position never taken. (D) misstates the paragraph — advocates haven’t “explained,” which is different from having “admitted” unreliability.","stem":"Defenders of the merger point to projected cost savings as justification. But projected savings, history shows, are routinely overestimated in merger proposals — a pattern this deal’s advocates have yet to convincingly explain why their projections should be trusted more than their predecessors’.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The projected cost savings from this merger have already been proven accurate."},{"k":"B","t":"The author is skeptical of the merger’s projected savings, citing a historical pattern of overestimation that advocates haven’t adequately addressed."},{"k":"C","t":"Mergers should never be pursued under any circumstances."},{"k":"D","t":"This particular merger’s advocates have admitted their projections are unreliable."}]},{"id":"ps-44","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Argumentative Paragraphs (Author’s Stance)","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures the author’s stated skepticism about attribution, given the pre-existing trend. (A) contradicts the author’s explicit doubt about crediting the curfew. (C) contradicts “modest drop.” (D) contradicts the author explicitly calling the critics’ point valid (“rightly note”).","stem":"Proponents of the new curfew law argue it will reduce youth crime, pointing to a modest drop in incidents in the months following implementation. Critics rightly note, however, that overall crime was already declining citywide before the law took effect, making it difficult to credit the curfew specifically for the change.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The curfew law is proven to have single-handedly caused the drop in youth crime."},{"k":"B","t":"The author suggests it’s difficult to credit the curfew specifically, since crime was already declining before it was implemented."},{"k":"C","t":"Youth crime has increased significantly since the curfew was introduced."},{"k":"D","t":"Critics of the curfew law have no legitimate concerns about it."}]},{"id":"ps-45","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Argumentative Paragraphs (Author’s Stance)","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures the author’s balanced, evidence-based reconsideration. (A) contradicts the author’s explicit complication of that simple narrative. (C) directly contradicts “improved just as dramatically.” (D) overstates the author’s point — they suggest shared credit, not zero impact from the player.","stem":"It’s tempting to credit the team’s turnaround entirely to their star player’s return from injury. A closer look at the data, though, shows the team’s defensive performance — a unit largely unchanged in personnel — improved just as dramatically over the same stretch, suggesting coaching adjustments may deserve at least equal credit.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The star player’s return is definitively the sole cause of the team’s turnaround."},{"k":"B","t":"The author suggests coaching adjustments may deserve credit alongside the star player’s return, based on the defense’s independent improvement."},{"k":"C","t":"The team’s defense got significantly worse during this period."},{"k":"D","t":"The star player had no impact on the team’s performance at all."}]},{"id":"ps-46","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Argumentative Paragraphs (Author’s Stance)","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures the author’s stance and the specific evidence cited against the critics. (A) contradicts “early data suggests otherwise.” (C) overstates “no measurable change” into “improved dramatically.” (D) directly contradicts “significant drops in student-reported anxiety.”","stem":"Critics of the new grading policy warn it will lower academic standards. But early data suggests otherwise: schools that adopted the policy have seen no measurable change in standardized outcomes, while reporting significant drops in student-reported anxiety — hardly evidence of declining rigor.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The new grading policy has definitively lowered academic standards, as critics warned."},{"k":"B","t":"The author argues early data contradicts critics’ concerns, showing stable academic outcomes alongside reduced student anxiety."},{"k":"C","t":"Standardized test outcomes have improved dramatically under the new policy."},{"k":"D","t":"Student anxiety has increased significantly since the policy was introduced."}]},{"id":"ps-47","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Argumentative Paragraphs (Author’s Stance)","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures the author’s specific, conditional caution. (A) contradicts the author’s explicit hesitation to call it “a clear success.” (C) contradicts the entire premise linking transit and affordability. (D) overstates into a specific policy action the author never recommends.","stem":"Supporters call the new zoning law a necessary step toward affordability. Yet without accompanying investment in public transit, cheaper housing built farther from job centers may simply shift the cost burden from rent to commuting — a trade-off worth weighing before declaring the policy a clear success.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The new zoning law has already been declared a complete success by all parties."},{"k":"B","t":"The author cautions that without transit investment, the zoning law’s affordability gains may simply shift costs to commuting rather than genuinely reducing them."},{"k":"C","t":"Public transit investment has no relationship to housing affordability."},{"k":"D","t":"The zoning law should be repealed immediately."}]},{"id":"ps-48","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Argumentative Paragraphs (Author’s Stance)","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures the skeptics’ specific, reasoned objection about selection effects. (A) contradicts the explicit doubt raised about generalizability. (C) contradicts “early adopters’ weight loss.” (D) overstates into an unsupported blanket claim about all guidelines.","stem":"Enthusiasts of the new dietary guidelines point to early adopters’ weight loss as proof of concept. Skeptics counter that early adopters are, almost by definition, unusually motivated individuals — making it risky to assume similar results among the general population who might follow the guidelines with less discipline.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The dietary guidelines are proven to be effective for the general population."},{"k":"B","t":"The author raises doubt about generalizing early adopters’ results to the broader population, given their likely unusual motivation."},{"k":"C","t":"Early adopters of the guidelines experienced no weight loss whatsoever."},{"k":"D","t":"All dietary guidelines are inherently ineffective regardless of who follows them."}]},{"id":"ps-49","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Argumentative Paragraphs (Author’s Stance)","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures the author’s specific complication of the weather-only explanation. (A) contradicts the author’s explicit point about overlooked contributing factors. (C) directly contradicts “regulators had flagged… years before.” (D) contradicts the “documented history of deferred maintenance.”","stem":"Those who blame the power outage entirely on extreme weather overlook a documented history of deferred maintenance on the same grid infrastructure — a pattern regulators had flagged as a risk years before this specific storm arrived.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Extreme weather was the sole and complete cause of the power outage."},{"k":"B","t":"The author suggests that pre-existing, flagged infrastructure neglect, not just the storm, contributed significantly to the outage."},{"k":"C","t":"Regulators only became aware of infrastructure problems after the outage occurred."},{"k":"D","t":"The power grid has never experienced any maintenance issues before this incident."}]},{"id":"ps-50","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Argumentative Paragraphs (Author’s Stance)","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures the critics’ specific, conditional warning as presented by the author. (A) overstates a claim never made about elimination. (C) contradicts the point that investment is needed. (D) contradicts “proponents… argue” existing.","stem":"Proponents of the new sentencing guidelines argue they will reduce prison overcrowding. But without parallel investment in probation and reentry support services, critics warn the guidelines may simply shift the burden downstream rather than genuinely resolving it.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The new sentencing guidelines have already successfully eliminated prison overcrowding."},{"k":"B","t":"Critics warn the guidelines may merely relocate, rather than resolve, the underlying burden, absent investment in support services."},{"k":"C","t":"Probation services require no additional investment or resources."},{"k":"D","t":"The new sentencing guidelines have no supporters of any kind."}]},{"id":"ps-51","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Process / Explanatory Paragraphs","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures the described process and its specific outcomes. (A) overstates — only sufficiently massive stars follow this path, and the remnant may be a neutron star, not always a black hole. (C) overstates “briefly outshine a galaxy” into “brightest events… at all times.” (D) misapplies the process to the sun, which the paragraph specifies applies to stars “several times more massive.”","stem":"When a star several times more massive than the sun exhausts its nuclear fuel, its core collapses under gravity within seconds, triggering a supernova explosion that can, briefly, outshine an entire galaxy before the remnant collapses further into a neutron star or black hole.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"All stars eventually become black holes."},{"k":"B","t":"A massive star’s fuel exhaustion can trigger a supernova and ultimately leave behind a neutron star or black hole."},{"k":"C","t":"Supernovas are the brightest events in the observable universe at all times."},{"k":"D","t":"The sun will eventually explode as a supernova."}]},{"id":"ps-52","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Process / Explanatory Paragraphs","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures the described mechanism and its purpose. (A) contradicts “harmless component or weakened form.” (C) overstates a faster response into guaranteed immunity. (D) directly contradicts “produce antibodies.”","stem":"Vaccines work by introducing a harmless component or weakened form of a pathogen, prompting the immune system to produce antibodies without causing the actual disease; if the real pathogen is encountered later, the immune system can respond far more quickly than it could without this prior exposure.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Vaccines contain a full-strength, dangerous version of the disease-causing pathogen."},{"k":"B","t":"Vaccines train the immune system to respond faster to a real pathogen by first exposing it to a harmless version."},{"k":"C","t":"Vaccinated individuals can never contract the disease they were vaccinated against."},{"k":"D","t":"The immune system does not produce antibodies in response to vaccines."}]},{"id":"ps-53","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Process / Explanatory Paragraphs","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures the mechanism — specific conditions accelerating a natural process. (A) directly contradicts the described specific conditions required. (C) contradicts the description of microorganisms doing something “naturally occurring.” (D) directly contradicts the stated acceleration effect versus unmanaged decomposition.","stem":"Composting works by creating conditions — the right mix of moisture, oxygen, and carbon-to-nitrogen ratio — under which naturally occurring microorganisms can rapidly break down organic material into nutrient-rich soil, a process that would otherwise take years if left to decompose without intervention.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Composting requires no specific conditions and happens the same way regardless of moisture or oxygen levels."},{"k":"B","t":"Composting accelerates natural decomposition by creating optimal conditions for microorganisms to break down organic material."},{"k":"C","t":"Composting is a process invented entirely by humans that does not occur in nature."},{"k":"D","t":"All organic material decomposes at exactly the same rate whether composted or not."}]},{"id":"ps-54","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Process / Explanatory Paragraphs","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures the multi-factor process and its stated implication. (A) directly contradicts “rather than any single metric in isolation.” (C) contradicts the entire point that ratings can differ even with a shared factor. (D) directly contradicts cash flow being listed as a relevant factor.","stem":"A firm’s credit rating is determined by evaluating several factors together — existing debt levels, cash flow stability, and industry risk chief among them — rather than any single metric in isolation, which is why two companies with identical debt levels can still receive very different ratings.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"A firm’s credit rating is based entirely on its debt levels alone."},{"k":"B","t":"Credit ratings result from evaluating multiple factors together, which explains why companies with the same debt level can receive different ratings."},{"k":"C","t":"All companies within the same industry always receive identical credit ratings."},{"k":"D","t":"Cash flow stability is completely unrelated to a firm’s credit rating."}]},{"id":"ps-55","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Process / Explanatory Paragraphs","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures the process and its stated real-world constraint on adoption. (A) directly contradicts “energy-intensive” and “limited… adoption.” (C) reverses the actual mechanism described — water passes through, salt is blocked. (D) contradicts the described specialized membrane technology required.","stem":"Desalination plants remove salt from seawater primarily through reverse osmosis, forcing water through a semi-permeable membrane under high pressure that allows water molecules through while blocking salt ions — an energy-intensive process that has historically limited widespread adoption to water-scarce, energy-rich regions.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Desalination is a cheap and simple process used equally in every region of the world."},{"k":"B","t":"Reverse osmosis desalination is energy-intensive, which has historically limited its adoption mainly to water-scarce regions with sufficient energy resources."},{"k":"C","t":"Reverse osmosis allows salt through the membrane while blocking water molecules."},{"k":"D","t":"Desalination plants do not require any specialized equipment to operate."}]},{"id":"ps-56","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Process / Explanatory Paragraphs","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures the described mechanism and its stated timeframe. (A) directly contradicts “typically take several months.” (C) reverses the described effect entirely. (D) directly contradicts the entire causal chain described.","stem":"Interest rate hikes cool inflation by making borrowing more expensive, which reduces both consumer spending and business investment; the resulting slower demand growth eventually eases the upward pressure on prices, though the effects typically take several months to fully materialize in the broader economy.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Interest rate hikes have an immediate effect on prices within days."},{"k":"B","t":"Higher interest rates reduce spending and investment, which gradually eases inflationary pressure over several months."},{"k":"C","t":"Interest rate hikes increase consumer spending and business investment."},{"k":"D","t":"Interest rates have no relationship to overall inflation levels."}]},{"id":"ps-57","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Process / Explanatory Paragraphs","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures the full mechanism, including the explicitly stated importance of rest. (A) directly contradicts “recovery time… is essential to the process.” (C) contradicts “the body then repairs.” (D) reverses the described process — protein strands are added, not removed.","stem":"Muscle growth occurs when resistance training causes microscopic tears in muscle fibers, which the body then repairs during rest by fusing damaged fibers together and adding new protein strands — meaning recovery time, not just the workout itself, is essential to the process.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Muscle growth happens entirely during the workout itself, with rest playing no meaningful role."},{"k":"B","t":"Muscle growth results from the body repairing exercise-induced fiber damage during rest, making recovery time essential."},{"k":"C","t":"Resistance training causes permanent, unrepaired damage to muscle fibers."},{"k":"D","t":"Protein strands are removed from muscles during the repair process."}]},{"id":"ps-58","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Process / Explanatory Paragraphs","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures the multi-signal process and its stated implication. (A) directly contradicts “hundreds of signals.” (C) contradicts user engagement being explicitly listed as a signal. (D) directly contradicts the explicit exception described.","stem":"A search engine’s ranking algorithm evaluates hundreds of signals — including page relevance, site authority, and user engagement patterns — combining them into a single score used to order results, meaning a page can rank highly on one signal yet still appear low overall if other signals are weak.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Search engines rank pages based on a single signal, such as relevance alone."},{"k":"B","t":"Ranking algorithms combine many signals into one score, so strength in one area alone doesn’t guarantee a high overall ranking."},{"k":"C","t":"User engagement patterns have no influence on a page’s search ranking."},{"k":"D","t":"A page that ranks highly on one signal will always rank highly overall."}]},{"id":"ps-59","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Process / Explanatory Paragraphs","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures the full two-stage process described. (A) directly contradicts “two linked stages.” (C) reverses the described byproduct relationship. (D) contradicts the term “light-independent,” which the paragraph itself uses to distinguish this stage.","stem":"Photosynthesis converts light energy into chemical energy through two linked stages: light-dependent reactions that capture energy and produce oxygen as a byproduct, and light-independent reactions that use that captured energy to build sugar molecules from carbon dioxide.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Photosynthesis occurs in a single, unified stage with no distinct sub-processes."},{"k":"B","t":"Photosynthesis involves two linked stages, together converting light energy and carbon dioxide into stored chemical energy, releasing oxygen along the way."},{"k":"C","t":"Oxygen is consumed, not produced, during the light-dependent reactions."},{"k":"D","t":"Light-independent reactions require direct exposure to sunlight to occur."}]},{"id":"ps-60","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Process / Explanatory Paragraphs","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures the described mechanism and its security implication. (A) directly contradicts “tamper-resistant” and “immediately signaling.” (C) directly contradicts “linking each new block… to… the block before it.” (D) directly contradicts “each new block.”","stem":"A blockchain maintains a tamper-resistant record by linking each new block of transactions to a cryptographic hash of the block before it, meaning any attempt to alter a past transaction would change its hash and break the chain, immediately signaling that data has been tampered with.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Blockchain records can be easily and invisibly altered without detection."},{"k":"B","t":"Blockchain’s linked-hash structure makes past transactions tamper-resistant, since any alteration would visibly break the chain."},{"k":"C","t":"Each block in a blockchain exists entirely independently, with no connection to previous blocks."},{"k":"D","t":"Cryptographic hashes are only used in the very first block of a blockchain."}]},{"id":"ps-61","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Comparison / Contrast Paragraphs","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures both sides of the described trade-off. (A) contradicts the explicit contrast drawn. (C) is an unsupported value judgment the paragraph doesn’t make. (D) directly contradicts “backed by deposit insurance.”","stem":"Unlike traditional savings accounts, which offer modest but predictable returns backed by deposit insurance, many newer investment apps offer the potential for higher returns but expose users to volatility and, in some cases, risks not covered by the same insurance protections.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Traditional savings accounts and newer investment apps offer identical risk and return profiles."},{"k":"B","t":"Traditional savings accounts offer lower but more predictable, insured returns, while newer investment apps offer higher potential returns with greater, sometimes uninsured, risk."},{"k":"C","t":"Newer investment apps are always a better choice than traditional savings accounts."},{"k":"D","t":"Traditional savings accounts are not protected by any form of deposit insurance."}]},{"id":"ps-62","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Comparison / Contrast Paragraphs","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures both the shared trait and the specific, stated point of distinction. (A) is an unsupported overall intelligence comparison never made. (C) directly contradicts “both crows and chimpanzees have demonstrated tool use.” (D) directly reverses which species shows the specific behavior described.","stem":"While both crows and chimpanzees have demonstrated tool use in the wild, crows are notable for a skill chimpanzees have not shown: manufacturing tools of different, deliberately chosen shapes for different specific tasks, rather than using a single general-purpose tool.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Chimpanzees are more intelligent than crows in every measurable respect."},{"k":"B","t":"Both species use tools, but crows uniquely demonstrate task-specific tool shaping that hasn’t been observed in chimpanzees."},{"k":"C","t":"Crows have never been observed using tools of any kind."},{"k":"D","t":"Chimpanzees create differently shaped tools for different tasks, unlike crows."}]},{"id":"ps-63","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Comparison / Contrast Paragraphs","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures the stated contrast in technique and its critical reception. (A) contradicts the explicit contrast drawn. (C) reverses which film used which technique. (D) contradicts the critical assessment described, which is specifically about a weakness, not overall superiority.","stem":"Where the original film relied on practical effects and in-camera stunts to build tension, the reboot leans almost entirely on digital effects — a choice that critics argue produces visually impressive but emotionally weightless action sequences by comparison.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Both the original film and the reboot use identical filmmaking techniques."},{"k":"B","t":"The reboot’s reliance on digital effects, versus the original’s practical effects, has produced sequences critics find visually striking but emotionally less impactful."},{"k":"C","t":"The original film used only digital effects for all of its action sequences."},{"k":"D","t":"Critics unanimously praised the reboot as superior to the original in every way."}]},{"id":"ps-64","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Comparison / Contrast Paragraphs","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures both sides of the described trade-off. (A) contradicts the explicit contrast drawn. (C) reverses which type operates on-demand. (D) overstates the described upfront cost trade-off into a total-cost claim never made.","stem":"Whereas older water heaters continuously maintain a large tank of hot water regardless of demand, tankless models heat water only as it’s needed, resulting in lower standby energy loss but often at a higher upfront installation cost.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Tankless water heaters and tank-based models have identical energy usage and installation costs."},{"k":"B","t":"Tankless models reduce standby energy loss compared to tank-based heaters, but this often comes with a higher upfront installation cost."},{"k":"C","t":"Tank-based water heaters only heat water as it is needed."},{"k":"D","t":"Tankless water heaters always have lower total costs than tank-based models over any timeframe."}]},{"id":"ps-65","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Comparison / Contrast Paragraphs","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures the described contrast and its stated consequence. (A) contradicts the explicit contrast drawn. (C) is an unsupported claim about accuracy never made. (D) directly contradicts “constrained by fixed daily deadlines and page space.”","stem":"Print newspapers, constrained by fixed daily deadlines and page space, historically forced editors to make hard choices about story prioritization; digital news, unconstrained by either, has instead created an environment where volume of output can substitute for that same editorial discipline.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Print and digital news formats impose the exact same constraints on editors."},{"k":"B","t":"Unlike print’s deadline and space constraints, digital news’s lack of such limits has allowed high output volume to substitute for prioritization discipline."},{"k":"C","t":"Digital news is inherently more accurate than print news due to fewer constraints."},{"k":"D","t":"Print newspapers have no fixed deadlines or space limitations."}]},{"id":"ps-66","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Comparison / Contrast Paragraphs","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures both the contrast and its stated implication (complementary, not interchangeable). (A) contradicts the explicit contrast drawn. (C) directly contradicts “even in overcast or nighttime conditions.” (D) is an unsupported absolute comparison never made.","stem":"Solar panels generate electricity most efficiently in direct, consistent sunlight, making them well-suited to regions with reliably clear skies; wind turbines, by contrast, can generate power even in overcast or nighttime conditions, provided sufficient wind speed, making the two technologies often complementary rather than interchangeable.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Solar and wind power generate electricity under exactly the same conditions."},{"k":"B","t":"Solar and wind power have different optimal conditions, making them often complementary technologies rather than direct substitutes."},{"k":"C","t":"Wind turbines cannot generate any electricity at night under any conditions."},{"k":"D","t":"Solar panels generate more electricity than wind turbines in all conditions."}]},{"id":"ps-67","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Comparison / Contrast Paragraphs","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures the described trade-off. (A) contradicts the explicit distinction in risk drawn. (C) directly contradicts “can rise or fall later.” (D) directly reverses which type has the lower initial rate.","stem":"Unlike a fixed-rate mortgage, where the interest rate remains constant for the loan’s full term, an adjustable-rate mortgage typically offers a lower initial rate that can rise or fall later based on broader market conditions, introducing a level of long-term uncertainty fixed-rate borrowers don’t face.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Fixed-rate and adjustable-rate mortgages carry identical long-term risk profiles."},{"k":"B","t":"Adjustable-rate mortgages offer a lower initial rate but introduce long-term rate uncertainty that fixed-rate mortgages avoid."},{"k":"C","t":"Adjustable-rate mortgage interest rates never change after the loan is issued."},{"k":"D","t":"Fixed-rate mortgages typically have lower initial interest rates than adjustable-rate mortgages."}]},{"id":"ps-68","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Comparison / Contrast Paragraphs","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures the stated trade-off and its timeframe. (A) contradicts the explicit yield distinction drawn. (C) directly contradicts “relies on synthetic fertilizers.” (D) directly contradicts “accepting somewhat lower yields in early years.”","stem":"While conventional agriculture typically relies on synthetic fertilizers to maximize short-term yield, regenerative practices prioritize long-term soil health, often accepting somewhat lower yields in early years in exchange for reduced input costs and improved resilience over time.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Regenerative and conventional agriculture produce identical yields in every growing season."},{"k":"B","t":"Regenerative agriculture may involve lower early yields than conventional methods, trading that for long-term soil health and resilience."},{"k":"C","t":"Conventional agriculture never uses any synthetic fertilizers."},{"k":"D","t":"Regenerative agriculture always produces higher yields than conventional agriculture from the very first year."}]},{"id":"ps-69","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Comparison / Contrast Paragraphs","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures the described contrast and its stated consequence. (A) contradicts “vastly different completion timelines.” (C) directly contradicts the individualized pacing described. (D) directly reverses which model uses a fixed pace.","stem":"Traditional classrooms structure learning around a fixed pace set by the instructor for the whole group, whereas mastery-based programs allow each student to advance only once they’ve demonstrated proficiency, which can mean vastly different completion timelines across students in the same course.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Traditional and mastery-based classrooms produce identical completion timelines for all students."},{"k":"B","t":"Mastery-based programs let students advance individually upon demonstrating proficiency, unlike the fixed group pace of traditional classrooms, leading to varied completion times."},{"k":"C","t":"Mastery-based programs require every student to finish at exactly the same time."},{"k":"D","t":"Traditional classrooms allow students to advance at their own individual pace."}]},{"id":"ps-70","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Comparison / Contrast Paragraphs","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures the described historical shift and contrast. (A) directly contradicts “computational photography… using software.” (C) directly reverses which era used which approach. (D) directly contradicts the stated shift toward software/computational methods.","stem":"Whereas early smartphone cameras relied almost entirely on larger sensors and physical lens improvements to boost image quality, modern devices increasingly rely on computational photography — using software to combine multiple exposures — to achieve gains that hardware improvements alone could no longer easily deliver.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Modern smartphone cameras rely exclusively on larger physical sensors for image quality improvements."},{"k":"B","t":"Modern smartphones increasingly use computational, software-based techniques rather than relying solely on hardware improvements, unlike earlier devices."},{"k":"C","t":"Early smartphone cameras used computational photography extensively."},{"k":"D","t":"Hardware improvements have become more important than software in modern smartphone cameras."}]},{"id":"ps-71","topic":"ps","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Mixed / Hardest Set","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"C","explanation":"Correctly captures the author’s core reframing — evaluate the policy against its actual, stated goal. (A) contradicts the author’s more nuanced, encouraging framing. (B) directly contradicts “didn’t fall as quickly as projected.” (D) overstates — the policy does relate to the labor market, just not primarily through short-term employment numbers.","stem":"It would be a mistake to conclude that the policy failed simply because unemployment didn’t fall as quickly as projected; the policy’s own stated goal was long-term structural change in the labor market, not a short-term employment boost, and by that specific measure, early indicators are more encouraging.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The policy has completely failed by every measure that matters."},{"k":"B","t":"Unemployment fell exactly as quickly as originally projected under the policy."},{"k":"C","t":"Judging the policy by its actual stated goal — long-term structural change, not short-term employment — early signs look more promising than a surface reading of unemployment data suggests."},{"k":"D","t":"The policy was never intended to have any effect on unemployment at all."}]},{"id":"ps-72","topic":"ps","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Mixed / Hardest Set","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures both the scope of the actual recall and the consequence of the media’s incomplete coverage. (A) directly contradicts “only a specific batch number.” (C) directly contradicts “largely failed to mention this detail.” (D) directly contradicts “many consumers… discard.”","stem":"The recall affected only a specific batch number manufactured during a narrow two-week window, yet media coverage largely failed to mention this detail, leading many consumers to needlessly discard products from unaffected batches out of unwarranted caution.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The entire product line, regardless of batch, was subject to the recall."},{"k":"B","t":"Incomplete media coverage of the recall’s narrow scope led many consumers to unnecessarily discard unaffected products."},{"k":"C","t":"Consumers were fully and accurately informed about which specific batches were affected."},{"k":"D","t":"No consumers discarded any products in response to the recall."}]},{"id":"ps-73","topic":"ps","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Mixed / Hardest Set","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures the paragraph’s precise distinction between what is and isn’t disputed. (A) directly contradicts “doesn’t dispute… significant clean energy.” (C) directly contradicts the stated, specific objection. (D) overstates a report’s objection into a confirmed cancellation never mentioned.","stem":"The committee’s report doesn’t dispute that the proposed dam would generate significant clean energy; its objection rests entirely on a separate concern — the irreversible displacement of a specific indigenous community whose ancestral land would be flooded by the reservoir.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The committee disputes that the dam would generate any clean energy at all."},{"k":"B","t":"The committee’s specific objection concerns the irreversible displacement of an indigenous community, not the dam’s clean energy potential, which it does not dispute."},{"k":"C","t":"The committee has no concerns whatsoever about the proposed dam."},{"k":"D","t":"The dam project has already been permanently canceled due to the committee’s report."}]},{"id":"ps-74","topic":"ps","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Mixed / Hardest Set","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures the precise, narrow nature of the critique as stated. (A) and (D) overstate into blanket claims about pacing the paragraph explicitly rejects (“many acclaimed films share that quality”). (C) directly contradicts “failing to build the tension or characterization.”","stem":"The critique isn’t that the film’s pacing is too slow — many acclaimed films share that quality — but that its slowness serves no discernible purpose here, failing to build the tension or characterization that justifies a deliberate pace elsewhere.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"All slow-paced films are considered poorly made by critics."},{"k":"B","t":"The critique targets the film’s slow pace as purposeless in this specific case, not slow pacing in general, which the critic acknowledges can work well elsewhere."},{"k":"C","t":"The film’s pacing is praised for effectively building tension and character."},{"k":"D","t":"Fast-paced films are always superior to slow-paced ones."}]},{"id":"ps-75","topic":"ps","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Mixed / Hardest Set","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures the investigation’s specific finding, which directly contradicts surface appearances. (A) restates the surface appearance the paragraph explicitly says is misleading. (C) directly contradicts the described finding of coordination. (D) directly contradicts “public statements suggesting otherwise.”","stem":"What looks, on the surface, like two companies competing fiercely for market share is, according to a recent antitrust investigation, actually a case of tacit price coordination — neither company undercutting the other meaningfully in years, despite public statements suggesting otherwise.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The two companies are engaged in genuine, aggressive price competition, exactly as they publicly claim."},{"k":"B","t":"An antitrust investigation suggests the companies’ apparent competition masks tacit price coordination, contradicting their public statements."},{"k":"C","t":"The antitrust investigation found no evidence of any wrongdoing by either company."},{"k":"D","t":"The two companies have never made any public statements about their competitive relationship."}]},{"id":"ps-76","topic":"ps","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Mixed / Hardest Set","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures the paper’s precisely scoped claim. (A) contradicts the explicit distinction drawn between institutional trust and family/community trust. (C) directly contradicts the described sharp decline in institutional trust. (D) directly reverses the described relationship — institutional trust, not community trust, is what declined sharply.","stem":"The paper’s central claim isn’t that social trust has declined across the board, a point on which the data is genuinely mixed, but specifically that trust in large institutions — as distinct from trust in family, neighbors, or local community — has fallen sharply and consistently.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The paper claims that all forms of social trust, including trust in family and neighbors, have declined sharply."},{"k":"B","t":"The paper’s specific claim is a sharp, consistent decline in trust toward large institutions, distinct from more mixed data on overall social trust."},{"k":"C","t":"The paper found no decline in trust in any type of relationship or institution."},{"k":"D","t":"Trust in local community has declined more sharply than trust in large institutions."}]},{"id":"ps-77","topic":"ps","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Mixed / Hardest Set","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures the paragraph’s precise distinction — consistent treatment of inputs, but bias embedded via training data. (A) directly contradicts “treats similar inputs identically.” (C) contradicts the described “subtler problem” of replicated bias. (D) directly contradicts “trained on… decades of biased human decisions.”","stem":"It is not that the algorithm is biased in some crude, easily detectable way; audits show it treats similar inputs identically regardless of demographic group. The subtler problem is that the historical data it was trained on already reflected decades of biased human decisions, which the algorithm has simply learned to replicate with mathematical consistency.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The algorithm applies clearly different rules to different demographic groups."},{"k":"B","t":"While the algorithm treats similar inputs consistently, it has learned and replicates bias embedded in the historical data it was trained on."},{"k":"C","t":"Audits found the algorithm to be completely free of any bias, historical or otherwise."},{"k":"D","t":"The algorithm was trained on data with no relationship to past human decisions."}]},{"id":"ps-78","topic":"ps","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Mixed / Hardest Set","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures the paragraph’s precise distinction between genuine consensus and manufactured public doubt. (A) directly contradicts “quite strong” consensus. (C) directly contradicts the described failure to “translate into public perception.” (D) misattributes the cause — the paragraph blames a “coordinated, well-funded effort,” not scientists.","stem":"The problem isn’t a lack of scientific consensus on the underlying issue, which is in fact quite strong; the problem is that this consensus has failed to translate into public perception, largely due to a coordinated, well-funded effort to manufacture the appearance of ongoing scientific debate.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"There is no genuine scientific consensus on the underlying issue."},{"k":"B","t":"Despite strong scientific consensus, public perception has been skewed by a deliberate, funded campaign to manufacture the appearance of scientific debate."},{"k":"C","t":"The public fully and accurately understands the strength of the scientific consensus."},{"k":"D","t":"Scientists themselves are responsible for creating public doubt about the issue."}]},{"id":"ps-79","topic":"ps","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Mixed / Hardest Set","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures the paragraph’s precise distinction — the process, not the art, is the real issue. (A) directly contradicts “few have argued has artistic merit.” (C) directly contradicts “remain sealed to this day.” (D) directly contradicts “without any public consultation.”","stem":"The controversy isn’t really about the statue itself, which few have argued has artistic merit; it’s about the surrounding process — a decision made without any public consultation, by a committee whose meeting minutes remain sealed to this day.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The primary controversy centers on the statue’s artistic merit, which many defend passionately."},{"k":"B","t":"The controversy centers on the flawed, non-transparent decision-making process behind the statue, not its artistic merit, which is largely undefended."},{"k":"C","t":"The committee’s meeting minutes have been fully public since the decision was made."},{"k":"D","t":"The statue was selected through an extensive public consultation process."}]},{"id":"ps-80","topic":"ps","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Mixed / Hardest Set","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures the paragraph’s precise framing of what is and isn’t actually disputed. (A) directly contradicts “virtually all agree they were” harsh. (C) overstates — this is explicitly one contested possibility, not agreed fact. (D) directly contradicts the agreed-upon harshness.","stem":"The dispute among historians isn’t over whether the treaty’s terms were harsh — virtually all agree they were — but over whether those harsh terms were the primary cause of the subsequent conflict, or merely one contributing factor among several equally significant ones.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Historians disagree about whether the treaty’s terms were harsh at all."},{"k":"B","t":"The genuine historical debate concerns how much causal weight the treaty’s harsh terms deserve relative to other contributing factors, not whether the terms were harsh."},{"k":"C","t":"All historians agree the treaty’s harsh terms were the single, primary cause of the conflict."},{"k":"D","t":"The treaty’s terms have been universally described by historians as lenient and fair."}]},{"id":"ps-81","topic":"ps","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Data / Statistics-Heavy Paragraphs","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures the finding and its notable independence from the controlled factors. (A) directly contradicts the point that the effect held after controlling for these. (C) introduces a specific comparison never made regarding marital status rankings. (D) directly contradicts “significantly higher… reported.”","stem":"A ten-year longitudinal study tracking over five thousand participants found that individuals who maintained regular social contact with at least three close friends reported significantly higher life satisfaction scores than those with fewer close connections, an effect that held even after controlling for income, health status, and marital status.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Life satisfaction is determined entirely by income and marital status."},{"k":"B","t":"Maintaining several close friendships is linked to higher life satisfaction, independent of income, health, and marital status."},{"k":"C","t":"Married individuals reported the highest life satisfaction scores in the study."},{"k":"D","t":"The study found no meaningful relationship between friendships and life satisfaction."}]},{"id":"ps-82","topic":"ps","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Data / Statistics-Heavy Paragraphs","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures both the specific finding (for completers) and the notable, flagged limitation (dropout rate). (A) overstates the effect to all enrolled participants, ignoring the completer-only framing. (C) directly contradicts “nearly a third… dropped out.” (D) directly reverses the direction of the effect described.","stem":"In a randomized trial involving over two thousand participants, a new behavioral intervention reduced average daily screen time by roughly ninety minutes among participants who completed the full six-week program, though nearly a third of enrolled participants dropped out before completion, a rate researchers noted was higher than in similar past studies.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The intervention reduced screen time for all participants who initially enrolled in the study."},{"k":"B","t":"Among those who completed it, the intervention meaningfully reduced screen time, though the trial also saw a notably high dropout rate."},{"k":"C","t":"No participants dropped out of the study before its completion."},{"k":"D","t":"The intervention increased average screen time among participants."}]},{"id":"ps-83","topic":"ps","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Data / Statistics-Heavy Paragraphs","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures both stated effects of the kiosks — the benefit and the trade-off. (A) ignores the explicitly stated shrinkage increase. (C) directly reverses the described transaction-time finding. (D) directly reverses the described shrinkage finding.","stem":"Sales data across the retail chain’s four hundred stores showed that stores which introduced self-checkout kiosks saw average transaction times drop by forty percent, but also saw a measurable increase in reported inventory shrinkage compared to stores without the kiosks.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Self-checkout kiosks reduced transaction times with no other measurable effects."},{"k":"B","t":"Self-checkout kiosks sped up transactions but were also associated with increased inventory shrinkage compared to stores without them."},{"k":"C","t":"Stores without self-checkout kiosks had faster transaction times than those with kiosks."},{"k":"D","t":"Inventory shrinkage decreased in stores that introduced self-checkout kiosks."}]},{"id":"ps-84","topic":"ps","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Data / Statistics-Heavy Paragraphs","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures both the finding and the authors’ own explicitly stated caveat. (A) directly contradicts “a small but statistically significant benefit.” (C) directly contradicts “fourteen of the twenty-three studies were funded by the… manufacturer.” (D) overstates “small” benefit into “dramatic, life-changing” claims never made.","stem":"A meta-analysis combining results from twenty-three separate studies on a popular supplement found a small but statistically significant benefit for one specific health marker, though the authors noted that fourteen of the twenty-three studies were funded by the supplement’s manufacturer, raising questions about potential bias in the overall body of evidence.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The meta-analysis found the supplement to be completely ineffective for any health marker."},{"k":"B","t":"While the meta-analysis found a small, statistically significant benefit, the authors flagged a potential conflict of interest given manufacturer funding of most included studies."},{"k":"C","t":"All twenty-three studies included in the meta-analysis were independently funded with no conflicts of interest."},{"k":"D","t":"The supplement was proven to have dramatic, life-changing health benefits."}]},{"id":"ps-85","topic":"ps","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Data / Statistics-Heavy Paragraphs","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures both the overall figure and the crucial, uneven distribution detail. (A) and (C) directly contradict “concentrated in just two… districts” and “several other districts saw… declines.” (D) directly contradicts “grew by twelve percent.”","stem":"Census data revealed that the city’s population grew by twelve percent over the decade, but nearly all of that growth was concentrated in just two of the city’s fourteen districts, while several other districts saw modest population declines over the same period.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The city’s population grew evenly across all fourteen districts."},{"k":"B","t":"The city’s overall population growth was highly concentrated in two districts, while several others actually declined."},{"k":"C","t":"Every district in the city experienced population growth to some degree."},{"k":"D","t":"The city’s overall population declined over the decade."}]},{"id":"ps-86","topic":"ps","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Data / Statistics-Heavy Paragraphs","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures both the overall finding and the specific, notable subgroup exception. (A) directly contradicts the overall finding favoring flexible scheduling. (C) is unsupported and contradicted by the described overall preference for flexibility across the broader sample. (D) directly contradicts the described income-based difference.","stem":"A workplace survey of over ten thousand employees across multiple industries found that flexible scheduling was cited as the top factor in job satisfaction more often than salary, though salary remained the top factor specifically among employees earning below the industry median income.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Salary is the single most important factor in job satisfaction for every employee surveyed."},{"k":"B","t":"Flexible scheduling outranked salary as a satisfaction factor overall, though salary remained most important specifically for lower earners."},{"k":"C","t":"Flexible scheduling was cited as important only by employees earning above the industry median."},{"k":"D","t":"The survey found no relationship between income level and which factors employees prioritize."}]},{"id":"ps-87","topic":"ps","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Data / Statistics-Heavy Paragraphs","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures both the broad finding and the specific, notable exception. (A) and (D) directly contradict the described roughly-halved readmission rate for most groups. (C) directly reverses the described exception — this group is where no benefit was found, not where it was greatest.","stem":"An analysis of hospital readmission rates found that patients discharged with a detailed, written follow-up care plan were readmitted at roughly half the rate of those discharged without one, an effect that was consistent across nearly every age group and condition studied, with one notable exception: patients over eighty-five, where the written plans showed no measurable benefit.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Written follow-up care plans had no measurable effect on readmission rates for any age group."},{"k":"B","t":"Written care plans were linked to substantially lower readmission rates across most groups, except among patients over eighty-five, where no benefit was found."},{"k":"C","t":"Written care plans were most effective specifically for patients over eighty-five years old."},{"k":"D","t":"Readmission rates were unrelated to whether patients received a written care plan."}]},{"id":"ps-88","topic":"ps","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Data / Statistics-Heavy Paragraphs","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures both the overall effect and the specific, notable pattern in where gains concentrated. (A) contradicts the explicitly uneven distribution of gains described. (C) directly contradicts “largest gains… occurring on” these routes. (D) directly reverses which routes saw the greatest effect.","stem":"A city’s transit authority found that adding real-time arrival displays at bus stops increased ridership by eight percent overall, with the largest gains — nearly twenty percent — occurring on routes previously considered the least reliable, where uncertainty about wait times had most discouraged riders.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Real-time arrival displays had the same effect on ridership across every route regardless of prior reliability."},{"k":"B","t":"Real-time displays boosted overall ridership, with the greatest gains specifically on the previously least reliable routes."},{"k":"C","t":"The least reliable routes saw a decrease in ridership after the displays were added."},{"k":"D","t":"Real-time arrival displays only affected routes that were already highly reliable."}]},{"id":"ps-89","topic":"ps","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Data / Statistics-Heavy Paragraphs","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures both the observed improvement and the researchers’ explicit causal caveat. (A) directly contradicts the stated difficulty in isolating the cause. (C) directly contradicts “increased funding for teacher training.” (D) directly contradicts “modest improvements.”","stem":"A comparison of student outcomes before and after a school district reduced average class sizes found modest improvements in standardized test scores, but researchers cautioned that the same period also saw increased funding for teacher training, making it difficult to isolate class size as the sole driver of the improvement.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Reduced class size was conclusively proven to be the sole cause of improved test scores."},{"k":"B","t":"Test scores improved after class sizes were reduced, but researchers note a concurrent factor makes isolating class size’s specific role difficult."},{"k":"C","t":"Teacher training funding decreased during the same period as the class size reduction."},{"k":"D","t":"Standardized test scores declined significantly after class sizes were reduced."}]},{"id":"ps-90","topic":"ps","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Data / Statistics-Heavy Paragraphs","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures both the finding and the notably low adoption rate flagged in the study. (A) directly contradicts “fewer than one in five… had any such plan.” (C) directly contradicts “roughly twice as likely to survive.” (D) directly reverses the described relationship.","stem":"A multi-year tracking study of small businesses found that those which adopted formal succession planning were roughly twice as likely to survive a founder’s departure intact, though the study also noted that fewer than one in five small businesses surveyed had any such plan in place at all.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Nearly all small businesses surveyed already had formal succession plans in place."},{"k":"B","t":"Succession planning was linked to much higher survival rates after a founder’s departure, though the study found such planning to be rare among small businesses."},{"k":"C","t":"Succession planning had no measurable effect on small business survival rates."},{"k":"D","t":"Small businesses without succession plans were more likely to survive a founder’s departure."}]},{"id":"ps-91","topic":"ps","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Full Mixed Review","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures the overall positive trend alongside the acknowledged exception. (A) overstates “most” into “every.” (C) directly contradicts the described positive outcomes. (D) overgeneralizes from the specific, acknowledged exception of vehicle-access-dependent businesses.","stem":"The city’s decision to convert a major downtown avenue into a pedestrian-only zone was met with predictions of economic disaster from nearby merchants. Two years on, foot traffic data shows a marked increase, and while a handful of businesses reliant on quick vehicle access did close, most storefronts report higher revenue than before the conversion.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Every business on the avenue has seen increased revenue since the conversion."},{"k":"B","t":"Despite initial fears, the pedestrian conversion has generally boosted foot traffic and revenue for most businesses, though not universally."},{"k":"C","t":"The pedestrian zone has been an unqualified disaster exactly as merchants originally predicted."},{"k":"D","t":"Vehicle access is essential for all types of retail businesses to succeed."}]},{"id":"ps-92","topic":"ps","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Full Mixed Review","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures the authors’ own explicitly stated limitation and its likely effect. (A) directly contradicts the stated exclusion of non-treatment-seekers. (C) directly contradicts the described skew toward “more severe cases.” (D) directly contradicts the authors “carefully noting a key limitation.”","stem":"The study’s authors are careful to note a key limitation: their sample was drawn exclusively from patients who voluntarily sought treatment, meaning those with milder symptoms who never sought care — likely a substantial group — are entirely absent from the data, potentially skewing the findings toward more severe cases.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The study’s findings are representative of everyone with the condition, including those who never sought treatment."},{"k":"B","t":"The study’s sample, limited to those who sought treatment, likely skews toward more severe cases and excludes a potentially substantial group with milder symptoms."},{"k":"C","t":"Patients with mild symptoms make up the majority of the study’s sample."},{"k":"D","t":"The authors found no limitations worth noting in their study’s methodology."}]},{"id":"ps-93","topic":"ps","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Full Mixed Review","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures both the disproven assumption and the one dimension where a real difference (time) was found. (A) directly contradicts “no significant difference in sentencing severity.” (C) directly reverses which format took longer. (D) overstates “no significant difference” in severity into total identicalness, ignoring the stated time difference.","stem":"Contrary to the assumption that remote juries would show more leniency in sentencing due to reduced emotional immediacy, an analysis of case outcomes found no significant difference in sentencing severity between remote and in-person proceedings, though remote proceedings did take measurably longer to reach a verdict.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Remote juries were significantly more lenient than in-person juries, confirming the original assumption."},{"k":"B","t":"Sentencing severity showed no significant difference between remote and in-person proceedings, though remote cases took longer to decide, contradicting the original assumption of leniency."},{"k":"C","t":"In-person juries took significantly longer to reach verdicts than remote juries."},{"k":"D","t":"Remote proceedings were found to be entirely identical to in-person proceedings in every respect."}]},{"id":"ps-94","topic":"ps","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Full Mixed Review","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures the paragraph’s specific reframing of the primary driver based on the data. (A) restates the tempting-but-incorrect assumption the paragraph corrects. (C) directly contradicts “sharp rise in the app’s user base.” (D) directly contradicts “most new users are arriving through word-of-mouth.”","stem":"It’s tempting to attribute the sharp rise in the app’s user base entirely to its recent viral marketing campaign, but engagement data shows most new users are arriving through word-of-mouth referrals from existing users — suggesting the campaign may have merely accelerated a trend already driven primarily by organic user satisfaction.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The viral marketing campaign is definitively the sole cause of the app’s user growth."},{"k":"B","t":"Data suggests organic word-of-mouth referrals, not the marketing campaign alone, are the primary driver behind the app’s user growth."},{"k":"C","t":"The app’s user base has not grown at all despite the marketing campaign."},{"k":"D","t":"Word-of-mouth referrals have had no measurable impact on the app’s growth."}]},{"id":"ps-95","topic":"ps","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Full Mixed Review","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures the paragraph’s precise distinction between what wasn’t the problem (load capacity) and what actually was (corrosion). (A) directly contradicts “didn’t fail to anticipate heavy loads.” (C) directly contradicts “had not been well understood at the time of construction.” (D) directly contradicts “decades of exposure to a de-icing chemical.”","stem":"The bridge’s engineers didn’t fail to anticipate heavy loads — the structure was rated for far more weight than it would ever realistically carry. What they failed to anticipate was decades of exposure to a de-icing chemical whose corrosive effects on the specific steel alloy used had not been well understood at the time of construction.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The engineers fundamentally miscalculated the bridge’s load-bearing capacity."},{"k":"B","t":"The bridge’s failure stemmed not from a load-bearing miscalculation but from unanticipated long-term chemical corrosion of the steel alloy used."},{"k":"C","t":"De-icing chemicals were well understood to be corrosive to the specific alloy at the time of construction."},{"k":"D","t":"The bridge was never exposed to any de-icing chemicals throughout its service life."}]},{"id":"ps-96","topic":"ps","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Full Mixed Review","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures the paper’s stated multi-causal, gradual argument. (A) directly contradicts “doesn’t claim… a single catastrophic event.” (C) understates the multiple factors explicitly listed together. (D) directly contradicts “increasing social stratification” being identified as a factor.","stem":"The paper doesn’t claim the ancient civilization collapsed due to a single catastrophic event; rather, its central argument is that a combination of gradual soil depletion, prolonged drought, and increasing social stratification compounded over generations until the society could no longer sustain itself.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The ancient civilization collapsed due to one sudden, catastrophic event."},{"k":"B","t":"The paper argues the civilization’s collapse resulted from multiple gradually compounding factors over generations, not a single sudden event."},{"k":"C","t":"Soil depletion was the only factor the paper identifies as contributing to the collapse."},{"k":"D","t":"The paper found no evidence of any social stratification within the civilization."}]},{"id":"ps-97","topic":"ps","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Full Mixed Review","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures the paragraph’s complication of the common framing by introducing the overlooked actors. (A) and (C) both ignore the intermediaries the paragraph explicitly centers. (D) directly contradicts “capture a substantial… share of the price difference.”","stem":"The debate over the new drug’s pricing often gets framed as pharmaceutical companies versus patients, but insurers and pharmacy benefit managers — largely absent from public discussion — capture a substantial and often overlooked share of the price difference between list price and what patients actually pay.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Pharmaceutical companies alone determine the final price patients pay for the drug."},{"k":"B","t":"Insurers and pharmacy benefit managers play a significant, often overlooked role in the pricing gap, complicating the common framing of the debate as companies versus patients."},{"k":"C","t":"Patients pay the exact list price set by pharmaceutical companies with no intermediaries involved."},{"k":"D","t":"Insurers and pharmacy benefit managers have no financial role in drug pricing whatsoever."}]},{"id":"ps-98","topic":"ps","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Full Mixed Review","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures the paragraph’s specific evidence for policy’s role, based on the described regional pattern. (A) restates the “appears to be” surface explanation the paragraph complicates. (C) directly contradicts “adoption rates flattened almost immediately.” (D) directly contradicts the described regional variation tied to subsidy changes.","stem":"What appears to be a simple story of consumer preference shifting toward electric vehicles is, on closer inspection, substantially shaped by shifting government incentives — in regions where subsidies were recently reduced, adoption rates flattened almost immediately, suggesting policy, not preference alone, is doing much of the work.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Consumer preference alone, independent of any policy factors, fully explains the shift toward electric vehicles."},{"k":"B","t":"Government incentives appear to significantly influence EV adoption rates, given that adoption flattened quickly where subsidies were reduced."},{"k":"C","t":"Subsidy reductions had no observable effect on electric vehicle adoption rates in any region."},{"k":"D","t":"Electric vehicle adoption has increased uniformly across all regions regardless of policy changes."}]},{"id":"ps-99","topic":"ps","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Full Mixed Review","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures the paragraph’s precise, corrective framing of what actually happened. (A) directly contradicts “reach agreement on the majority of clauses.” (C) directly contradicts “may revisit separately.” (D) directly contradicts the described stalling on one specific provision.","stem":"It would be misleading to say the negotiations “failed,” given that both sides did reach agreement on the majority of clauses; a more accurate description is that talks stalled specifically over a single, narrow provision regarding intellectual property licensing, which both sides have indicated they may revisit separately.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The negotiations failed completely, with no agreement reached on any provision."},{"k":"B","t":"While most clauses were agreed upon, talks stalled specifically on one narrow provision, which both sides may revisit separately."},{"k":"C","t":"The dispute over intellectual property licensing has been permanently and irreversibly abandoned by both sides."},{"k":"D","t":"Both sides reached full and complete agreement on every single clause under discussion."}]},{"id":"ps-100","topic":"ps","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Full Mixed Review","src":"doc","type":"mcq","answer":"B","explanation":"Accurately captures the paragraph’s specific reframing based on the cited roster data. (A) and (D) restate the “popular narrative” the paragraph explicitly complicates. (C) directly reverses the described roster advantage (younger and faster, not older and slower).","stem":"The popular narrative credits the coach’s tactical genius for the team’s championship run, but a closer look at the roster reveals a simpler explanation: the team’s core players were, on average, four years younger and statistically faster than every other team in the league that season — an advantage that tactics alone cannot fully explain away.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The coach’s tactical decisions were the sole and complete reason for the championship win."},{"k":"B","t":"A closer look suggests the team’s underlying roster advantages in age and speed, not tactics alone, offer a simpler explanation for the championship run."},{"k":"C","t":"The team’s roster was, on average, older and slower than every other team in the league."},{"k":"D","t":"No factors other than coaching tactics contributed to the team’s success in any way."}]},{"id":"ooo-1","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Off-Topic Tangent Within a Shared Broad Subject","src":"doc","sentences":["Bees communicate the location of food sources to other members of the hive through a precise, repeatable movement pattern.","This “waggle dance” encodes both direction and distance relative to the sun’s position.","Honey has been used in wound care for centuries due to its antibacterial properties.","Researchers have successfully decoded the dance’s basic grammar using robotic bee models.","The angle of the dance relative to vertical indicates the direction other bees should fly."],"type":"tita","answer":"3","explanation":"The paragraph is specifically about the waggle dance as a communication system; sentence 3 pivots to an unrelated fact about honey’s medicinal use, sharing only the broad topic of bees.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-2","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Off-Topic Tangent Within a Shared Broad Subject","src":"doc","sentences":["The Antarctic ice sheet has been losing mass at an accelerating rate over the past two decades.","Satellite gravity measurements allow scientists to track this loss with remarkable precision.","Antarctica is technically classified as a desert due to its extremely low annual precipitation.","Much of the loss is concentrated in a handful of glaciers along the West Antarctic coast.","Continued loss at current rates would contribute significantly to global sea level rise."],"type":"tita","answer":"3","explanation":"The paragraph tracks ice mass loss and its measurement/consequences; sentence 3 is a true but unrelated classification fact about Antarctica’s climate type, not about the ice sheet’s mass changes.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-3","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Off-Topic Tangent Within a Shared Broad Subject","src":"doc","sentences":["The novel’s protagonist narrates events almost a decade after they occurred, coloring every memory with hindsight.","This unreliable, retrospective narration is central to the book’s exploration of how memory distorts truth.","The author spent several years researching the historical period the novel is set in.","Key details of the plot shift subtly each time the protagonist revisits them.","Critics have specifically praised this narrative technique as the novel’s defining achievement."],"type":"tita","answer":"3","explanation":"The paragraph is about the narrative technique and its thematic effect; sentence 3 is an unrelated fact about the author’s research process, not about the narration style itself.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-4","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Off-Topic Tangent Within a Shared Broad Subject","src":"doc","sentences":["Coffee cultivation requires specific conditions: consistent rainfall, moderate temperatures, and shaded, high-altitude terrain.","These narrow requirements mean climate change is already shrinking the land suitable for growing it.","Coffee is the second most heavily traded commodity in the world by some estimates.","Farmers in traditional growing regions are being forced to move to higher, cooler elevations.","Some researchers are experimenting with more heat-tolerant coffee varieties as a longer-term solution."],"type":"tita","answer":"3","explanation":"The paragraph is about coffee’s climate vulnerability and adaptation; sentence 3 is an unrelated trade-statistics fact that doesn’t address the growing-conditions theme.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-5","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Off-Topic Tangent Within a Shared Broad Subject","src":"doc","sentences":["The city’s subway system was originally designed to carry a fraction of its current daily ridership.","Decades of population growth have pushed the system well past its intended capacity.","The subway map itself is considered a landmark of graphic design, still studied by designers today.","Peak-hour overcrowding has become a routine and significant source of commuter frustration.","Planners are now debating costly expansions that were never part of the system’s original design."],"type":"tita","answer":"3","explanation":"The paragraph is about capacity strain and its consequences; sentence 3 is an unrelated aesthetic/design-history fact about the map, not about ridership or capacity.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-6","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Off-Topic Tangent Within a Shared Broad Subject","src":"doc","sentences":["Octopuses possess three hearts, two of which pump blood specifically to the gills.","Their blood is blue rather than red because it’s based on copper, not iron.","Octopuses are considered among the most intelligent invertebrates, capable of solving complex puzzles.","The third heart stops beating when the octopus swims, which is one reason they prefer crawling.","This unusual circulatory system is highly efficient at low temperatures and low oxygen levels."],"type":"tita","answer":"3","explanation":"The paragraph is specifically about the circulatory system’s structure and function; sentence 3 pivots to an unrelated claim about intelligence, sharing only the broad topic of octopuses.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-7","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Off-Topic Tangent Within a Shared Broad Subject","src":"doc","sentences":["Venice’s historic buildings rest on wooden piles driven deep into the lagoon’s soft clay and sand.","Remarkably, these submerged wooden foundations have remained stable for centuries due to a lack of oxygen underwater, which prevents rot.","The city relies heavily on tourism, which accounts for a significant share of its local economy.","Rising sea levels now threaten the very foundations that have held for so long.","Engineers have proposed several ambitious flood-barrier systems to protect the city’s foundations."],"type":"tita","answer":"3","explanation":"The paragraph is about the foundations’ engineering and their vulnerability; sentence 3 is an unrelated economic fact about tourism, not about the structural foundations.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-8","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Off-Topic Tangent Within a Shared Broad Subject","src":"doc","sentences":["Migratory birds rely on the Earth’s magnetic field, among other cues, to navigate over vast distances.","Specialized proteins in their eyes are thought to allow them to visually perceive this magnetic field.","Many bird species also use stars and the position of the sun as navigational references.","Bird migration patterns have shifted measurably in recent decades due to changing climate conditions.","Researchers continue to debate the exact mechanism behind this magnetic sensing ability."],"type":"tita","answer":"4","explanation":"The paragraph is specifically about the mechanism of magnetic-field navigation; sentence 4 pivots to an unrelated point about migration pattern shifts due to climate, not about the navigation mechanism itself.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-9","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Off-Topic Tangent Within a Shared Broad Subject","src":"doc","sentences":["Mount Everest’s summit height is recalculated periodically as measurement technology improves.","The mountain continues to grow slowly due to ongoing tectonic activity beneath the Himalayas.","Everest was first successfully summited in 1953 by Edmund Hillary and Tenzing Norgay.","Snowfall and glacial ice at the summit can also affect measured height from year to year.","The most recent joint survey by Nepal and China aimed to settle years of measurement disputes."],"type":"tita","answer":"3","explanation":"The paragraph is about the mountain’s measured height and why it changes; sentence 3 is an unrelated historical fact about the first ascent, not about height measurement.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-10","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Off-Topic Tangent Within a Shared Broad Subject","src":"doc","sentences":["Origami-inspired folding patterns are increasingly used in engineering to design compact, deployable structures.","Satellites now use similar folding principles to pack large solar panels into small launch spaces.","Traditional origami originated in Japan and has a documented history spanning several centuries.","Medical devices, such as expandable stents, borrow the same underlying folding mathematics.","Researchers say the approach could soon extend to folding architecture for emergency shelters."],"type":"tita","answer":"3","explanation":"The paragraph is about engineering applications of origami folding principles; sentence 3 is an unrelated historical/cultural fact about origami’s origins, not about its engineering applications.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-11","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Unsignaled Contradiction","src":"doc","sentences":["The city’s new bus rapid transit line was projected to cut average commute times by nearly a third.","Early ridership data suggests commute times have indeed fallen close to projected levels.","Survey respondents report significantly higher satisfaction with public transit since the line opened.","Several transit advocates have criticized the project as poorly planned and unlikely to succeed.","Officials now plan to extend the rapid transit model to two additional corridors next year."],"type":"tita","answer":"4","explanation":"The paragraph builds a consistent narrative of the project’s measured success (data, satisfaction, expansion); sentence 4 introduces an unsignaled negative claim that contradicts this direction without any transition acknowledging the shift.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-12","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Unsignaled Contradiction","src":"doc","sentences":["The company’s new CEO inherited a business with declining sales and low employee morale.","Within a year, she had restructured three major departments and cut overhead costs sharply.","Employee surveys since the restructuring show significantly improved satisfaction and retention.","Analysts increasingly doubt the company can recover from years of poor strategic decisions.","Quarterly revenue has grown for four consecutive quarters under her leadership."],"type":"tita","answer":"4","explanation":"The paragraph traces a consistent turnaround narrative; sentence 4 injects an unsignaled, contradicting pessimistic claim with no acknowledgment of the positive trend the other sentences establish.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-13","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Unsignaled Contradiction","src":"doc","sentences":["The new curriculum places heavy emphasis on project-based, hands-on learning over rote memorization.","Teachers report students engaging more actively with material under the new approach.","Standardized test scores under the new curriculum have remained essentially unchanged.","Most parents surveyed say their children now seem more excited about school than before.","Rote memorization has been shown in numerous studies to have no lasting educational value whatsoever."],"type":"tita","answer":"5","explanation":"The paragraph is a measured account of one curriculum’s specific, mixed outcomes; sentence 5 makes a sweeping, absolute claim about rote memorization in general that neither fits the paragraph’s cautious tone nor its specific focus on this particular curriculum’s results.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-14","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Unsignaled Contradiction","src":"doc","sentences":["The startup’s founders spent eighteen months building a product few investors initially believed in.","A single well-timed demo at an industry conference changed the trajectory of the entire company.","Within weeks, the founders had secured funding offers from several major venture firms.","Most startups that struggle to secure early funding ultimately fail within their first two years.","The company used its new funding to double its engineering team within six months."],"type":"tita","answer":"4","explanation":"The paragraph is a specific success narrative building toward a positive turning point; sentence 4 introduces an unrelated, generalized statistical claim that sits oddly against the specific, ultimately successful story the other four sentences tell.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-15","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Unsignaled Contradiction","src":"doc","sentences":["The museum’s decision to waive admission fees for local residents was initially controversial among board members.","Attendance from local residents more than tripled within the first six months of the policy.","Revenue from gift shop and café sales also rose significantly during the same period.","Museum attendance nationwide has been steadily declining for over a decade.","The board has since voted to make the free-admission policy permanent."],"type":"tita","answer":"4","explanation":"The paragraph traces a specific, successful policy outcome at one museum; sentence 4 introduces an unrelated broader statistic about nationwide trends that doesn’t fit the specific, positive local narrative being built.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-16","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Unsignaled Contradiction","src":"doc","sentences":["The pilot program equipped a dozen classrooms with real-time air quality monitors.","Teachers used the data to adjust ventilation and reduce pollutant exposure during lessons.","Student attendance in monitored classrooms improved measurably over the semester.","Air quality monitoring technology has become significantly cheaper over the past decade.","Reported instances of asthma-related absences dropped by nearly a third in the same classrooms."],"type":"tita","answer":"4","explanation":"The paragraph tracks the specific outcomes of this pilot program; sentence 4 is a general, tangential fact about monitoring technology costs, not about the program’s classroom-level outcomes.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-17","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Unsignaled Contradiction","src":"doc","sentences":["The nonprofit’s microloan program provides small, interest-free loans to first-time entrepreneurs.","Recipients are paired with a volunteer mentor for the loan’s full repayment period.","Repayment rates under the program have consistently exceeded ninety-five percent.","Most first-time business owners fail within their first eighteen months of operation.","Several past recipients have gone on to mentor newer entrants to the program themselves."],"type":"tita","answer":"4","explanation":"The paragraph is a specific, positive account of this program’s structure and outcomes; sentence 4 introduces an unrelated, generalized statistic about business failure rates that sits in unsignaled tension with the program’s described success.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-18","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Unsignaled Contradiction","src":"doc","sentences":["The library’s extended weekend hours were introduced on a trial basis last spring.","Weekend foot traffic increased by nearly sixty percent compared to the previous year.","Staff reported that the extended hours required significant schedule adjustments and new hires.","Public libraries nationwide have seen funding cuts in many regions over the past several years.","The trial was made permanent after a survey found overwhelming patron support."],"type":"tita","answer":"4","explanation":"The paragraph is a specific account of one library’s successful trial; sentence 4 introduces an unrelated national funding trend that doesn’t connect to the specific outcomes of this trial.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-19","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Unsignaled Contradiction","src":"doc","sentences":["The vaccine’s developers used a novel delivery method never before approved for public use.","Clinical trials found the method produced a stronger immune response than traditional approaches.","Regulatory approval took significantly longer due to the delivery method’s novelty.","Most vaccine development timelines have shortened considerably over the past two decades.","The vaccine has since become a model for how the same delivery method might be applied elsewhere."],"type":"tita","answer":"4","explanation":"The paragraph tracks the specific development and approval story of this one vaccine; sentence 4 introduces an unrelated general industry trend that doesn’t connect to this specific vaccine’s longer-than-usual approval timeline.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-20","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Unsignaled Contradiction","src":"doc","sentences":["The community garden was established on a vacant lot that had sat unused for over a decade.","Volunteers now maintain more than forty individual plots across the site.","A portion of each harvest is donated to a local food bank every season.","Urban vacant lots are increasingly being redeveloped into commercial real estate nationwide.","The garden has become a regular gathering space for neighborhood events and workshops."],"type":"tita","answer":"4","explanation":"The paragraph describes this specific garden’s establishment and community role; sentence 4 introduces an unrelated, and mildly contradictory, general trend about vacant lots being redeveloped commercially rather than turned into gardens.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-21","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Redundancy","src":"doc","sentences":["The new water treatment facility can process nearly twice the volume of its predecessor.","Its expanded capacity allows it to handle roughly double the water volume of the old plant.","Construction took just under three years, slightly ahead of the original schedule.","The facility uses an advanced filtration process that removes a wider range of contaminants.","Local officials say the upgrade will comfortably meet the region’s needs for the next two decades."],"type":"tita","answer":"2","explanation":"Sentence 2 restates the exact same capacity point already made in sentence 1 in nearly identical words, adding no new information and disrupting the paragraph’s otherwise varied progression of distinct facts.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-22","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Redundancy","src":"doc","sentences":["The film’s score was composed entirely using instruments built from recycled materials.","This unconventional approach gave the soundtrack a distinctly raw, unpolished texture.","Critics have singled out the score as one of the film’s most memorable elements.","The composer built each instrument from recycled materials specifically for this project.","The director has said the score’s unusual texture directly shaped several key scenes."],"type":"tita","answer":"4","explanation":"Sentence 4 essentially restates the recycled-instruments fact already established in sentence 1, adding little beyond repetition and breaking the paragraph’s forward progression toward the score’s reception and influence.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-23","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Redundancy","src":"doc","sentences":["The bridge’s cables are inspected annually using a combination of visual checks and sensor data.","Engineers rely on both visual inspection and embedded sensors to assess the cables each year.","Any cable showing signs of unusual stress is flagged for more detailed follow-up testing.","The inspection process typically takes engineering teams about six weeks to complete.","Results from each year’s inspection are compared against a decade of historical data."],"type":"tita","answer":"2","explanation":"Sentence 2 repeats the annual visual-plus-sensor inspection method already described in sentence 1 in different words, without advancing the paragraph’s account of the inspection process.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-24","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Redundancy","src":"doc","sentences":["The orchestra’s new hall was designed with acoustics as the primary architectural priority.","Every design decision, architects say, was made with the hall’s sound quality as the top concern.","Suspended panels above the stage can be adjusted to fine-tune sound reflection for different performances.","Musicians have praised the hall as one of the best-sounding venues they have performed in.","The hall’s seating capacity is notably smaller than the orchestra’s previous venue."],"type":"tita","answer":"2","explanation":"Sentence 2 restates the acoustics-as-priority point from sentence 1 almost word for word, adding nothing new to the paragraph’s account of specific design features and reception.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-25","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Redundancy","src":"doc","sentences":["The app’s new privacy settings allow users to control exactly which data is shared with advertisers.","Users can now precisely control what information advertisers receive through the new settings.","Early adoption of the new settings has been higher than the company anticipated.","Privacy advocates have generally welcomed the change as a meaningful step forward.","The company says further privacy controls are planned for a future update."],"type":"tita","answer":"2","explanation":"Sentence 2 repeats the same point as sentence 1 about user control over advertiser data sharing, contributing no new information to the paragraph.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-26","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Redundancy","src":"doc","sentences":["The recipe’s use of fermented ingredients gives the dish its distinctive tangy flavor profile.","Fermentation is what gives this particular dish its characteristic tangy taste.","The technique has been part of the region’s culinary tradition for generations.","Modern chefs have begun experimenting with faster fermentation methods to speed up preparation.","Traditionalists argue the shortcuts compromise the depth of flavor the slow process produces."],"type":"tita","answer":"2","explanation":"Sentence 2 restates the fermentation-to-tanginess link already made in sentence 1, adding nothing new before the paragraph moves into tradition and modern adaptation.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-27","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Redundancy","src":"doc","sentences":["The satellite’s new imaging sensor can detect changes in vegetation health invisible to the human eye.","This heightened sensitivity allows researchers to spot early signs of drought stress in crops.","The sensor can pick up on plant stress that would otherwise go completely unnoticed visually.","Farmers using the data have reported adjusting irrigation schedules earlier than in previous years.","Several agricultural agencies are now piloting the technology across multiple regions."],"type":"tita","answer":"3","explanation":"Sentence 3 restates the same point already made in sentence 1 about detecting invisible plant stress, contributing nothing beyond what’s already established before the paragraph moves to real-world application.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-28","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Redundancy","src":"doc","sentences":["The startup’s onboarding process was redesigned to reduce the number of steps from twelve to four.","New users can now complete signup in under two minutes, down from nearly ten.","The redesign cut the onboarding process down from twelve steps to just four.","User drop-off during signup has fallen sharply since the redesign was implemented.","The design team says further simplification is being tested for a future release."],"type":"tita","answer":"3","explanation":"Sentence 3 restates the exact twelve-to-four step reduction already stated in sentence 1, adding no new information to the paragraph’s account of the redesign’s effects.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-29","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Redundancy","src":"doc","sentences":["The new highway bypass was built specifically to reduce truck traffic through the town center.","Since opening, noise complaints from residents along the old route have dropped significantly.","The bypass route was designed to divert truck traffic away from the town’s central streets.","Local shop owners report a modest increase in foot traffic since the bypass opened.","Officials credit the bypass with improving both safety and quality of life downtown."],"type":"tita","answer":"3","explanation":"Sentence 3 restates the bypass’s stated purpose already given in sentence 1, adding nothing new before the paragraph moves into its actual, measured effects.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-30","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"easy","cluster":"Redundancy","src":"doc","sentences":["The clinic’s new scheduling software automatically fills last-minute cancellations from a waitlist.","Empty appointment slots from cancellations are now automatically filled using a waitlist system.","Average time from booking to appointment has fallen by nearly a week since implementation.","Patient satisfaction scores related to scheduling have risen noticeably in the same period.","The clinic plans to roll out the same software across its two other locations next year."],"type":"tita","answer":"2","explanation":"Sentence 2 restates the automatic cancellation-filling feature already described in sentence 1, adding no new information before the paragraph moves into measured outcomes.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-31","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Mismatched Scope (Too General or Too Specific)","src":"doc","sentences":["A specific strain of gut bacteria was found to break down a common plastic additive in laboratory conditions.","Researchers isolated the strain from soil samples collected near an industrial waste site.","Bacteria play an essential role in ecosystems ranging from soil formation to digestion in animals.","The strain’s enzyme was found to degrade the additive nearly twice as fast as previously known methods.","Scientists are now testing whether the process can be scaled up for industrial waste treatment."],"type":"tita","answer":"3","explanation":"The paragraph is built entirely from specific facts about this one bacterial strain and its discovery; sentence 3 shifts to an extremely broad, general statement about bacteria’s ecological role that doesn’t match the paragraph’s specific focus.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-32","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Mismatched Scope (Too General or Too Specific)","src":"doc","sentences":["The startup’s founder decided early on to keep the company entirely self-funded, rejecting all outside investment offers.","This decision meant slower growth in the company’s first three years compared to funded competitors.","Businesses generally grow faster when they accept external investment rather than relying solely on revenue.","By year five, the company had become profitable without ever diluting the founder’s ownership stake.","The founder has since become a vocal advocate for bootstrapped business models within her industry."],"type":"tita","answer":"3","explanation":"The paragraph is a specific account of one founder’s choices and their specific consequences; sentence 3 is a broad general claim about businesses in general that doesn’t fit among the surrounding specific, individual facts.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-33","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Mismatched Scope (Too General or Too Specific)","src":"doc","sentences":["The city’s flood barrier was engineered to withstand a storm surge of up to four meters.","Coastal cities around the world face increasing risk from rising sea levels and storm intensity.","Sensors embedded along the barrier continuously monitor water pressure and structural stress.","The barrier can be raised into position within twenty minutes of an activation order.","Its design was based on a similar system used successfully in the Netherlands for decades."],"type":"tita","answer":"2","explanation":"The paragraph consists of specific technical facts about this one barrier’s design and function; sentence 2 is a broad general statement about coastal cities worldwide that doesn’t match the paragraph’s specific, technical focus.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-34","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Mismatched Scope (Too General or Too Specific)","src":"doc","sentences":["The professor’s grading rubric assigns specific point values to five distinct essay components.","Clarity of argument accounts for forty percent of the total score under this rubric.","Good writing is generally considered essential across nearly every academic discipline.","Students receive detailed written feedback on each of the five components separately.","The rubric has been adopted by two other departments since its introduction three years ago."],"type":"tita","answer":"3","explanation":"The paragraph is a specific account of one particular grading rubric’s structure; sentence 3 is a broad, generic claim about writing’s importance that doesn’t match the paragraph’s specific, structural focus.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-35","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Mismatched Scope (Too General or Too Specific)","src":"doc","sentences":["A particular species of deep-sea fish generates its own light through a chemical reaction in specialized organs.","Bioluminescence has evolved independently multiple times across many different, unrelated species.","This species uses the light specifically to lure smaller prey close enough to strike.","Researchers recently mapped the exact chemical pathway responsible for the light production.","The same mechanism, when studied further, may have applications in low-energy lighting technology."],"type":"tita","answer":"2","explanation":"The paragraph is built from specific facts about this one species’ light-generating mechanism and its uses; sentence 2 shifts to a broad, general statement about bioluminescence across many species that doesn’t match the specific focus.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-36","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Mismatched Scope (Too General or Too Specific)","src":"doc","sentences":["The regional airline’s new pricing model charges based on real-time seat demand rather than fixed fare classes.","Airlines in general have used various forms of dynamic pricing for decades.","Under the new model, prices can change several times within a single hour for the same flight.","Passenger complaints about pricing transparency have risen noticeably since the model was introduced.","The airline says it is reviewing the model’s communication to passengers in response to the complaints."],"type":"tita","answer":"2","explanation":"The paragraph tracks this specific airline’s specific new model and its specific consequences; sentence 2 is a broad general statement about the airline industry that doesn’t match the paragraph’s narrow, specific focus.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-37","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Mismatched Scope (Too General or Too Specific)","src":"doc","sentences":["The archive’s newly digitized collection includes over two hundred thousand individual photographs.","Historical archives are considered essential resources for researchers studying the past.","Metadata for each photograph includes the date, location, and photographer where known.","Researchers can now search the collection by keyword rather than browsing physical folders.","The digitization project was funded primarily through a grant from a national heritage foundation."],"type":"tita","answer":"2","explanation":"The paragraph consists of specific facts about this one archive’s digitization; sentence 2 is a broad, generic statement about archives in general that doesn’t fit among the surrounding specific details.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-38","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Mismatched Scope (Too General or Too Specific)","src":"doc","sentences":["The startup’s app uses a specific algorithm to match freelance workers with short-term local jobs.","Technology has transformed how people find work in countless industries over the past decade.","The algorithm weighs proximity, skill match, and past ratings to rank potential matches.","Average time from job posting to acceptance has fallen to under ten minutes on the platform.","The company plans to expand the algorithm’s matching criteria to include availability windows next."],"type":"tita","answer":"2","explanation":"The paragraph is a specific, technical account of this one app’s matching algorithm; sentence 2 is a broad, generic claim about technology’s effect on work that doesn’t match the paragraph’s narrow focus.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-39","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Mismatched Scope (Too General or Too Specific)","src":"doc","sentences":["The bakery’s sourdough starter has been maintained continuously for over forty years.","Sourdough bread has experienced a significant resurgence in popularity over the past decade.","The starter is fed twice daily with a specific ratio of flour and water.","Regular customers say the bread’s flavor has remained remarkably consistent over the decades.","The current head baker learned to maintain the starter from her predecessor over a two-year apprenticeship."],"type":"tita","answer":"2","explanation":"The paragraph is built from specific facts about this one bakery’s specific starter; sentence 2 is a broad, general statement about a food trend that doesn’t match the paragraph’s narrow, specific focus.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-40","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Mismatched Scope (Too General or Too Specific)","src":"doc","sentences":["The observatory’s new telescope uses adaptive optics to correct for atmospheric distortion in real time.","Ground-based telescopes generally face significant limitations due to Earth’s atmosphere.","The correction system adjusts its mirror shape thousands of times per second.","This allows the telescope to achieve image clarity comparable to space-based instruments.","Astronomers using the telescope have already identified several previously undetectable exoplanets."],"type":"tita","answer":"2","explanation":"The paragraph is a specific, technical account of this one telescope’s adaptive optics system; sentence 2 is a broad, general statement about ground-based telescopes that doesn’t match the paragraph’s narrow, specific focus.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-41","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Shifting the Argument’s Direction","src":"doc","sentences":["Proponents of the new zoning reform argue it will significantly increase the city’s housing supply.","Early data from similar reforms in other cities shows measurable increases in new housing permits.","The reform also includes provisions requiring a percentage of new units to be designated affordable.","Construction costs have risen sharply across the industry due to persistent supply chain issues.","Officials expect these combined provisions to ease the city’s ongoing housing shortage over time."],"type":"tita","answer":"4","explanation":"The paragraph builds a consistent case for the reform’s specific mechanisms and expected effects; sentence 4 shifts to an unrelated, broader industry trend (construction costs) that isn’t tied back to the reform’s argument.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-42","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Shifting the Argument’s Direction","src":"doc","sentences":["The vaccine campaign specifically targeted rural communities with historically low immunization rates.","Mobile clinics were deployed to reach villages without permanent healthcare facilities.","Community health workers, trained locally, played a central role in building trust with residents.","Vaccine hesitancy remains a significant global public health challenge in many different contexts.","Immunization rates in the targeted communities rose by over forty percentage points within a year."],"type":"tita","answer":"4","explanation":"The paragraph is a specific account of this one campaign’s methods and results; sentence 4 shifts to a broad, general statement about a different topic (global hesitancy) that isn’t the paragraph’s specific focus.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-43","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Shifting the Argument’s Direction","src":"doc","sentences":["The publisher’s decision to release the novel exclusively as an audiobook first was widely seen as a risky experiment.","Sales figures suggest the experiment paid off, with the audiobook significantly outselling initial projections.","Audiobooks as a format have grown substantially as a share of overall book sales in recent years.","The novel’s traditional print edition, released three months later, also performed well upon release.","The publisher says it plans to test the audiobook-first strategy with two more titles next year."],"type":"tita","answer":"3","explanation":"The paragraph traces the specific outcome and follow-up of this one publisher’s specific experiment; sentence 3 shifts to a broader industry statistic that isn’t the paragraph’s specific focus on this one book’s release strategy.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-44","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Shifting the Argument’s Direction","src":"doc","sentences":["The research team’s initial hypothesis was that the drug primarily affected a single receptor type.","Follow-up experiments revealed the drug also interacts with two previously unstudied receptor pathways.","This unexpected finding significantly complicated the team’s original model of how the drug works.","Drug development timelines have lengthened considerably across the pharmaceutical industry in recent years.","The team is now revising its research approach to account for the newly discovered pathways."],"type":"tita","answer":"4","explanation":"The paragraph traces this specific team’s specific findings and their response; sentence 4 shifts to an unrelated industry-wide trend that doesn’t connect to this team’s particular research trajectory.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-45","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Shifting the Argument’s Direction","src":"doc","sentences":["The city council’s proposal would convert a disused rail corridor into a linear public park.","Supporters argue the project would connect several currently disconnected neighborhoods.","Similar rail-to-park conversions in other cities have significantly increased nearby property values.","Public parks are generally associated with improved community health outcomes.","A public vote on the proposal’s funding is scheduled for later this year."],"type":"tita","answer":"4","explanation":"The paragraph builds a specific case for this particular project’s specific expected benefits; sentence 4 shifts to a broad, generic claim about parks in general that isn’t tied to this specific project’s argument.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-46","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Shifting the Argument’s Direction","src":"doc","sentences":["The negotiation team spent the first two days establishing shared, non-controversial priorities.","This deliberate sequencing was designed to build trust before addressing the more contentious clauses.","International negotiations often stall when contentious issues are raised too early in the process.","By the third day, both sides had moved on to discussing the disputed territorial boundary.","The strategy is credited with helping both sides remain at the table through difficult later sessions."],"type":"tita","answer":"3","explanation":"The paragraph traces this specific negotiation’s specific sequencing strategy and outcome; sentence 3 shifts to a general statement about negotiations broadly, breaking from the paragraph’s focus on this particular case.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-47","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Shifting the Argument’s Direction","src":"doc","sentences":["The film studio’s decision to shoot entirely on location, rather than in a studio, added significantly to the budget.","Directors have often argued that location shooting produces a more authentic feel than studio sets.","Reviewers have specifically praised the film’s setting as central to its emotional impact.","The studio has said the added cost was worth the creative result the director achieved.","Box office returns have so far exceeded the studio’s original projections."],"type":"tita","answer":"2","explanation":"The paragraph traces this specific film’s specific choices and their specific outcomes; sentence 2 shifts to a general claim about directors’ opinions broadly, which isn’t the paragraph’s specific focus on this one production.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-48","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Shifting the Argument’s Direction","src":"doc","sentences":["The hospital’s new triage software prioritizes patients using a weighted combination of vital signs.","Emergency room wait times for critical patients have fallen by nearly a quarter since implementation.","Hospital emergency rooms nationwide continue to struggle with overcrowding and long wait times.","Staff say the software has also reduced the cognitive burden of manual triage decisions.","The hospital plans to share its triage model with two partner hospitals in the region."],"type":"tita","answer":"3","explanation":"The paragraph tracks this specific hospital’s specific software and its specific results; sentence 3 shifts to a broad, general statement about hospitals nationwide that isn’t tied to this hospital’s specific story.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-49","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Shifting the Argument’s Direction","src":"doc","sentences":["The orchestra’s outreach program brings free performances to schools with no music curriculum.","Music education has been shown to correlate with improved outcomes in other academic subjects.","Over the program’s five years, more than fifteen thousand students have attended a performance.","Several participating schools have since started their own small music programs with local support.","The orchestra credits the program with helping build a more diverse audience for its main concert hall."],"type":"tita","answer":"2","explanation":"The paragraph is a specific account of this one program’s reach and outcomes; sentence 2 shifts to a broad, general research claim about music education that isn’t tied to the specific program being described.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-50","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Shifting the Argument’s Direction","src":"doc","sentences":["The farm switched to a no-till planting method to reduce soil erosion on its sloped fields.","Yields in the first two seasons after the switch were slightly lower than under the previous method.","Soil health, measured by organic matter content, improved steadily over the same period.","Sustainable farming practices have gained significant attention from policymakers worldwide.","By the fourth season, yields had recovered and, in some fields, exceeded pre-switch levels."],"type":"tita","answer":"4","explanation":"The paragraph traces this specific farm’s specific experience over several seasons; sentence 4 shifts to a broad, general statement about policy trends that isn’t tied to this farm’s particular story.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-51","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Wrong Register or Purpose","src":"doc","sentences":["The quarterly report details a fifteen percent increase in operating expenses across all divisions.","Rising material costs accounted for roughly half of the total increase.","Honestly, it’s been a rough few months for everyone trying to keep budgets under control.","Labor costs rose more modestly, reflecting planned wage adjustments announced earlier in the year.","Management has outlined three specific measures aimed at controlling costs in the coming quarter."],"type":"tita","answer":"3","explanation":"The paragraph maintains a formal, data-driven reporting register throughout; sentence 3 breaks into casual, informal, first-person commentary that doesn’t match the surrounding factual, analytical tone.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-52","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Wrong Register or Purpose","src":"doc","sentences":["The peer-reviewed study analyzed outcomes across a sample of over three thousand participants.","Researchers controlled for age, income, and pre-existing health conditions in their analysis.","Honestly, anyone could have guessed the results before the study even started.","The primary finding showed a statistically significant, though modest, effect size.","The authors recommend further research with a more geographically diverse sample."],"type":"tita","answer":"3","explanation":"The paragraph maintains a formal, academic register throughout; sentence 3 injects a casual, dismissive, first-person opinion that clashes with the surrounding methodical, evidence-based tone.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-53","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Wrong Register or Purpose","src":"doc","sentences":["The court’s ruling clarified the specific legal standard for evaluating similar future cases.","Legal scholars have described the decision as a significant, if narrow, shift in precedent.","Wow, this is honestly going to change everything for so many people, it’s kind of amazing.","The ruling directly affects three ongoing cases currently before lower courts.","Appeals of the decision are expected, though legal experts consider them unlikely to succeed."],"type":"tita","answer":"3","explanation":"The paragraph maintains a formal, analytical legal register throughout; sentence 3 breaks into casual, exclamatory, informal commentary that doesn’t fit the surrounding measured tone.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-54","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Wrong Register or Purpose","src":"doc","sentences":["The engineering team completed the bridge’s structural stress tests two weeks ahead of schedule.","All test results fell comfortably within the safety margins required by regulatory standards.","I mean, honestly, bridges are just really cool feats of engineering when you think about it.","A final inspection is scheduled before the bridge opens to public traffic next month.","The project remained within its approved budget throughout the testing phase."],"type":"tita","answer":"3","explanation":"The paragraph maintains a formal, technical, factual register throughout; sentence 3 injects casual, conversational commentary that doesn’t match the surrounding precise, procedural tone.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-55","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Wrong Register or Purpose","src":"doc","sentences":["The central bank’s policy statement signaled a cautious approach to future interest rate changes.","Analysts interpreted the language as leaving the door open to further adjustments if needed.","Basically nobody really knows what’s going to happen with the economy at this point, honestly.","Bond markets reacted with relatively modest movement following the announcement.","The next policy meeting is scheduled for early next quarter."],"type":"tita","answer":"3","explanation":"The paragraph maintains a formal, analytical economic register throughout; sentence 3 breaks into casual, informal, opinion-based commentary that clashes with the surrounding precise, measured tone.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-56","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Wrong Register or Purpose","src":"doc","sentences":["The archaeological team catalogued each artifact according to a strict, standardized protocol.","Every item was photographed, measured, and cross-referenced against the site’s excavation grid.","It’s honestly wild to think people were using tools like this thousands of years ago.","The full catalogue will be made available to researchers through an open digital archive.","Preliminary analysis suggests the site was occupied continuously for several centuries."],"type":"tita","answer":"3","explanation":"The paragraph maintains a formal, procedural, scientific register throughout; sentence 3 injects casual, informal, first-person wonder that doesn’t fit the surrounding methodical documentation tone.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-57","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Wrong Register or Purpose","src":"doc","sentences":["The committee’s final report recommends three specific changes to the current oversight framework.","Each recommendation includes a projected implementation timeline and estimated cost.","Look, at the end of the day, somebody just needs to actually get this done already.","The report will be formally presented to the full board at its next scheduled session.","Board members will vote on each recommendation individually rather than as a single package."],"type":"tita","answer":"3","explanation":"The paragraph maintains a formal, procedural, institutional register throughout; sentence 3 breaks into casual, frustrated, conversational commentary that clashes with the surrounding formal tone.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-58","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Wrong Register or Purpose","src":"doc","sentences":["The satellite’s onboard instruments recorded atmospheric composition data continuously for six months.","Scientists cross-referenced the readings against ground-based measurement stations for accuracy.","Space is just endlessly fascinating, honestly, there’s always something new to discover up there.","The data revealed previously undetected seasonal variation in a specific trace gas.","Findings from the mission have been submitted for publication in a peer-reviewed journal."],"type":"tita","answer":"3","explanation":"The paragraph maintains a formal, technical, scientific register throughout; sentence 3 injects casual, informal enthusiasm that doesn’t match the surrounding precise, data-focused tone.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-59","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Wrong Register or Purpose","src":"doc","sentences":["The manufacturing plant implemented a new quality control checklist following last year’s recall.","Each unit now undergoes seven distinct inspection stages before leaving the factory floor.","Defect rates have fallen to less than a tenth of what they were before the changes.","Honestly, recalls are just the worst thing that can happen to a company’s reputation.","The plant’s quality manager credits the new protocol with restoring client confidence."],"type":"tita","answer":"4","explanation":"The paragraph maintains a formal, procedural, factual register throughout; sentence 4 breaks into casual, generalized, opinion-based commentary that clashes with the surrounding specific, measured tone.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-60","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"Wrong Register or Purpose","src":"doc","sentences":["The university’s endowment committee released its annual investment performance summary this week.","Returns for the fiscal year exceeded the committee’s own benchmark by a modest margin.","Diversification across asset classes was cited as a key factor in the year’s stability.","Money is honestly just such a stressful thing to think about, especially for a university.","The committee outlined its investment strategy for the coming fiscal year in the same report."],"type":"tita","answer":"4","explanation":"The paragraph maintains a formal, financial reporting register throughout; sentence 4 injects casual, informal, unrelated commentary that clashes with the surrounding precise, institutional tone.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-61","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"True but Irrelevant to the Specific Angle","src":"doc","sentences":["The paragraph’s focus: why a particular airline’s on-time performance improved sharply last quarter.","The airline reassigned ground crew scheduling to a new predictive software system.","The new system anticipates delays before they occur based on historical weather and traffic patterns.","The airline was founded over thirty years ago as a small regional carrier.","On-time departures rose from seventy-two percent to eighty-nine percent within three months of the change."],"type":"tita","answer":"4","explanation":"The paragraph’s specific angle is the cause of this quarter’s improvement; sentence 4 is a true but irrelevant historical fact about the airline’s founding that has nothing to do with the specific performance improvement being explained.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-62","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"True but Irrelevant to the Specific Angle","src":"doc","sentences":["The paragraph’s focus: how a specific software bug caused a major service outage.","A routine update introduced an unhandled edge case in the system’s authentication process.","The error caused the authentication service to crash under high traffic volume.","The company’s headquarters is located in a technology hub known for many similar firms.","Engineers restored service within four hours by rolling back to the previous stable version."],"type":"tita","answer":"4","explanation":"The paragraph’s specific angle is the technical cause and resolution of the outage; sentence 4 is a true but irrelevant fact about the company’s office location that has no bearing on the bug or its resolution.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-63","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"True but Irrelevant to the Specific Angle","src":"doc","sentences":["The paragraph’s focus: why a specific coral reef has shown unusual resilience to bleaching.","The reef sits in a zone with unusually strong, consistent water currents.","These currents keep water temperatures more stable than in surrounding areas.","Coral reefs support an estimated quarter of all known marine species globally.","Researchers believe this thermal stability explains the reef’s comparatively low bleaching rates."],"type":"tita","answer":"4","explanation":"The paragraph’s specific angle is why this particular reef resists bleaching; sentence 4 is a true but irrelevant general fact about coral reefs’ biodiversity value that doesn’t address the specific resilience mechanism.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-64","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"True but Irrelevant to the Specific Angle","src":"doc","sentences":["The paragraph’s focus: why a specific factory reduced its workplace injury rate by half.","The factory introduced mandatory short breaks every ninety minutes on the production line.","Fatigue-related incidents had previously accounted for the majority of reported injuries.","Workplace injuries cost businesses billions of dollars annually across all industries.","Injury reports fell sharply within the first six months after the new break policy began."],"type":"tita","answer":"4","explanation":"The paragraph’s specific angle is the cause of this factory’s specific improvement; sentence 4 is a true but irrelevant general economic statistic that doesn’t explain this factory’s specific outcome.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-65","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"True but Irrelevant to the Specific Angle","src":"doc","sentences":["The paragraph’s focus: why a specific city’s downtown retail vacancy rate has fallen sharply.","The city eliminated a longstanding permit requirement that had delayed new store openings by months.","Applications for new retail permits have nearly tripled since the requirement was removed.","Retail vacancy rates have been a persistent challenge for downtown areas across the country.","Several previously vacant storefronts have been filled by new small businesses within the past year."],"type":"tita","answer":"4","explanation":"The paragraph’s specific angle is why this city’s vacancy rate improved; sentence 4 is a true but irrelevant general statement about downtown retail nationwide that doesn’t explain this specific city’s specific policy change.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-66","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"True but Irrelevant to the Specific Angle","src":"doc","sentences":["The paragraph’s focus: why a specific patient’s rare condition was finally correctly diagnosed.","A specialist recognized a subtle symptom pattern that had been previously overlooked.","Genetic testing subsequently confirmed a rare mutation responsible for the condition.","Rare diseases collectively affect a significant portion of the global population.","The correct diagnosis allowed doctors to begin an effective, targeted treatment plan."],"type":"tita","answer":"4","explanation":"The paragraph’s specific angle is this one patient’s specific diagnostic journey; sentence 4 is a true but irrelevant general statistic about rare diseases collectively that doesn’t relate to this specific case.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-67","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"True but Irrelevant to the Specific Angle","src":"doc","sentences":["The paragraph’s focus: why a specific bridge’s paint coating now lasts three times longer than before.","Engineers developed a new coating formula specifically resistant to the region’s salty coastal air.","Laboratory tests showed the new formula resisted corrosion far longer than the previous coating.","Bridges around the world require regular maintenance to remain structurally sound.","The new coating has already been applied to two other bridges in similarly corrosive environments."],"type":"tita","answer":"4","explanation":"The paragraph’s specific angle is why this particular coating formula performs better; sentence 4 is a true but irrelevant general statement about bridge maintenance that doesn’t address the specific coating improvement.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-68","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"True but Irrelevant to the Specific Angle","src":"doc","sentences":["The paragraph’s focus: why a specific school’s reading scores improved dramatically in one year.","The school introduced a structured, phonics-based reading program for all early grades.","Teachers received dedicated training specifically in the new program’s instructional methods.","Reading proficiency is considered a strong predictor of later academic success generally.","Third-grade reading scores rose by nearly twenty percentage points within a single school year."],"type":"tita","answer":"4","explanation":"The paragraph’s specific angle is why this school’s scores improved; sentence 4 is a true but irrelevant general claim about reading proficiency’s importance that doesn’t explain this school’s specific improvement.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-69","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"True but Irrelevant to the Specific Angle","src":"doc","sentences":["The paragraph’s focus: why a specific river’s fish population has rebounded after decades of decline.","A dam removal project restored the river’s natural flow pattern for the first time in fifty years.","Fish populations require unobstructed pathways to reach their traditional spawning grounds.","Dams have historically been built worldwide to generate hydroelectric power and manage water supply.","Surveys conducted two years after the dam’s removal recorded fish populations at levels not seen since the 1960s."],"type":"tita","answer":"4","explanation":"The paragraph’s specific angle is why this river’s population rebounded; sentence 4 is a true but irrelevant general statement about why dams are built, which doesn’t address this river’s specific recovery.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-70","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","cluster":"True but Irrelevant to the Specific Angle","src":"doc","sentences":["The paragraph’s focus: why a specific company’s employee turnover rate dropped sharply last year.","The company introduced a transparent internal promotion process with clearly published criteria.","Exit interviews had previously identified unclear advancement paths as the top reason employees left.","Employee turnover is generally costly for companies due to recruiting and training expenses.","Voluntary turnover fell by nearly forty percent within the first year of the new process."],"type":"tita","answer":"4","explanation":"The paragraph’s specific angle is why this company’s turnover specifically dropped; sentence 4 is a true but irrelevant general statement about turnover costs that doesn’t explain this specific company’s specific improvement.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-71","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Chronology / Logical Thread Breaks","src":"doc","sentences":["The team spent the first month of the project defining clear technical requirements with stakeholders.","Development began only after those requirements were formally signed off by all parties.","A critical bug was discovered during final testing, just two weeks before the planned launch.","The requirements-gathering phase involved interviews with representatives from five different departments.","Engineers worked around the clock to resolve the bug before the launch date."],"type":"tita","answer":"4","explanation":"The paragraph otherwise follows a clear chronological sequence (requirements → development → late bug → resolution); sentence 4 interrupts this timeline by circling back to elaborate on the requirements phase after the narrative has already moved forward.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-72","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Chronology / Logical Thread Breaks","src":"doc","sentences":["The expedition team departed base camp at dawn, aiming to reach the summit before the afternoon winds picked up.","By midmorning, unexpectedly heavy snowfall had already slowed their progress significantly.","Base camp itself had been established three weeks earlier after an initial supply airlift.","The team made the difficult decision to turn back roughly two hundred meters from the summit.","All members returned to base camp safely by early evening, exhausted but unharmed."],"type":"tita","answer":"3","explanation":"The paragraph otherwise follows the day’s chronological events in order; sentence 3 interrupts this sequence by jumping back to an earlier, unrelated point in time (base camp’s establishment weeks prior).","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-73","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Chronology / Logical Thread Breaks","src":"doc","sentences":["The negotiations opened with both delegations presenting their initial, maximalist positions.","Over the following week, both sides gradually narrowed their demands through informal side meetings.","A breakthrough came on the final scheduled day, when a compromise on the key disputed clause was reached.","The lead negotiators had first met years earlier at an unrelated international conference.","A formal agreement was signed the following morning in a brief public ceremony."],"type":"tita","answer":"4","explanation":"The paragraph otherwise follows the negotiation’s chronological arc in sequence; sentence 4 interrupts this flow with an unrelated historical detail about the negotiators’ earlier acquaintance that doesn’t belong in this specific timeline.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-74","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Chronology / Logical Thread Breaks","src":"doc","sentences":["Construction on the new terminal began after nearly two years of design and permitting work.","The airport had first proposed expanding its facilities almost a decade before construction started.","Major structural work was completed within eighteen months, ahead of the original schedule.","Interior fit-out and systems testing took an additional year to complete fully.","The terminal officially opened to passengers three months after final testing concluded."],"type":"tita","answer":"2","explanation":"The paragraph otherwise follows a clear chronological sequence from construction start to opening; sentence 2 interrupts this flow by jumping back to an earlier point (the original proposal a decade before) that breaks the established timeline.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-75","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Chronology / Logical Thread Breaks","src":"doc","sentences":["The restoration team first removed centuries of accumulated grime using specialized, non-abrasive solvents.","Underlying paint layers, once revealed, showed colors far brighter than anyone had expected.","The painting had originally been commissioned for a wealthy merchant family in the sixteenth century.","A final protective varnish was applied once the cleaning and color assessment were complete.","The restored painting was unveiled to the public in a ceremony attended by art historians from around the world."],"type":"tita","answer":"3","explanation":"The paragraph otherwise follows the restoration process in clear chronological order; sentence 3 interrupts this sequence with an unrelated historical fact about the painting’s original commissioning, breaking the flow of the restoration narrative.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-76","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Chronology / Logical Thread Breaks","src":"doc","sentences":["Researchers first collected water samples from twelve sites along the river over a six-month period.","Each sample was tested for a specific set of twenty common industrial pollutants.","The river had been the subject of environmental concern since a major spill decades earlier.","Results showed pollutant levels had declined significantly compared to a similar study a decade prior.","The team published its findings and recommended continued monitoring at reduced frequency."],"type":"tita","answer":"3","explanation":"The paragraph otherwise follows the study’s process and findings in clear sequence; sentence 3 interrupts this flow with an unrelated historical detail about a decades-old spill that isn’t part of this specific study’s timeline.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-77","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Chronology / Logical Thread Breaks","src":"doc","sentences":["The theater company began rehearsals eight weeks before the play’s opening night.","The playwright made significant revisions to the second act just two weeks into rehearsals.","The same playwright had won a national award for a different work several years earlier.","The cast adapted quickly, and the revised scenes were fully integrated within days.","Opening night received a standing ovation and uniformly positive reviews the following morning."],"type":"tita","answer":"3","explanation":"The paragraph otherwise follows the production’s timeline in clear sequence; sentence 3 interrupts this flow with an unrelated biographical detail about the playwright’s past award that doesn’t belong in this specific production’s chronology.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-78","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Chronology / Logical Thread Breaks","src":"doc","sentences":["The company launched its beta testing program with a small group of five hundred early users.","Feedback from this group led to three significant design changes within the first month.","The company’s original prototype had been developed by just two engineers working nights and weekends.","A wider public launch followed two months later, incorporating all the beta feedback.","Within its first week of public availability, the app had been downloaded over a hundred thousand times."],"type":"tita","answer":"3","explanation":"The paragraph otherwise follows the product’s rollout in clear chronological sequence; sentence 3 interrupts this flow with an unrelated detail about the earlier prototype-building phase that breaks from the established timeline.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-79","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Chronology / Logical Thread Breaks","src":"doc","sentences":["The election campaign’s final debate took place just five days before voting began.","Both candidates had first announced their campaigns nearly a year earlier.","Post-debate polling showed a narrow but measurable shift toward one candidate.","That shift held steady through the remaining days before the vote.","Final results confirmed the shift was enough to determine the outcome of a closely contested race."],"type":"tita","answer":"2","explanation":"The paragraph otherwise follows the final days of the campaign in clear sequence; sentence 2 interrupts this flow by jumping back to the campaign’s announcement nearly a year earlier, breaking the established near-term timeline.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-80","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Chronology / Logical Thread Breaks","src":"doc","sentences":["The surgical team completed final pre-operative checks exactly as scheduled that morning.","The procedure itself took nearly six hours, longer than the average for this type of surgery.","The lead surgeon had trained at a specialized center known for this exact procedure years before.","Recovery in the first forty-eight hours proceeded without any notable complications.","The patient was discharged five days after the operation, ahead of the typical recovery timeline."],"type":"tita","answer":"3","explanation":"The paragraph otherwise follows the patient’s procedure and recovery in clear chronological sequence; sentence 3 interrupts this flow with an unrelated biographical detail about the surgeon’s training years earlier.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-81","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Subtle Thematic Drift","src":"doc","sentences":["The company’s remote-work policy allows employees to choose their own working hours within broad limits.","Managers report that project deadlines are still consistently met under the flexible arrangement.","Employee-reported stress levels have declined modestly since the policy was introduced.","Office real estate costs represent a significant expense for companies across many industries.","A follow-up survey found strong employee support for making the flexible policy permanent."],"type":"tita","answer":"4","explanation":"The paragraph is specifically about this company’s flexible-hours policy and its effects on employees; sentence 4 subtly drifts toward a different, related topic (office real estate costs generally) that isn’t the paragraph’s actual focus.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-82","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Subtle Thematic Drift","src":"doc","sentences":["The city’s composting program requires residents to separate food waste into a designated bin.","Participation rates rose sharply after the city began providing bins free of charge.","Diverted food waste is processed at a facility that converts it into usable soil additive.","Landfills release significant amounts of methane as organic waste decomposes without oxygen.","Officials report the program has meaningfully reduced the city’s overall landfill contributions."],"type":"tita","answer":"4","explanation":"The paragraph is specifically about this city’s composting program and its participation/outcomes; sentence 4 subtly drifts into general landfill science that, while related, isn’t the paragraph’s actual focus on the specific program.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-83","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Subtle Thematic Drift","src":"doc","sentences":["The bank’s new fraud detection system flags unusual transaction patterns in real time.","Suspicious transactions are automatically paused pending manual review by a specialist.","False positive rates have fallen significantly since the system was refined last year.","Financial fraud costs consumers and institutions billions of dollars globally each year.","Customers report that legitimate transactions are rarely delayed under the current system."],"type":"tita","answer":"4","explanation":"The paragraph is specifically about this bank’s system and its performance; sentence 4 subtly drifts into a broader statistic about fraud’s global cost that isn’t the paragraph’s specific focus on this system.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-84","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Subtle Thematic Drift","src":"doc","sentences":["The clinic’s new mental health screening protocol is administered during every routine physical exam.","Patients who screen positive are immediately offered a same-day consultation with a counselor.","Early data suggests the protocol has significantly increased early identification of treatable conditions.","Mental health conditions remain widely underdiagnosed across many healthcare systems worldwide.","The clinic plans to expand the same-day consultation model to two additional specialties."],"type":"tita","answer":"4","explanation":"The paragraph is specifically about this clinic’s protocol and its measured effects; sentence 4 subtly drifts into a general global healthcare statistic that isn’t the paragraph’s specific focus on this clinic’s approach.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-85","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Subtle Thematic Drift","src":"doc","sentences":["The theater’s new pricing model offers significantly discounted tickets for same-day purchases.","Attendance on weeknights, previously the theater’s weakest performance nights, has risen noticeably.","Overall revenue has held steady despite the lower average ticket price under the new model.","Live theater attendance nationwide has declined gradually over the past two decades.","The theater credits the model with attracting a younger, more price-sensitive audience segment."],"type":"tita","answer":"4","explanation":"The paragraph is specifically about this theater’s pricing model and its specific effects; sentence 4 subtly drifts into a broader industry trend that isn’t the paragraph’s specific focus on this theater’s approach.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-86","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Subtle Thematic Drift","src":"doc","sentences":["The farm cooperative negotiates bulk seed purchases on behalf of all its member farmers.","This collective bargaining has reduced average seed costs by nearly a fifth for members.","Members also share access to specialized equipment that few could afford individually.","Small farms face significant financial pressure from rising input costs across the agricultural sector.","Cooperative membership has grown by nearly a third over the past three years."],"type":"tita","answer":"4","explanation":"The paragraph is specifically about this cooperative’s specific benefits to members; sentence 4 subtly drifts into a broader sector-wide statement about financial pressure that isn’t the paragraph’s specific focus on the cooperative’s model.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-87","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Subtle Thematic Drift","src":"doc","sentences":["The app’s new accessibility features include full compatibility with screen-reading software.","Text size and color contrast can now be independently adjusted by each user.","Feedback from visually impaired users has been overwhelmingly positive since the update.","Digital accessibility remains a significant unmet need across much of the software industry.","The company says further accessibility improvements are planned for its next major release."],"type":"tita","answer":"4","explanation":"The paragraph is specifically about this app’s specific accessibility features and reception; sentence 4 subtly drifts into a broader industry-wide observation that isn’t the paragraph’s specific focus on this particular app.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-88","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Subtle Thematic Drift","src":"doc","sentences":["The startup’s supply chain relies on three regional suppliers rather than a single overseas manufacturer.","This diversification allowed the company to avoid the shipping delays that affected many competitors.","Production costs are somewhat higher under this model than with centralized overseas manufacturing.","Global supply chains have faced significant disruption from a range of factors in recent years.","The founders say the reliability trade-off has been worth the additional cost."],"type":"tita","answer":"4","explanation":"The paragraph is specifically about this startup’s specific supply chain choices and trade-offs; sentence 4 subtly drifts into a broader global observation that isn’t the paragraph’s specific focus on this company’s decisions.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-89","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Subtle Thematic Drift","src":"doc","sentences":["The school district’s new bus routing software optimizes pickup order using real-time traffic data.","Average bus ride times for students have fallen by nearly fifteen minutes under the new system.","Fuel costs for the district’s bus fleet have also decreased as a result of more efficient routes.","School transportation budgets have come under increasing pressure across many districts nationally.","Parents have reported fewer instances of buses arriving significantly behind schedule."],"type":"tita","answer":"4","explanation":"The paragraph is specifically about this district’s specific software and its specific effects; sentence 4 subtly drifts into a broader national budget trend that isn’t the paragraph’s specific focus on this particular system.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-90","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Subtle Thematic Drift","src":"doc","sentences":["The gallery’s new curatorial approach groups works thematically rather than strictly chronologically.","Visitor surveys indicate this approach has made the collection feel more accessible to newcomers.","Average visit duration has increased noticeably since the reorganization was completed.","Museums and galleries worldwide have increasingly experimented with non-chronological display methods.","The gallery plans to apply the same thematic approach to its upcoming temporary exhibitions."],"type":"tita","answer":"4","explanation":"The paragraph is specifically about this gallery’s specific curatorial change and its effects; sentence 4 subtly drifts into a broader industry-wide trend that isn’t the paragraph’s specific focus on this particular gallery.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-91","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Full Mixed Review (Hardest Set)","src":"doc","sentences":["The polar research station operates on solar and wind power for nearly eight months of the year.","During the remaining winter months, diesel generators provide the station’s primary power source.","Antarctica holds roughly seventy percent of the world’s fresh water, locked in its ice sheets.","Fuel for the generators must be shipped in during a narrow window each summer before sea ice returns.","Researchers say a battery storage upgrade could eventually reduce winter diesel dependence significantly."],"type":"tita","answer":"3","explanation":"The paragraph is specifically about this station’s power system and its logistics; sentence 3 is a true but unrelated general fact about Antarctica’s fresh water that doesn’t connect to the station’s power sourcing.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-92","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Full Mixed Review (Hardest Set)","src":"doc","sentences":["The publishing house’s decision to cap advances for debut novelists was initially met with criticism from literary agents.","Agents argued the policy would discourage new writers from submitting their work to the house at all.","A year later, submission volume from debut authors had, in fact, declined by roughly a fifth.","The publishing industry has undergone significant consolidation over the past two decades.","The publishing house has since revised the policy following an internal review of submission trends."],"type":"tita","answer":"4","explanation":"The paragraph traces this specific publisher’s specific policy and its specific consequences; sentence 4 shifts to an unrelated broader industry trend that doesn’t connect to this specific policy story.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-93","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Full Mixed Review (Hardest Set)","src":"doc","sentences":["The city’s new stormwater management system uses permeable pavement to absorb rainfall directly into the ground.","This reduces the volume of runoff that would otherwise overwhelm the aging sewer system during heavy storms.","Basement flooding complaints in the treated areas fell by more than half in the first year.","Climate change is expected to increase the frequency of heavy rainfall events in many regions.","The city plans to extend the permeable pavement approach to twelve additional neighborhoods."],"type":"tita","answer":"4","explanation":"The paragraph is specifically about this city’s specific system and its measured local effects; sentence 4 shifts to a broader, general climate statement that isn’t the paragraph’s specific focus on this particular intervention.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-94","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Full Mixed Review (Hardest Set)","src":"doc","sentences":["The airline’s frequent flyer program was completely redesigned last year, shifting from miles-flown to money-spent as its basis.","Loyal customers on shorter, more expensive routes benefited most from the change.","Customers on longer, cheaper routes generally saw their earned rewards decrease under the new system.","Frequent flyer programs generate significant ancillary revenue for airlines through partnerships with banks.","The airline reported a small net increase in program enrollment despite the mixed reaction."],"type":"tita","answer":"4","explanation":"The paragraph traces this specific airline’s specific program redesign and its specific effects on different customer groups; sentence 4 shifts to a general industry fact about revenue that isn’t the paragraph’s specific focus.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-95","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Full Mixed Review (Hardest Set)","src":"doc","sentences":["The vineyard began experimenting with drought-resistant grape varieties following several consecutive dry years.","Yields from the new varieties were initially lower than from the traditional grapes they replaced.","Wine critics noted a distinctly different, though not necessarily worse, flavor profile in early bottlings.","Global wine consumption patterns have shifted notably over the past several decades.","By the third harvest, both yield and critical reception had improved significantly."],"type":"tita","answer":"4","explanation":"The paragraph traces this specific vineyard’s specific experiment and its specific outcomes over several years; sentence 4 shifts to an unrelated global consumption trend that doesn’t connect to this vineyard’s specific story.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-96","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Full Mixed Review (Hardest Set)","src":"doc","sentences":["The hospital reduced average patient wait times by restructuring how incoming cases are initially assessed.","A dedicated intake nurse now conducts a brief assessment within five minutes of a patient’s arrival.","This allows more urgent cases to be identified and prioritized far earlier than before.","Healthcare staffing shortages have affected hospitals in many regions over the past several years.","Patient satisfaction scores related specifically to wait times have risen substantially since the change."],"type":"tita","answer":"4","explanation":"The paragraph is specifically about this hospital’s specific intake restructuring and its specific effects; sentence 4 shifts to a broader, general staffing trend that isn’t the paragraph’s specific focus.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-97","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Full Mixed Review (Hardest Set)","src":"doc","sentences":["The town’s volunteer fire department relies entirely on donated equipment and community fundraising.","A recent grant allowed the department to replace its aging fleet of response vehicles for the first time in fifteen years.","Response times have improved measurably since the newer vehicles entered service.","Volunteer fire departments serve a significant portion of rural communities across the country.","The department is now raising funds for updated protective gear as its next priority."],"type":"tita","answer":"4","explanation":"The paragraph is specifically about this town’s specific department and its specific improvements; sentence 4 shifts to a broader national statistic about volunteer departments generally that isn’t the paragraph’s specific focus.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-98","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Full Mixed Review (Hardest Set)","src":"doc","sentences":["The research consortium pooled data from twelve separate long-term studies to increase statistical power.","Combining datasets allowed researchers to detect a smaller effect size than any single study could have found alone.","Meta-analyses combining multiple studies have become an increasingly common approach in medical research.","The pooled analysis required standardizing measurement methods that had differed slightly across the original studies.","Findings from the pooled analysis have already influenced updated clinical guidelines in two countries."],"type":"tita","answer":"3","explanation":"The paragraph is specifically about this particular consortium’s specific pooling effort and its specific outcomes; sentence 3 shifts to a broad, general statement about meta-analyses as a method that isn’t the paragraph’s specific focus on this one project.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-99","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Full Mixed Review (Hardest Set)","src":"doc","sentences":["The city’s participatory budgeting program allows residents to directly vote on how a portion of municipal funds is spent.","Proposals are submitted by residents themselves and refined through public workshops before the vote.","Voter turnout for the budgeting process has grown steadily since the program’s first year.","Municipal budgets typically allocate the largest share of spending to public safety and infrastructure.","Winning proposals from the most recent cycle include a new community garden and a youth sports facility."],"type":"tita","answer":"4","explanation":"The paragraph is specifically about this city’s specific participatory program and its specific process and outcomes; sentence 4 shifts to a broad, general statement about typical municipal budget allocation that isn’t the paragraph’s specific focus.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"ooo-100","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"hard","cluster":"Full Mixed Review (Hardest Set)","src":"doc","sentences":["The observatory’s citizen science project asks volunteers to classify galaxy images from a public database.","Each image is reviewed independently by several volunteers to ensure classification accuracy.","The project has already contributed to the discovery of several previously uncatalogued galaxy clusters.","Citizen science projects have grown increasingly common as a way to process large scientific datasets.","Researchers say volunteer classifications have proven remarkably consistent with expert assessments."],"type":"tita","answer":"4","explanation":"The paragraph is specifically about this particular project’s specific method and specific results; sentence 4 shifts to a broad, general statement about citizen science as a trend that isn’t the paragraph’s specific focus on this one observatory’s project.","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit."},{"id":"rc-1","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p1","difficulty":"medium","stem":"Which of the following best captures the passage’s central argument?","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Neuroscience has definitively proven that free will does not exist."},{"k":"B","t":"The neuroscience findings on decision-making are less conclusive than commonly assumed, and the deeper disagreement is about what “free will” should even mean."},{"k":"C","t":"Compatibilism is the only philosophically defensible position on free will."},{"k":"D","t":"Punishment can never be morally justified under any definition of free will."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"The passage’s structure — questioning the neuroscience’s conclusiveness, then reframing the debate around competing definitions — matches this option precisely. (A) is an extreme the passage explicitly complicates (“would be a mistake… to treat this finding as more conclusive”). (C) overstates the passage’s neutral presentation of compatibilism as one option among several, not the “only defensible” one. (D) misstates the passage’s actual claim, which is conditional (“if free will requires…then”), not a blanket assertion.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-2","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p1","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The kitchen analogy in the second paragraph is used primarily to:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"prove that neural activity has no relationship to conscious decisions."},{"k":"B","t":"illustrate how preparatory activity need not determine the final outcome it precedes."},{"k":"C","t":"argue that cooking decisions are fundamentally different from moral decisions."},{"k":"D","t":"show that the original experiments were poorly designed."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"The analogy directly follows and illustrates the point about preparation not dictating outcome — the passage’s specific counter to the “settled” reading of the experiments. (A) overstates into “no relationship,” while the passage says activity may simply reflect preparation, not zero connection. (C) and (D) are not the analogy’s stated purpose.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-3","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p1","difficulty":"hard","stem":"Based on the passage, a strict libertarian view of free will would be most challenged by which of the following?","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Evidence that brain states are shaped by prior causes within a physical universe."},{"k":"B","t":"Evidence that people sometimes act against their own stated preferences."},{"k":"C","t":"Evidence that compatibilists disagree among themselves about definitions."},{"k":"D","t":"Evidence that punishment can deter future crime."}],"answer":"A","explanation":"The passage explicitly states libertarian free will is “increasingly difficult to reconcile with a physical universe governed by cause and effect” — meaning evidence of brain states being causally determined directly challenges it. (B), (C), and (D) are not the specific tension the passage identifies for the libertarian position.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-4","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p1","difficulty":"easy","stem":"As used in the passage, “incoherent” (final paragraph) most nearly means:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"loud and disorganized"},{"k":"B","t":"logically inconsistent or unable to be sustained"},{"k":"C","t":"emotionally distressing"},{"k":"D","t":"scientifically unproven"}],"answer":"B","explanation":"In context (“strict moral responsibility becomes incoherent”), the word describes a logical breakdown of the concept under a certain premise, not a literal sound or emotional quality. (A) and (C) are common but wrong senses of the word outside this context. (D) confuses “incoherent” with “unverified,” which isn’t the meaning here.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-5","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p1","difficulty":"hard","stem":"It can be inferred from the passage that the author regards the debate over free will as:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"fully resolved in favor of determinism."},{"k":"B","t":"ultimately a matter of definitional and conceptual clarity as much as empirical fact."},{"k":"C","t":"irrelevant to real-world questions about punishment and responsibility."},{"k":"D","t":"settled decisively by the compatibilist position."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"The passage’s closing line explicitly frames the disagreement as being “underneath… about which questions are even the right ones to be asking” — a definitional/conceptual framing. (A) and (D) both overstate resolution the passage never claims. (C) directly contradicts the passage’s explicit discussion of the debate’s real-world stakes for punishment and responsibility.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-6","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p2","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The passage’s primary purpose is to:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"argue definitively that consciousness cannot be explained by physical science."},{"k":"B","t":"explain what distinguishes the “hard problem” of consciousness from the “easy” problems and why it has proven resistant to resolution."},{"k":"C","t":"prove that philosophical zombies actually exist."},{"k":"D","t":"advocate for a specific solution to the hard problem."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"The passage’s entire structure — defining the distinction, explaining the resistance to resolution, presenting multiple viewpoints — matches this descriptive, explanatory purpose. (A) overstates into a definitive argument the passage doesn’t make; it presents multiple views. (C) misreads the “zombie” concept, which is used as a conceivability thought experiment, not a claim of actual existence. (D) is contradicted by the passage’s balanced presentation of competing views without endorsing one.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-7","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p2","difficulty":"easy","stem":"According to the passage, the “easy” problems of consciousness are called “easy” because:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"they require no scientific expertise to solve."},{"k":"B","t":"we can in principle imagine what a complete mechanistic answer to them would look like."},{"k":"C","t":"neuroscientists have already fully solved them."},{"k":"D","t":"they do not involve the brain at all."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"The passage explicitly defines “easy” in this specific, technical sense: “we can, in principle, imagine a complete answer.” (A) directly contradicts the passage’s statement that they “represent some of the most sophisticated ongoing work in neuroscience.” (C) overstates — being imaginable in principle isn’t the same as being solved. (D) directly contradicts the passage’s description of these as being about brain processing.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-8","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p2","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The “philosophical zombie” thought experiment is used in the passage to:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"prove that consciousness does not exist."},{"k":"B","t":"suggest that physical processing and subjective experience may not be identical, since a being with the former but not the latter seems conceivable."},{"k":"C","t":"demonstrate that all animals lack consciousness."},{"k":"D","t":"show that neuroscience has made no progress in understanding the brain."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This is precisely how the passage frames the thought experiment’s function — as a conceivability argument suggesting non-identity between processing and experience. (A) overstates the thought experiment’s actual claim, which concerns conceivability, not proof of non-existence. (C) and (D) are unrelated to the specific point the thought experiment is used to make.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-9","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p2","difficulty":"hard","stem":"Which of the following, if true, would most directly support the view (mentioned in paragraph 3) that the hard problem is an illusion produced by cognitive architecture?","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Neuroscientists develop a complete map of every neuron involved in visual processing."},{"k":"B","t":"A study shows that the intuition that “something is left out” of mechanistic explanations weakens significantly as people learn more neuroscience."},{"k":"C","t":"A new philosopher proposes an entirely new definition of consciousness."},{"k":"D","t":"Behavioral evidence about animal cognition improves significantly."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This option directly supports the specific claim being tested — that the intuition itself, not the underlying reality, is what’s driving perceived difficulty, and that intuition can fade with understanding. (A), (C), and (D) don’t address the specific “illusion of intuition” claim being supported.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-10","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p2","difficulty":"easy","stem":"As used in the final paragraph, “adjudicate” most nearly means:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"to fight or argue aggressively"},{"k":"B","t":"to settle or decide (a dispute) authoritatively"},{"k":"C","t":"to ignore completely"},{"k":"D","t":"to translate into another language"}],"answer":"B","explanation":"In context (“difficult to adjudicate, rather than merely difficult to resolve”), the word clearly means to settle or decide a dispute, paired with and distinguished from “resolve.” (A), (C), and (D) are not supported by the context.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-11","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p3","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The passage’s central purpose is to:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"argue that legal systems should never consider outcomes when assigning punishment."},{"k":"B","t":"present the puzzle of moral luck and the tension it creates between two persistent, competing moral intuitions."},{"k":"C","t":"prove that constitutive luck makes all moral judgment meaningless."},{"k":"D","t":"advocate for treating both distracted drivers with identical leniency."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"The passage’s structure — introducing the phenomenon, showing the tension between control-based and outcome-based intuitions, presenting unresolved responses — matches this framing. (A) and (D) overstate into specific advocacy positions the passage explicitly declines to fully endorse (“no fully satisfying resolution… has emerged”). (C) overstates the passage’s presentation of constitutive luck as “unsettling,” not as proof that all judgment is meaningless.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-12","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p3","difficulty":"easy","stem":"According to the passage, “circumstantial luck” refers to:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"the outcome of an action being determined by chance after a choice is made."},{"k":"B","t":"the fact that a person’s life circumstances may never test them the way another person’s circumstances do."},{"k":"C","t":"the genetic basis of a person’s temperament."},{"k":"D","t":"the legal system’s tendency to be inconsistent."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This matches the passage’s explicit definition and example (a comfortably raised person never facing the pressures another person faced). (A) describes outcome luck, a different category defined earlier. (C) describes constitutive luck, a distinct third category. (D) is not a category the passage defines at all.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-13","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p3","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The two drivers example is used in the passage primarily to:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"prove that all traffic laws are unjust."},{"k":"B","t":"illustrate how identical choices can lead to very different moral judgments based purely on chance outcomes."},{"k":"C","t":"show that phone use while driving should be legal."},{"k":"D","t":"demonstrate that only intentions, never outcomes, should matter morally."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This is exactly the example’s stated function — illustrating the disconnect between identical choices and different outcome-based judgments. (A) and (C) are claims never made by the passage. (D) actually states the opposite of what the passage argues is our persistent intuition (that outcomes do seem to matter to us).","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-14","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p3","difficulty":"hard","stem":"It can be inferred that the author views the “bite the bullet” response (final paragraph) as:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"fully correct and the only reasonable position."},{"k":"B","t":"a coherent position, but one that may ask more of ordinary moral psychology than is realistic or perhaps desirable."},{"k":"C","t":"logically incoherent and self-contradictory."},{"k":"D","t":"irrelevant to the discussion of moral luck."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"The passage explicitly presents the counter-response as noting this position “asks too much of moral psychology,” implying the author sees a real, unresolved tension rather than a clean victory for either side. (A) overstates the passage’s neutral, unresolved framing. (C) is not supported — the position is presented as coherent, just contested. (D) directly contradicts its central role in the passage’s final paragraph.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-15","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p3","difficulty":"easy","stem":"As used in the passage, “bites the bullet” most nearly means:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"refuses to engage with a difficult topic."},{"k":"B","t":"accepts an uncomfortable but logically consistent conclusion, despite its unpleasant implications."},{"k":"C","t":"offers a compromise between two extreme positions."},{"k":"D","t":"misunderstands the nature of the original question."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This is the standard idiomatic meaning, matched by context — the response that “insists” on the strict, uncomfortable, logically consistent conclusion about responsibility. (A), (C), and (D) don’t match the context of a response that directly engages and commits to a specific, difficult position.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-16","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p4","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The passage’s primary purpose is to:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"argue that nudge policies should be banned as manipulative."},{"k":"B","t":"explain the behavioral economics findings behind nudge policy, and present both its philosophical justification and the concerns raised against it."},{"k":"C","t":"prove that classical economic theory has been completely discredited."},{"k":"D","t":"argue that opt-out retirement schemes are the only effective policy tool available to governments."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"The passage’s balanced structure — findings, the nudge response, philosophical objections, and empirical caveats — matches this descriptive, multi-sided purpose. (A) overstates into advocacy the passage never takes. (C) overstates “fails in specific, predictable ways” into total discrediting of the whole framework. (D) is an unsupported exclusivity claim never made.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-17","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p4","difficulty":"easy","stem":"According to the passage, the opt-out retirement scheme example illustrates:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"a case where individual choice is eliminated entirely."},{"k":"B","t":"how changing a default option can shift outcomes without changing the actual menu of choices available."},{"k":"C","t":"why loss aversion has no practical policy applications."},{"k":"D","t":"a failed nudge policy that did not increase participation."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This is exactly how the passage frames the example — “the actual menu of choices… remains exactly the same,” with only the default changed. (A) directly contradicts “no one is forced to save.” (C) contradicts the entire premise of the example illustrating a practical application. (D) directly contradicts “dramatically increase participation rates.”","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-18","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p4","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The author’s attitude toward nudge policy throughout the passage can best be described as:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"uncritically enthusiastic"},{"k":"B","t":"dismissively hostile"},{"k":"C","t":"measured, acknowledging both its rationale and legitimate concerns about it"},{"k":"D","t":"entirely indifferent to the topic"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"The passage explicitly presents the rationale (paragraph 3), the critics’ concerns (paragraph 3), and empirical caveats (paragraph 4) without dismissing any side outright. (A) and (B) both overstate into one-sided positions the balanced structure contradicts. (D) contradicts the passage’s clear, substantive engagement with the topic throughout.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-19","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p4","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The “decline effect” is mentioned in the passage primarily to:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"prove that nudges never work under any circumstances."},{"k":"B","t":"caution that some early, striking nudge results may not hold up as reliably at larger scale."},{"k":"C","t":"argue that all social science research is fundamentally unreliable."},{"k":"D","t":"show that the retirement-savings finding has been discredited."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This matches the passage’s specific, hedged framing — a caution about some results, not a blanket dismissal. (A) and (C) both overstate into absolutes the passage explicitly avoids (“this does not mean nudges don’t work”). (D) directly contradicts the passage’s statement that this specific finding “has proven remarkably robust.”","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-20","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p4","difficulty":"easy","stem":"As used in the passage, “canonical” (paragraph 2) most nearly means:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"religious or sacred"},{"k":"B","t":"widely recognized as the standard or representative example"},{"k":"C","t":"recently discovered and untested"},{"k":"D","t":"legally mandated"}],"answer":"B","explanation":"In context (“the canonical example is retirement savings”), the word describes a standard, most-cited representative case. (A) is a different, unrelated sense of the word. (C) directly contradicts the description of an established, repeatedly-shown effect. (D) is unsupported by context.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-21","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p5","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The passage’s central argument is that:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"meritocracy should be immediately replaced by a hereditary system."},{"k":"B","t":"both the practical implementation and the philosophical premises of meritocracy face serious, unresolved challenges."},{"k":"C","t":"intelligence and effort have no relationship to individual success."},{"k":"D","t":"all wealthy countries have achieved perfect intergenerational income mobility."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"The passage’s two-part structure — an “empirical challenge” and a “philosophical challenge,” both explicitly named — matches this framing precisely. (A) is directly contradicted by “none of this establishes that meritocratic ideals should be abandoned outright.” (C) overstates the passage’s point, which is about the origins of these traits, not their relevance to outcomes. (D) is the opposite of what the passage’s cited studies find.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-22","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p5","difficulty":"easy","stem":"According to the passage, the philosophical challenge to meritocracy is distinct from the empirical challenge in that it:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"focuses on unequal access to education and resources."},{"k":"B","t":"questions whether even meritocracy’s ideal form rests on a coherent notion of “earned” reward."},{"k":"C","t":"argues that intergenerational income mobility studies are methodologically flawed."},{"k":"D","t":"focuses exclusively on aristocratic societies of the past."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"The passage explicitly states this challenge applies “even setting aside unequal access entirely” — targeting the ideal form itself, not implementation. (A) describes the empirical challenge, explicitly distinguished from this one. (C) is not a claim the passage makes about the studies. (D) is not the philosophical challenge’s actual focus, which is about individuals’ traits, not historical systems.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-23","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p5","difficulty":"hard","stem":"It can be inferred from the passage that intergenerational income mobility studies are cited primarily to:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"prove meritocracy has been entirely eliminated in modern societies."},{"k":"B","t":"support the claim that formally open systems still substantially reproduce existing family-based hierarchies."},{"k":"C","t":"show that talent has no influence on income at all."},{"k":"D","t":"demonstrate that hereditary aristocracies had higher mobility than modern meritocracies."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This matches the passage’s explicit use of the studies to support “persistent correlations between parental and child income” despite formal openness. (A) overstates — the passage argues reproduction persists, not that meritocracy has vanished. (C) overstates into an absolute the passage doesn’t claim. (D) is a comparison never made in the passage.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-24","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p5","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The author’s tone in the final paragraph can best be described as:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"dismissive of any concerns about meritocracy"},{"k":"B","t":"cautionary, urging a more nuanced view rather than treating meritocracy as an unqualified good"},{"k":"C","t":"enthusiastically supportive of unrestrained meritocracy"},{"k":"D","t":"entirely neutral, offering no evaluative perspective at all"}],"answer":"B","explanation":"The final paragraph explicitly says the finding “should give pause to anyone tempted to treat ‘meritocracy’ as a simple, unqualified good” — a clearly cautionary stance. (A) and (C) contradict this explicit caution. (D) contradicts the clearly evaluative language used throughout (“should give pause,” “real social harm”).","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-25","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p5","difficulty":"easy","stem":"As used in the passage, “cultural capital” most nearly refers to:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"money inherited directly from one’s parents."},{"k":"B","t":"non-monetary social and cultural advantages, such as networks and know-how, that aid success."},{"k":"C","t":"government funding for arts and culture programs."},{"k":"D","t":"formal educational credentials only."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"The term is introduced alongside “education” and “networks” as part of a broader category of advantages beyond direct financial inheritance. (A) narrows the term to money alone, contradicting its listing alongside non-monetary items. (C) is an unrelated meaning not used here. (D) narrows the term too specifically to formal credentials, when the passage’s usage is broader (including “modeling of what an ambitious life trajectory looks like”).","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-26","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p6","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The passage’s primary purpose is to:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"argue that the current wave of automation will definitely cause permanent mass unemployment."},{"k":"B","t":"examine the historical pattern of technological displacement and adaptation, and assess whether the current wave of automation genuinely breaks from that pattern."},{"k":"C","t":"prove that ATMs caused a permanent decline in banking employment."},{"k":"D","t":"advocate for a total ban on machine learning technologies in the workplace."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"The passage’s structure — establishing the historical pattern, examining the current wave’s potential difference, and reframing the debate around transition management — matches this analytical purpose. (A) is directly contradicted by the passage’s statement that even concerned observers “tend to stop short of predicting mass, permanent unemployment.” (C) directly contradicts “their numbers, initially, actually grew.” (D) is a policy position never suggested anywhere in the passage.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-27","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p6","difficulty":"easy","stem":"According to the passage, the ATM/bank teller example illustrates:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"a case where technology eliminated an entire profession outright."},{"k":"B","t":"the pattern of task displacement followed by adaptation into a new, related role."},{"k":"C","t":"why automation always increases unemployment in the short term."},{"k":"D","t":"the unique unpredictability of the current wave of automation."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This is exactly how the passage frames the example, including the specific detail about tellers shifting to advisory tasks. (A) directly contradicts “their numbers, initially, actually grew.” (C) is not supported by an example the passage uses to show adaptation, not increased unemployment. (D) misapplies an example about a past wave to a claim about the “current” wave, which the passage discusses separately.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-28","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p6","difficulty":"hard","stem":"Which of the following, if true, would most directly support the passage’s argument that the current automation wave may differ from previous ones?","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Machine learning systems have become significantly better at drafting written documents and analyzing complex data than they were a decade ago."},{"k":"B","t":"A new law requires all displaced workers to receive severance pay."},{"k":"C","t":"The overall unemployment rate has remained stable for the past five years."},{"k":"D","t":"A study finds that most companies still primarily use automation for repetitive manual tasks."}],"answer":"A","explanation":"This directly supports the passage’s specific claim that current automation is reaching into cognitive/creative domains previously considered safe, expanding the boundary described in paragraph 2. (B) and (C) are unrelated to the specific “different in kind” claim being tested. (D) would actually undercut, not support, the claim that this wave differs by reaching into cognitive work.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-29","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p6","difficulty":"easy","stem":"The passage’s discussion of “distributional character” (final paragraph) refers primarily to:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"how technology companies distribute their profits among shareholders."},{"k":"B","t":"the fact that the workers displaced by automation are often not the same workers who benefit from newly created roles."},{"k":"C","t":"the geographic spread of internet access across different countries."},{"k":"D","t":"how automated systems distribute tasks among different machines."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"The passage explicitly defines this in context — displaced and benefiting workers requiring “different skills, often concentrated in different geographic regions.” (A), (C), and (D) are all unrelated meanings not supported by the passage’s specific usage.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-30","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p6","difficulty":"hard","stem":"It can be inferred from the passage that the author believes policy discussions about automation should:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"focus primarily on banning automating technologies outright."},{"k":"B","t":"shift emphasis toward managing the transition for workers on the losing side, rather than only debating aggregate employment effects."},{"k":"C","t":"ignore the transition entirely and trust that markets will resolve all problems automatically."},{"k":"D","t":"focus exclusively on retraining truck drivers specifically."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This matches the passage’s explicit closing statement about debates “shifting… toward the more tractable and arguably more urgent question of how to manage the transition.” (A) is never suggested anywhere in the passage. (C) contradicts the passage’s clear concern about uneven distribution needing active attention. (D) overly narrows a general point to one example used only for illustration.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-31","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p7","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The passage’s central purpose is to:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"argue that all advertising-funded platforms are deliberately, consciously manipulative."},{"k":"B","t":"explain the structural incentives of attention-based business models and present the genuine tension between critiques and defenses of that model."},{"k":"C","t":"prove that users are entirely powerless against platform design."},{"k":"D","t":"advocate for banning digital advertising entirely."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"The passage’s structure — explaining incentives, then presenting both a critique and a defense, then acknowledging genuine tension — matches this balanced, explanatory purpose. (A) is explicitly contradicted by “does not require any single feature to be deliberately, consciously ‘addictive.’” (C) is directly contradicted by the passage’s explicit statement that “users are not, in fact, powerless.” (D) is a policy position never suggested in the passage.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-32","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p7","difficulty":"easy","stem":"According to the passage, why might the “market” fail to correct for the costs of attention capture?","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Because users are legally prohibited from switching platforms."},{"k":"B","t":"Because the costs to users are diffuse and delayed, unlike a typical dissatisfaction with a physical product."},{"k":"C","t":"Because advertisers refuse to pay for any platform."},{"k":"D","t":"Because platforms do not track any user engagement data."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This is precisely the “structural problem” the passage identifies — the disconnect between diffuse, delayed costs and typical, quicker market feedback. (A) and (C) are claims never made in the passage. (D) directly contradicts the passage’s discussion of “engagement metrics” being actively tracked and optimized against.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-33","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p7","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The phrase “revealed preference” (final paragraph) is used in the passage to refer to:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"preferences people explicitly state when surveyed about their values."},{"k":"B","t":"what people’s actual, repeated choices over time suggest about what they value, regardless of stated preferences."},{"k":"C","t":"preferences that are illegal to act upon."},{"k":"D","t":"preferences that platforms actively conceal from users."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"The passage explicitly defines this as “what people actually choose to spend time on, repeatedly, over years,” distinguished from what “users say, when surveyed” — i.e., distinct from stated preference. (A) directly describes the opposite concept (stated preference), which the passage distinguishes from revealed preference. (C) and (D) are unsupported, unrelated claims.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-34","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p7","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The author’s stance on the debate presented in the passage can best be described as:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"firmly siding with the critics of attention-based business models."},{"k":"B","t":"firmly siding with the defenders of attention-based business models."},{"k":"C","t":"presenting genuine merit on both sides while highlighting an unresolved tension between them."},{"k":"D","t":"entirely dismissive of the entire debate as unimportant."}],"answer":"C","explanation":"The final paragraph explicitly calls for taking “both halves of this picture seriously at once” and names the unresolved question directly. (A) and (B) both overstate into one-sided advocacy the passage’s balanced structure doesn’t support. (D) directly contradicts the passage’s substantial, serious engagement with the topic throughout.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-35","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p7","difficulty":"easy","stem":"As used in the passage, “paternalism” most nearly means:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"a father’s specific role within a family structure."},{"k":"B","t":"the practice of substituting others’ judgment about what is good for them in place of their own choices."},{"k":"C","t":"a legal system based on inherited titles."},{"k":"D","t":"a business model based on advertising revenue."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This matches the passage’s explicit context — “substituting the critic’s judgment about what people should want for what people… actually do want.” (A) and (C) are unrelated, literal or unrelated senses of the word. (D) confuses the term with the passage’s separate topic (the business model itself).","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-36","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p8","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The passage’s primary purpose is to:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"prove that microfinance is a complete failure that should be abandoned."},{"k":"B","t":"trace how rigorous evaluation of microfinance revealed a more modest reality than its early, utopian reputation suggested, while drawing broader lessons about development interventions."},{"k":"C","t":"argue that all development interventions are oversold and worthless."},{"k":"D","t":"advocate for banning group lending models specifically."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"The passage’s arc — from early utopian rhetoric, through rigorous evaluation findings, to broader lessons and a specific complication — matches this analytical purpose. (A) is explicitly contradicted by “the lesson… is not that microfinance is worthless.” (C) overgeneralizes a specific, well-documented case into a sweeping claim about all interventions the passage doesn’t make. (D) is a specific policy stance never suggested in the passage.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-37","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p8","difficulty":"easy","stem":"According to the passage, randomized controlled trials of microfinance found:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"no effects of any kind from access to microloans."},{"k":"B","t":"measurable but modest effects, generally falling short of the dramatic poverty reduction originally promised."},{"k":"C","t":"that microfinance caused widespread economic collapse in every country studied."},{"k":"D","t":"that microloans were used exclusively to start new, ambitious businesses."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This directly matches the passage’s specific findings — “measurable effects” alongside “little to no evidence” of the dramatic, broader claims. (A) directly contradicts “did produce measurable effects.” (C) is an extreme claim never made anywhere in the passage. (D) directly contradicts “many borrowers used loans not to start ambitious new ventures but to smooth consumption.”","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-38","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p8","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The passage mentions microfinance’s high early visibility (paragraph 3) primarily to explain:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"why microfinance is more profitable than other development interventions."},{"k":"B","t":"why microfinance has been unusually well studied compared to other, similarly confident development interventions."},{"k":"C","t":"why microfinance institutions charge higher interest rates than banks."},{"k":"D","t":"why microfinance was invented in the first place."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This is precisely the stated reason — visibility attracted “an unusually large volume of serious academic scrutiny” other interventions lacked. (A), (C), and (D) are all claims not supported by this specific point in the passage.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-39","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p8","difficulty":"hard","stem":"The over-indebtedness complication described in the final paragraph is best characterized in the passage as:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"proof that microfinance institutions always intend to harm borrowers."},{"k":"B","t":"an unintended, harmful outcome that can emerge from rapid expansion without sufficient regulatory safeguards."},{"k":"C","t":"evidence that group lending never works under any circumstances."},{"k":"D","t":"unrelated to the broader lessons the passage draws about development interventions."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This matches the passage’s explicit framing — “despite microfinance’s founding intentions being close to the opposite” and tied directly to “limited regulatory oversight.” (A) directly contradicts the stated benign founding intentions. (C) overstates a specific complication into a blanket condemnation of the entire model. (D) directly contradicts the passage’s explicit statement that this “illustrate[s] a pattern worth remembering” broadly.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-40","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p8","difficulty":"easy","stem":"As used in the passage, “recalibration” (paragraph 3) most nearly means:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"a complete and total abandonment of a prior approach"},{"k":"B","t":"an adjustment or fine-tuning of expectations or methods based on new evidence"},{"k":"C","t":"a financial audit of an organization’s accounts"},{"k":"D","t":"a legal dispute between two parties"}],"answer":"B","explanation":"In context (“a useful, broader recalibration in how development interventions are evaluated”), the word describes an adjustment in evaluative approach, not a total abandonment. (A) overstates the word’s meaning into something far more drastic than “adjustment.” (C) and (D) are unrelated senses not supported by the context.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-41","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p9","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The passage’s primary purpose is to:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"prove definitively that language models genuinely understand language exactly as humans do."},{"k":"B","t":"present the debate over whether language models “understand” language, and suggest a more precise, componential way of framing the question."},{"k":"C","t":"argue that the Chinese Room thought experiment has been thoroughly debunked by modern AI."},{"k":"D","t":"advocate for banning the development of large language models."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"The passage’s structure — presenting both sides, then proposing a more granular reframing in the final paragraph — matches this analytical purpose. (A) overstates into a definitive proof the passage never makes; it presents an open debate. (C) misreads the passage, which uses the thought experiment as a live, unresolved argument, not something “debunked.” (D) is a policy position never suggested anywhere in the passage.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-42","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p9","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The Chinese Room thought experiment is used in the passage to:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"prove that all computer systems are incapable of any useful function."},{"k":"B","t":"illustrate the skeptics’ argument that sophisticated symbol manipulation is not the same as genuine understanding."},{"k":"C","t":"show that Chinese is a uniquely difficult language for computers to process."},{"k":"D","t":"demonstrate that language models cannot produce fluent responses."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This is precisely the stated function of the thought experiment as presented by “skeptics of the ‘understanding’ framing.” (A) and (D) are extreme claims never made or contradicted by the passage’s description of fluent output. (C) misreads the thought experiment’s purpose, which is about symbol manipulation generally, not Chinese specifically.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-43","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p9","difficulty":"easy","stem":"According to the passage, defenders of a more expansive view of AI “understanding” argue that:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"human cognition may itself be describable in similarly mechanistic terms without most people concluding human understanding is illusory."},{"k":"B","t":"the Chinese Room thought experiment proves language models are conscious."},{"k":"C","t":"understanding is a single, unified capacity that either exists fully or not at all."},{"k":"D","t":"language models have direct, unmediated access to the true meaning of words."}],"answer":"A","explanation":"This is the specific rejoinder attributed to defenders in paragraph 3. (B) overstates a claim never made — the passage doesn’t discuss consciousness via this thought experiment. (C) directly contradicts the passage’s final paragraph, which argues understanding is likely a cluster of separable capacities, not this claim. (D) directly contradicts the passage’s point that human cognition lacks such direct access either.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-44","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p9","difficulty":"hard","stem":"The final paragraph’s proposed reframing suggests that the question “does this system understand?” should be replaced with:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"a question about whether the system is legally a person."},{"k":"B","t":"a more precise inquiry into which specific components of understanding a system does or doesn’t reliably exhibit."},{"k":"C","t":"a question about how much electricity the system consumes."},{"k":"D","t":"a question about whether the system was trained on copyrighted material."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This is exactly the passage’s stated proposal — asking “which specific components of understanding a given system does and doesn’t reliably exhibit, and in which specific contexts.” (A), (C), and (D) are all unrelated questions never proposed in the passage.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-45","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p9","difficulty":"easy","stem":"As used in the passage, “rejoinder” most nearly means:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"an unrelated new topic introduced into a conversation."},{"k":"B","t":"a response or reply, especially one offered in argument or rebuttal."},{"k":"C","t":"a formal legal document."},{"k":"D","t":"an apology for a previous mistake."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"In context (“defenders… raise a pointed rejoinder”), the word clearly refers to a rebuttal-style response within the ongoing debate. (A), (C), and (D) are unrelated or incorrect senses not supported by the context.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-46","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p10","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The passage’s primary purpose is to:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"argue that all forms of gene editing should be permanently banned worldwide."},{"k":"B","t":"explain the therapeutic potential of gene editing while examining the distinct and escalating ethical questions it raises, particularly around germline editing."},{"k":"C","t":"prove that the 2018 controversy was based on a scientifically unsound technique."},{"k":"D","t":"advocate specifically for allowing genetic enhancement for cognitive traits."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"The passage’s arc — therapeutic potential, the somatic/germline distinction, the 2018 controversy, and the harder enhancement question ahead — matches this comprehensive, analytical purpose. (A) is contradicted by the passage’s substantial discussion of legitimate therapeutic uses. (C) directly contradicts “not primarily because the underlying editing technique was scientifically unsound.” (D) is a specific advocacy position never taken; the passage presents this as an open, difficult question.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-47","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p10","difficulty":"easy","stem":"According to the passage, the key distinction between somatic and germline editing is that:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"somatic editing is illegal everywhere, while germline editing is legal everywhere."},{"k":"B","t":"germline changes are inherited by future generations, while somatic changes affect only the treated individual."},{"k":"C","t":"somatic editing is a newer technique than germline editing."},{"k":"D","t":"germline editing has never been successfully performed on any organism."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This is precisely the passage’s explicit definition of the distinction. (A) directly contradicts the passage, which never states this legal claim and instead discusses ongoing regulatory efforts. (C) is an unsupported claim about relative novelty never made. (D) directly contradicts the passage’s account of the 2018 case, where germline editing was performed on human embryos.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-48","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p10","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The 2018 controversy is presented in the passage primarily as an example of:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"a case where the scientific community praised a scientist for pioneering breakthrough work."},{"k":"B","t":"a case where bypassing ethical review and societal deliberation, not the science itself, drove near-universal condemnation."},{"k":"C","t":"proof that gene editing technology does not actually work as intended."},{"k":"D","t":"an example of legitimate, well-regulated germline editing."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This is exactly how the passage frames the reason for condemnation — process, not the underlying science. (A) directly contradicts “near-universal condemnation.” (C) directly contradicts the passage’s statement that the technique itself was not scientifically unsound. (D) directly contradicts the passage’s description of the intervention as having “bypassed” required review processes.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-49","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p10","difficulty":"hard","stem":"It can be inferred from the passage that the author views the disease/enhancement distinction as:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"a clear, easily maintained line that raises no further difficulty."},{"k":"B","t":"potentially difficult to maintain with philosophical rigor, given that many traits exist on a spectrum."},{"k":"C","t":"entirely irrelevant to future ethical debates about gene editing."},{"k":"D","t":"already fully resolved by current international regulations."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"The passage explicitly states geneticists’ concern that the distinction “will prove difficult to maintain with any philosophical rigor… since many traits exist on a continuous spectrum.” (A) directly contradicts this stated difficulty. (C) directly contradicts the passage’s extensive discussion of this exact distinction as a looming, urgent issue. (D) directly contradicts the passage’s framing of this as a question “looming on the horizon” that “the current moratorium does not resolve.”","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-50","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p10","difficulty":"easy","stem":"As used in the passage, “moratorium” most nearly means:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"a permanent, irreversible ban with no possibility of future change."},{"k":"B","t":"a temporary prohibition or suspension of an activity."},{"k":"C","t":"a formal scientific award or honor."},{"k":"D","t":"a type of gene-editing technique."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"The context (“the current moratorium does not resolve” a question about the technology’s future once safety improves) implies a temporary, not permanent, suspension pending further development. (A) overstates the term’s meaning into permanence, contradicted by the passage’s discussion of the issue evolving as technology “continues to improve.” (C) and (D) are unrelated, unsupported meanings.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-51","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p11","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The passage’s primary purpose is to:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"advocate strongly for the immediate global deployment of solar radiation management."},{"k":"B","t":"explain the appeal of geoengineering while examining the serious technical, moral, and institutional risks it poses."},{"k":"C","t":"prove that volcanic eruptions are the primary cause of long-term climate change."},{"k":"D","t":"argue that emissions reduction efforts should be entirely abandoned in favor of geoengineering."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"The passage’s structure — presenting the appeal, then substantial risks (regional harm, termination shock, governance gaps) — matches this balanced, analytical purpose. (A) is contradicted by the passage’s extensive, serious treatment of risks that goes well beyond simple advocacy. (C) misreads the volcanic reference, used only as an analogy for the cooling mechanism, not a causal claim about long-term climate change. (D) is never suggested; the passage discusses geoengineering as a response to the pace gap, not a replacement for emissions reduction.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-52","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p11","difficulty":"easy","stem":"According to the passage, “termination shock” refers to:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"the immediate cooling effect produced when solar radiation management begins."},{"k":"B","t":"the rapid reassertion of full warming if a solar radiation management program is abruptly halted."},{"k":"C","t":"a sudden, unexpected increase in global emissions."},{"k":"D","t":"the political conflict caused by disagreements over climate policy."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This is precisely the passage’s explicit definition of the term. (A) describes the opposite effect — this term concerns halting, not beginning, the program. (C) and (D) are unrelated concepts not defined this way anywhere in the passage.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-53","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p11","difficulty":"hard","stem":"The passage suggests that solar radiation management differs from emissions reduction in which specific way?","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Emissions reduction is significantly more expensive to implement in every case."},{"k":"B","t":"Solar radiation management could redistribute climate harms unevenly across regions, unlike emissions reduction."},{"k":"C","t":"Emissions reduction produces effects within months, while solar radiation management takes decades."},{"k":"D","t":"Solar radiation management has no associated risks of any kind."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This is explicitly stated: emissions reduction “does not by its nature redistribute climate harms from one region onto another,” unlike solar radiation management. (A) reverses the passage’s actual claim about relative cost-effectiveness. (C) directly reverses the passage’s stated timelines (months for SRM, decades for emissions reduction to show comparable effect). (D) directly contradicts the passage’s extensive discussion of serious risks.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-54","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p11","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The author’s attitude toward geoengineering, as conveyed in the passage, can best be described as:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"uncritically supportive"},{"k":"B","t":"seriously engaged with both its practical appeal and its severe, largely unresolved risks"},{"k":"C","t":"completely dismissive of the technology’s potential value"},{"k":"D","t":"entirely neutral, with no discernible perspective offered"}],"answer":"B","explanation":"The passage substantively engages with both the appeal (speed, cost) and severe risks (regional harm, termination shock, governance gaps) without resolving into simple advocacy or dismissal. (A) and (C) both overstate into one-sided positions the passage’s balanced treatment doesn’t support. (D) contradicts the passage’s clearly evaluative language throughout (“severe enough,” “deepest concern,” “moral and geopolitical hazard”).","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-55","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p11","difficulty":"easy","stem":"As used in the passage, “hazard” (paragraph 3) most nearly means:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"a game of chance played with dice."},{"k":"B","t":"a source of danger or risk."},{"k":"C","t":"a legal contract between two parties."},{"k":"D","t":"a type of atmospheric particle."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"In context (“a moral and geopolitical hazard distinct from anything posed by emissions reduction”), the word clearly refers to a source of danger or risk. (A) is an unrelated, archaic meaning. (C) and (D) are unrelated senses not supported by the context.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-56","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p12","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The passage’s primary purpose is to:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"prove that quantum computers will never have any practical use."},{"k":"B","t":"clarify a common misconception about how quantum computing works, and outline both its realistic scope of advantage and its remaining engineering challenges."},{"k":"C","t":"argue that classical computers are superior to quantum computers in every respect."},{"k":"D","t":"explain, in full technical detail, how to build a working quantum computer."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"The passage’s structure — correcting the “trying all answers simultaneously” misconception, narrowing the realistic scope of advantage, and detailing engineering challenges — matches this clarifying, analytical purpose. (A) is contradicted by the passage’s discussion of “genuine, well-established promise” for specific problem classes. (C) overstates a comparison the passage doesn’t make in these absolute terms — quantum computers are described as advantageous for specific tasks, not universally superior or inferior. (D) misreads the passage’s level and purpose, which is conceptual and evaluative, not a technical build guide.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-57","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p12","difficulty":"easy","stem":"According to the passage, why is the “trying all possible answers simultaneously” description misleading?","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Because qubits cannot actually be placed into superposition at all."},{"k":"B","t":"Because measurement collapses superposition into a single random outcome, and the real source of advantage lies in interference effects that amplify correct answers before measurement."},{"k":"C","t":"Because classical computers can also achieve the same effect using standard bits."},{"k":"D","t":"Because quantum computers do not use qubits at all."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This is precisely the passage’s stated correction, distinguishing mere superposition from the actual mechanism (interference) that produces useful results. (A) and (D) directly contradict the passage’s own description of qubits and superposition as real phenomena. (C) is an unsupported claim never made in the passage.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-58","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p12","difficulty":"hard","stem":"The passage suggests that quantum computers are expected to offer a meaningful advantage over classical computers primarily for:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"all everyday computing tasks, including word processing and video streaming."},{"k":"B","t":"a narrower set of problems, including certain cryptographic and chemical simulation tasks, with a specific mathematical structure."},{"k":"C","t":"no computational tasks whatsoever, according to current theoretical understanding."},{"k":"D","t":"exclusively tasks related to video game graphics rendering."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This directly matches the passage’s specific, explicit list of promising problem categories. (A) is directly contradicted by “no quantum algorithm currently known offers any meaningful advantage” for such everyday tasks. (C) directly contradicts the passage’s explicit discussion of “genuine, well-established promise” for specific problems. (D) is a claim never made anywhere in the passage.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-59","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p12","difficulty":"easy","stem":"As used in the passage, “decoherence” refers to:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"the process by which a classical bit is set to either 0 or 1."},{"k":"B","t":"the loss of a qubit’s delicate quantum properties due to unwanted interaction with its environment."},{"k":"C","t":"a type of quantum algorithm used for cryptography."},{"k":"D","t":"the deliberate cooling of a quantum computer’s hardware."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This is the passage’s explicit definition of the term. (A) describes a classical, not quantum, process, unrelated to this term. (C) and (D) are unrelated, unsupported meanings not given by the passage’s usage.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-60","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p12","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The author’s overall tone toward claims about quantum computing’s near-term potential can best be described as:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"uncritically enthusiastic, endorsing all optimistic industry projections."},{"k":"B","t":"measured and clarifying, correcting popular misconceptions while acknowledging genuine, if narrower, promise alongside real open challenges."},{"k":"C","t":"entirely dismissive, treating quantum computing as a field with no real scientific merit."},{"k":"D","t":"angry and hostile toward researchers working in the field."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"The passage carefully distinguishes real promise from popular overstatement and explicitly leaves the field’s near-term trajectory as “one of the genuinely open empirical questions.” (A) is contradicted by the passage’s explicit correction of “popular coverage” overstatement. (C) is contradicted by the passage’s substantial, respectful discussion of genuine scientific promise. (D) is an unsupported, uncharacteristic tone not reflected anywhere in the passage’s measured language.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-61","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p13","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The passage’s central argument is that:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"human memory functions like an accurate video recording that never changes once formed."},{"k":"B","t":"memory is a reconstructive process vulnerable to alteration each time it is recalled, with significant practical consequences, particularly for legal testimony."},{"k":"C","t":"false memories can never be distinguished from real ones by any means whatsoever."},{"k":"D","t":"eyewitness testimony should never be used as evidence under any circumstances."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"The passage’s arc — overturning the storage metaphor, presenting reconstructive evidence, explaining the mechanism, and tracing legal implications — matches this comprehensive argument. (A) directly contradicts the passage’s central thesis, which explicitly overturns this “storage metaphor.” (C) overstates the passage’s actual, more nuanced claim about subjective indistinguishability from the rememberer’s own perspective, not an absolute claim about all possible means of distinguishing them. (D) is a specific policy conclusion never stated; the passage discusses reforms to how testimony is weighed, not its total exclusion.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-62","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p13","difficulty":"easy","stem":"According to the passage, “reconsolidation” refers to:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"the initial encoding of a new memory at the moment an event occurs."},{"k":"B","t":"the process by which a retrieved memory becomes temporarily modifiable before being re-stored."},{"k":"C","t":"a technique used by police to deliberately implant false memories."},{"k":"D","t":"the permanent, complete erasure of an old memory."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This is precisely the passage’s stated definition. (A) describes original encoding, explicitly distinguished from reconsolidation in the passage. (C) misreads the term as a deliberate technique rather than a natural cognitive process. (D) is an unsupported and inaccurate characterization — the passage describes modification and re-storage, not erasure.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-63","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p13","difficulty":"hard","stem":"The passage suggests that the reconstructive nature of memory might be evolutionarily advantageous because:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"it allows memories to remain perfectly unchanged over a lifetime."},{"k":"B","t":"it allows a memory system to incorporate newly learned, contextually relevant information over time."},{"k":"C","t":"it prevents any new information from ever being learned."},{"k":"D","t":"it eliminates the need for memory altogether."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This matches the passage’s explicit “plausible trade-off” framing — updating with “new, contextually relevant information” as a practical benefit. (A) directly contradicts the reconstructive thesis the entire passage argues for. (C) and (D) are extreme, unsupported claims contradicted by the passage’s actual, more measured framing.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-64","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p13","difficulty":"hard","stem":"Which of the following, if true, would most directly support the passage’s discussion of eyewitness testimony reform?","options":[{"k":"A","t":"A study finds that witnesses who express high confidence in their testimony are, on average, no more accurate than those expressing low confidence."},{"k":"B","t":"A study finds that all eyewitnesses are always completely accurate in every case."},{"k":"C","t":"A study finds that memory reconsolidation does not occur in any human beings."},{"k":"D","t":"A study finds that police interviews have no influence on witness recall whatsoever."}],"answer":"A","explanation":"This directly supports the passage’s specific claim that confidence “bears no reliable relationship to the memory’s actual accuracy.” (B) directly contradicts the passage’s entire thesis about memory’s fallibility. (C) directly contradicts the passage’s central mechanism explaining the phenomenon. (D) directly contradicts the passage’s explicit discussion of misleading information (including question wording) shaping recall.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-65","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p13","difficulty":"easy","stem":"As used in the passage, “labile” most nearly means:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"permanently fixed and unchangeable."},{"k":"B","t":"prone to change or alteration; unstable."},{"k":"C","t":"related to laboratory scientific equipment."},{"k":"D","t":"emotionally distressing."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"In context (“neural circuits… become temporarily labile — open to modification”), the passage itself glosses the word as meaning open to modification. (A) is the direct opposite of the intended meaning. (C) and (D) are unrelated, unsupported senses of the word.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-66","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p14","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The passage’s central argument is that:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"colonial trade was entirely coercive with absolutely no economic agency exercised by colonized populations."},{"k":"B","t":"revisionist economic history has substantially challenged the earlier, more benign framing of colonial trade by emphasizing the coercive political structures underlying it, without denying colonized populations’ genuine economic agency."},{"k":"C","t":"colonial trade patterns had no relationship whatsoever to contemporary global economic inequality."},{"k":"D","t":"the earlier, benign framing of colonial trade remains the most accurate account available."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"The passage’s careful structure — presenting revisionist evidence while explicitly rejecting the “cruder” version that denies agency entirely — matches this nuanced argument. (A) directly contradicts the passage’s explicit third paragraph acknowledging “considerable, well-documented economic agency.” (C) directly contradicts the passage’s final paragraph, which explicitly connects this history to “contemporary debates” about inequality. (D) directly contradicts the passage’s central, sustained critique of that framing.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-67","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p14","difficulty":"easy","stem":"According to the passage, the revisionist scholarship’s key contribution was to:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"prove that no colonial economic activity ever involved genuine market exchange."},{"k":"B","t":"take seriously the question of what coercive political conditions shaped the apparent structure of colonial trade."},{"k":"C","t":"argue that colonizing powers provided no infrastructure investment of any kind."},{"k":"D","t":"demonstrate that colonized populations had no interest in economic activity."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This is precisely the specific reframing the passage attributes to the revisionist scholarship in the first paragraph. (A) overstates into an absolute the passage’s more measured claim about power asymmetries shaping, not eliminating, exchange. (C) directly contradicts the passage’s discussion of railway and port infrastructure investment (albeit built for extractive purposes). (D) directly contradicts the passage’s explicit discussion of agency and adaptation.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-68","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p14","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The passage’s discussion of infrastructure investment (paragraph 2) is used primarily to:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"argue that colonizing powers were generous in developing colonized regions."},{"k":"B","t":"illustrate how infrastructure was designed to serve extraction rather than genuine internal regional economic development."},{"k":"C","t":"prove that railways had no economic function of any kind."},{"k":"D","t":"show that colonized regions had superior infrastructure to colonizing countries."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This is exactly the point the example is used to support — infrastructure “designed overwhelmingly to move raw materials outward… rather than to connect and develop internal regional economies.” (A) directly contradicts the critical framing of the example. (C) misreads the example, which acknowledges railways had a function, just an extractive rather than developmental one. (D) is a claim never made in the passage.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-69","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p14","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The author addresses a potential objection to the revisionist view in paragraph 3 primarily by:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"ignoring it entirely and refusing to engage with the counterargument."},{"k":"B","t":"acknowledging genuine economic agency existed while arguing this doesn’t rescue the earlier, more benign framing, since available opportunities were themselves shaped by coercion."},{"k":"C","t":"conceding that the revisionist view is entirely mistaken."},{"k":"D","t":"arguing that colonized populations had complete economic freedom throughout the colonial period."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This precisely describes the passage’s careful, concessive-but-ultimately-unyielding argumentative move in paragraph 3. (A) directly contradicts the passage’s substantial, explicit engagement with the objection. (C) directly contradicts the passage’s continued defense of the revisionist framing after raising the objection. (D) is an extreme claim the passage explicitly avoids making.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-70","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p14","difficulty":"easy","stem":"As used in the passage, “comparative advantage” most nearly refers to:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"a military strategy used during colonial conquest."},{"k":"B","t":"an economic concept describing a region’s relative efficiency advantage in producing particular goods, used here to question whether trade patterns reflected this or were imposed."},{"k":"C","t":"a legal term describing property rights under colonial law."},{"k":"D","t":"a specific tax imposed by colonial administrations."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This is the standard economic meaning being invoked and directly questioned throughout the passage — whether colonial specialization reflected genuine comparative advantage or was “actively and deliberately imposed.” (A), (C), and (D) are unrelated or incorrect senses not supported by the passage’s usage.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-71","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p15","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The passage’s central argument is that:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"oral tradition is always more reliable than written records in every possible case."},{"k":"B","t":"both oral and written historical sources require the same kind of specific, source-critical scrutiny, rather than assuming written sources are inherently more reliable."},{"k":"C","t":"written records should be abandoned entirely in favor of oral history."},{"k":"D","t":"oral traditions from West African societies have never been checked against any independent evidence."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"The passage’s explicit conclusion — “the more useful methodological lesson… is not that either… deserve a blanket presumption of superior reliability” — matches this precisely. (A) directly contradicts the passage’s explicit caution against treating them as “simply interchangeable” or claiming blanket superiority for oral tradition either. (C) is an extreme position never suggested anywhere in the passage. (D) directly contradicts the passage’s explicit discussion of checking oral traditions “against independent written sources.”","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-72","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p15","difficulty":"easy","stem":"According to the passage, the specific oral transmission methods described (paragraph 2) were designed to:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"deliberately introduce variation into historical accounts over time."},{"k":"B","t":"catch and correct the kind of transmission errors a naive model of oral tradition would predict as inevitable."},{"k":"C","t":"prevent any written records from ever being created."},{"k":"D","t":"replace genealogical information with fictional narratives."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This is precisely the passage’s stated purpose of these structured, redundant methods. (A) is the opposite of their described purpose — accuracy preservation, not deliberate variation. (C) and (D) are claims never made anywhere in the passage.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-73","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p15","difficulty":"easy","stem":"The passage identifies which specific vulnerability as unique to oral tradition, as compared to written records?","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Oral traditions can never preserve genealogical information accurately."},{"k":"B","t":"Oral traditions, as living performed traditions, are susceptible to gradual reshaping to serve present political needs during ongoing performance."},{"k":"C","t":"Oral traditions are always immediately destroyed by colonial administrations."},{"k":"D","t":"Oral traditions cannot be cross-checked against any other source."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This is precisely the specific vulnerability the passage attributes to oral tradition’s nature as a “living, actively performed” tradition. (A) directly contradicts the passage’s evidence of accurate genealogical preservation. (C) is an unsupported claim never made. (D) directly contradicts the passage’s explicit discussion of cross-checking against written sources and archaeological evidence.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-74","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p15","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The final paragraph’s central claim is that treating “written” as inherently more reliable than “oral”:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"is scientifically well-supported by all available evidence."},{"k":"B","t":"mistakenly conflates a source’s medium of transmission with its actual evidentiary reliability."},{"k":"C","t":"has never been questioned by any historian."},{"k":"D","t":"applies equally well to every society throughout history."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This is the passage’s precise, explicit closing claim. (A) directly contradicts the passage’s sustained critique of this assumption throughout. (C) directly contradicts the passage’s extensive discussion of historians and anthropologists challenging this assumption. (D) directly contradicts the passage’s specific point that this correlation was particular to “the specific societies where modern academic historical scholarship first developed.”","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-75","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p15","difficulty":"easy","stem":"As used in the passage, “epistemic authority” most nearly means:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"legal power to enforce a written contract."},{"k":"B","t":"the presumed credibility or reliability granted to a source of knowledge."},{"k":"C","t":"the political power held by colonial administrations."},{"k":"D","t":"the physical durability of written documents compared to spoken words."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"In context (“written records have long enjoyed a kind of default epistemic authority”), the phrase refers to a presumed status of reliability or credibility as a knowledge source. (A) and (C) are unrelated senses involving legal or political power, not knowledge credibility. (D) narrows the term to a physical property, which is a separate point the passage makes elsewhere, not what “epistemic authority” itself means.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-76","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p16","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The passage’s primary purpose is to:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"argue that all museum holdings from colonized regions should be immediately and unconditionally returned."},{"k":"B","t":"examine the justifications museums have offered for retaining contested artifacts and the substantial counterarguments each has faced."},{"k":"C","t":"prove that repatriation movements have entirely failed to achieve any policy change."},{"k":"D","t":"argue that formerly colonized nations lack adequate infrastructure to preserve returned artifacts."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"The passage’s structure — presenting three distinct museum justifications, then substantial counterarguments to each, then evolving policy — matches this balanced, analytical purpose. (A) is a specific advocacy position never taken by the passage, which presents multiple perspectives without fully endorsing one. (C) is directly contradicted by the passage’s explicit discussion of “several prominent museums and national governments” shifting policy. (D) directly contradicts the passage’s discussion of this argument having “grown markedly weaker” given new infrastructure.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-77","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p16","difficulty":"easy","stem":"According to the passage, critics argue the “universal museum” justification is weakened because:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"it was formulated largely after most relevant acquisitions had already taken place, functioning as retroactive justification."},{"k":"B","t":"universal museums have never educated anyone about other cultures."},{"k":"C","t":"it is based entirely on forged historical documents."},{"k":"D","t":"no museum has ever used this argument in practice."}],"answer":"A","explanation":"This is precisely the specific critique the passage attributes to critics of this argument. (B) is an extreme claim never made in the passage, which doesn’t dispute the museums’ educational value generally, only the argument’s historical timing. (C) and (D) are unsupported claims never made in the passage.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-78","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p16","difficulty":"hard","stem":"The passage suggests that the conservation-capacity argument has weakened over time primarily because:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"all artifacts have become significantly more durable due to new preservation technology."},{"k":"B","t":"formerly colonized nations have increasingly built museum and conservation infrastructure capable of professional-standard preservation."},{"k":"C","t":"museums in Europe and North America have lost their own conservation capabilities."},{"k":"D","t":"international law now prohibits any form of artifact conservation."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This is precisely the specific development the passage cites as weakening this argument. (A) misattributes the change to artifact durability rather than the actual cited cause (new infrastructure elsewhere). (C) and (D) are claims never made anywhere in the passage.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-79","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p16","difficulty":"hard","stem":"The passage characterizes the current, case-by-case repatriation approach as:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"fully satisfying both restitution advocates and museum defenders."},{"k":"B","t":"satisfying few observers on either side completely, given differing concerns about pace, criteria, and precedent."},{"k":"C","t":"a complete failure that has resulted in zero artifact returns."},{"k":"D","t":"something no museum has actually attempted to implement."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This is precisely the passage’s explicit characterization of the current approach’s reception. (A) directly contradicts “satisfies few observers on either side… completely.” (C) directly contradicts the passage’s explicit discussion of “negotiated, case-by-case return of specific objects” actually occurring. (D) is directly contradicted by the same discussion of museums actively “shifting policy.”","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-80","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p16","difficulty":"easy","stem":"As used in the passage, “provenance” most nearly means:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"the current market value of an artifact."},{"k":"B","t":"the origin and subsequent history of ownership of an object."},{"k":"C","t":"the physical material an artifact is made from."},{"k":"D","t":"the museum currently displaying an artifact."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This is the standard meaning of the term, matched by its context (“sorting any specific object’s actual provenance into one of these categories” of acquisition circumstance). (A), (C), and (D) are unrelated or incorrect senses not supported by the passage’s usage, which is specifically about acquisition history.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-81","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p17","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The passage’s primary purpose is to:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"argue that language death is a purely natural, unproblematic process that requires no attention or concern."},{"k":"B","t":"explain the causes and consequences of language extinction, and distinguish between the related but distinct efforts of documentation and revitalization."},{"k":"C","t":"prove that Hebrew’s revival can be easily replicated for every endangered language worldwide."},{"k":"D","t":"argue that all state-mandated schooling should be immediately abolished."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"The passage’s structure — causes of language death, the case for its significance, documentation versus revitalization as distinct goals — matches this comprehensive, explanatory purpose. (A) is directly contradicted by the passage’s substantial case for “actively caring about this loss.” (C) directly contradicts the passage’s explicit statement that this combination is “unlikely to be replicable at anything approaching the scale” needed generally. (D) is a policy position never suggested in the passage.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-82","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p17","difficulty":"easy","stem":"According to the passage, why might a language’s disappearance represent an irrecoverable loss?","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Because all languages are grammatically identical to one another."},{"k":"B","t":"Because a language encodes accumulated cultural and cognitive knowledge that is often not straightforwardly translatable or recoverable once lost."},{"k":"C","t":"Because languages can always be perfectly reconstructed from written records alone."},{"k":"D","t":"Because linguists have never attempted to document endangered languages."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This is precisely the passage’s stated case for the loss’s significance. (A) directly contradicts the passage’s discussion of genuinely different grammatical structures across languages. (C) directly contradicts the passage’s explicit statement that documentation does not fully recover what is lost. (D) directly contradicts the passage’s extensive discussion of expanding documentation efforts.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-83","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p17","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The passage distinguishes “documentation” from “revitalization” by noting that:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"they are entirely unrelated processes with no connection to one another."},{"k":"B","t":"documentation preserves a linguistic record, while revitalization aims to restore or increase a language’s number of active, everyday speakers — a more difficult, distinct goal."},{"k":"C","t":"documentation is always more difficult to achieve than revitalization."},{"k":"D","t":"revitalization requires no political will or institutional resources."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This is precisely the passage’s explicit distinction between the two, made clear in the transition between paragraphs 3 and 4. (A) directly contradicts the passage’s statement that they are “frequently complementary and often pursued together in practice.” (C) directly reverses the passage’s clear framing of revitalization as “the more ambitious and considerably more difficult project.” (D) directly contradicts the passage’s explicit list of what revitalization requires.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-84","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p17","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The Hebrew example is used in the passage to:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"prove that language revitalization is generally easy to achieve for any language."},{"k":"B","t":"illustrate a genuine, well-documented success in large-scale language revitalization, while the passage cautions this success is difficult to replicate broadly."},{"k":"C","t":"show that liturgical languages should never be used in everyday speech."},{"k":"D","t":"argue that Hebrew should be considered an endangered language today."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"The passage explicitly frames this as a “genuinely inspiring success” immediately followed by an explicit caution about the difficulty of replicating the required conditions elsewhere. (A) directly contradicts this explicit caution about replicability. (C) misreads the example, which describes a shift from liturgical to everyday use as a success, not an argument against liturgical use itself. (D) directly contradicts the passage’s use of Hebrew as an example of successful revival, the opposite of an endangered language today.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-85","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p17","difficulty":"easy","stem":"As used in the passage, “vernacular” most nearly means:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"a formal, ceremonial style of writing used only in religious contexts."},{"k":"B","t":"the everyday, ordinary spoken language of a particular community."},{"k":"C","t":"a dead language no longer used by anyone."},{"k":"D","t":"a written alphabet system."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"In context (“Hebrew’s large-scale revival as an everyday spoken vernacular”), the term clearly refers to ordinary, everyday spoken language, explicitly contrasted with prior “liturgical and scholarly use.” (A) is close to the opposite of the intended meaning — vernacular contrasts with formal/liturgical use here. (C) directly contradicts the described revival into active use. (D) is an unrelated, incorrect sense of the word.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-86","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p18","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The passage’s primary purpose is to:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"argue that all modernist urban planning projects were secretly, deliberately designed to fail."},{"k":"B","t":"trace how observational critique of modernist urban renewal revealed the undervalued social function of dense, mixed-use neighborhoods, while noting this lesson remains incompletely applied."},{"k":"C","t":"prove that no urban planning has ever successfully improved any city."},{"k":"D","t":"advocate for the complete abolition of all future urban planning regulation."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"The passage’s arc — modernist confidence, the observational critique that followed, the “eyes on the street” explanation, and the incomplete, ongoing nature of the resulting shift — matches this analytical purpose precisely. (A) is an unsupported, uncharitable claim never made; the passage describes miscalibrated confidence, not deliberate sabotage. (C) overstates a critique of specific projects into a blanket claim never made. (D) is a specific policy position never suggested in the passage.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-87","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p18","difficulty":"easy","stem":"According to the passage, “eyes on the street” refers to:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"formal, government-installed security cameras."},{"k":"B","t":"the informal, low-level social surveillance generated naturally by mixed-use neighborhoods with steady foot traffic throughout the day."},{"k":"C","t":"a specific law requiring windows to face public streets."},{"k":"D","t":"a planning theory advocating for wider, car-oriented roads."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This is precisely the passage’s explicit definition of the term. (A) directly contradicts the passage’s specific description of this as “informal” and “unplanned,” distinct from formal security measures. (C) and (D) are unsupported, incorrect characterizations not given by the passage.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-88","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p18","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The monumental plaza example (paragraph 3) is used in the passage to:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"show that all large public plazas are poorly designed and should never be built."},{"k":"B","t":"illustrate how a space lacking ongoing, informal reasons for people to be present can become functionally dead despite impressive planned design."},{"k":"C","t":"prove that office towers should never be built near public plazas."},{"k":"D","t":"demonstrate that architectural renderings are always inaccurate."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This is precisely the specific point the example illustrates — the gap between planned appearance and actual, sustained use given the absence of “ongoing reason to actually be there.” (A) and (C) overstate the specific example into sweeping, general prohibitions never suggested. (D) misreads the example’s actual point, which is about use patterns, not rendering accuracy generally.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-89","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p18","difficulty":"hard","stem":"The passage’s final paragraph suggests that the shift toward mixed-use planning has been:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"total and complete across all urban development globally."},{"k":"B","t":"influential in professional planning theory but incompletely applied in actual ongoing practice."},{"k":"C","t":"entirely rejected by contemporary urban planners."},{"k":"D","t":"irrelevant to how cities are currently being built."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This is precisely the passage’s explicit, hedged final assessment — influential “in principle” but not consistently applied “in the actual, ongoing practice.” (A) directly contradicts the passage’s explicit statement that this “would… be an overstatement.” (C) and (D) both directly contradict the passage’s discussion of the critique’s substantial, ongoing influence on planning theory.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-90","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p18","difficulty":"easy","stem":"As used in the passage, “sterile” (paragraph 2) most nearly means:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"medically clean and free of germs."},{"k":"B","t":"lacking vitality, activity, or genuine social function."},{"k":"C","t":"biologically unable to reproduce."},{"k":"D","t":"extremely expensive to construct."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"In context (“functionally sterile despite their considerable design sophistication”), the word describes a lack of vibrant social use and function, not a literal medical or biological property. (A) and (C) are the word’s literal, unrelated senses not supported by this figurative context. (D) is an unsupported, unrelated meaning.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-91","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p19","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The passage’s primary purpose is to:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"argue that all first-person narratives in literature should be considered completely untrustworthy and therefore worthless."},{"k":"B","t":"examine how the unreliable narrator technique works craft-wise, and explore its deeper thematic significance beyond mere formal trickery."},{"k":"C","t":"prove that unreliable narrators are a recent literary invention with no historical precedent."},{"k":"D","t":"argue that third-person narration is always superior to first-person narration."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"The passage’s structure — explaining the craft challenge, the “gap” technique, and the deeper thematic payoff — matches this analytical, appreciative purpose. (A) overstates the passage’s actual, more nuanced point about narrative construction into a dismissive claim never made. (C) is a claim about historical origins never made or discussed in the passage. (D) is a comparison never made anywhere in the passage.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-92","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p19","difficulty":"easy","stem":"According to the passage, an author who reveals a narrator’s unreliability “too heavily, too early” risks:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"making the reader trust the narrator even more than intended."},{"k":"B","t":"causing the reader to disengage from the narrative voice and read the work as a decoding exercise rather than an immersive story."},{"k":"C","t":"making the novel too short to be published."},{"k":"D","t":"eliminating the need for any plot at all."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This is precisely the specific risk the passage describes for over-signaling unreliability too early. (A) is the opposite outcome of what the passage describes. (C) and (D) are unrelated, unsupported claims never made in the passage.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-93","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p19","difficulty":"easy","stem":"The term “the gap,” as used in the passage, refers to:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"a plot hole or unresolved inconsistency the author failed to address."},{"k":"B","t":"the space between what a narrator explicitly claims and what other incidental details in the narrative inadvertently reveal."},{"k":"C","t":"the physical break between chapters in a novel."},{"k":"D","t":"the difference in page count between two competing editions of a book."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This is precisely the passage’s stated definition of the term as used by literary critics. (A) misreads the term as an authorial flaw, when the passage frames it as a deliberate technique. (C) and (D) are unrelated, unsupported meanings never suggested by the passage.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-94","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p19","difficulty":"hard","stem":"The passage’s final paragraph suggests that skillfully executed unreliable narration:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"has no connection to how people understand their own lives and memories."},{"k":"B","t":"dramatizes, through narrative technique itself, the broader idea that all first-person accounts, including our own self-narratives, are inherently partial and constructed."},{"k":"C","t":"proves that all novels with unreliable narrators are objectively superior to those without."},{"k":"D","t":"is primarily valued for making novels more difficult to summarize."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This is precisely the passage’s explicit closing claim about the technique’s deeper significance. (A) directly contradicts the passage’s explicit connection to “the internal narratives each of us constructs… about our own lives.” (C) and (D) are unsupported, overstated claims never made in the passage.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-95","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p19","difficulty":"easy","stem":"As used in the passage, “recontextualization” most nearly means:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"the physical relocation of a story’s setting to a new place."},{"k":"B","t":"a reframing that changes how earlier information in a narrative should be understood."},{"k":"C","t":"a legal process for republishing an out-of-print book."},{"k":"D","t":"the translation of a text into a different language."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"In context (“a carefully earned recontextualization of everything that preceded it”), the term refers to a reveal that reshapes understanding of earlier narrative content. (A), (C), and (D) are unrelated, unsupported senses not matching the literary-critical context.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-96","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p20","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The passage’s primary purpose is to:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"argue that literary translation is impossible and should never be attempted."},{"k":"B","t":"examine the deep, structural challenges of literary translation and the competing theoretical frameworks developed to address them."},{"k":"C","t":"prove that domesticating translation is always the superior approach."},{"k":"D","t":"argue that only bilingual readers should be permitted to read translated literature."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"The passage’s structure — the fluency/fidelity paradox, the deeper-than-vocabulary nature of the challenge, and the domesticating/foreignizing framework — matches this analytical purpose. (A) directly contradicts the passage’s substantial, serious engagement with how translation is actually practiced and theorized, not dismissed as impossible. (C) directly contradicts the passage’s explicit statement that neither approach is “universally ‘correct.’” (D) is a position never suggested anywhere in the passage.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-97","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p20","difficulty":"easy","stem":"According to the passage, focusing the “untranslatability” discussion on isolated vocabulary examples:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"fully captures the true nature and scope of the translation challenge."},{"k":"B","t":"somewhat understates the deeper, more pervasive nature of the challenge, which operates at every level of a text including syntax and connotation."},{"k":"C","t":"is a discussion translation theorists have entirely abandoned as irrelevant."},{"k":"D","t":"applies only to translations between closely related languages."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This is precisely the passage’s explicit critique of the narrower, vocabulary-focused framing. (A) directly contradicts the passage’s stated critique of this framing as understating the real challenge. (C) is directly contradicted, since the passage continues to engage with vocabulary examples as a real, if incomplete, part of the discussion. (D) is a claim never made anywhere in the passage.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-98","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p20","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The passage distinguishes “domesticating” from “foreignizing” translation by explaining that:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"domesticating translation prioritizes natural readability, while foreignizing translation deliberately preserves linguistic and cultural strangeness."},{"k":"B","t":"foreignizing translation is always more accurate than domesticating translation."},{"k":"C","t":"domesticating translation was invented more recently than foreignizing translation."},{"k":"D","t":"the two approaches produce identical results in every case."}],"answer":"A","explanation":"This is precisely the passage’s explicit definitional distinction between the two approaches. (B) is a claim never made — the passage explicitly avoids ranking one as universally superior. (C) is an unsupported historical claim never made in the passage. (D) directly contradicts the passage’s entire framing of these as genuinely distinct approaches.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-99","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p20","difficulty":"hard","stem":"The passage’s final paragraph suggests that the choice between translation approaches should account for:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"only the personal preference of the individual translator."},{"k":"B","t":"the specific power relationship between the source and target languages and cultures, among other contextual factors."},{"k":"C","t":"exclusively the commercial marketability of the translated work."},{"k":"D","t":"nothing beyond strict grammatical accuracy."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This is precisely the specific factor the passage highlights as “perhaps most importantly and most easily overlooked.” (A), (C), and (D) all narrow the passage’s multi-factor, explicitly broader consideration (“purpose,” “readership,” “power relationship”) down to a single, unsupported criterion.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-100","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p20","difficulty":"easy","stem":"As used in the passage, “connotative weight” most nearly refers to:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"the literal, dictionary definition of a word."},{"k":"B","t":"the additional associative and emotional meaning a word carries beyond its literal definition, from its history of use."},{"k":"C","t":"the number of syllables in a given word."},{"k":"D","t":"the grammatical category (noun, verb, etc.) a word belongs to."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"In context (“the connotative weight a given word… carries by virtue of its accumulated prior use… within that language’s own literary tradition”), the phrase clearly refers to associative meaning beyond literal definition. (A) directly contradicts “connotative,” which specifically means beyond the literal/denotative meaning. (C) and (D) are unrelated, unsupported senses not matched by the passage’s context.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-101","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p21","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The passage’s primary purpose is to:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"argue that all literary criticism is entirely subjective and therefore worthless."},{"k":"B","t":"present two conflicting conceptions of critical authority, examine the difficulties with each taken to an extreme, and propose a more defensible middle position."},{"k":"C","t":"prove that credentialed critics are always more insightful than general readers."},{"k":"D","t":"argue that popular and genre fiction should never be taken seriously by critics."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"The passage’s structure — presenting the credentialist view, the suspicious/relativist counter-view, difficulties with both extremes, and a proposed middle position — matches this balanced, analytical purpose precisely. (A) directly contradicts the passage’s final paragraph, which explicitly argues against pure relativism. (C) directly contradicts the passage’s substantial critique of pure credentialism. (D) directly contradicts the passage’s explicit criticism of dismissing such traditions as a flaw of traditional criticism.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-102","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p21","difficulty":"easy","stem":"According to the passage, the “credentialist” view of critical authority risks, in its extreme form:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"becoming too open to entirely new and unfamiliar forms of literature."},{"k":"B","t":"calcifying into a closed system that fails to recognize innovation unresembling already-established, validated forms."},{"k":"C","t":"eliminating the need for any formal literary education."},{"k":"D","t":"treating all works of literature as equally valuable."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This is precisely the specific risk the passage attributes to this view taken to its logical extreme. (A) is the opposite of the described risk, which is excessive closedness, not openness. (C) directly contradicts the credentialist view’s actual emphasis on formal training. (D) describes the opposing, relativist view’s risk, not this one.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-103","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p21","difficulty":"hard","stem":"The passage suggests that a purely relativist view of aesthetic judgment struggles to account for:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"the fact that all critics agree completely on every judgment."},{"k":"B","t":"the persistent, widely shared intuition that some aesthetic judgments are better reasoned and more perceptive than others."},{"k":"C","t":"the existence of formal literary education programs."},{"k":"D","t":"the popularity of genre fiction among general readers."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This is precisely the specific difficulty the passage identifies for the relativist position. (A) is an overstated claim never made — critics disagreeing is compatible with the passage’s point, which is about a shared underlying intuition, not unanimous agreement. (C) and (D) are unrelated to the specific difficulty being described.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-104","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p21","difficulty":"hard","stem":"The passage’s proposed “middle position” characterizes critical authority as:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"an unquestionable credential immune to any challenge."},{"k":"B","t":"entirely illusory and without any genuine basis."},{"k":"C","t":"earned, situated expertise that remains open to challenge and revision by better-reasoned competing accounts."},{"k":"D","t":"something that depends solely on a critic’s job title."}],"answer":"C","explanation":"This is precisely the passage’s explicit description of the proposed middle position. (A) directly contradicts the passage’s explicit statement that this expertise “remains itself always open to challenge… rather than being treated… as simply final.” (B) describes the relativist extreme the middle position explicitly rejects. (D) is an unsupported, overly narrow characterization not given by the passage.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-105","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p21","difficulty":"easy","stem":"As used in the passage, “calcifying” most nearly means:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"becoming increasingly flexible and adaptable over time."},{"k":"B","t":"hardening or becoming rigid and resistant to change."},{"k":"C","t":"dissolving completely and ceasing to exist."},{"k":"D","t":"growing rapidly in size or influence."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"In context (“risks calcifying into a closed, self-referential system”), the word describes a process of rigidification, matching the described risk of a system becoming closed and resistant to recognizing new forms. (A) is the opposite of the intended meaning. (C) and (D) are unrelated, unsupported senses not matching this context.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-106","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p22","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The passage’s primary purpose is to:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"argue that genre fiction is objectively superior to literary fiction in every case."},{"k":"B","t":"examine the literary/genre fiction hierarchy, present challenges to it, and note the hierarchy’s persistence despite growing critical reassessment."},{"k":"C","t":"prove that no meaningful distinction between literary and genre fiction has ever existed."},{"k":"D","t":"advocate for eliminating all genre categories from bookstores and libraries."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"The passage’s structure — describing the hierarchy, presenting two distinct lines of critique, and noting uneven but real institutional change alongside persistence — matches this analytical purpose. (A) is an extreme reversal never argued by the passage, which questions the hierarchy without simply inverting it. (C) overstates the passage’s actual, more measured claim about the boundary being unstable and retrospectively constructed, not entirely nonexistent. (D) is a policy position never suggested in the passage.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-107","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p22","difficulty":"easy","stem":"According to the passage, the first line of criticism against the literary/genre hierarchy points out that:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"genre fiction has never been commercially successful."},{"k":"B","t":"many now-canonized literary classics were originally marketed and received as genre fiction."},{"k":"C","t":"literary fiction did not exist as a category until the twenty-first century."},{"k":"D","t":"all genre fiction eventually becomes literary fiction over time."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This is precisely the specific historical point the passage attributes to this line of criticism. (A) directly contradicts the passage’s implicit point, since the examples given were popular works, and is unrelated to the specific critique cited. (C) and (D) are unsupported, overstated claims never made in the passage.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-108","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p22","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The mystery novel example (paragraph 3) is used in the passage to:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"prove that mystery novels are more artistically valuable than literary novels."},{"k":"B","t":"illustrate that genre fiction can demonstrate its own considerable, differently-oriented craft, not straightforwardly inferior to literary fiction’s different kind of craft."},{"k":"C","t":"show that mystery novels never contain any suspense or narrative resolution."},{"k":"D","t":"argue that literary prizes should exclusively reward mystery novels going forward."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This is precisely the specific, carefully balanced point the example is used to illustrate — “neither straightforwardly inferior nor superior… but simply differently oriented.” (A) overstates the passage’s careful, balanced claim into a one-sided superiority claim it explicitly avoids making. (C) directly contradicts the passage’s description of these craft elements being present and well-executed. (D) is an extreme policy position never suggested anywhere in the passage.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-109","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p22","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The phrase “transcends the genre,” as discussed in the final paragraph, is presented by the author as:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"a phrase that has entirely disappeared from contemporary critical writing."},{"k":"B","t":"a seemingly complimentary phrase that nonetheless reveals and perpetuates the underlying hierarchy it claims to move beyond."},{"k":"C","t":"proof that the literary/genre hierarchy has been fully resolved and no longer exists."},{"k":"D","t":"an insult that no genre author would ever want applied to their work."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This is precisely the passage’s explicit, nuanced characterization of the phrase’s function. (A) directly contradicts the passage’s description of it as “genuinely common” in current use. (C) directly contradicts the entire point of citing the phrase as evidence of the hierarchy’s persistence. (D) is an unsupported claim about author reception never discussed in the passage.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-110","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p22","difficulty":"easy","stem":"As used in the passage, “valorized” most nearly means:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"financially devalued or made cheaper."},{"k":"B","t":"assigned or celebrated with special value, worth, or prestige."},{"k":"C","t":"legally prohibited or banned."},{"k":"D","t":"translated into a foreign language."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"In context (“the specific, valorized hallmarks of literary fiction”), the word describes qualities given elevated status or prestige within the described hierarchy. (A) is close to the opposite of the intended meaning. (C) and (D) are unrelated, unsupported senses not matching the context.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-111","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p23","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The passage’s primary purpose is to:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"argue that all humans are equally overconfident in every domain of judgment without exception."},{"k":"B","t":"distinguish between three related but analytically separable forms of overconfidence, and identify feedback quality as a key factor moderating their occurrence."},{"k":"C","t":"prove that professional weather forecasters never make inaccurate predictions."},{"k":"D","t":"argue that overconfidence is a fixed personality trait some people simply have more of than others."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"The passage’s structure — defining overestimation, overplacement, and overprecision separately, then identifying feedback quality as the key moderating factor — matches this analytical purpose precisely. (A) directly contradicts the passage’s explicit discussion of domain-specific variability. (C) overstates a claim never made — the passage discusses lower rates of overconfidence in this field, not the elimination of all forecasting error. (D) directly contradicts the passage’s explicit statement that overconfidence is “less a single fixed personality trait… and more a systematic, predictable cognitive tendency” shaped by context.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-112","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p23","difficulty":"easy","stem":"According to the passage, “overplacement” specifically refers to:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"believing one’s own performance is better than it objectively is, in absolute terms."},{"k":"B","t":"believing one is better than other people specifically, relative to a comparison group."},{"k":"C","t":"expressing excessive certainty about the accuracy of a specific belief or estimate."},{"k":"D","t":"the tendency to change one’s mind frequently based on new evidence."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This is precisely the passage’s explicit definition, distinguished clearly from the other two forms. (A) describes “overestimation,” a distinct, separately defined term. (C) describes “overprecision,” also separately and distinctly defined. (D) is an unrelated concept never discussed in the passage.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-113","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p23","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The driving skill example (paragraph 3) is used in the passage to illustrate:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"that all three forms of overconfidence always occur together in the same person."},{"k":"B","t":"that a person can avoid one form of overconfidence (overplacement) in a domain while still exhibiting another (overprecision) in that same domain."},{"k":"C","t":"that driving is the most dangerous activity most people regularly engage in."},{"k":"D","t":"that professional drivers are less overconfident than amateur drivers."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This is precisely the specific point the example is constructed to illustrate — the domain-specific dissociation between the different forms of overconfidence. (A) is the opposite of what the example demonstrates. (C) and (D) are unrelated claims never made using this example.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-114","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p23","difficulty":"hard","stem":"The passage suggests that long-range political and economic forecasting shows relatively higher rates of overconfidence primarily because:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"political and economic forecasters are less intelligent than chess players."},{"k":"B","t":"feedback in this domain is often delayed, ambiguous, or easily rationalized away after an inaccurate prediction."},{"k":"C","t":"political and economic events are entirely random and unpredictable."},{"k":"D","t":"forecasters in this domain receive no financial compensation for their predictions."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This is precisely the specific mechanism the passage identifies for this domain’s comparatively higher overconfidence. (A) is an unsupported, uncharitable claim never made in the passage. (C) overstates a claim never made — the passage discusses feedback quality, not a claim about total unpredictability. (D) is an unrelated, unsupported claim never discussed.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-115","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p23","difficulty":"easy","stem":"As used in the passage, “moderating factor” most nearly means:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"a factor that completely eliminates a phenomenon under all circumstances."},{"k":"B","t":"a factor that influences the strength or occurrence of a phenomenon without being its sole cause."},{"k":"C","t":"a person who mediates a formal debate or discussion."},{"k":"D","t":"a financial regulation imposed by a government body."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"In context (“the single most consistent and well-replicated moderating factor”), the phrase refers to a variable that shapes the degree to which overconfidence occurs, matching standard usage in psychological research. (A) overstates the term into complete elimination, which the passage doesn’t claim. (C) and (D) are unrelated senses of “moderator/moderating” not supported by this specific research context.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-116","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p24","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The passage’s primary purpose is to:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"prove that institutional trust has increased steadily across all wealthy democracies in recent decades."},{"k":"B","t":"document the pattern of declining institutional trust, examine its uneven distribution and competing explanations, and note its real-world consequences and unresolved interpretation."},{"k":"C","t":"argue that scientific institutions are the least trustworthy of all major institutions."},{"k":"D","t":"advocate for the immediate abolition of all national governments."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"The passage’s comprehensive structure — the pattern, its unevenness, competing explanations, consequences, and an explicitly unresolved final question — matches this analytical purpose precisely. (A) directly contradicts the passage’s central finding of a “sustained, measurable decline.” (C) directly contradicts the passage’s statement that scientific institutions have “held up somewhat better on average” compared to other tracked institutions. (D) is a policy position never suggested anywhere in the passage.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-117","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p24","difficulty":"easy","stem":"According to the passage, the decline in trust toward national government is best characterized as:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"uniform and identical in pace across every wealthy democracy studied."},{"k":"B","t":"closely correlated with specific national events like scandals or policy failures, rather than a single uniform global trend."},{"k":"C","t":"entirely unrelated to any specific historical events."},{"k":"D","t":"less severe than the decline in trust toward scientific institutions."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This is precisely the passage’s explicit characterization of this specific decline’s pattern. (A) directly contradicts the passage’s explicit statement that pace and severity “vary considerably by country.” (C) directly contradicts the passage’s explicit correlation with “specific, identifiable national events.” (D) directly reverses the passage’s comparative claim, which states government trust “declined more sharply” than most other categories.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-118","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p24","difficulty":"easy","stem":"The passage identifies which of the following as a proposed explanation for declining institutional trust?","options":[{"k":"A","t":"A global decrease in literacy rates across wealthy democracies."},{"k":"B","t":"Increased information access making institutional failures and misconduct more visible than in earlier eras."},{"k":"C","t":"A decline in the total number of institutions operating worldwide."},{"k":"D","t":"Increased trust in scientific expertise specifically."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This is explicitly listed as one of the passage’s proposed explanations. (A) and (C) are claims never made anywhere in the passage. (D) is the opposite of a proposed cause — it describes an outcome, and one the passage actually complicates rather than straightforwardly affirms.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-119","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p24","difficulty":"hard","stem":"The passage’s final paragraph presents which of the following as a genuinely unresolved question?","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Whether institutional trust has declined at all in recent decades."},{"k":"B","t":"Whether declining trust reflects a rational response to institutional failure or a less rational, self-reinforcing cultural drift."},{"k":"C","t":"Whether political scientists study democratic institutions."},{"k":"D","t":"Whether public health guidance exists in any country."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This is precisely the specific question the passage explicitly frames as “genuinely and seriously contested… without any clear scholarly consensus yet in sight.” (A) is not in dispute in the passage — the decline itself is presented as an established finding, not a contested question. (C) and (D) are not genuine questions raised anywhere in the passage.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-120","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p24","difficulty":"easy","stem":"As used in the passage, “monocausal” most nearly means:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"attributing an outcome to a single cause, rather than multiple contributing factors."},{"k":"B","t":"relating to money or financial systems specifically."},{"k":"C","t":"occurring only once in history, without repetition."},{"k":"D","t":"impossible to measure using any research method."}],"answer":"A","explanation":"In context (“rather than any single, monocausal explanation”), the word clearly means attributing an effect to one sole cause, distinguished explicitly from a multi-factor explanation. (B), (C), and (D) are unrelated or incorrect senses not supported by the passage’s context.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-121","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p25","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The passage’s primary purpose is to:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"prove that the placebo effect can cure any disease, including cancer, if a patient believes strongly enough."},{"k":"B","t":"trace how understanding of the placebo effect has evolved from dismissive skepticism to serious study of its specific, bounded neurobiological mechanisms."},{"k":"C","t":"argue that all clinical trials should be discontinued because of the placebo effect."},{"k":"D","t":"prove that deceiving patients is always ethically justified if it produces a placebo benefit."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"The passage’s arc — early dismissive framing, physiological evidence, the pattern of variation by condition type, and the open-label finding — matches this evolutionary, analytical purpose precisely. (A) directly contradicts the passage’s explicit statement about “minimal to no measurable effect” on objective measures like tumor size. (C) is a position never suggested anywhere in the passage. (D) directly contradicts the passage’s explicit description of deception as “seriously and legitimately problematic.”","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-122","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p25","difficulty":"easy","stem":"According to the passage, early explanations of the placebo effect tended to:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"treat it as a genuine, well-documented physiological phenomenon with measurable brain changes."},{"k":"B","t":"dismissively attribute it to patient suggestibility or self-deception, rather than a genuine physiological phenomenon."},{"k":"C","t":"immediately recognize it as equally effective for both subjective and objective medical outcomes."},{"k":"D","t":"recommend it as a first-line treatment for cancer."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This is precisely the passage’s explicit description of the earlier, more dismissive framing. (A) describes the later, more rigorous understanding the passage says developed subsequently, not the early view. (C) and (D) are claims never made anywhere in the passage about the early explanations specifically.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-123","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p25","difficulty":"easy","stem":"The passage indicates that placebo effects are strongest for which category of medical conditions?","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Conditions substantially mediated by subjective, self-reported experience, such as pain and self-assessed quality of life."},{"k":"B","t":"Conditions measured exclusively through objective physiological markers like tumor size."},{"k":"C","t":"Viral infections with a directly measurable viral load."},{"k":"D","t":"All medical conditions equally, without meaningful variation."}],"answer":"A","explanation":"This is precisely the passage’s explicit description of where the effect shows “comparatively strong, well-replicated effects.” (B) and (C) directly describe the categories the passage explicitly says show “minimal to no measurable effect.” (D) directly contradicts the passage’s central point about the effect’s magnitude varying “dramatically” by condition type.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-124","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p25","difficulty":"easy","stem":"The “open-label placebo” finding is significant, according to the passage, because it:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"proves that patients can never be told the truth about their treatment."},{"k":"B","t":"complicates the earlier assumption that deception or uncertainty was strictly necessary for the placebo effect to occur, opening an ethical path to harnessing the effect."},{"k":"C","t":"shows that placebo effects only occur when patients are actively deceived."},{"k":"D","t":"demonstrates that the placebo effect has no relationship to patient belief at all."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"This is precisely the passage’s explicit description of the finding’s significance. (A) directly contradicts the passage’s actual point, which is about honest disclosure still producing an effect. (C) is the exact opposite of what this finding demonstrates. (D) directly contradicts the passage’s overall framing of the effect as related to expectation and belief throughout.","src":"doc"},{"id":"rc-125","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-p25","difficulty":"easy","stem":"As used in the passage, “mediated by” most nearly means:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"resolved through formal legal mediation or arbitration."},{"k":"B","t":"brought about or influenced through the operation of a particular process or mechanism."},{"k":"C","t":"completely unrelated to or independent of."},{"k":"D","t":"broadcast publicly through mass media channels."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"In context (“conditions substantially mediated by subjective, self-reported experience”), the phrase means these conditions are significantly shaped or influenced through this specific channel. (A) and (D) are unrelated senses of “mediate/media” not supported by this context. (C) is close to the opposite of the intended meaning.","src":"doc"},{"id":"vocab-n1","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"The minister's apology was so _______ — hedged with conditionals and passive constructions — that critics argued it amounted to no apology at all.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"mealy-mouthed"},{"k":"B","t":"effusive"},{"k":"C","t":"perfunctory"},{"k":"D","t":"contrite"}],"answer":"A","explanation":"Mealy-mouthed means unwilling to speak plainly, evasive — matches 'hedged with conditionals and passive constructions'. Perfunctory (done with minimal effort) is close but describes lack of effort, not evasiveness. Contrite (genuinely remorseful) and effusive (gushing) contradict the critics' reading."},{"id":"vocab-n2","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"What began as a minor procedural disagreement soon _______ into a full-blown constitutional crisis.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"abated"},{"k":"B","t":"dissipated"},{"k":"C","t":"coalesced"},{"k":"D","t":"metastasized"}],"answer":"D","explanation":"Metastasized (spread and worsened uncontrollably) captures the escalation. Abated and dissipated mean lessened — the opposite. Coalesced means came together into one, which doesn't describe growth from small to large."},{"id":"vocab-n3","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"The reviewer praised the novel's _______ prose, noting that not a single sentence felt superfluous.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"prolix"},{"k":"B","t":"spare"},{"k":"C","t":"florid"},{"k":"D","t":"turgid"}],"answer":"B","explanation":"Spare means lean, without excess — the opposite of superfluous. Prolix (tediously lengthy), florid (over-ornate), and turgid (pompous, swollen) all imply excess."},{"id":"vocab-n4","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Far from being _______, the activist's demands had been carefully calibrated to what the legislature could realistically pass.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"measured"},{"k":"B","t":"incremental"},{"k":"C","t":"pragmatic"},{"k":"D","t":"quixotic"}],"answer":"D","explanation":"'Far from being' signals contrast with 'carefully calibrated to what could realistically pass' — so the blank must mean unrealistic. Quixotic = idealistic and impractical. The other three describe exactly what the demands were, not what they were 'far from'."},{"id":"vocab-n5","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"The historian's account was criticised as _______: it selected only the evidence that flattered the dynasty and ignored the rest.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"dispassionate"},{"k":"B","t":"equivocal"},{"k":"C","t":"exhaustive"},{"k":"D","t":"tendentious"}],"answer":"D","explanation":"Tendentious means promoting a particular cause or point of view — fits selective, flattering use of evidence. Exhaustive and dispassionate are the opposite. Equivocal (ambiguous) doesn't fit a clearly one-sided account."},{"id":"vocab-n6","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Her _______ manner in negotiations — never raising her voice, never conceding a point — unnerved opponents who expected theatrics.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"obsequious"},{"k":"B","t":"volatile"},{"k":"C","t":"imperturbable"},{"k":"D","t":"diffident"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"Imperturbable means calm and unable to be upset — fits never raising her voice. Volatile is the opposite. Obsequious (excessively eager to please) contradicts 'never conceding'. Diffident (shy, lacking confidence) doesn't fit someone who unnerves opponents."},{"id":"vocab-n7","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"The committee's report was a _______ of jargon so dense that even specialists struggled to extract its recommendations.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"distillation"},{"k":"B","t":"farrago"},{"k":"C","t":"paragon"},{"k":"D","t":"synopsis"}],"answer":"B","explanation":"Farrago means a confused mixture or hodgepodge — fits a dense, impenetrable jumble. Paragon (model of excellence), synopsis (summary) and distillation (essence) all imply clarity."},{"id":"vocab-n8","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Critics dismissed the policy as a _______: a symbolic gesture that addressed none of the underlying causes.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"panacea"},{"k":"B","t":"linchpin"},{"k":"C","t":"palliative"},{"k":"D","t":"catalyst"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"A palliative relieves symptoms without curing the cause — exactly 'addressed none of the underlying causes'. A panacea is a cure-all (the opposite claim). Catalyst and linchpin imply real, central effect."},{"id":"vocab-n9","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"The once-_______ town, crowded with pilgrims every summer, had become eerily quiet after the shrine closed.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"teeming"},{"k":"B","t":"moribund"},{"k":"C","t":"desolate"},{"k":"D","t":"sedate"}],"answer":"A","explanation":"Teeming means full of people — fits 'crowded with pilgrims'. The 'once-' contrast with 'eerily quiet' requires a word meaning busy. Moribund, desolate and sedate describe the present quiet state, not the past bustle."},{"id":"vocab-n10","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"His reputation for _______ meant that colleagues double-checked every figure he submitted.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"candour"},{"k":"B","t":"punctiliousness"},{"k":"C","t":"probity"},{"k":"D","t":"carelessness"}],"answer":"D","explanation":"Colleagues double-check figures from someone known for errors — carelessness. Probity (honesty), punctiliousness (attention to detail) and candour would make double-checking unnecessary. The trap is choosing an impressive-sounding word over logic."},{"id":"vocab-n11","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"The author's _______ toward the self-help industry is evident in her description of its gurus as 'merchants of manufactured hope'.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"indifference"},{"k":"B","t":"ambivalence"},{"k":"C","t":"reverence"},{"k":"D","t":"disdain"}],"answer":"D","explanation":"'Merchants of manufactured hope' is openly contemptuous — disdain. Ambivalence (mixed feelings) and indifference (no feeling) are too mild; reverence is the opposite."},{"id":"vocab-n12","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Unlike his _______ predecessor, who had made decisions on a whim, the new director insisted on consultation and data before any change.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"deliberate"},{"k":"B","t":"capricious"},{"k":"C","t":"methodical"},{"k":"D","t":"circumspect"}],"answer":"B","explanation":"Capricious means given to sudden, unaccountable changes — matches 'decisions on a whim'. The other three describe the new director, not the contrasting predecessor."},{"id":"vocab-n13","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"The scientist was careful to _______ her claims, stressing that the results held only for the specific population studied.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"amplify"},{"k":"B","t":"extrapolate"},{"k":"C","t":"qualify"},{"k":"D","t":"corroborate"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"To qualify a claim is to limit or restrict it — 'held only for the specific population'. Amplify and extrapolate extend claims; corroborate means confirm with evidence, which isn't what restricting scope does."},{"id":"vocab-n14","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Years of _______ funding had left the archive unable to digitise even a fraction of its holdings.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"copious"},{"k":"B","t":"lavish"},{"k":"C","t":"exiguous"},{"k":"D","t":"munificent"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"Exiguous means very small in amount, meagre — explains the inability to digitise. Munificent, lavish and copious all mean generous or plentiful."},{"id":"vocab-n15","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"The debate quickly descended into _______, with each side trading personal insults rather than arguments.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"polemic"},{"k":"B","t":"rhetoric"},{"k":"C","t":"exegesis"},{"k":"D","t":"invective"}],"answer":"D","explanation":"Invective is abusive, insulting language — 'personal insults'. Polemic is a strong argumentative attack on ideas, which still involves argument. Rhetoric is persuasive language in general. Exegesis is critical interpretation of a text."},{"id":"vocab-n16","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Though the treaty was hailed as historic, its provisions were largely _______, binding no signatory to any concrete action.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"hortatory"},{"k":"B","t":"punitive"},{"k":"C","t":"enforceable"},{"k":"D","t":"stringent"}],"answer":"A","explanation":"Hortatory means urging or encouraging rather than commanding — fits provisions that bind no one. Punitive, enforceable and stringent all imply binding force."},{"id":"vocab-n17","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"The memoir's tone is _______: it mourns the lost village of the author's childhood while admitting that memory has gilded it.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"elegiac"},{"k":"B","t":"sardonic"},{"k":"C","t":"didactic"},{"k":"D","t":"jubilant"}],"answer":"A","explanation":"Elegiac means expressing sorrow or lament for something lost — 'mourns the lost village'. Sardonic (mocking), didactic (instructive) and jubilant (joyful) miss the mournful tone."},{"id":"vocab-n18","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Rather than confront the board directly, the CEO chose to _______, letting the controversial proposal quietly lapse.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"capitulate"},{"k":"B","t":"prevaricate"},{"k":"C","t":"remonstrate"},{"k":"D","t":"temporise"}],"answer":"D","explanation":"Temporise means to delay or avoid committing in order to gain time — letting a proposal 'quietly lapse'. Capitulate means surrender; remonstrate means protest forcefully; prevaricate means speak evasively/lie — about speech, not delay through inaction."},{"id":"vocab-n19","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"The documentary was praised for its _______, presenting the warring factions' claims without editorial comment.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"credulity"},{"k":"B","t":"sensationalism"},{"k":"C","t":"partisanship"},{"k":"D","t":"even-handedness"}],"answer":"D","explanation":"Presenting both sides without comment = even-handedness. Partisanship and sensationalism are opposite qualities; credulity (believing too readily) is unrelated."},{"id":"vocab-n20","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"The firm's _______ in entering new markets — it waited until competitors had absorbed all the early risks — proved shrewd.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"alacrity"},{"k":"B","t":"temerity"},{"k":"C","t":"circumspection"},{"k":"D","t":"impetuosity"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"Circumspection means caution, careful consideration of risks — waiting for others to absorb risk. Temerity, alacrity (eager speed) and impetuosity all imply rushing in."},{"id":"vocab-n21","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"What the audience took for spontaneous wit was, in fact, a carefully _______ performance rehearsed for weeks.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"choreographed"},{"k":"B","t":"improvised"},{"k":"C","t":"extemporised"},{"k":"D","t":"haphazard"}],"answer":"A","explanation":"Choreographed means carefully planned and arranged — contrasting with 'spontaneous'. Extemporised and improvised mean spontaneous; haphazard means random."},{"id":"vocab-n22","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Investors grew _______ of the startup's claims after three successive quarters of missed targets.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"enamoured"},{"k":"B","t":"chary"},{"k":"C","t":"credulous"},{"k":"D","t":"sanguine"}],"answer":"B","explanation":"Chary means cautiously wary — the natural response to repeated missed targets. Enamoured, credulous and sanguine (optimistic) all suggest continued trust."},{"id":"vocab-n23","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"The professor's lectures, though _______, were never dull: he could move from Kant to cricket within a single sentence.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"digressive"},{"k":"B","t":"focused"},{"k":"C","t":"monotonous"},{"k":"D","t":"pedantic"}],"answer":"A","explanation":"Digressive means wandering from the main topic — 'from Kant to cricket'. 'Though … never dull' implies the blank is a potentially negative quality that is redeemed. Focused contradicts the example; monotonous contradicts 'never dull'; pedantic doesn't fit the example."},{"id":"vocab-n24","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"The reform was _______ rather than radical: it adjusted existing rules instead of replacing them.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"incremental"},{"k":"B","t":"revolutionary"},{"k":"C","t":"seismic"},{"k":"D","t":"sweeping"}],"answer":"A","explanation":"Incremental means proceeding in small steps — 'adjusted existing rules'. The others all describe radical change, which the sentence explicitly contrasts."},{"id":"vocab-n25","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Her critique was _______ but fair: it exposed every flaw in the argument without once descending into personal attack.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"fawning"},{"k":"B","t":"trenchant"},{"k":"C","t":"anodyne"},{"k":"D","t":"scurrilous"}],"answer":"B","explanation":"Trenchant means vigorous, incisive — exposing every flaw. Anodyne (inoffensive, bland) and fawning (flattering) contradict exposing flaws. Scurrilous means scandalously abusive, contradicting 'without personal attack'."},{"id":"vocab-n26","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"The essay's central irony is that a writer so _______ about the dangers of rhetoric should himself deploy it with such evident relish.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"agnostic"},{"k":"B","t":"sanguine"},{"k":"C","t":"censorious"},{"k":"D","t":"nonchalant"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"Censorious means severely critical — the irony needs someone who strongly condemns rhetoric yet uses it. Sanguine (optimistic) and nonchalant (unconcerned) remove the irony; agnostic (non-committal) also weakens it."},{"id":"vocab-n27","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Choose the sentence in which the word 'fulsome' is used in its traditional (and CAT-preferred) sense.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"She received a fulsome welcome, warm and entirely sincere."},{"k":"B","t":"The report gave a fulsome, comprehensive account of every transaction."},{"k":"C","t":"The meal was fulsome, leaving every guest satisfied."},{"k":"D","t":"The chairman offered fulsome praise, so excessive that it embarrassed the honoree."}],"answer":"D","explanation":"Traditionally, 'fulsome' means excessive to the point of insincerity or distaste — option D. B, A and C use the popular but contested sense of 'abundant/generous', which careful usage treats as an error."},{"id":"vocab-n28","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"The philosopher's argument is not so much wrong as _______: each step follows, but the conclusion is so hedged that it commits us to nothing.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"specious"},{"k":"B","t":"fallacious"},{"k":"C","t":"vacuous"},{"k":"D","t":"tautological"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"Vacuous means empty of substance — a valid argument whose conclusion 'commits us to nothing'. Fallacious and specious imply faulty reasoning (contradicted by 'not so much wrong'; 'each step follows'). Tautological means saying the same thing twice — the problem here is emptiness, not repetition."},{"id":"vocab-n29","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Select the word that does NOT belong with the others in meaning.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"obdurate"},{"k":"B","t":"intransigent"},{"k":"C","t":"tractable"},{"k":"D","t":"recalcitrant"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"Obdurate, intransigent and recalcitrant all mean stubbornly resistant to change or persuasion. Tractable means easily managed or influenced — the opposite."},{"id":"vocab-n30","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"The biography resists the _______ impulse of its genre: rather than recasting every setback as a lesson, it leaves failures as failures.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"hagiographic"},{"k":"B","t":"iconoclastic"},{"k":"C","t":"revisionist"},{"k":"D","t":"picaresque"}],"answer":"A","explanation":"Hagiographic means idealising the subject (originally, writing about saints) — the impulse to spin every failure into a lesson. Iconoclastic and revisionist mean attacking accepted views — the biography is doing that, not resisting it. Picaresque refers to episodic adventure narratives."},{"id":"vocab-n31","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Though the critic's tone was _______, the substance of his review was devastating — a velvet glove over an iron fist.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"acerbic"},{"k":"B","t":"emollient"},{"k":"C","t":"strident"},{"k":"D","t":"vituperative"}],"answer":"B","explanation":"Emollient means soothing, softening — the 'velvet glove' over devastating substance. Acerbic, strident and vituperative are all harsh, which would remove the contrast the sentence is built on."},{"id":"vocab-n32","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Choose the sentence in which the word 'restive' is used correctly.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"After a long week, she spent a restive weekend sleeping late and reading."},{"k":"B","t":"His restive demeanour calmed the anxious patients."},{"k":"C","t":"The crowd grew restive as the speaker's delay stretched past an hour."},{"k":"D","t":"The restive music lulled the infant to sleep."}],"answer":"C","explanation":"Restive means unable to keep still, impatient or restless — a crowd waiting an hour. A, D and B confuse it with 'restful', which means the opposite."},{"id":"vocab-n33","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"The government's _______ on the issue — supporting the policy in the capital and opposing it in the provinces — eventually cost it credibility in both.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"rectitude"},{"k":"B","t":"forbearance"},{"k":"C","t":"tergiversation"},{"k":"D","t":"equanimity"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"Tergiversation means evasion or repeated change of position — saying opposite things to different audiences. Equanimity (calmness), forbearance (patient restraint) and rectitude (moral correctness) don't describe double-dealing."},{"id":"vocab-n34","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Her scholarship was _______ without being pedantic: she knew everything about the period but wore her learning lightly.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"sciolistic"},{"k":"B","t":"specious"},{"k":"C","t":"erudite"},{"k":"D","t":"jejune"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"Erudite means having deep learning — 'knew everything about the period'. Sciolistic means having superficial knowledge (the opposite), jejune means naive or simplistic, specious means misleadingly plausible."},{"id":"vocab-n35","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Choose the pair in which the relationship between the words is most similar to: LACONIC : WORDS.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"verbose : speech"},{"k":"B","t":"generous : gifts"},{"k":"C","t":"frugal : money"},{"k":"D","t":"wealthy : assets"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"A laconic person is sparing with words; a frugal person is sparing with money. Generous and wealthy imply abundance; verbose is excessive in speech — the reverse relation."},{"id":"vocab-n36","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"The minister's denial was carefully _______: it rejected one specific allegation while leaving the broader charge untouched.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"unequivocal"},{"k":"B","t":"categorical"},{"k":"C","t":"circumscribed"},{"k":"D","t":"sweeping"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"Circumscribed means limited, restricted in scope — fits denying only one specific allegation. Categorical, unequivocal and sweeping imply a total, unqualified denial."},{"id":"vocab-n37","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"The novel's protagonist is less a hero than an _______ — a man to whom things happen rather than one who makes them happen.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"antagonist"},{"k":"B","t":"iconoclast"},{"k":"C","t":"cipher"},{"k":"D","t":"ingénue"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"A cipher is a person of no influence or individuality, a nonentity — 'to whom things happen'. Antagonist is an opponent; ingénue is an innocent young woman; iconoclast attacks established beliefs (an active role)."},{"id":"vocab-n38","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Choose the word whose meaning is closest to 'inveigle'.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"inveigh"},{"k":"B","t":"denounce"},{"k":"C","t":"investigate"},{"k":"D","t":"cajole"}],"answer":"D","explanation":"Inveigle means to persuade by flattery or deception — closest to cajole. Inveigh (protest strongly) is the classic sound-alike trap; denounce and investigate are unrelated."},{"id":"vocab-n39","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Far from being _______, the reform was the culmination of a decade of patient, largely invisible groundwork.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"an anomaly"},{"k":"B","t":"an epiphenomenon"},{"k":"C","t":"a bolt from the blue"},{"k":"D","t":"an aberration"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"'Far from' + 'culmination of a decade of groundwork' requires a phrase meaning a sudden, unforeseen event — 'a bolt from the blue'. Aberration and anomaly mean deviations from the norm, not suddenness; an epiphenomenon is a secondary by-product."},{"id":"vocab-n40","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"The witness's testimony was _______ in its details yet curiously vague on the one point that mattered.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"perfunctory"},{"k":"B","t":"nebulous"},{"k":"C","t":"laconic"},{"k":"D","t":"circumstantial"}],"answer":"D","explanation":"'Circumstantial' in its older sense means full of detail, particular — the contrast is with vagueness on the key point. Perfunctory and laconic imply brevity; nebulous means vague — the opposite of what the first clause needs."},{"id":"vocab-n41","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Which word best completes the analogy: PALLIATE : PAIN :: MOLLIFY : _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"fear"},{"k":"B","t":"grief"},{"k":"C","t":"anger"},{"k":"D","t":"illness"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"Palliate means to lessen pain (without curing); mollify means to soothe or appease anger. Mollify specifically targets anger/hostility, making 'anger' the precise match."},{"id":"vocab-n42","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"The reviewer called the debut 'promising but _______' — the work of a writer who had absorbed every influence but not yet found a voice.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"derivative"},{"k":"B","t":"seminal"},{"k":"C","t":"recondite"},{"k":"D","t":"sui generis"}],"answer":"A","explanation":"Derivative means imitative of others' work — 'absorbed every influence but not yet found a voice'. Seminal (highly influential) and sui generis (unique) are opposites; recondite means obscure, little known."},{"id":"vocab-n43","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Choose the sentence in which 'enervate' is used correctly.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The strong coffee enervated her before the exam."},{"k":"B","t":"His enervating optimism inspired the whole office."},{"k":"C","t":"The coach's speech enervated the team, who charged onto the field."},{"k":"D","t":"Weeks of humid heat had enervated the workers, leaving them listless."}],"answer":"D","explanation":"Enervate means to weaken or drain of energy — 'listless' workers. C, A and B use it as if it meant 'energise', a common error."},{"id":"vocab-n44","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"The scholar's position was _______: she accepted the data but denied that it supported any of the conclusions drawn from it.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"nuanced"},{"k":"B","t":"nihilistic"},{"k":"C","t":"dogmatic"},{"k":"D","t":"credulous"}],"answer":"A","explanation":"Accepting evidence while rejecting the inferences drawn from it is a nuanced (subtly differentiated) position. Dogmatic and credulous imply uncritical acceptance; nihilistic implies rejecting everything, including the data."},{"id":"vocab-n45","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Select the word most nearly OPPOSITE in meaning to 'pusillanimous'.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"craven"},{"k":"B","t":"timorous"},{"k":"C","t":"diffident"},{"k":"D","t":"dauntless"}],"answer":"D","explanation":"Pusillanimous means cowardly, lacking courage. Dauntless (fearless) is the opposite. Timorous and craven are synonyms; diffident means shy — closer in meaning, not opposite."},{"id":"vocab-n46","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"The author treats the old certainties with _______ — affectionate, but never forgetting that they were wrong.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"studied indifference"},{"k":"B","t":"bitter contempt"},{"k":"C","t":"indulgent irony"},{"k":"D","t":"uncritical nostalgia"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"'Affectionate, but never forgetting that they were wrong' combines warmth with critical distance — indulgent irony. Bitter contempt lacks affection; uncritical nostalgia lacks the critical awareness; studied indifference lacks both."},{"id":"vocab-n47","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"In the sentence 'The evidence militates against the hypothesis', 'militates' is closest in meaning to:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"fights alongside"},{"k":"B","t":"is irrelevant to"},{"k":"C","t":"mitigates"},{"k":"D","t":"weighs"}],"answer":"D","explanation":"'Militate against' means to be a powerful factor against — the evidence weighs against the hypothesis. 'Mitigates' (lessens severity) is the classic confusion. 'Fights alongside' is a literal misreading; 'irrelevant to' is the opposite."},{"id":"vocab-n48","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"The policy's defenders mistook its _______ for subtlety; in truth, it was ambiguous because its authors had never agreed on its purpose.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"rigour"},{"k":"B","t":"opacity"},{"k":"C","t":"transparency"},{"k":"D","t":"parsimony"}],"answer":"B","explanation":"Opacity means obscurity, difficulty of understanding — the ambiguity defenders mistook for subtlety. Transparency is the opposite; rigour and parsimony don't connect to ambiguity."},{"id":"vocab-n49","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Choose the word that best describes an argument that appears sound on the surface but is actually flawed.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"cogent"},{"k":"B","t":"specious"},{"k":"C","t":"sophomoric"},{"k":"D","t":"spurious"}],"answer":"B","explanation":"Specious means superficially plausible but actually wrong — exactly the definition. Spurious means false or fake, without the 'appears plausible' element. Cogent means genuinely convincing. Sophomoric means immature, not necessarily plausible."},{"id":"vocab-n50","topic":"vocab","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"The general's victory was _______: it won the battle but so depleted his army that the war was lost within the year.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Sisyphean"},{"k":"B","t":"Promethean"},{"k":"C","t":"Herculean"},{"k":"D","t":"Pyrrhic"}],"answer":"D","explanation":"A Pyrrhic victory is won at such cost that it is tantamount to defeat. Herculean (requiring great strength), Sisyphean (endless and futile) and Promethean (boldly creative/defiant) don't describe a ruinously costly win."},{"id":"pc-n1","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Universities increasingly market degrees by citing graduates' average starting salaries. The figure is easy to understand and easy to compare. But it conceals as much as it reveals: students who enrol in elite programmes were often already advantaged, with networks and preparation that would have served them anywhere. _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Starting salaries are the only reliable indicator of a degree's worth."},{"k":"B","t":"Elite programmes are thus clearly overrated by employers."},{"k":"C","t":"The salary premium, in other words, may measure who gets in more than what the programme adds."},{"k":"D","t":"Salary data should therefore be banned from university marketing altogether."}],"answer":"C","explanation":"The paragraph's logic is selection bias: outcomes reflect who is admitted. C states this conclusion precisely. D overreaches into a policy prescription; B introduces employers' views, which are not discussed; A contradicts the paragraph's scepticism."},{"id":"pc-n2","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"For centuries, sailors navigated by the stars. The method worked well at latitude — the angle of the pole star gave it directly — but longitude was another matter, since it required knowing the exact time at a reference point while far out at sea. _______ The marine chronometer, perfected by John Harrison in the eighteenth century, finally solved the problem.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The pole star is not visible from the southern hemisphere."},{"k":"B","t":"Sailors therefore preferred to stay close to coastlines."},{"k":"C","t":"Many governments offered cash prizes for better telescopes."},{"k":"D","t":"Pendulum clocks, the most accurate of the day, were useless on a rolling ship."}],"answer":"D","explanation":"The gap must explain why keeping time at sea was hard, setting up the chronometer as the solution. D does exactly this. B is a possible consequence but doesn't bridge to the chronometer; A is about latitude, already settled; C mentions telescopes, which aren't relevant to timekeeping."},{"id":"pc-n3","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Recommendation algorithms are often blamed for trapping users in 'filter bubbles' of agreeable content. Yet several large studies find that most people encounter a wider range of political views online than offline, where neighbours, colleagues and friends tend to share their outlook. _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Algorithms are, therefore, entirely harmless to democratic discourse."},{"k":"B","t":"The bubble, if it exists, may be less a product of technology than of the social lives people already lead."},{"k":"C","t":"These studies prove that online debate is always more civil than offline conversation."},{"k":"D","t":"Users should therefore abandon social media in favour of local newspapers."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"The evidence shifts the source of homogeneity from algorithms to offline social life — B. A overstates ('entirely harmless'); D is an unsupported prescription; C introduces civility, which the paragraph never discusses."},{"id":"pc-n4","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"A company that cuts its research budget will usually see profits rise in the short term. Analysts applaud, and the share price climbs. The costs of the decision arrive only years later, when the products that research would have produced fail to appear. _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Share prices are, in fact, an accurate measure of long-term value."},{"k":"B","t":"By then, the executives responsible have often moved on, and the connection is rarely made."},{"k":"C","t":"Research is therefore the most important function of any business."},{"k":"D","t":"Analysts are generally more intelligent than executives."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"The paragraph describes a time lag between decision and cost; B completes the thought by explaining why accountability is lost. C overgeneralises; A contradicts the implied critique of short-termism; D is irrelevant."},{"id":"pc-n5","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Many cities have tried to revive declining downtowns by building stadiums. The logic seems sound: a stadium draws tens of thousands of visitors, who will surely spend money nearby. Economists who have studied these projects, however, are almost unanimous in their scepticism. _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Sports fans typically come from wealthy suburbs."},{"k":"B","t":"Stadiums are architecturally unappealing and spoil city skylines."},{"k":"C","t":"Spending on games largely substitutes for spending residents would have made elsewhere in the same city, rather than adding to it."},{"k":"D","t":"Most cities lack the land needed for a stadium."}],"answer":"C","explanation":"The gap must explain why economists are sceptical of the spending logic. C supplies the substitution effect. B is aesthetic, not economic; A is a demographic detail that doesn't explain the scepticism; D is about feasibility, not impact."},{"id":"pc-n6","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"_______ Some species of fig can be pollinated only by a single species of tiny wasp, which in turn can reproduce only inside that fig. If either partner disappears, the other is doomed. Such tight mutual dependence makes these pairs fascinating to biologists and alarming to conservationists.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Conservationists have successfully saved many endangered species."},{"k":"B","t":"Figs are among the oldest cultivated fruits in human history."},{"k":"C","t":"Wasps are generally considered pests by farmers."},{"k":"D","t":"Not all relationships in nature are competitive; some are so interdependent that neither partner can survive alone."}],"answer":"D","explanation":"An opening sentence must introduce the theme of mutual dependence that the example illustrates. D does so. B and C focus on the wrong aspects of figs/wasps; A jumps ahead and contradicts the 'alarming' tone."},{"id":"pc-n7","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"The four-day work week has been trialled by hundreds of companies in recent years. Most report stable or improved productivity, and employees overwhelmingly prefer it. Sceptics point out, however, that the firms volunteering for trials were likely those best suited to the change. _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The four-day week should therefore be mandated by law immediately."},{"k":"B","t":"Whether the results would hold across the economy as a whole remains an open question."},{"k":"C","t":"Employees who prefer it are simply lazy."},{"k":"D","t":"Productivity is impossible to measure accurately."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"The sceptics' point is self-selection; the natural conclusion is uncertainty about generalisation — B. A ignores the caveat; C is an unwarranted judgement; D is an extreme claim unrelated to the self-selection point."},{"id":"pc-n8","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Early critics of photography warned that it would destroy painting. Why, they asked, would anyone commission a portrait when a camera could capture a likeness perfectly? _______ Freed from the burden of mere resemblance, artists turned to colour, light and abstraction — giving rise to Impressionism and much of modern art.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Photography was, in fact, extremely expensive in its early years."},{"k":"B","t":"The prediction proved half right: photography did end painting's monopoly on likeness, but this liberated rather than destroyed it."},{"k":"C","t":"Portrait painters were usually poorly paid."},{"k":"D","t":"Most early photographs have since faded beyond recognition."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"The gap must bridge the prediction and the 'freed from … resemblance' outcome. B explicitly does so. A, C and D are side details that don't connect to the liberation the next sentence describes."},{"id":"pc-n9","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"In negotiations, the first number put on the table exerts a surprisingly strong pull. Even when both sides know it is arbitrary, subsequent offers tend to cluster around it. _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Most negotiations end without any agreement."},{"k":"B","t":"Negotiations are always won by the more aggressive party."},{"k":"C","t":"Numbers have no real effect on how people think."},{"k":"D","t":"Experienced negotiators therefore try to make the first offer, or to explicitly reject an opponent's anchor before discussing it."}],"answer":"D","explanation":"Anchoring implies a practical lesson; D draws it. B overgeneralises; C contradicts the paragraph; A is irrelevant."},{"id":"pc-n10","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Plastic was once celebrated as a miracle material: cheap, durable and endlessly versatile. _______ Plastic bottles discarded today will persist in the environment for centuries, fragmenting into ever-smaller particles that now turn up in fish, drinking water and even human blood.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Plastic was invented in the early twentieth century."},{"k":"B","t":"Glass is a far better material for most purposes."},{"k":"C","t":"Many countries have banned single-use plastic bags."},{"k":"D","t":"Its very durability, the quality that made it so useful, has become its greatest liability."}],"answer":"D","explanation":"The missing sentence must pivot from praise to the problem of persistence described next. D does so by turning 'durable' into the liability. A is a dated fact; B introduces an unsupported comparison; C jumps to policy."},{"id":"pc-n11","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"The success of a reintroduced species depends on more than releasing animals into a suitable habitat. Wolves returned to a forest must learn to hunt local prey; birds must learn migration routes that their captive-bred parents never flew. _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Wolves are more intelligent than birds."},{"k":"B","t":"Captive breeding should be abandoned entirely."},{"k":"C","t":"Most reintroductions fail because habitats are too small."},{"k":"D","t":"Reintroduction programmes therefore increasingly include training, or pair captive animals with wild mentors."}],"answer":"D","explanation":"The paragraph stresses learned behaviour; D gives the logical response. A is an irrelevant comparison; C introduces a new cause; B is an extreme overreach."},{"id":"pc-n12","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Many of history's 'lone geniuses' were, on closer inspection, nothing of the kind. Edison ran a laboratory employing dozens of engineers; Darwin corresponded with hundreds of naturalists who supplied data and specimens. _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Edison was a better businessman than inventor."},{"k":"B","t":"Genius, in other words, is often less a solitary spark than the visible tip of a collaborative network."},{"k":"C","t":"Darwin's theory of evolution has been disproved."},{"k":"D","t":"Modern scientists are less creative than those of the past."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"The examples support a general reframing of genius as collaborative — B. A and C are unsupported claims about the individuals; D introduces a comparison the paragraph doesn't make."},{"id":"pc-n13","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Lie detectors measure physiological signs of stress — heart rate, sweating, breathing. The problem is that stress is not the same as deception. _______ Conversely, a practised liar who feels no anxiety may pass the test with ease.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Lie detectors are therefore used widely by police around the world."},{"k":"B","t":"An innocent person who is frightened of being disbelieved may well fail."},{"k":"C","t":"Heart rate is the most reliable of the three measures."},{"k":"D","t":"Most people find lying uncomfortable."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"'Conversely' signals that the missing sentence is the mirror case: a false positive. B provides exactly that. A ignores the critique; C introduces an unsupported comparison; D supports the instrument rather than setting up the contrast."},{"id":"pc-n14","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Rising sea levels are often discussed in terms of averages: a few millimetres a year. But damage is rarely done by averages. _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Millimetres are too small to notice."},{"k":"B","t":"It is done by storm surges riding on top of those higher seas, which turn once-a-century floods into once-a-decade events."},{"k":"C","t":"Coastal cities are generally wealthier than inland ones."},{"k":"D","t":"Sea levels have risen and fallen many times in Earth's history."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"'Damage is rarely done by averages' demands a sentence identifying what does the damage — extremes. B does this. D is a historical aside; C is irrelevant; A contradicts the paragraph's concern."},{"id":"pc-n15","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Traditional performance reviews, conducted once a year, are falling out of favour in many large companies. Managers complain that they consume enormous time; employees find them anxiety-inducing and often unfair. _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Managers are generally poor judges of performance."},{"k":"B","t":"Employees should never receive feedback."},{"k":"C","t":"In their place, firms are experimenting with frequent, informal check-ins focused on development rather than ratings."},{"k":"D","t":"Performance reviews were invented by the military."}],"answer":"C","explanation":"After describing the decline and complaints, the natural next step is what replaces reviews — C. D is a trivia aside; B is absurdly extreme; A overgeneralises a single complaint."},{"id":"pc-n16","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Antibiotics are among the most important medicines ever developed, yet pharmaceutical companies have largely abandoned research into new ones. _______ A new antibiotic must be held in reserve, used only when older drugs fail, so that resistance does not develop — which means it will sell in tiny quantities.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The reason is not scientific but economic."},{"k":"B","t":"Antibiotics are easy and cheap to discover."},{"k":"C","t":"Bacteria evolve resistance extremely slowly."},{"k":"D","t":"Most infections are caused by viruses rather than bacteria."}],"answer":"A","explanation":"The next sentence explains a market problem (small sales), so the gap must announce an economic reason — A. C contradicts the resistance concern; B contradicts the abandonment puzzle; D is irrelevant."},{"id":"pc-n17","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Classical music audiences are ageing in most Western countries, and orchestras worry about their future. Some have responded by playing film scores and pop arrangements to attract younger listeners. _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Young people do not like music."},{"k":"B","t":"Film scores are more difficult to perform than symphonies."},{"k":"C","t":"Purists object that this risks winning an audience for the orchestra while losing one for the music it exists to play."},{"k":"D","t":"Orchestras were first formed in the seventeenth century."}],"answer":"C","explanation":"After the strategy, the paragraph needs a response or tension — C. B is a dubious comparison; A is absurd; D is an irrelevant historical fact."},{"id":"pc-n18","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Before the invention of refrigeration, ice was harvested from frozen lakes in New England and shipped as far as India. _______ Insulated with sawdust, a substantial portion of the cargo survived months at sea, and the trade made fortunes for its pioneers.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"New England is famous for its autumn foliage."},{"k":"B","t":"Ice is formed when water drops below zero degrees Celsius."},{"k":"C","t":"The idea sounded absurd to many contemporaries."},{"k":"D","t":"India has a hot climate."}],"answer":"C","explanation":"The following sentence describes how the trade succeeded despite apparent impossibility, so the gap sets up scepticism — C. B and D are trivial facts that don't advance the narrative; A is irrelevant."},{"id":"pc-n19","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"A ban on a product does not eliminate demand for it. When the United States prohibited alcohol in the 1920s, consumption fell but did not disappear; instead, supply shifted to criminal networks that grew rich and violent. _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Policymakers considering bans, therefore, must weigh not only the harms of the product but the harms of the market a ban creates."},{"k":"B","t":"Criminal networks existed only in the United States."},{"k":"C","t":"Prohibition was popular with most Americans throughout the 1920s."},{"k":"D","t":"Alcohol is more harmful than any other drug."}],"answer":"A","explanation":"The paragraph's lesson is about unintended consequences of bans; A generalises it. D overreaches; B is false and irrelevant; C is a historical claim not supported."},{"id":"pc-n20","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Recycling rates are often cited as a measure of environmental progress. But recycling is the third of the three familiar R's, not the first. _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Reducing consumption and reusing goods save far more resources than recycling them after use."},{"k":"B","t":"Plastic is harder to recycle than glass."},{"k":"C","t":"Recycling plants are often located in industrial zones."},{"k":"D","t":"Many people do not know how to recycle correctly."}],"answer":"A","explanation":"The pointed reminder that recycling is 'third, not first' requires a sentence explaining why reduce and reuse matter more — A. The others are true-sounding details that don't complete the argument."},{"id":"pc-n21","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"The success of a vaccination campaign can make it look unnecessary. As diseases like measles disappear from daily life, parents no longer see their devastating effects. _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Vaccines are developed through a long process of clinical trials."},{"k":"B","t":"Measles was first identified in the ninth century."},{"k":"C","t":"Vaccine hesitancy then rises, and the diseases return — a cycle public health experts call the paradox of prevention."},{"k":"D","t":"Parents generally make good decisions about their children's health."}],"answer":"C","explanation":"The logic leads to complacency and resurgence — C, which also names the paradox. B and A are off-topic facts; D undercuts the paragraph's concern."},{"id":"pc-n22","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"A growing body of research suggests that time spent in nature improves mood and concentration. _______ Even a view of trees from a hospital window has been linked to faster recovery after surgery.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Many people prefer cities to the countryside."},{"k":"B","t":"The effects appear even with remarkably brief or indirect exposure."},{"k":"C","t":"Forests cover about thirty per cent of the Earth's land area."},{"k":"D","t":"Hospitals are often built far from natural areas."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"'Even a view of trees' is an intensifying example of brief/indirect exposure, so the gap must introduce that idea — B. C is a statistic; A contradicts; D is irrelevant."},{"id":"pc-n23","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Online reviews were supposed to empower consumers by replacing marketing with honest peer opinion. _______ Platforms now struggle with fake reviews bought in bulk, while genuine reviewers tend to post only when delighted or furious.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Consumers have always trusted advertisements."},{"k":"B","t":"Reviews were invented by newspapers."},{"k":"C","t":"The reality has proved messier."},{"k":"D","t":"Most products receive five-star ratings."}],"answer":"C","explanation":"The paragraph contrasts promise and outcome; C is the pivot sentence. A contradicts; D is a detail that doesn't serve as a transition; B is irrelevant."},{"id":"pc-n24","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Monarch butterflies migrate up to four thousand kilometres from Canada to central Mexico. Remarkably, no individual butterfly makes the round trip; the journey spans several generations. _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Many birds also migrate long distances."},{"k":"B","t":"Mexico has a warmer climate than Canada."},{"k":"C","t":"The butterflies that arrive in Mexico have therefore never been there before, yet they find the same forests their great-grandparents left."},{"k":"D","t":"Monarch butterflies are orange and black."}],"answer":"C","explanation":"The multigenerational detail sets up the puzzle of navigation — C draws it out. The rest are trivia or tangents."},{"id":"pc-n25","topic":"pc","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Governments often justify subsidies for fossil fuels as a way of protecting poor households from high energy prices. _______ Since richer households consume far more energy, the bulk of the subsidy flows to them.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Studies of such subsidies, however, find them a remarkably inefficient way of doing so."},{"k":"B","t":"Fossil fuels are the main cause of climate change."},{"k":"C","t":"Energy prices have generally fallen over the past century."},{"k":"D","t":"Poor households rarely own cars."}],"answer":"A","explanation":"The next sentence explains why the subsidy is poorly targeted, so the gap must assert inefficiency — A. B changes topic; C is unsupported; D is a detail that doesn't function as the claim being supported."},{"id":"pc-n26","topic":"pc","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"There is a familiar complaint that modern philosophy has become a technical specialism, its debates conducted in a jargon unintelligible to outsiders. The complaint has force. But it assumes that philosophy was once different — that Kant or Aristotle wrote for the general reader. _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Kant and Aristotle were the greatest philosophers who ever lived."},{"k":"B","t":"They did not; the difficulty of philosophy is not a modern corruption but a feature of thinking carefully about hard questions."},{"k":"C","t":"Modern philosophers should therefore write only for specialists."},{"k":"D","t":"The general reader has, in any case, lost interest in philosophy."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"The paragraph sets up a hidden assumption to be challenged; B challenges it and draws the implication. A is evaluative and off-point; C is a prescription the paragraph doesn't support; D introduces a new claim."},{"id":"pc-n27","topic":"pc","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"A map that showed every detail of the territory would have to be as large as the territory itself — and would therefore be useless. The value of a model, whether in cartography or economics, lies precisely in what it leaves out. _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Maps are more accurate than economic models."},{"k":"B","t":"The most detailed models are always the most useful."},{"k":"C","t":"Economists should therefore stop using models entirely."},{"k":"D","t":"The real question is never whether a model is simplified, but whether it has simplified away the features that matter for the question at hand."}],"answer":"D","explanation":"Having established that simplification is necessary, the paragraph needs the criterion for a good model — D. C and B contradict the logic; A introduces an unsupported comparison."},{"id":"pc-n28","topic":"pc","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"The historian's task, it is often said, is to understand the past on its own terms. Yet the questions historians ask are inevitably shaped by the concerns of their own time; histories of women, of the environment and of disease proliferated precisely when those subjects became urgent in the present. _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Historians should therefore ignore present concerns entirely."},{"k":"B","t":"This is not a failure of objectivity so much as the condition of historical inquiry: the present determines which parts of the past become visible."},{"k":"C","t":"The past can never be understood at all."},{"k":"D","t":"Histories of women are more important than political histories."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"The paragraph presents a tension; the best completion reframes it as a constitutive condition — B. A is impossible given the argument; D is an unsupported ranking; C is an extreme overreach the paragraph does not endorse."},{"id":"pc-n29","topic":"pc","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Advocates of meritocracy argue that rewarding talent and effort is both efficient and fair. Critics have noted a less obvious cost. _______ Those who fail, meanwhile, are left with no consolation, for in a meritocracy failure is by definition deserved.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Most societies are not truly meritocratic."},{"k":"B","t":"Meritocracies are less efficient than aristocracies."},{"k":"C","t":"Talent cannot be measured reliably."},{"k":"D","t":"Those who succeed in a meritocracy tend to believe they owe their position entirely to their own virtue, and may feel little obligation to others."}],"answer":"D","explanation":"'Those who fail, meanwhile' requires a preceding sentence about those who succeed, describing the psychological cost — D. C and A are different critiques that don't set up 'meanwhile'; B contradicts the advocates' efficiency claim without support."},{"id":"pc-n30","topic":"pc","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"When a scientific consensus is challenged by a lone dissenter, we like to recall Galileo. The comparison flatters the dissenter but misleads the rest of us. _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"For every Galileo there were hundreds of dissenters who were simply wrong, and history remembers only the ones who were vindicated."},{"k":"B","t":"Most dissenters are motivated by money."},{"k":"C","t":"Scientific consensus is always correct."},{"k":"D","t":"Galileo was in fact wrong about the tides."}],"answer":"A","explanation":"The paragraph criticises the Galileo comparison; A gives the reason (survivorship bias). D is a true but irrelevant detail; C is an absolute overreach; B introduces an unsupported motive."},{"id":"pc-n31","topic":"pc","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"_______ The word 'robot' was coined in a 1920 Czech play about artificial workers who rise up against their makers. 'Cyberspace' was invented by a science-fiction novelist years before the internet became public. Fiction does not merely anticipate technology; it supplies the vocabulary, and often the anxieties, through which we come to understand it.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Czech literature is underappreciated internationally."},{"k":"B","t":"Science fiction is often dismissed as escapism, but its influence on how we think about technology is hard to overstate."},{"k":"C","t":"Robots are now used widely in manufacturing."},{"k":"D","t":"Most science-fiction predictions turn out to be wrong."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"The paragraph argues for fiction's influence on technological thinking; B frames that argument and its examples. A is too narrow; D contradicts the drift; C is a tangent."},{"id":"pc-n32","topic":"pc","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Economists measure a nation's prosperity by its gross domestic product, the total value of goods and services sold. GDP rises when a forest is felled for timber, but not when it stands, purifying air and water. _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Forests are important for biodiversity."},{"k":"B","t":"Most countries now have higher GDP than a century ago."},{"k":"C","t":"GDP was first calculated in the 1930s."},{"k":"D","t":"It counts the money spent cleaning up an oil spill as a gain, and the unpaid care of children and the elderly as nothing at all."}],"answer":"D","explanation":"The paragraph is building a list of GDP's perverse blind spots; D continues it in parallel. C is a dated fact; A is a tangent about forests; B is irrelevant to the critique."},{"id":"pc-n33","topic":"pc","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Grief, many psychologists once held, proceeds through fixed stages: denial, anger, bargaining, depression and acceptance. The model was intuitive and widely taught. _______ Many bereaved people never experience some stages at all, and most move back and forth between emotions in no particular order.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"It was first proposed in a book about terminally ill patients, not the bereaved."},{"k":"B","t":"Psychologists have since developed better treatments for depression."},{"k":"C","t":"It was also, as later research made clear, largely unsupported by evidence."},{"k":"D","t":"Acceptance is the most important stage of grief."}],"answer":"C","explanation":"The next sentence presents evidence against the model, so the gap must announce that verdict — C. A is a true historical detail but doesn't prepare for the empirical refutation; B and D are off-point."},{"id":"pc-n34","topic":"pc","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"The Library of Alexandria is usually remembered as destroyed in a single catastrophic fire. Historians now think its decline was slower and less dramatic: funding dried up, scholars were expelled during political purges, and the collection was neglected over centuries. _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Fires were common in ancient cities."},{"k":"B","t":"Alexandria was founded by Alexander the Great."},{"k":"C","t":"The library contained hundreds of thousands of scrolls."},{"k":"D","t":"The myth of the fire endures, perhaps, because sudden catastrophe is easier to mourn than slow institutional neglect — and less uncomfortable to recognise in ourselves."}],"answer":"D","explanation":"A completion that reflects on why the myth survives, with a pointed contemporary implication, fits the paragraph's revisionist arc. C, A and B are factual asides that don't complete the thought."},{"id":"pc-n35","topic":"pc","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Free speech is often defended on the grounds that truth will win out in an open marketplace of ideas. But markets can fail. They reward what sells, not what is true, and falsehoods that flatter their audience may out-compete truths that discomfort it. _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Markets are the best way to allocate economic resources."},{"k":"B","t":"Free speech should therefore be abolished."},{"k":"C","t":"Truth always wins in the end."},{"k":"D","t":"None of this necessarily justifies censorship, but it does suggest that the marketplace metaphor cannot, on its own, bear the weight its defenders place on it."}],"answer":"D","explanation":"The paragraph critiques one justification without rejecting free speech; D preserves that nuance. B overreaches; C contradicts; A shifts to economics."},{"id":"pc-n36","topic":"pc","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Self-driving cars must sometimes be programmed to choose between harms: swerve and endanger the passenger, or continue and endanger a pedestrian. Surveys show that most people want other people's cars to minimise total harm. _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Self-driving cars will eliminate all road accidents."},{"k":"B","t":"When asked which car they would buy, however, most prefer one that protects its passengers at all costs."},{"k":"C","t":"Most surveys are poorly designed."},{"k":"D","t":"Pedestrians are more vulnerable than passengers."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"'Other people's cars' sets up a self-interest contrast; B supplies the 'however'. A is unrealistic; D is a factual aside; C undermines the paragraph's data without reason."},{"id":"pc-n37","topic":"pc","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"It is commonly assumed that the more choice consumers have, the better off they are. Experiments complicate the picture. Shoppers offered twenty-four varieties of jam were far less likely to buy any than those offered six. _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Most shoppers dislike jam."},{"k":"B","t":"Beyond a certain point, choice may become a burden, inducing paralysis rather than satisfaction."},{"k":"C","t":"Jam is a popular breakfast food."},{"k":"D","t":"Consumers should be offered only one product at a time."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"The experiment supports a qualified conclusion — B ('beyond a certain point'). D overreaches to an extreme; C and A are irrelevant."},{"id":"pc-n38","topic":"pc","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"The ruins of ancient cities are often taken as warnings about collapse. Yet the inhabitants of many such cities did not vanish; they simply moved elsewhere, adapting to drought or shifting trade routes. _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Archaeologists have excavated many ruined cities."},{"k":"B","t":"What looks to us like collapse may, from their perspective, have been a successful adaptation."},{"k":"C","t":"Ancient cities were generally smaller than modern ones."},{"k":"D","t":"Drought is the single cause of all historical collapses."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"The paragraph reframes abandonment as adaptation; B states that reframing. D is an absolute claim; C and A are tangents."},{"id":"pc-n39","topic":"pc","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Many of the world's languages lack words for 'left' and 'right', using absolute directions — north, south, east, west — instead. Speakers of such languages maintain a constant sense of orientation that most English speakers find astonishing. _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Most people have a poor sense of direction."},{"k":"B","t":"Languages without 'left' and 'right' are more primitive."},{"k":"C","t":"English is the most widely spoken language in the world."},{"k":"D","t":"Whether the language produces the skill, or the skill the language, is a question linguists still debate."}],"answer":"D","explanation":"The correlation invites the causation question — D. B is an unjustified value judgement; C and A are off-topic or overgeneralised."},{"id":"pc-n40","topic":"pc","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Inequality is often measured by income. Yet two households with identical incomes can lead very different lives if one owns its home and the other rents, or if one can call on wealthy relatives in a crisis. _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Renting is always a poor financial decision."},{"k":"B","t":"Most households own their homes."},{"k":"C","t":"Wealth, not income, may be the better measure of how secure — and how free — a household really is."},{"k":"D","t":"Income inequality has fallen in recent decades."}],"answer":"C","explanation":"The examples (property, family support) point to wealth as a superior measure — C. A and B are overgeneralisations; D is an unsupported empirical claim."},{"id":"pc-n41","topic":"pc","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"The paradox of the expert witness is that the qualities that make testimony credible to a jury — confidence, fluency, unwavering certainty — are not the qualities that make it reliable. _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Expert witnesses are usually paid by one side of the case."},{"k":"B","t":"Juries are generally made up of ordinary citizens."},{"k":"C","t":"Most trials do not involve expert witnesses."},{"k":"D","t":"Careful scientists hedge, qualify and acknowledge uncertainty, which juries may mistake for weakness."}],"answer":"D","explanation":"The paragraph contrasts credibility with reliability; D explains how genuine reliability (hedging) can backfire. A introduces a different issue (bias); B and C are tangential facts."},{"id":"pc-n42","topic":"pc","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Literary critics once sought the 'intended meaning' of a text — what its author meant to say. In the mid-twentieth century, a group of critics argued that this was a fallacy: the author's intentions are usually unknowable, and in any case a poem means what its words mean, not what its writer hoped. _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Most authors write poetry rather than prose."},{"k":"B","t":"The mid-twentieth century produced little important literature."},{"k":"C","t":"Later theorists pushed further, arguing that meaning resides not in the text at all but in the reader's encounter with it."},{"k":"D","t":"Authors' intentions are always clearly stated in their letters."}],"answer":"C","explanation":"The paragraph traces a sequence of theories; C continues the progression logically. D contradicts 'usually unknowable'; A and B are irrelevant."},{"id":"pc-n43","topic":"pc","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"The invention of the lightbulb is usually credited to Edison. In fact, more than twenty inventors had produced working incandescent lamps before him. _______ His real achievement was a system — generators, wiring, meters and a long-lasting filament — that made electric light practical for ordinary homes.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Edison's bulb was not the first, nor even the brightest."},{"k":"B","t":"The other inventors were all European."},{"k":"C","t":"Edison was born in Ohio."},{"k":"D","t":"Incandescent bulbs have now been largely replaced by LEDs."}],"answer":"A","explanation":"The sentence before the gap rejects 'first'; the sentence after describes his real achievement. A bridges by restating what he did not do. C and D are tangents; B is unsupported."},{"id":"pc-n44","topic":"pc","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"A city that bans cars from its centre will see congestion fall there and often rise on the surrounding ring roads. _______ Over months, many drivers do not merely reroute but switch modes, combine trips or stop making some journeys altogether.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Ring roads are expensive to build."},{"k":"B","t":"Most cities were not designed for cars."},{"k":"C","t":"Cars are the main source of air pollution in cities."},{"k":"D","t":"Yet the predicted gridlock on those ring roads frequently fails to materialise."}],"answer":"D","explanation":"The next sentence explains why traffic doesn't simply relocate ('traffic evaporation'), so the gap must assert that the expected gridlock fails to appear — D. The others are tangents."},{"id":"pc-n45","topic":"pc","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Folk wisdom holds that opposites attract. Research on long-term couples finds the reverse: partners tend to be similar in education, values and even personality. _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The appeal of the folk saying may lie in the drama of the exceptions, which are memorable precisely because they are rare."},{"k":"B","t":"Most people marry before the age of thirty."},{"k":"C","t":"Personality cannot be measured scientifically."},{"k":"D","t":"Couples who are similar never argue."}],"answer":"A","explanation":"Having refuted the folk belief, the best completion explains its persistence — A. D is an absolute claim; C undermines the research without basis; B is irrelevant."},{"id":"pc-n46","topic":"pc","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"The ideal of objectivity in journalism — reporting facts without taking sides — is relatively recent. Nineteenth-century newspapers were openly partisan. _______ Objectivity, in other words, was as much a business strategy as an ethical principle.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Most nineteenth-century journalists were poorly educated."},{"k":"B","t":"The shift came partly because publishers realised that a neutral paper could sell to readers of every party, multiplying its audience and its advertising revenue."},{"k":"C","t":"Partisan newspapers are now illegal in many countries."},{"k":"D","t":"Objectivity is impossible to achieve."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"'In other words … business strategy' requires a preceding sentence giving a commercial motive — B. A, C and D don't establish the commercial logic that the final sentence summarises."},{"id":"pc-n47","topic":"pc","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Conservation efforts tend to focus on charismatic animals — tigers, pandas, whales. _______ Yet the creatures that hold ecosystems together are more often insects, fungi and microbes, which attract a fraction of the funding.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"These species are effective at mobilising public sympathy and donations."},{"k":"B","t":"Tigers are found only in Asia."},{"k":"C","t":"Whales are the largest animals that have ever lived."},{"k":"D","t":"Pandas eat mainly bamboo."}],"answer":"A","explanation":"'Yet' contrasts a reason for focusing on charismatic animals with their lesser ecological importance; A supplies the reason. B, D and C are trivia that don't set up the contrast."},{"id":"pc-n48","topic":"pc","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Nations that discover large reserves of oil or minerals often grow more slowly, and govern themselves worse, than resource-poor neighbours. _______ Rulers who can fund the state from oil revenues need not tax their citizens, and so need not answer to them.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Part of the explanation is political rather than economic."},{"k":"B","t":"Resource-poor countries usually have larger populations."},{"k":"C","t":"Oil prices fluctuate significantly from year to year."},{"k":"D","t":"Minerals are found in almost every country."}],"answer":"A","explanation":"The next sentence gives a political mechanism (taxation and accountability), so the gap must announce a political explanation — A. C names a real economic factor but doesn't prepare for the next sentence; B and D are irrelevant."},{"id":"pc-n49","topic":"pc","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"The great twentieth-century planning disasters — collectivised farms, high-rise housing estates, purpose-built capital cities — were not failures of intelligence. Their designers were often brilliant. What they shared was a conviction that a society could be made legible from above and rearranged according to a rational plan. _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Most planned cities are located in developing countries."},{"k":"B","t":"Brilliant designers are therefore always dangerous."},{"k":"C","t":"High-rise housing is unsuitable for families with children."},{"k":"D","t":"What that conviction overlooked was the practical, local knowledge — illegible to planners — on which ordinary life quietly depends."}],"answer":"D","explanation":"The paragraph says the failures were not about intelligence but about a shared conviction; the completion must name what that conviction missed — D. B overgeneralises; C and A are narrow tangents."},{"id":"pc-n50","topic":"pc","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"We tend to think of forgetting as a failure of memory. Neuroscientists increasingly see it as an essential function. A brain that retained every detail of every experience would struggle to generalise, unable to see the forest for the trees. _______","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Neuroscientists disagree about almost everything."},{"k":"B","t":"Memory declines inevitably with age."},{"k":"C","t":"Most people forget about half of what they learn within a day."},{"k":"D","t":"Forgetting, on this view, is not memory's enemy but the editor that makes memory useful."}],"answer":"D","explanation":"The paragraph reframes forgetting as functional; D crystallises that reframing. C is a detail; B shifts to ageing; A is an unsupported generalisation."},{"id":"ps-n1","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Cities that build more housing see slower rent growth than those that restrict construction, even when demand is strong. Opponents of new development often argue that new units, typically priced at the top of the market, do nothing for affordability. But studies of 'filtering' show that when higher-income households move into new buildings, they vacate older units, which then become available to lower-income renters, easing pressure throughout the market.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Even expensive new housing can improve affordability by freeing up older units further down the market."},{"k":"B","t":"New housing is always priced at the top of the market and therefore benefits only the wealthy."},{"k":"C","t":"Lower-income renters prefer older buildings to new ones."},{"k":"D","t":"Cities with strong demand should restrict construction to protect existing renters."}],"answer":"A","explanation":"A captures the paragraph's main claim (filtering makes new supply help affordability). B states the opponents' view the paragraph rebuts. D contradicts the evidence. C distorts the mechanism into a preference."},{"id":"ps-n2","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Smartphones are often blamed for declining attention spans. The evidence is mixed. Laboratory studies show that the mere presence of a phone on a desk slightly reduces performance on cognitive tasks. Yet longitudinal studies have found little evidence of a lasting decline in people's underlying capacity to concentrate. What has changed may be less our ability to pay attention than our willingness to tolerate boredom.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Smartphones have permanently damaged human attention spans."},{"k":"B","t":"Longitudinal studies are more reliable than laboratory experiments."},{"k":"C","t":"The presence of a phone on a desk slightly reduces cognitive performance."},{"k":"D","t":"Evidence that phones erode our capacity to concentrate is weak; they may instead have lowered our tolerance for boredom."}],"answer":"D","explanation":"D summarises both the mixed evidence and the paragraph's alternative explanation. A overstates. C is one detail — too narrow. B draws a methodological conclusion the paragraph doesn't make."},{"id":"ps-n3","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Workplace wellness programmes — gym subsidies, health screenings, mindfulness sessions — have become a multibillion-dollar industry. Employers adopt them hoping to cut healthcare costs and boost productivity. A large randomised trial in the United States, however, found that after eighteen months, employees enrolled in such a programme were no healthier, no more productive and no less costly than those who were not. Earlier, more positive studies had mostly compared volunteers with non-volunteers — and those who volunteer for wellness programmes tend to be healthier to begin with.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Randomised trials are expensive and rarely conducted in workplaces."},{"k":"B","t":"Employees who volunteer for wellness programmes are healthier than those who don't."},{"k":"C","t":"Employers should cancel all wellness programmes immediately."},{"k":"D","t":"Wellness programmes have no effect on employee health, and earlier positive findings likely reflected who chose to participate."}],"answer":"D","explanation":"D captures both the finding and the explanation for earlier results. B is a supporting detail only. C is a prescription the paragraph doesn't make. A is off-focus."},{"id":"ps-n4","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"The idea that people use only ten per cent of their brains is one of the most persistent myths in popular culture. Brain imaging shows that virtually every region is active over the course of a day, and damage to almost any area produces noticeable deficits. The myth survives, perhaps, because it is flattering: it implies that we all possess vast untapped potential waiting to be unlocked.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Damage to any brain region produces noticeable deficits."},{"k":"B","t":"Brain imaging is the most important tool in modern neuroscience."},{"k":"C","t":"Most people have vast untapped mental potential."},{"k":"D","t":"Although disproved by evidence, the ten per cent myth persists because it flatters our sense of untapped potential."}],"answer":"D","explanation":"D combines the debunking with the explanation of persistence. B is off-focus; C endorses the myth's implication; A is one supporting detail."},{"id":"ps-n5","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Tipping practices vary dramatically across countries. In the United States, a tip of fifteen to twenty per cent is effectively mandatory, and servers' wages assume it. In Japan, leaving a tip can be seen as mildly insulting, implying that good service is not already standard. Such differences suggest that tipping is less a natural expression of gratitude than a social convention, shaped by local history and labour arrangements.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Japanese restaurants provide better service than American ones."},{"k":"B","t":"Variation in tipping customs shows that tipping is a social convention rather than a universal expression of gratitude."},{"k":"C","t":"Servers in the United States are underpaid."},{"k":"D","t":"Tipping in the United States is excessive compared with other countries."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"B states the general conclusion drawn from the examples. D and C are evaluative claims the paragraph doesn't make; A is an unsupported comparison."},{"id":"ps-n6","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"For decades, dietary guidelines warned against eating eggs because of their high cholesterol content. Later research found that, for most people, dietary cholesterol has only a modest effect on blood cholesterol, which is more strongly influenced by saturated fat and genetics. Guidelines have since been relaxed, though nutritionists caution that the earlier advice was based on the best evidence available at the time.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Nutritionists were wrong to warn against eggs and should apologise."},{"k":"B","t":"Guidance against eggs has been relaxed as research showed dietary cholesterol matters less than once thought, though the old advice reflected the evidence then available."},{"k":"C","t":"Saturated fat is the main cause of heart disease."},{"k":"D","t":"Eggs are a healthy food and should be eaten daily."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"B captures the change in guidance, its reason, and the caveat. D and C go beyond the paragraph; A contradicts the nutritionists' defence."},{"id":"ps-n7","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"The bystander effect describes the tendency of individuals to be less likely to help a victim when others are present. Early research attributed this to a diffusion of responsibility: each person assumes someone else will act. More recent analyses of real-world CCTV footage, however, found that in most public conflicts at least one bystander intervened — and the more bystanders present, the more likely it was that someone would help.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"People are generally unwilling to help strangers in public."},{"k":"B","t":"Real-world footage challenges the bystander effect, showing that intervention is common and more likely with larger crowds."},{"k":"C","t":"CCTV cameras should be installed in all public places."},{"k":"D","t":"Diffusion of responsibility explains why people do not help in emergencies."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"B captures the paragraph's turn: new evidence challenges the classic effect. A and D restate the old view; C is an unsupported prescription."},{"id":"ps-n8","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Many languages have dozens of words for kinship relations that English lumps together. Where English has 'uncle', Hindi distinguishes between a father's elder brother, a father's younger brother and a mother's brother, each with a different term. Such distinctions are not arbitrary: they typically mirror differences in the roles, obligations and inheritance rights these relatives hold within the family.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Kinship vocabulary in different languages reflects the social roles and obligations relatives hold."},{"k":"B","t":"The father's elder brother has the most authority in Indian families."},{"k":"C","t":"English kinship terms are imprecise and should be reformed."},{"k":"D","t":"Hindi has more words than English."}],"answer":"A","explanation":"A states the general claim illustrated by the Hindi example. D is an unwarranted generalisation; C is an evaluation not made; B is too specific and unstated."},{"id":"ps-n9","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Self-checkout machines were introduced to cut labour costs and speed up shopping. Retailers have since discovered unexpected downsides: rates of theft, both deliberate and accidental, are higher at self-checkouts, and customers often need staff assistance anyway. Some chains have begun reducing the number of machines or restricting them to small baskets.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Self-checkout machines are a failure and will soon disappear."},{"k":"B","t":"Theft is the biggest problem facing retailers today."},{"k":"C","t":"Customers prefer staffed checkouts."},{"k":"D","t":"Unforeseen costs such as higher theft have led some retailers to scale back self-checkouts introduced to save labour."}],"answer":"D","explanation":"D captures the purpose, the downsides and the response. A overstates; C is unsupported; B overgeneralises."},{"id":"ps-n10","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"The phrase 'survival of the fittest' suggests that nature rewards strength and aggression. Yet cooperation is everywhere in the living world: cells within bodies, ants within colonies, and many species of birds and mammals that share food and raise one another's young. Evolutionary theory now recognises that cooperation can be favoured by natural selection just as competition can.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Ants are the most cooperative species."},{"k":"B","t":"Cooperation is as much a product of natural selection as competition is."},{"k":"C","t":"Nature rewards strength and aggression above all else."},{"k":"D","t":"The phrase 'survival of the fittest' was coined by Darwin."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"B states the paragraph's main point. C is the view challenged; A is an unsupported superlative; D is a factual claim not made (and false)."},{"id":"ps-n11","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Lotteries are sometimes described as a tax on the poor, since low-income households spend a larger share of their income on tickets. Defenders argue that buyers are not coerced and that the revenue funds education and other public goods. Critics reply that the voluntary nature of the purchase is undermined by heavy advertising aimed precisely at those most likely to overestimate their chances.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Lottery advertising is always misleading."},{"k":"B","t":"Low-income households should be banned from buying lottery tickets."},{"k":"C","t":"Lotteries fund education and are therefore beneficial."},{"k":"D","t":"The paragraph presents the debate over whether lotteries unfairly burden the poor, including the dispute over how voluntary ticket purchases really are."}],"answer":"D","explanation":"The paragraph presents both sides; D captures the debate. C adopts only one side; B and A are extreme claims not made."},{"id":"ps-n12","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"The Great Barrier Reef is often described as a single structure. In fact, it consists of nearly three thousand individual reefs and hundreds of islands spread over an area larger than Italy. This fragmentation has implications for conservation: some reefs may recover from bleaching by drawing larvae from healthier neighbours, but only if the network of connections between them remains intact.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The reef cannot recover from bleaching."},{"k":"B","t":"The reef is a network of many reefs, and its resilience depends on maintaining connections among them."},{"k":"C","t":"Bleaching is the only threat to the reef."},{"k":"D","t":"The Great Barrier Reef is larger than Italy."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"B captures the structural fact and its conservation implication. D is a detail (and a distortion — the area is larger, not the reef as a structure); C and A are extreme claims."},{"id":"ps-n13","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Early electric cars, popular around 1900, were quiet, clean and easy to drive. They lost out to petrol cars not because they were inferior in every respect, but because petrol vehicles had far greater range, and cheap oil and the electric starter motor removed their main disadvantages. Today's revival of electric vehicles depends on overcoming the same range problem that defeated them a century ago.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Electric cars lost out a century ago chiefly because of limited range — the same challenge their revival must now overcome."},{"k":"B","t":"Petrol cars are cleaner than electric cars."},{"k":"C","t":"Electric cars were inferior to petrol cars in every respect."},{"k":"D","t":"The electric starter motor was the most important invention in automotive history."}],"answer":"A","explanation":"A links the historical cause and present challenge. C contradicts 'not … inferior in every respect'; D overstates a detail; B is false according to the paragraph."},{"id":"ps-n14","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Many organisations reward employees for individual performance. Yet in jobs requiring close collaboration, such incentives can backfire: employees may hoard information, avoid helping colleagues and compete for credit. Research suggests that team-based rewards, or a mix of individual and team incentives, produce better results where success depends on cooperation.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Employees naturally hoard information."},{"k":"B","t":"Where work is collaborative, individual rewards can undermine cooperation, and team-based incentives tend to perform better."},{"k":"C","t":"Team-based rewards should replace all other forms of pay."},{"k":"D","t":"Individual incentives always reduce productivity."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"B preserves the condition ('in collaborative work'). D and C drop the condition and overstate; A is a distortion presenting a conditional behaviour as a natural trait."},{"id":"ps-n15","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Volcanic eruptions are usually associated with destruction, but volcanic soils are among the most fertile on Earth. Ash weathers into soils rich in minerals such as potassium and phosphorus, which is why dense agricultural populations have long lived on the slopes of active volcanoes in Java, Italy and Central America, accepting the risks in exchange for the harvests.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Volcanic eruptions are less dangerous than commonly believed."},{"k":"B","t":"Java has more volcanoes than any other island."},{"k":"C","t":"Fertile volcanic soils explain why people farm densely near active volcanoes despite the danger."},{"k":"D","t":"Potassium and phosphorus are essential nutrients for plants."}],"answer":"C","explanation":"C states the main point. A distorts — the paragraph accepts the danger. B is an unsupported detail; D is background, not the main point."},{"id":"ps-n16","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Standardised tests are criticised for measuring only a narrow slice of ability. Their defenders concede the point but argue that the alternatives — interviews, essays, recommendation letters — are even more susceptible to bias, favouring applicants who have access to coaching and connections. A flawed but uniform measure, they contend, may be fairer than a richer but more manipulable one.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Defenders argue that despite their narrowness, standardised tests may be fairer than alternatives that are more open to bias."},{"k":"B","t":"Recommendation letters should be abolished."},{"k":"C","t":"Standardised tests measure all important abilities accurately."},{"k":"D","t":"Interviews are the best way to assess applicants."}],"answer":"A","explanation":"A captures the defenders' argument including the concession. C contradicts the concession; D contradicts the argument; B is a prescription not made."},{"id":"ps-n17","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Archaeologists once believed that agriculture spread across Europe as farming knowledge passed from one hunter-gatherer group to the next. Ancient DNA analysis has overturned this view. Genetic evidence shows that farming arrived largely with migrating populations from Anatolia, who gradually replaced or absorbed the existing hunter-gatherers.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"DNA analysis has solved every major question in archaeology."},{"k":"B","t":"Hunter-gatherers in Europe quickly learned to farm from their neighbours."},{"k":"C","t":"Anatolia was the birthplace of agriculture."},{"k":"D","t":"Ancient DNA shows farming spread into Europe mainly through migration of people, not merely through the transfer of ideas."}],"answer":"D","explanation":"D states the new finding and the contrast. B is the old view; C is an overstatement not made; A is an absurd overgeneralisation."},{"id":"ps-n18","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Bilingual children sometimes start speaking slightly later than monolingual children and may initially have smaller vocabularies in each language. Parents sometimes take this as a sign of confusion. But when vocabulary in both languages is counted together, bilingual children typically match or exceed their monolingual peers, and the early lag disappears within a few years.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Bilingualism confuses young children and delays their development."},{"k":"B","t":"Bilingual children have larger vocabularies than adults."},{"k":"C","t":"Apparent early delays in bilingual children largely disappear when both languages are considered together."},{"k":"D","t":"Parents should speak only one language to their children."}],"answer":"C","explanation":"C summarises the key reframing. A is the parents' misconception; D is an unwarranted prescription; B is absurd."},{"id":"ps-n19","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Large dams were once seen as symbols of national progress, supplying electricity, irrigation and flood control. Over time, their costs have become clearer: displaced communities, silted reservoirs, damaged fisheries and, in some cases, significant methane emissions from decaying vegetation. Many countries now favour smaller, decentralised projects, though large dams continue to be built where demand for power is pressing.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Once celebrated, large dams are now viewed more critically because of their social and environmental costs, though they are still built where power needs are acute."},{"k":"B","t":"Smaller projects produce more electricity than large dams."},{"k":"C","t":"Methane is the main environmental problem caused by dams."},{"k":"D","t":"Large dams should never be built."}],"answer":"A","explanation":"A captures the shift and the qualification. D is extreme; C overemphasises one detail; B is an unsupported claim."},{"id":"ps-n20","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"The 'Mozart effect' — the claim that listening to classical music makes children smarter — began with a small 1993 study showing a brief improvement in spatial reasoning among college students. The finding was quickly exaggerated in the media, and some governments even distributed classical CDs to new parents. Subsequent studies failed to replicate the effect, finding that any benefit was small, short-lived and attributable to improved mood or arousal.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Governments should not distribute CDs to parents."},{"k":"B","t":"Classical music makes children more intelligent."},{"k":"C","t":"Mood has a strong effect on intelligence."},{"k":"D","t":"A modest, short-lived finding was exaggerated into the 'Mozart effect', which later research did not support."}],"answer":"D","explanation":"D traces the arc from small finding to exaggeration to non-replication. B states the myth; A and C are narrow or distorted inferences."},{"id":"ps-n21","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"In many countries, the share of people living alone has risen sharply over the past half-century. This is often presented as evidence of growing loneliness. But living alone and being lonely are not the same: many who live alone have rich social lives, while some people living with family feel profoundly isolated. Policies aimed at loneliness should therefore target social connection rather than household composition.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The number of people living alone has doubled."},{"k":"B","t":"Living alone causes loneliness."},{"k":"C","t":"People who live with family are lonelier than those who live alone."},{"k":"D","t":"Since living alone is not the same as loneliness, policy should focus on social connection rather than household arrangements."}],"answer":"D","explanation":"D combines the distinction and its policy conclusion. B is the view challenged; C distorts the comparison; A is an unstated detail."},{"id":"ps-n22","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Historians have long debated whether the Industrial Revolution improved living standards for ordinary workers. Real wages rose only slowly in its early decades, while working hours lengthened and urban mortality worsened. Over the longer term, however, industrialisation produced sustained growth in incomes and life expectancy unknown in earlier centuries.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The Industrial Revolution harmed workers in the short run but led to long-term gains in living standards."},{"k":"B","t":"Workers' living standards improved immediately with industrialisation."},{"k":"C","t":"Urban mortality was the most important effect of industrialisation."},{"k":"D","t":"Historians agree that the Industrial Revolution was harmful."}],"answer":"A","explanation":"A captures both time frames. B contradicts the short-term evidence; C overstates a detail; D misstates the debate."},{"id":"ps-n23","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Crowdfunding platforms have allowed thousands of independent creators to finance projects without studios, publishers or banks. But success on these platforms is highly uneven. A small number of campaigns raise the bulk of the money, often those by creators who already have large online followings. Rather than levelling the playing field, crowdfunding may partly reproduce existing inequalities of visibility.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Crowdfunding has completely replaced traditional sources of finance."},{"k":"B","t":"Most crowdfunding campaigns are fraudulent."},{"k":"C","t":"Creators with large followings should not use crowdfunding."},{"k":"D","t":"Though it bypasses traditional gatekeepers, crowdfunding tends to reward those already visible, partly reproducing existing inequalities."}],"answer":"D","explanation":"D captures the promise and its limitation. A is an overstatement; C is a prescription not made; B is unsupported."},{"id":"ps-n24","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Translation software has improved dramatically, producing fluent output for many common language pairs. Yet professional translators remain in demand for legal, medical and literary work. In these fields, a small error can have serious consequences, and fluency can disguise mistakes that only an expert would notice.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Machine translation is always inaccurate."},{"k":"B","t":"Despite advances in machine translation, human experts remain essential where errors are costly and fluent output may hide mistakes."},{"k":"C","t":"Translation software has made human translators obsolete."},{"k":"D","t":"Literary translation is the most difficult kind."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"B states the main point with its reason. C contradicts; D is an unstated ranking; A is an absolute the paragraph rejects."},{"id":"ps-n25","topic":"ps","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Ancient Roman concrete has survived for two thousand years in harbour walls exposed to seawater, while much modern concrete degrades within decades. Researchers have found that Roman builders mixed volcanic ash with lime and seawater, triggering chemical reactions that actually strengthen the material over time. Engineers are now studying the recipe in hopes of creating more durable, lower-carbon cement.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Roman concrete is stronger than modern concrete in every application."},{"k":"B","t":"Volcanic ash is found only in Italy."},{"k":"C","t":"The unusual chemistry that makes Roman marine concrete so durable may help engineers design better modern cement."},{"k":"D","t":"Modern concrete is poorly made."}],"answer":"C","explanation":"C combines the discovery and its application. A overstates; D is an unwarranted judgement; B is an irrelevant, unsupported claim."},{"id":"ps-n26","topic":"ps","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"It is often said that history is written by the victors. The phrase captures something real — conquerors control archives, monuments and schoolbooks — but it obscures as much as it reveals. The defeated frequently write history too, sometimes more prolifically, since loss demands explanation in a way victory does not. The Lost Cause mythology of the American South, crafted by the defeated Confederacy, shaped popular understanding of the Civil War for a century.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"History is always written by the victors, who control archives and schoolbooks."},{"k":"B","t":"The defeated, needing to explain loss, can shape historical memory as powerfully as the victors, complicating the familiar adage."},{"k":"C","t":"The Lost Cause mythology was the most influential historical narrative of the twentieth century."},{"k":"D","t":"Victors rarely bother to write history at all."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"B captures the qualified critique of the adage. A restates the adage the paragraph complicates; C overstates the example; D reverses the claim into an extreme."},{"id":"ps-n27","topic":"ps","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Economists generally favour taxing pollution over regulating it directly. A tax lets each firm decide how much to cut emissions, so reductions happen where they are cheapest. Critics argue that this logic treats pollution as merely a cost to be priced rather than a harm to be prevented, and that it allows wealthy firms to 'buy' the right to pollute communities that are often poor. The economists' reply is that what matters to the atmosphere is the total quantity of emissions, not who emits them.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Wealthy firms should be banned from polluting poor communities."},{"k":"B","t":"Total emissions are the only thing that matters in environmental policy."},{"k":"C","t":"Pollution taxes are more efficient than regulation, and critics who object to them misunderstand economics."},{"k":"D","t":"The paragraph sets out the efficiency case for pollution taxes, the moral and distributional objection to them, and the economists' response."}],"answer":"D","explanation":"The paragraph presents argument, objection and reply without endorsing either side; D reflects that structure. C takes sides; A is a prescription not made; B elevates the economists' reply into the paragraph's conclusion."},{"id":"ps-n28","topic":"ps","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"The modern concept of childhood as a protected, distinct stage of life is often treated as natural and universal. Historians have argued, controversially, that it is largely a product of the early modern period, when schooling expanded and child labour began to decline. Critics of this view point out that parents in every era mourned their dead children and recognised the young as needing care; what changed, they argue, was not whether childhood existed but how long it lasted and how sharply it was separated from adulthood.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Historians disagree over whether childhood was 'invented' in the early modern era or whether only its duration and separation from adulthood changed."},{"k":"B","t":"Childhood did not exist before the early modern period."},{"k":"C","t":"Schooling is the main cause of modern childhood."},{"k":"D","t":"Parents in earlier eras did not love their children."}],"answer":"A","explanation":"A captures both positions. B states one side in extreme form; D is a distortion contradicted by the critics' point; C overstates one factor."},{"id":"ps-n29","topic":"ps","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Placebos, by definition, contain no active ingredient. Yet a growing number of studies report that patients benefit even from 'open-label' placebos — pills they are told are inert. Researchers suggest that the ritual of treatment itself, the act of taking a pill under a clinician's care, may trigger expectations and bodily responses independent of deception. If so, the long-held assumption that placebos work only when patients are fooled may need revision.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Placebos are as effective as real medicines."},{"k":"B","t":"Benefits from placebos that patients know are inert suggest the treatment ritual itself may produce effects, challenging the view that placebos require deception."},{"k":"C","t":"Patients who take placebos are easily fooled."},{"k":"D","t":"Doctors should prescribe placebos instead of active drugs."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"B combines the finding, the proposed mechanism and the implication. A and D overreach; C contradicts the open-label point."},{"id":"ps-n30","topic":"ps","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Moral philosophers have long distinguished between doing harm and allowing harm. Most people feel that pushing someone into a river is worse than failing to rescue a drowning stranger, even if the outcome is the same. Consequentialists dismiss this intuition as irrational: what matters is the result. Others defend it, arguing that a morality without the distinction would make impossible demands, requiring each of us to devote our lives to preventing every harm we could.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The distinction between doing and allowing harm, widely felt but disputed, is rejected by consequentialists and defended by others on the grounds that abandoning it would make morality impossibly demanding."},{"k":"B","t":"People are morally obliged to prevent every harm they can."},{"k":"C","t":"Consequentialism is the most rational moral theory."},{"k":"D","t":"Doing harm is always morally worse than allowing it."}],"answer":"A","explanation":"A captures the intuition, the challenge and the defence. D asserts one side as fact; C endorses one position; B states the consequence the defenders reject."},{"id":"ps-n31","topic":"ps","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Measures of national happiness often show a puzzling pattern: within a country, richer people report greater life satisfaction, yet over decades, as countries grow richer, average happiness barely rises. One explanation is that people judge their well-being relative to others; when everyone's income rises, relative positions stay the same. Recent data, however, suggest that the pattern may be partly an artefact of measurement, since happiness scales have fixed upper limits that make long-term increases hard to detect.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Happiness scales are useless for research."},{"k":"B","t":"The apparent failure of national growth to raise happiness may reflect relative comparisons, or partly the limits of happiness scales themselves."},{"k":"C","t":"Richer people are happier than poorer people in every country."},{"k":"D","t":"Money cannot buy happiness."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"B captures the puzzle and both proposed explanations. D is a cliché that oversimplifies; C is a detail; A is an extreme overstatement of the measurement caveat."},{"id":"ps-n32","topic":"ps","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Scientific revolutions, Thomas Kuhn argued, do not proceed by the steady accumulation of facts. A dominant paradigm guides research until anomalies accumulate that it cannot explain; eventually a new paradigm replaces it, not because it is proven superior by neutral criteria, but because it solves the problems that now seem most pressing and attracts a new generation of researchers. Critics charged that this made science sound irrational. Kuhn replied that he had described how science actually works, not argued that it was arbitrary.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Kuhn argued science advances through paradigm shifts driven partly by community judgement, a view critics called irrational but which Kuhn defended as descriptive rather than relativist."},{"k":"B","t":"Younger scientists are more open-minded than older ones."},{"k":"C","t":"Science progresses by the steady accumulation of facts."},{"k":"D","t":"Kuhn proved that science is arbitrary."}],"answer":"A","explanation":"A captures Kuhn's thesis, the criticism and his reply. C is the view Kuhn rejected; D distorts and contradicts his reply; B overgeneralises a detail."},{"id":"ps-n33","topic":"ps","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Free trade, economists argue, raises overall national income. But the gains are diffuse, spread thinly across millions of consumers in the form of slightly lower prices, while the losses are concentrated among workers in particular industries and regions who lose their jobs. This asymmetry helps explain why trade remains politically contentious: those who lose are few but organised and acutely aware of their losses, while those who gain barely notice.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The diffuse gains and concentrated losses of trade explain why it remains politically divisive despite raising overall income."},{"k":"B","t":"Consumers do not benefit from free trade."},{"k":"C","t":"Workers who lose jobs to trade should be compensated by the government."},{"k":"D","t":"Free trade harms national income."}],"answer":"A","explanation":"A captures the asymmetry and its political consequence. D and B contradict the paragraph; C is a prescription not made."},{"id":"ps-n34","topic":"ps","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"The idea of wilderness — land untouched by human hands — has shaped conservation for over a century. Yet many landscapes celebrated as pristine, from the Amazon to the American West, were in fact shaped for millennia by indigenous peoples through burning, planting and hunting. Treating such lands as wilderness has sometimes justified evicting the very communities whose practices sustained them.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The ideal of untouched wilderness misreads landscapes long shaped by indigenous peoples and has been used to justify their displacement."},{"k":"B","t":"The Amazon was entirely created by human activity."},{"k":"C","t":"Burning is the most effective conservation practice."},{"k":"D","t":"Conservation has been a complete failure."}],"answer":"A","explanation":"A captures the misreading and its consequence. B overstates; D is extreme; C is unsupported."},{"id":"ps-n35","topic":"ps","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Biographers of artists face a dilemma. Readers want to know how a life shaped the work, and the temptation is to read every painting or poem as a coded autobiography. Yet the most powerful art often transforms experience beyond recognition, and the attempt to trace it back to its sources can diminish what the artist made of them. The best biographies hold both truths in tension, illuminating the life without reducing the work to it.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Good artistic biography connects life and work without reducing the work to a mere reflection of the life."},{"k":"B","t":"Biographies of artists are pointless."},{"k":"C","t":"Every work of art is a coded autobiography."},{"k":"D","t":"Artists rarely draw on their own experiences."}],"answer":"A","explanation":"A captures the balanced conclusion. B and D are extreme; C is the temptation the paragraph warns against."},{"id":"ps-n36","topic":"ps","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Measures of poverty based on income alone can mislead. A household may have income above the poverty line but lack access to clean water, healthcare or schooling; another may have little cash income but be adequately fed from its own land. Multidimensional poverty indices attempt to capture these deprivations directly, though critics argue that combining them into a single number involves arbitrary choices about how much each deprivation should count.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Multidimensional indices capture deprivations income measures miss, though weighting those deprivations into one figure involves contestable judgements."},{"k":"B","t":"Households with their own land are never poor."},{"k":"C","t":"Clean water is more important than income."},{"k":"D","t":"Income-based poverty measures are completely useless."}],"answer":"A","explanation":"A captures the advantage and the criticism. D is extreme; B overgeneralises an example; C is an unstated ranking."},{"id":"ps-n37","topic":"ps","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"In the nineteenth century, many doctors resisted the idea that they themselves were spreading infection. Ignaz Semmelweis showed that handwashing dramatically reduced deaths in maternity wards, yet his colleagues largely rejected his findings. Part of the resistance was scientific — germ theory had not yet been established — but part was professional pride: accepting the evidence meant accepting that physicians had been killing their patients.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Semmelweis was the first scientist to discover germs."},{"k":"B","t":"Nineteenth-century doctors were generally incompetent."},{"k":"C","t":"Doctors resisted Semmelweis's handwashing evidence partly for lack of a supporting theory and partly because it implicated them in their patients' deaths."},{"k":"D","t":"Handwashing is the single most important medical practice."}],"answer":"C","explanation":"C captures both reasons for resistance. A misstates history; B overgeneralises; D is an unsupported superlative."},{"id":"ps-n38","topic":"ps","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Critics of social media argue that it has polarised politics. Yet polarisation in the United States began rising in the 1970s, long before the internet, and has risen fastest among older Americans, the group least likely to use social media. This does not mean social media has no effect, but it suggests that deeper forces — party realignment, partisan cable news, geographic sorting — may matter more.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The timing and demographics of polarisation suggest social media is at most one factor among deeper and older causes."},{"k":"B","t":"Polarisation began with the internet."},{"k":"C","t":"Social media has no effect on political polarisation."},{"k":"D","t":"Older Americans are the most polarised group because they watch cable news."}],"answer":"A","explanation":"A captures the qualified conclusion. C contradicts 'does not mean social media has no effect'; D asserts a causal link not established; B contradicts the evidence."},{"id":"ps-n39","topic":"ps","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Traditional economics assumes that people have stable preferences, which markets then satisfy. But preferences are partly shaped by the very markets meant to satisfy them: advertising creates desires, and what we come to want depends on what is available and what others around us have. If so, the claim that markets simply give people what they want becomes circular.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Advertising should be banned."},{"k":"B","t":"People's preferences never change."},{"k":"C","t":"Markets are the best way to satisfy human needs."},{"k":"D","t":"If markets shape the preferences they claim to satisfy, the argument that they merely give people what they want loses its force."}],"answer":"D","explanation":"D captures the argument and its conclusion. A is a prescription; B contradicts the paragraph; C is the claim the paragraph undermines."},{"id":"ps-n40","topic":"ps","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Archives are often imagined as neutral storehouses of the past. But every archive reflects decisions about what was worth keeping. Colonial archives, for instance, preserve the correspondence of administrators in exhaustive detail while recording the colonised mainly as subjects of taxation, law and policing. Historians who rely on them must read 'against the grain', attending to silences as carefully as to what is recorded.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Archives should be destroyed to avoid bias."},{"k":"B","t":"Colonial archives are worthless to historians."},{"k":"C","t":"Because archives reflect choices about what to preserve, historians must read them critically, attending to their silences."},{"k":"D","t":"Administrators kept detailed records of their correspondence."}],"answer":"C","explanation":"C captures the claim and its methodological implication. B and A are extreme; D is a detail."},{"id":"ps-n41","topic":"ps","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"The legal principle that ignorance of the law is no excuse made sense when laws were few and rooted in widely shared moral norms. Today, the volume of statutes and regulations is so vast that even lawyers cannot know them all, and many offences involve technical rules with no obvious moral content. Some legal scholars argue that the principle has become unfair, punishing people for violations they could not reasonably have known about.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Lawyers do not understand the law."},{"k":"B","t":"The growth and technicality of modern law have led some scholars to question the fairness of the principle that ignorance is no excuse."},{"k":"C","t":"Ignorance of the law should always be accepted as an excuse."},{"k":"D","t":"Modern laws have no moral basis."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"B captures the historical contrast and the scholarly challenge. C is an extreme prescription; A is a distortion; D overgeneralises."},{"id":"ps-n42","topic":"ps","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Charity appeals typically feature a single identifiable victim — a named child, a photograph — rather than statistics about thousands in need. The strategy works: people give more to one identifiable person than to many statistical ones. Some ethicists find this troubling, arguing that it leads donors to fund causes that are emotionally compelling rather than those where their money would do the most good.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Statistics are more persuasive than individual stories."},{"k":"B","t":"Appeals featuring identifiable victims raise more money, which some ethicists worry steers donations toward emotional rather than effective causes."},{"k":"C","t":"Charities should stop using photographs."},{"k":"D","t":"Donors are generally irrational."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"B captures the effect and the ethical concern. A contradicts the evidence; C is a prescription not made; D overgeneralises."},{"id":"ps-n43","topic":"ps","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Automation has historically destroyed specific jobs while creating new ones, leaving overall employment largely intact. Optimists expect the same pattern with artificial intelligence. Pessimists counter that previous technologies replaced physical or routine tasks, leaving humans to move into cognitive work, whereas AI targets cognition itself, potentially leaving fewer places for displaced workers to go.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Cognitive work is safe from automation."},{"k":"B","t":"Artificial intelligence will destroy all jobs."},{"k":"C","t":"Optimists expect AI to follow past automation in creating new jobs, while pessimists argue that because it targets cognition, the pattern may not hold."},{"k":"D","t":"Automation has always increased unemployment."}],"answer":"C","explanation":"C presents both positions and the key distinction. B and D are extreme or contrary to the paragraph; A contradicts the pessimists' point."},{"id":"ps-n44","topic":"ps","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Words for colours vary widely across languages: some have only two or three basic terms, others a dozen. Early researchers concluded that colour perception itself differed between cultures. Later studies found that people perceive colour differences similarly regardless of language, though having separate words for two shades can make people slightly faster at telling them apart.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Colour vocabulary varies across languages, but perception is largely shared, with language exerting only a modest effect on discrimination."},{"k":"B","t":"Speakers of different languages see colours completely differently."},{"k":"C","t":"Languages with more colour terms are more advanced."},{"k":"D","t":"Language has no effect at all on colour perception."}],"answer":"A","explanation":"A captures both the shared perception and the modest linguistic effect. B is the discredited early view; D ignores the modest effect; C is an unwarranted value judgement."},{"id":"ps-n45","topic":"ps","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"National parks are often promoted as places to escape modern life. Yet the experience of 'wild' nature they offer depends on roads, visitor centres, ranger services and carefully managed trails. The more popular a park becomes, the more infrastructure it requires — and the further it moves from the untouched ideal that draws visitors in the first place.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Roads are the main threat to national parks."},{"k":"B","t":"National parks are completely artificial."},{"k":"C","t":"National parks should be closed to visitors."},{"k":"D","t":"The experience of wilderness in national parks paradoxically depends on infrastructure that grows with popularity and erodes the ideal visitors seek."}],"answer":"D","explanation":"D captures the paradox. C is a prescription not made; B overstates; A is an unsupported narrowing."},{"id":"ps-n46","topic":"ps","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"The bestseller lists of a century ago are dominated by books almost no one reads today, while some works that sold poorly in their time are now considered classics. This suggests that contemporary popularity is a poor predictor of lasting value. It may also suggest, less comfortably, that our own judgements of which contemporary books matter are just as likely to be overturned.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Classics always sell poorly when first published."},{"k":"B","t":"Readers today have better taste than those of a century ago."},{"k":"C","t":"The gap between past popularity and lasting reputation suggests present judgements of literary value are also provisional."},{"k":"D","t":"Bestsellers are always poorly written."}],"answer":"C","explanation":"C captures the observation and its uncomfortable extension. D and A overgeneralise; B contradicts the paragraph's humility."},{"id":"ps-n47","topic":"ps","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Courts increasingly rely on algorithms to predict whether defendants will reoffend. Proponents argue that algorithms are more consistent than human judges, who may be swayed by fatigue or prejudice. Critics respond that algorithms trained on historical arrest data can reproduce the biases embedded in past policing, lending them a false appearance of objectivity.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Historical arrest data is completely accurate."},{"k":"B","t":"Algorithms are always fairer than human judges."},{"k":"C","t":"Human judges should be replaced by algorithms."},{"k":"D","t":"Algorithmic risk assessment promises consistency but may encode historical biases while appearing objective."}],"answer":"D","explanation":"D captures both the promise and the critique. B and C take one side to an extreme; A contradicts the critics' point."},{"id":"ps-n48","topic":"ps","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"The history of medicine is often told as a progression from superstition to science. But many traditional remedies contained active ingredients later confirmed by laboratory research: willow bark for pain, which led to aspirin; or sweet wormwood for fever, which led to a leading anti-malarial drug. At the same time, many traditional remedies were useless or harmful. The relationship between folk medicine and modern pharmacology is less a story of replacement than of selective testing and extraction.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Willow bark is the source of all modern painkillers."},{"k":"B","t":"Traditional remedies are as effective as modern drugs."},{"k":"C","t":"Rather than simply replacing folk medicine, modern pharmacology has tested it selectively, confirming some remedies and discarding others."},{"k":"D","t":"Modern medicine has no connection to traditional remedies."}],"answer":"C","explanation":"C captures the nuanced conclusion. B and D are opposite extremes; A overgeneralises one example."},{"id":"ps-n49","topic":"ps","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Productivity statistics have puzzled economists for two decades: despite the spread of computers, smartphones and the internet, measured productivity growth in rich countries has been sluggish. Some argue that the new technologies are simply less transformative than electricity or the combustion engine. Others contend that their benefits — free search, free maps, free communication — are real but poorly captured by statistics built to measure goods sold for a price.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Electricity was the most important invention in history."},{"k":"B","t":"Productivity statistics are deliberately manipulated by governments."},{"k":"C","t":"Digital technologies have failed to improve human welfare."},{"k":"D","t":"Sluggish measured productivity amid digital innovation may reflect either genuinely modest technologies or statistics ill-suited to capturing free digital benefits."}],"answer":"D","explanation":"D presents the puzzle and both explanations. C takes one side to an extreme; A is a tangent; B is an unsupported conspiracy claim."},{"id":"ps-n50","topic":"ps","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"The habit of dividing history into neat periods — the Middle Ages, the Renaissance, the Enlightenment — is indispensable for teaching but treacherous for understanding. The labels were often coined by later writers with an agenda: 'Middle Ages' was invented by Renaissance humanists eager to cast the preceding millennium as a dark interval between classical glory and their own revival of it. Periodisation, in other words, smuggles in judgements under the guise of mere chronology.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Historical period labels are useful but can embed the evaluative agendas of those who coined them, disguised as neutral chronology."},{"k":"B","t":"Renaissance humanists were the greatest historians of their age."},{"k":"C","t":"The Middle Ages were not as dark as commonly believed."},{"k":"D","t":"History should not be divided into periods."}],"answer":"A","explanation":"A captures the double point: useful yet value-laden. C is a related but narrower inference; B is unsupported; D contradicts 'indispensable for teaching'."},{"id":"grammar-n1","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","segments":["One of the reasons","the project failed","were the lack of","clear ownership."],"options":[{"k":"A","t":"One of the reasons"},{"k":"B","t":"the project failed"},{"k":"C","t":"were the lack of"},{"k":"D","t":"clear ownership."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"The subject is 'One' (of the reasons), which is singular: 'One of the reasons … was the lack of clear ownership.'"},{"id":"grammar-n2","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","segments":["Had the committee","acted sooner,","the crisis would not escalate","as quickly as it did."],"options":[{"k":"A","t":"Had the committee"},{"k":"B","t":"acted sooner,"},{"k":"C","t":"the crisis would not escalate"},{"k":"D","t":"as quickly as it did."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"A third-conditional (past unreal) requires 'would not have escalated' to match 'Had the committee acted'."},{"id":"grammar-n3","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","segments":["Each of the candidates","were asked","to submit","a written statement."],"options":[{"k":"A","t":"Each of the candidates"},{"k":"B","t":"were asked"},{"k":"C","t":"to submit"},{"k":"D","t":"a written statement."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}],"answer":"B","explanation":"'Each' is singular and takes a singular verb: 'Each of the candidates was asked'."},{"id":"grammar-n4","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","segments":["The new policy","is more effective","than any policy","the government has tried."],"options":[{"k":"A","t":"The new policy"},{"k":"B","t":"is more effective"},{"k":"C","t":"than any policy"},{"k":"D","t":"the government has tried."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"Comparing a member of a group with the group requires 'any other': 'more effective than any other policy' — otherwise the policy is compared with itself."},{"id":"grammar-n5","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","segments":["Walking into the auditorium,","the stage lights","blinded the speaker","for a moment."],"options":[{"k":"A","t":"Walking into the auditorium,"},{"k":"B","t":"the stage lights"},{"k":"C","t":"blinded the speaker"},{"k":"D","t":"for a moment."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}],"answer":"B","explanation":"Dangling modifier: 'Walking into the auditorium' must modify the speaker, not the stage lights. Correct: 'Walking into the auditorium, the speaker was blinded…'. The error surfaces at (B), where the wrong subject appears."},{"id":"grammar-n6","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","segments":["She is not only","a gifted mathematician","but also writes","poetry of real distinction."],"options":[{"k":"A","t":"She is not only"},{"k":"B","t":"a gifted mathematician"},{"k":"C","t":"but also writes"},{"k":"D","t":"poetry of real distinction."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"Parallelism with 'not only … but also': 'not only a gifted mathematician but also a poet of real distinction', or 'not only is a … but also writes …'. Noun phrase vs verb phrase breaks the parallel."},{"id":"grammar-n7","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","segments":["The data","from the latest survey","suggests","a sharp shift in consumer sentiment."],"options":[{"k":"A","t":"The data"},{"k":"B","t":"from the latest survey"},{"k":"C","t":"suggests"},{"k":"D","t":"a sharp shift in consumer sentiment."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}],"answer":"E","explanation":"No error. In modern usage 'data' is widely accepted as a mass noun with a singular verb; CAT does not treat 'data suggests' as an error. Don't over-correct."},{"id":"grammar-n8","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","segments":["Neither the CEO","nor her advisers","was willing","to comment on the merger."],"options":[{"k":"A","t":"Neither the CEO"},{"k":"B","t":"nor her advisers"},{"k":"C","t":"was willing"},{"k":"D","t":"to comment on the merger."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"With neither…nor, the verb agrees with the nearer subject — 'advisers' (plural): 'were willing'."},{"id":"grammar-n9","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","segments":["The reason she resigned","was because","she disagreed","with the board's strategy."],"options":[{"k":"A","t":"The reason she resigned"},{"k":"B","t":"was because"},{"k":"C","t":"she disagreed"},{"k":"D","t":"with the board's strategy."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}],"answer":"B","explanation":"'The reason … was because' is redundant; use 'The reason … was that she disagreed'."},{"id":"grammar-n10","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","segments":["By the time the rescue team arrived,","the climbers","had already descended","to the base camp."],"options":[{"k":"A","t":"By the time the rescue team arrived,"},{"k":"B","t":"the climbers"},{"k":"C","t":"had already descended"},{"k":"D","t":"to the base camp."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}],"answer":"E","explanation":"No error. Past perfect 'had descended' correctly marks the action completed before the team arrived."},{"id":"grammar-n11","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","segments":["The committee","has submitted","their report","to the director."],"options":[{"k":"A","t":"The committee"},{"k":"B","t":"has submitted"},{"k":"C","t":"their report"},{"k":"D","t":"to the director."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"'The committee has submitted' treats it as a singular unit; the pronoun must agree: 'its report'."},{"id":"grammar-n12","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","segments":["Scarcely had the meeting begun","than the fire alarm","went off,","forcing an evacuation."],"options":[{"k":"A","t":"Scarcely had the meeting begun"},{"k":"B","t":"than the fire alarm"},{"k":"C","t":"went off,"},{"k":"D","t":"forcing an evacuation."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}],"answer":"B","explanation":"'Scarcely' pairs with 'when' (or 'before'), not 'than': 'Scarcely had the meeting begun when the fire alarm went off'."},{"id":"grammar-n13","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","segments":["The museum's collection","of Mughal miniatures","is larger than","the British Library."],"options":[{"k":"A","t":"The museum's collection"},{"k":"B","t":"of Mughal miniatures"},{"k":"C","t":"is larger than"},{"k":"D","t":"the British Library."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}],"answer":"D","explanation":"Faulty comparison: a collection is compared with a library. Correct: 'larger than that of the British Library'."},{"id":"grammar-n14","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","segments":["If I was","in your position,","I would accept","the offer immediately."],"options":[{"k":"A","t":"If I was"},{"k":"B","t":"in your position,"},{"k":"C","t":"I would accept"},{"k":"D","t":"the offer immediately."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}],"answer":"A","explanation":"Hypothetical (contrary-to-fact) conditions take the subjunctive 'were': 'If I were in your position'."},{"id":"grammar-n15","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","segments":["Between you and I,","the proposal","has little chance","of being approved."],"options":[{"k":"A","t":"Between you and I,"},{"k":"B","t":"the proposal"},{"k":"C","t":"has little chance"},{"k":"D","t":"of being approved."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}],"answer":"A","explanation":"Prepositions take object pronouns: 'Between you and me'."},{"id":"grammar-n16","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","segments":["The professor insisted","that every student","submits","the assignment by Friday."],"options":[{"k":"A","t":"The professor insisted"},{"k":"B","t":"that every student"},{"k":"C","t":"submits"},{"k":"D","t":"the assignment by Friday."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"After verbs of demand/insistence, the mandative subjunctive is used: 'insisted that every student submit'."},{"id":"grammar-n17","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","segments":["Less people","attended the conference","this year","than in any previous year."],"options":[{"k":"A","t":"Less people"},{"k":"B","t":"attended the conference"},{"k":"C","t":"this year"},{"k":"D","t":"than in any previous year."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}],"answer":"A","explanation":"Countable nouns take 'fewer': 'Fewer people attended'. 'Less' is for uncountable quantities."},{"id":"grammar-n18","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","segments":["The manager, along with","her entire team,","are expected","to attend the review."],"options":[{"k":"A","t":"The manager, along with"},{"k":"B","t":"her entire team,"},{"k":"C","t":"are expected"},{"k":"D","t":"to attend the review."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"'Along with' does not create a compound subject; the subject is 'The manager' (singular): 'is expected'."},{"id":"grammar-n19","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","segments":["He is one of those writers","who has never","sought","publicity for his work."],"options":[{"k":"A","t":"He is one of those writers"},{"k":"B","t":"who has never"},{"k":"C","t":"sought"},{"k":"D","t":"publicity for his work."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}],"answer":"B","explanation":"In 'one of those writers who …', the relative pronoun 'who' refers to 'writers' (plural), so the verb is plural: 'who have never sought'."},{"id":"grammar-n20","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","segments":["The new regulations","will effect","every small business","in the region."],"options":[{"k":"A","t":"The new regulations"},{"k":"B","t":"will effect"},{"k":"C","t":"every small business"},{"k":"D","t":"in the region."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}],"answer":"B","explanation":"'Affect' (verb) means to influence; 'effect' as a verb means to bring about. Correct: 'will affect every small business'."},{"id":"grammar-n21","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Choose the grammatically correct sentence.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Hardly anyone noticed that the clock had stopped, nor did they care."},{"k":"B","t":"Hardly anyone noticed that the clock has stopped, and nor they cared."},{"k":"C","t":"Hardly no one noticed that the clock had stopped."},{"k":"D","t":"Hardly anyone didn't notice that the clock had stopped."}],"answer":"A","explanation":"A is correct: 'hardly anyone' is negative in sense, and 'nor did they care' correctly inverts. C and D are double negatives; B mixes tenses ('had noticed … has stopped') and has an ungrammatical 'and nor they cared'."},{"id":"grammar-n22","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Choose the grammatically correct sentence.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Unanimously accepting the proposal, everyone was surprised by the board."},{"k":"B","t":"The proposals acceptance unanimously surprised everyone."},{"k":"C","t":"The proposal being accepted unanimously surprised everyone who were present."},{"k":"D","t":"The proposal was accepted unanimously, which surprised everyone."}],"answer":"D","explanation":"D is correct ('which' refers to the whole preceding clause, acceptable in standard usage). C: 'everyone … were' — 'everyone' takes singular. B: missing apostrophe and misplaced adverb. A: dangling modifier — 'everyone' didn't accept the proposal."},{"id":"grammar-n23","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","segments":["Due to","heavy rainfall,","the match","was postponed."],"options":[{"k":"A","t":"Due to"},{"k":"B","t":"heavy rainfall,"},{"k":"C","t":"the match"},{"k":"D","t":"was postponed."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}],"answer":"E","explanation":"No error. While traditionalists prefer 'owing to' when modifying a verb, 'due to' in this position is accepted in modern standard English and is not the kind of error CAT tests. The sentence is correct."},{"id":"grammar-n24","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","segments":["The quality of","the apples","in this orchard","are excellent."],"options":[{"k":"A","t":"The quality of"},{"k":"B","t":"the apples"},{"k":"C","t":"in this orchard"},{"k":"D","t":"are excellent."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}],"answer":"D","explanation":"The subject is 'quality' (singular), not 'apples': 'is excellent'."},{"id":"grammar-n25","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","segments":["Having been warned","about the risks,","the decision to proceed","was taken by the investors anyway."],"options":[{"k":"A","t":"Having been warned"},{"k":"B","t":"about the risks,"},{"k":"C","t":"the decision to proceed"},{"k":"D","t":"was taken by the investors anyway."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"Dangling modifier: 'Having been warned' must refer to the investors, but the subject is 'the decision'. Correct: 'Having been warned …, the investors decided to proceed anyway.'"},{"id":"grammar-n26","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","segments":["Not until the final report was published","did the public learn","how extensively the agency had","misled them."],"options":[{"k":"A","t":"Not until the final report was published"},{"k":"B","t":"did the public learn"},{"k":"C","t":"how extensively the agency had"},{"k":"D","t":"misled them."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}],"answer":"E","explanation":"No error. Negative opening 'Not until …' correctly triggers inversion ('did the public learn'); past perfect 'had misled' marks the earlier action; 'them' refers to the public considered as people, acceptable here."},{"id":"grammar-n27","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","segments":["The number of applicants","who were rejected","for reasons unrelated to merit","have prompted an inquiry."],"options":[{"k":"A","t":"The number of applicants"},{"k":"B","t":"who were rejected"},{"k":"C","t":"for reasons unrelated to merit"},{"k":"D","t":"have prompted an inquiry."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}],"answer":"D","explanation":"The subject is 'The number' (singular), separated from its verb by two modifiers: 'has prompted an inquiry'. The long gap is the trap."},{"id":"grammar-n28","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","segments":["Like the economists of the 1930s,","today's policymakers","face a crisis whose scale","few of them had anticipated."],"options":[{"k":"A","t":"Like the economists of the 1930s,"},{"k":"B","t":"today's policymakers"},{"k":"C","t":"face a crisis whose scale"},{"k":"D","t":"few of them had anticipated."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}],"answer":"E","explanation":"No error. 'Like the economists' correctly compares people with people (policymakers); 'whose' can refer to things; past perfect 'had anticipated' is apt."},{"id":"grammar-n29","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","segments":["Unlike in France,","where the state funds most research,","American universities depend","heavily on private endowments."],"options":[{"k":"A","t":"Unlike in France,"},{"k":"B","t":"where the state funds most research,"},{"k":"C","t":"American universities depend"},{"k":"D","t":"heavily on private endowments."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}],"answer":"A","explanation":"Faulty comparison: 'Unlike in France' compares a place-context with 'American universities'. Correct: 'Unlike French universities, which depend on state funding, American universities …' or 'Unlike in France, in America universities …'."},{"id":"grammar-n30","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","segments":["So convincing was","the forgery that","even experts were deceived,","not to mention the casual buyer."],"options":[{"k":"A","t":"So convincing was"},{"k":"B","t":"the forgery that"},{"k":"C","t":"even experts were deceived,"},{"k":"D","t":"not to mention the casual buyer."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}],"answer":"E","explanation":"No error. 'So convincing was the forgery that …' is a correct inversion for emphasis; 'not to mention' is idiomatic."},{"id":"grammar-n31","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","segments":["The board demanded that","the CFO not only explain","the discrepancies but also","that she resign."],"options":[{"k":"A","t":"The board demanded that"},{"k":"B","t":"the CFO not only explain"},{"k":"C","t":"the discrepancies but also"},{"k":"D","t":"that she resign."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}],"answer":"D","explanation":"Parallelism: 'not only explain … but also resign'. Repeating 'that she' after 'but also' breaks the parallel structure set up after 'not only'."},{"id":"grammar-n32","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","segments":["The research, which","was conducted over a decade","and involving thousands of participants,","overturned earlier findings."],"options":[{"k":"A","t":"The research, which"},{"k":"B","t":"was conducted over a decade"},{"k":"C","t":"and involving thousands of participants,"},{"k":"D","t":"overturned earlier findings."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"Parallelism within the relative clause: 'which was conducted … and involved thousands'. 'Involving' (participle) is not parallel with 'was conducted' (finite verb)."},{"id":"grammar-n33","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","segments":["It is high time","the government","addresses","the growing housing shortage."],"options":[{"k":"A","t":"It is high time"},{"k":"B","t":"the government"},{"k":"C","t":"addresses"},{"k":"D","t":"the growing housing shortage."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"'It is high time' is followed by the past subjunctive: 'It is high time the government addressed the shortage'."},{"id":"grammar-n34","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","segments":["The senator's speech","was as eloquent","if not more eloquent than,","any she had delivered before."],"options":[{"k":"A","t":"The senator's speech"},{"k":"B","t":"was as eloquent"},{"k":"C","t":"if not more eloquent than,"},{"k":"D","t":"any she had delivered before."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"Incomplete comparison: 'as eloquent as, if not more eloquent than, any …'. The second 'as' is missing, and the comma placement must enclose the parenthetical."},{"id":"grammar-n35","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","segments":["Only after the auditors","had examined every ledger","the firm admitted","that its accounts were misstated."],"options":[{"k":"A","t":"Only after the auditors"},{"k":"B","t":"had examined every ledger"},{"k":"C","t":"the firm admitted"},{"k":"D","t":"that its accounts were misstated."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"A sentence opening with 'Only after …' requires subject–auxiliary inversion in the main clause: 'did the firm admit'."},{"id":"grammar-n36","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","segments":["The CEO, as well as","the members of the board,","were present","at the shareholders' meeting."],"options":[{"k":"A","t":"The CEO, as well as"},{"k":"B","t":"the members of the board,"},{"k":"C","t":"were present"},{"k":"D","t":"at the shareholders' meeting."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"'As well as' introduces a parenthetical, not a compound subject; the subject 'The CEO' is singular: 'was present'."},{"id":"grammar-n37","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","segments":["Each of the three proposals","has its merits,","but every one of them","fail to address the core issue."],"options":[{"k":"A","t":"Each of the three proposals"},{"k":"B","t":"has its merits,"},{"k":"C","t":"but every one of them"},{"k":"D","t":"fail to address the core issue."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}],"answer":"D","explanation":"'Every one of them' is singular and takes a singular verb: 'fails to address'. The plural 'them' sitting next to the verb is the trap."},{"id":"grammar-n38","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","segments":["Rarely has","a single invention","so transformed","the way people communicates."],"options":[{"k":"A","t":"Rarely has"},{"k":"B","t":"a single invention"},{"k":"C","t":"so transformed"},{"k":"D","t":"the way people communicates."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}],"answer":"D","explanation":"'People' is plural: 'the way people communicate'. The opening inversion ('Rarely has … so transformed') is correct and acts as a distraction."},{"id":"grammar-n39","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","segments":["The findings were","so different from","what the team expected","that they doubted their instruments."],"options":[{"k":"A","t":"The findings were"},{"k":"B","t":"so different from"},{"k":"C","t":"what the team expected"},{"k":"D","t":"that they doubted their instruments."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}],"answer":"E","explanation":"No error. 'Different from' is the preferred idiom; 'so … that' correctly expresses result; 'they' clearly refers to the team members."},{"id":"grammar-n40","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","segments":["To truly understand","the crisis, one must","examine its origins,","as well as it's consequences."],"options":[{"k":"A","t":"To truly understand"},{"k":"B","t":"the crisis, one must"},{"k":"C","t":"examine its origins,"},{"k":"D","t":"as well as it's consequences."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}],"answer":"D","explanation":"'It's' = 'it is'; the possessive is 'its consequences'. The split infinitive in (A) is accepted in modern English and is a decoy."},{"id":"grammar-n41","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","segments":["The more the regulator","scrutinised the accounts,","the more irregularities","it was uncovering."],"options":[{"k":"A","t":"The more the regulator"},{"k":"B","t":"scrutinised the accounts,"},{"k":"C","t":"the more irregularities"},{"k":"D","t":"it was uncovering."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}],"answer":"D","explanation":"The correlative 'the more … the more …' needs parallel simple verbs: 'the more it scrutinised … the more irregularities it uncovered'. The progressive 'was uncovering' breaks the parallel."},{"id":"grammar-n42","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Choose the grammatically correct sentence.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Were the results replicated, the theory will gain wide acceptance."},{"k":"B","t":"If the results would be replicated, the theory would gain wide acceptance."},{"k":"C","t":"Were the results to be replicated, the theory would gain wide acceptance."},{"k":"D","t":"Had the results be replicated, the theory would have gained wide acceptance."}],"answer":"C","explanation":"C correctly uses the inverted subjunctive 'Were … to be' with 'would'. A mixes subjunctive with future 'will'. B uses 'would' in the if-clause. D needs 'Had the results been replicated'."},{"id":"grammar-n43","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Choose the sentence that is grammatically correct AND unambiguous.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"When Priya met Anjali, she was nervous."},{"k":"B","t":"Priya was nervous when she met Anjali."},{"k":"C","t":"Priya told Anjali that her proposal had been rejected."},{"k":"D","t":"When she met Anjali, Priya and her sister were nervous about her."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"B is clear: 'she' can only refer to Priya, the subject. A and C have ambiguous pronouns (who was nervous? whose proposal?). C's 'her' could refer to Priya, Anjali or the sister."},{"id":"grammar-n44","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Choose the grammatically correct sentence.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Neither the director nor any of the managers was able to explain the delay."},{"k":"B","t":"A series of errors were responsible for the delay."},{"k":"C","t":"The reason for the delay, as well as the costs involved, were explained to the shareholders."},{"k":"D","t":"Neither of the explanations offered were convincing to the auditors."}],"answer":"A","explanation":"A: with 'neither … nor', the verb agrees with the nearer subject 'any of the managers' — 'any' here is treated as singular, so 'was' is correct. C: subject is 'the reason' → 'was explained'. D: 'neither' (singular) → 'was convincing'. B: 'A series' → 'was responsible'."},{"id":"grammar-n45","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Choose the sentence with correct parallel structure.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The programme aims to reduce costs, quality improvement, and to expand access."},{"k":"B","t":"The programme aims at reducing costs, to improve quality, and expanding access."},{"k":"C","t":"The programme aims to reduce costs, improve quality, and expand access."},{"k":"D","t":"The programme aims to reduce costs, improving quality, and the expansion of access."}],"answer":"C","explanation":"C keeps all three items as bare infinitives after 'to'. The other options mix infinitives, gerunds and noun phrases."},{"id":"grammar-n46","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Choose the sentence in which the modifier is placed correctly.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Only she ate half the cake, so the rest was for later."},{"k":"B","t":"She only ate half the cake, leaving the rest for later."},{"k":"C","t":"She ate half the cake only, the rest for later leaving."},{"k":"D","t":"She ate only half the cake, leaving the rest for later."}],"answer":"D","explanation":"'Only' should sit immediately before what it limits — 'half the cake'. B implies she did nothing but eat; A means no one else ate; C is ungrammatical."},{"id":"grammar-n47","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Choose the grammatically correct sentence.","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Despite of the warnings, the expedition continued."},{"k":"B","t":"Despite the warnings, the expedition continued."},{"k":"C","t":"Despite that the warnings, the expedition continued."},{"k":"D","t":"In spite the warnings, the expedition continued."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"'Despite' takes a direct object with no 'of'; 'in spite of' needs 'of'. Only B is idiomatic."},{"id":"grammar-n48","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","segments":["Of the two candidates,","Ravi is","the most qualified","for the position."],"options":[{"k":"A","t":"Of the two candidates,"},{"k":"B","t":"Ravi is"},{"k":"C","t":"the most qualified"},{"k":"D","t":"for the position."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"When comparing exactly two, use the comparative: 'the more qualified'."},{"id":"grammar-n49","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","segments":["Lying on the table","was a letter","addressed to","whomever had rented the flat before."],"options":[{"k":"A","t":"Lying on the table"},{"k":"B","t":"was a letter"},{"k":"C","t":"addressed to"},{"k":"D","t":"whomever had rented the flat before."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}],"answer":"D","explanation":"Within its clause, the pronoun is the subject of 'had rented', so it must be 'whoever'. The preposition 'to' governs the whole clause, not the pronoun — a classic hard trap. The inversion 'Lying on the table was a letter' is correct."},{"id":"grammar-n50","topic":"grammar","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"mcq","stem":"Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.","segments":["The committee is considering","whether the funds should be","allocated to research","or if they should be returned to donors."],"options":[{"k":"A","t":"The committee is considering"},{"k":"B","t":"whether the funds should be"},{"k":"C","t":"allocated to research"},{"k":"D","t":"or if they should be returned to donors."},{"k":"E","t":"No Error"}],"answer":"D","explanation":"Parallelism in a 'whether … or …' construction: 'whether the funds should be allocated … or returned to donors'. Switching to 'or if they should be' breaks the parallel."},{"id":"pj-n1","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers.","sentences":["For most of the twentieth century, economists treated the household as a single decision-making unit with one set of preferences.","Money given to mothers, for instance, was spent far more on children's food and schooling than identical sums given to fathers.","This 'unitary' model was convenient, but it assumed away the possibility that spouses might want different things.","Field studies soon showed that it mattered a great deal whose hands income passed through."],"answer":"1342","explanation":"(1) states the old assumption. (3) names it ('This unitary model') and flags its flaw. (4) gives the evidence against it. (2) is a specific example ('for instance') of (4)'s general finding."},{"id":"pj-n2","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers.","sentences":["The Mercator projection, for example, preserves angles, which made it invaluable to sailors.","Early cartographers faced an impossible task: representing a curved surface on a flat page.","Every projection they devised therefore distorted something — area, shape, distance or direction.","But it does so by grossly inflating the size of regions near the poles, making Greenland look as large as Africa."],"answer":"2314","explanation":"(2) sets up the problem. (3) draws the consequence ('therefore distorted'). (1) offers an example of one projection. (4) gives the trade-off of that same projection ('But it does so')."},{"id":"pj-n3","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers.","sentences":["In response, many have reinvented themselves as community spaces, offering classes, maker labs and internet access.","The idea of a 'library' has changed more in the past thirty years than in the previous three hundred.","Critics worry that, in becoming everything, libraries risk losing the quiet that made them distinctive.","Once valued primarily as storehouses of books, libraries now compete with search engines that answer questions instantly."],"answer":"2413","explanation":"(2) is the broad thesis. (4) explains the old role and the new competitor. (1) describes the response ('In response'). (3) raises the critique of that response."},{"id":"pj-n4","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers.","sentences":["Bamboo is technically a grass, not a tree.","Engineers, however, have struggled with its tendency to split and its vulnerability to insects.","Yet some species can grow nearly a metre in a single day, faster than any other plant on earth.","This extraordinary growth rate has made bamboo attractive as a sustainable building material."],"answer":"1342","explanation":"(1) introduces bamboo. (3) contrasts ('Yet') with a striking fact. (4) refers back to that fact ('This extraordinary growth rate'). (2) introduces the counterpoint ('however')."},{"id":"pj-n5","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers.","sentences":["And yet patients given one often report real relief from pain.","Researchers once dismissed this as mere suggestion or wishful reporting.","A placebo is, by definition, an inert substance.","Brain-imaging studies, however, show that placebos can trigger the release of the body's own painkilling chemicals."],"answer":"3124","explanation":"(3) defines the term. (1) gives the paradox. (2) states the old dismissive explanation of 'this'. (4) overturns that explanation with new evidence ('however')."},{"id":"pj-n6","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers.","sentences":["Most doctors of the time blamed 'miasma', or bad air.","In 1854, a cholera outbreak killed over five hundred people in a single London neighbourhood within ten days.","John Snow, a local physician, suspected the water instead and mapped every death in the area.","The deaths clustered tightly around a single public pump on Broad Street, and removing its handle helped end the outbreak."],"answer":"2134","explanation":"Chronological narrative: event ((2)), prevailing view ((1)), the dissenter's method ((3)), the result ((4)). (4)'s 'The deaths' refers to the deaths Snow mapped in (3)."},{"id":"pj-n7","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers.","sentences":["Many companies now offer employees unlimited paid leave.","In practice, studies suggest workers with unlimited leave often take fewer days off than those with a fixed allowance.","On paper, this looks like a generous benefit.","Without a clear entitlement, employees fear that taking time off will signal a lack of commitment."],"answer":"1324","explanation":"(1) introduces the policy. (3) gives the apparent view ('On paper'). (2) contrasts with reality ('In practice'). (4) explains why."},{"id":"pj-n8","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers.","sentences":["These 'nectar guides' function like landing strips, directing bees toward the flower's centre.","Many flowers that look plain to us are, in ultraviolet, marked with vivid patterns.","They can, however, see ultraviolet light, which is invisible to humans.","Honeybees cannot see the colour red."],"answer":"4321","explanation":"(4) states a limitation, (3) the compensating ability ('however'). (2) shows the consequence for flowers. (1) names those patterns ('These nectar guides') and their function."},{"id":"pj-n9","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers.","sentences":["Those hit hardest tend to be people who hold most of their wealth in cash.","Inflation is often described as a hidden tax.","When prices rise, the real value of money held in savings falls, even though no one has formally taken anything away.","The wealthy, by contrast, typically own assets such as property and shares, whose prices often rise along with inflation."],"answer":"2314","explanation":"(2) gives the metaphor; (3) explains it; (1) identifies who suffers most; (4) contrasts with those who don't ('by contrast')."},{"id":"pj-n10","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers.","sentences":["Indeed, historians now believe the Black Death reached Europe partly along these same trade networks.","It was a shifting network of routes connecting China with Central Asia, Persia and the Mediterranean.","The Silk Road was never a single road.","Along these routes travelled not just silk but spices, glassware, religions and diseases."],"answer":"3241","explanation":"(3) corrects a misconception; (2) gives the correct description; (4) lists what moved along 'these routes'; (1) intensifies ('Indeed') with the most dramatic example — disease."},{"id":"pj-n11","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers.","sentences":["Many trips that would have been made by bus, bicycle or on foot are now made by car instead.","Ride-hailing apps promised to reduce urban traffic by making car ownership unnecessary.","Data from several American cities suggests the opposite has happened.","The logic was simple: fewer private cars would mean fewer vehicles on the road."],"answer":"2431","explanation":"(2) states the promise; (4) explains its logic; (3) reports the contrary evidence; (1) explains why traffic increased."},{"id":"pj-n12","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers.","sentences":["Language learners often feel that they understand far more than they can say.","Producing it, by contrast, demands retrieving it unprompted, under time pressure, along with the grammar to deploy it.","This gap between comprehension and production is entirely normal.","Recognising a word requires only matching it to a meaning already stored in memory."],"answer":"1342","explanation":"(1) states the experience; (3) names it ('This gap') and normalises it; (4) and (2) form a contrast pair explaining why ('Recognising' vs 'Producing … by contrast')."},{"id":"pj-n13","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers.","sentences":["Successive generations of builders inherited plans they had not drawn and frequently altered them to suit new tastes.","Medieval cathedrals often took more than a century to build.","Their original architects therefore rarely lived to see them finished.","This is why many cathedrals combine sharply contrasting styles within a single structure."],"answer":"2314","explanation":"(2): the fact; (3): consequence ('therefore'); (1): what followed; (4): the visible result ('This is why')."},{"id":"pj-n14","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers.","sentences":["The term 'introvert' is widely misunderstood.","It does not mean shy or socially anxious.","An introvert may therefore be perfectly confident in company, yet still need time alone afterward.","Rather, it describes someone who finds social interaction draining and recovers energy in solitude."],"answer":"1243","explanation":"(1): the misunderstanding; (2): what it doesn't mean; (4): 'Rather' — the correct definition; (3): implication ('therefore')."},{"id":"pj-n15","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers.","sentences":["Only gradually did norms such as editorial review and scholarly citation emerge to separate the reliable from the fraudulent.","In its first decades, it was used to churn out sensational pamphlets, astrological almanacs and religious polemics.","The printing press did not immediately produce a more informed public.","Misinformation spread as quickly as knowledge, sometimes faster."],"answer":"3241","explanation":"(3) is the counter-intuitive thesis; (2) gives evidence; (4) draws the implication; (1) describes the eventual corrective ('Only gradually')."},{"id":"pj-n16","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers.","sentences":["Octopuses have roughly five hundred million neurons.","Each arm can thus taste, touch and even make simple decisions semi-independently.","This distributed intelligence makes the octopus a fascinating test case for theories of what a 'mind' requires.","Strikingly, about two-thirds of them are located not in the brain but in the arms."],"answer":"1423","explanation":"(1): the fact; (4): 'them' = the neurons, with the surprising distribution; (2): consequence ('thus'); (3): 'This distributed intelligence' sums up (4)–(2) and draws the larger implication."},{"id":"pj-n17","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers.","sentences":["There it often overwhelms local markets and is eventually burned or dumped.","The average consumer now buys far more garments than a generation ago and keeps each for about half as long.","Fast fashion has made clothing cheaper than ever before.","Much of this discarded clothing ends up in landfills or is shipped to developing countries."],"answer":"3241","explanation":"(3): cause; (2): behavioural effect; (4): 'this discarded clothing' — where it goes; (1): 'There' refers to the developing countries in (4)."},{"id":"pj-n18","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers.","sentences":["During deep sleep, for example, it replays the day's experiences and consolidates them into long-term memory.","It also flushes out metabolic waste that accumulates during waking hours.","Sleep was once thought to be a period of near-total inactivity for the brain.","We now know that the sleeping brain is extraordinarily busy."],"answer":"3412","explanation":"(3): old belief; (4): current understanding; (1): first example ('for example'); (2): second function ('also')."},{"id":"pj-n19","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers.","sentences":["Borrowers did start more businesses, but average household incomes barely changed.","Small, collateral-free loans would let the poor start businesses and work their way up.","Microloans were hailed in the 1990s as a cure for global poverty.","Rigorous trials conducted two decades later painted a more modest picture."],"answer":"3241","explanation":"(3): initial enthusiasm; (2): the mechanism behind it; (4): later, more sober evidence; (1): the specifics of that evidence."},{"id":"pj-n20","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers.","sentences":["Most people assume that memory works like a video recording.","The brain stitches together fragments and fills in gaps with plausible guesses.","In fact, every act of remembering is an act of reconstruction.","This is why confident eyewitnesses can be completely, and sincerely, wrong."],"answer":"1324","explanation":"(1): common assumption; (3): correction ('In fact'); (2): mechanism; (4): real-world implication ('This is why')."},{"id":"pj-n21","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers.","sentences":["Asphalt and concrete absorb sunlight during the day and release it slowly at night.","Planting trees and painting roofs white are among the cheapest ways to reduce this 'heat island' effect.","Cities have always generated more heat than the surrounding countryside.","As a result, urban temperatures can be several degrees higher than those of nearby rural areas."],"answer":"3142","explanation":"(3): phenomenon; (1): cause; (4): result ('As a result'); (2): remedy for 'this heat island effect'."},{"id":"pj-n22","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers.","sentences":["Groundwater tables fell sharply, and decades of heavy fertiliser use degraded the soil.","The Green Revolution of the 1960s dramatically increased grain yields across Asia.","Its success, however, came at an ecological cost.","High-yielding seed varieties, combined with fertiliser and irrigation, helped avert widely predicted famines."],"answer":"2431","explanation":"(2): introduces the event; (4): explains how it succeeded; (3): 'Its success, however' pivots; (1): specifies the costs."},{"id":"pj-n23","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers.","sentences":["Online platforms have meanwhile turned top players into celebrities with millions of followers.","Chess engines now routinely defeat the strongest human players.","Yet chess itself has never been more popular.","Far from making the game obsolete, engines have become training partners, helping players study positions in unprecedented depth."],"answer":"2341","explanation":"(2): fact; (3): paradox ('Yet'); (4): first explanation ('Far from making …'); (1): additional factor ('meanwhile')."},{"id":"pj-n24","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers.","sentences":["Coral reefs cover less than one per cent of the ocean floor.","This concentration of life makes reefs exceptionally valuable — and exceptionally vulnerable.","A single bleaching event, triggered by a few weeks of unusually warm water, can wipe out a reef that took centuries to grow.","Yet they support roughly a quarter of all known marine species."],"answer":"1423","explanation":"(1) and (4) form a contrast pair; (2) 'This concentration of life' draws the implication; (3) illustrates the vulnerability."},{"id":"pj-n25","topic":"pj","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers.","sentences":["Removing walls, the theory went, would lead to more spontaneous conversations between colleagues.","Workers, it seems, retreated into headphones and email to recover the privacy the walls had once provided.","Open-plan offices were designed to encourage collaboration.","A Harvard study tracking employees who moved into open-plan spaces found that face-to-face interaction actually fell by about seventy per cent."],"answer":"3142","explanation":"(3): purpose; (1): underlying theory; (4): contradicting evidence; (2): explanation ('it seems')."},{"id":"pj-n26","topic":"pj","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers.","sentences":["But tools are never simply means to ends we have already chosen; they quietly reshape what we take our ends to be.","The bathroom scale, for instance, did not just measure an existing concern with weight — it helped manufacture one.","Seen this way, the question is not only what we do with our technologies, but what they make of us.","To call a technology 'neutral' is to imagine that it merely awaits our choices."],"answer":"4123","explanation":"(4) states the view under attack. (1) rebuts it ('But'). (2) exemplifies the rebuttal ('for instance'). (3) generalises ('Seen this way'). A tempting wrong opener is (1), but its 'But' needs a prior claim."},{"id":"pj-n27","topic":"pj","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers.","sentences":["The reader of a translation, then, is never reading only the author, but also the translator's sustained argument about what the author meant.","The translator must decide which of a word's many shades to preserve and which to sacrifice.","Every translation is, in a sense, an interpretation.","These decisions accumulate, so that two faithful translations of the same poem can read like different poems."],"answer":"3241","explanation":"(3): thesis; (2): why ('must decide'); (4): 'These decisions' accumulate; (1): conclusion ('then'). The pronoun/demonstrative chain ((2)→(4)) and the concluding 'then' fix the order."},{"id":"pj-n28","topic":"pj","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers.","sentences":["It now seems more accurate to say that Newton straddled two worlds, and that his physics grew from the same soil as his occult pursuits.","Historians of science once told a tidy story in which reason steadily triumphed over superstition.","The discovery of his voluminous alchemical and theological manuscripts complicated the picture considerably.","Newton, in that story, was the first great modern."],"answer":"2431","explanation":"(2) presents the old narrative; (4) places Newton within 'that story'; (3) introduces the complicating evidence ('his' = Newton's); (1) gives the revised view. (4) must directly follow (2) because 'that story' needs its antecedent."},{"id":"pj-n29","topic":"pj","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers.","sentences":["Whether such artificial scarcity serves the public good, or merely preserves old business models, is one of the defining policy questions of our time.","Yet some of the most valuable goods of the digital age — software, recorded music, encyclopaedic knowledge — can be copied endlessly at almost no cost.","Scarcity, economists insist, is the fundamental condition of economic life.","Markets built on scarcity have responded by manufacturing it artificially, through patents, paywalls and digital locks."],"answer":"3241","explanation":"(3): the premise; (2): the challenge ('Yet'); (4): the market's response; (1): the open question about 'such artificial scarcity' — which must follow (4)."},{"id":"pj-n30","topic":"pj","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers.","sentences":["The museum's quiet transformation of tool into artwork is so complete that we rarely notice it has happened at all.","For the community that made it, however, it was an instrument — something worn, danced and perhaps deliberately destroyed after use.","The modern museum presents objects as if they had always been meant for display.","A ritual mask hung under glass appears to us as sculpture, valued for its form."],"answer":"3421","explanation":"(3): general claim; (4): example ('A ritual mask'); (2): the contrast ('however') with original use — 'it' = the mask; (1): concluding reflection on 'the museum's transformation'."},{"id":"pj-n31","topic":"pj","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers.","sentences":["Physicists face no such difficulty — electrons do not adjust their orbits after reading the latest journal.","Social scientists have long struggled with a peculiar problem: their subjects can read.","A theory about how people behave, once published, can change how people behave.","Economic forecasts may thus become self-fulfilling, or self-defeating, simply by being believed."],"answer":"2341","explanation":"(2): puzzle; (3): explanation; (4): consequence ('thus'); (1): contrast with natural science ('no such difficulty' refers back to the problem). (1) could seem an opener but 'no such difficulty' needs an antecedent."},{"id":"pj-n32","topic":"pj","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers.","sentences":["This shift from space to time is revealing: modern life uproots us less from places than from the eras we grew up in.","Nostalgia was first diagnosed in the seventeenth century as a disease afflicting Swiss mercenaries far from home.","Over time, the word drifted from medicine to sentiment, coming to mean a bittersweet longing for the past rather than for a place.","Its symptoms were said to include fever, fainting and even death."],"answer":"2431","explanation":"Historical origin ((2)), symptoms ((4) — 'Its' = the disease), semantic drift ((3)), interpretation ((1), 'This shift from space to time' = (3)'s change)."},{"id":"pj-n33","topic":"pj","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers.","sentences":["The danger of statistical averages is that they describe no one in particular.","The solution — adjustable seats and pedals — now seems obvious, but it required abandoning the ideal of the average itself.","When the US Air Force measured thousands of pilots in 1950 to design a cockpit for the 'average' pilot, it found that not one pilot was average on all ten key dimensions.","The cockpit built for everyone, in other words, fitted no one."],"answer":"1342","explanation":"(1): principle; (3): illustration; (4): restatement ('in other words'); (2): resolution. (2)'s 'The solution' requires the problem in (4)."},{"id":"pj-n34","topic":"pj","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers.","sentences":["It is tempting to see the decline of handwritten letters as a simple loss.","But letter-writing was also, for most of its history, an elite practice, requiring literacy, leisure and costly postage.","Letters, after all, preserved a slowness and intimacy that email seems to lack.","The democratisation of written communication may therefore have done more for intimacy than its critics allow, simply by extending it to millions who never had it."],"answer":"1324","explanation":"(1): the tempting view; (3): supports it ('after all'); (2): counter ('But'); (4): the author's conclusion ('therefore'). The structure is concession–rebuttal."},{"id":"pj-n35","topic":"pj","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers.","sentences":["Much of what we call expertise, it turns out, is the second kind of knowledge masquerading as the first.","Philosophers distinguish between knowing that and knowing how.","I can know that a bicycle stays upright through a combination of momentum and steering without being able to ride one.","Conversely, a child may ride perfectly well while having no idea why she does not fall."],"answer":"2341","explanation":"(2): distinction; (3): example of knowing-that without knowing-how; (4): 'Conversely' — the reverse case; (1): conclusion using 'the second kind' and 'the first', which presuppose (2)."},{"id":"pj-n36","topic":"pj","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers.","sentences":["Politicians, being rational, respond by tailoring their promises to older voters.","Young people, seeing little in politics that addresses their concerns, become even less inclined to vote.","In most democracies, voter turnout among the young is markedly lower than among the old.","The result is a self-reinforcing cycle that is easy to describe and remarkably hard to break."],"answer":"3124","explanation":"A causal chain: fact ((3)) → politicians' response ((1)) → effect on the young ((2)) → summary ('The result … cycle', (4))."},{"id":"pj-n37","topic":"pj","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers.","sentences":["The ancient Greeks had no word that maps neatly onto our 'art'.","The idea that certain crafts are 'fine arts', set apart from mere usefulness, is an eighteenth-century European invention.","Their term techne covered shoemaking and shipbuilding as readily as sculpture and poetry.","To read that later distinction back into the ancient world is to misunderstand both."],"answer":"1324","explanation":"(1): claim; (3): evidence about 'Their term'; (2): contrast with the later idea; (4): 'that later distinction' refers to (2)."},{"id":"pj-n38","topic":"pj","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers.","sentences":["In fields where feedback is slow or ambiguous, such as long-term political forecasting, experts can remain highly confident for entire careers while performing barely better than chance.","In fields with rapid, clear feedback, such as weather forecasting, experts become well calibrated over time.","A curious feature of expert judgement is that confidence and accuracy are only loosely related.","What distinguishes reliable expertise, then, is less the expert than the environment in which the expertise was learned."],"answer":"3214","explanation":"(3): puzzle; (2) and (1): contrasting cases (fast vs slow feedback); (4): conclusion ('then'). (2) must precede (1), which sets the less-flattering case up for the conclusion."},{"id":"pj-n39","topic":"pj","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers.","sentences":["Every map of a city tells a story about who the city is for.","Tourist maps foreground monuments and shopping streets, while omitting the neighbourhoods where most residents actually live.","To read a map critically, therefore, is to ask not only what it shows, but whose city it imagines.","Transit maps are no more neutral: they distort geography to emphasise connections, quietly shrinking the outer suburbs."],"answer":"1243","explanation":"Thesis ((1)), first example ((2)), second example ((4)) — whose 'no more neutral' presupposes a previous non-neutral example — and conclusion ((3), 'therefore')."},{"id":"pj-n40","topic":"pj","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers.","sentences":["Yet the steam engine mattered less in its first decades than a quieter transformation: the reorganisation of work around the clock.","Workers who had once laboured by the task and the season now had to learn to labour by the hour — a discipline we have long since forgotten was ever learned.","The Industrial Revolution is usually told as a story of machines.","Factory owners imposed strict hours, fines for lateness and bells to mark shifts."],"answer":"3142","explanation":"(3): conventional view; (1): author's correction ('Yet'); (4): details of the 'transformation'; (2): its human consequence."},{"id":"pj-n41","topic":"pj","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers.","sentences":["Yet we judge the second far more harshly, as though an accident of circumstance had altered the moral quality of the act.","A drunk driver who reaches home safely and one who kills a pedestrian made the same reckless choice.","Moral philosophers sometimes speak of 'moral luck'.","Whether this intuition reflects a deep truth about responsibility or a simple failure of consistency remains fiercely contested."],"answer":"3214","explanation":"Concept ((3)), illustration ((2)), the puzzle ('Yet we judge', (1)), the open debate about 'this intuition' ((4))."},{"id":"pj-n42","topic":"pj","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers.","sentences":["Domestic interiors were rearranged to provide individual rooms, and reading itself became a silent, solitary act.","The rise of the novel in eighteenth-century England coincided with a new ideal of private life.","The novel, with its intimate access to characters' inner thoughts, was perfectly suited to this new reader.","It is no exaggeration to say that the genre both reflected and helped create the modern sense of an inner self."],"answer":"2134","explanation":"(2): correlation; (1): details of the private-life ideal; (3): why the novel fit 'this new reader'; (4): concluding claim about 'the genre'."},{"id":"pj-n43","topic":"pj","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers.","sentences":["Biodiversity loss is often framed as a problem of extinction.","Conservationists increasingly speak, therefore, of 'functional extinction', a quieter crisis that precedes the final one.","But a species need not vanish entirely to cease mattering ecologically.","When a population falls below a certain threshold, it can no longer perform its role — dispersing seeds, controlling prey, pollinating crops — even though some individuals survive."],"answer":"1342","explanation":"Framing ((1)), objection ('But', (3)), mechanism ((4)), new concept ((2), 'therefore')."},{"id":"pj-n44","topic":"pj","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers.","sentences":["Defenders reply that biases are identified against objective standards like probability theory, not against anyone's intuitions — though critics doubt the standards are always so objective.","Behavioural economists have catalogued dozens of cognitive biases.","The catalogue itself, however, raises a troubling question.","If human judgement is riddled with systematic errors, what entitles the economists to trust their own judgement about which behaviours count as errors?"],"answer":"2341","explanation":"(2) introduces the catalogue; (3) 'The catalogue itself' raises 'a troubling question'; (4) states it; (1) gives the reply and counter-reply."},{"id":"pj-n45","topic":"pj","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers.","sentences":["The deeper loss, linguists argue, lies in its grammar.","A grammar like this encodes a whole theory of knowledge, one that no dictionary of individual words can preserve.","Some languages, for example, require speakers to mark whether they witnessed an event directly or learned of it second-hand.","When a language dies, it is common to lament the loss of its words."],"answer":"4132","explanation":"(4): common view; (1): author's deeper claim; (3): example of a grammatical feature; (2): 'A grammar like this' — the implication."},{"id":"pj-n46","topic":"pj","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers.","sentences":["Shame and guilt are often treated as synonyms.","Psychologists, however, draw a sharp line between them.","Shame focuses on the self — 'I am bad' — and tends to provoke hiding, denial or aggression instead.","Guilt focuses on an action — 'I did a bad thing' — and tends to motivate repair."],"answer":"1243","explanation":"Common view ((1)), the psychologists' distinction ((2), 'between them'), then the two terms defined in turn ((4), (3)). (3) closes the paragraph because its 'instead' contrasts directly with (4)'s 'tends to motivate repair'."},{"id":"pj-n47","topic":"pj","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers.","sentences":["The phrase, coined by Herbert Spencer rather than Darwin, was quickly enlisted to justify laissez-faire capitalism and colonial conquest.","A species of parasite or a cooperative colony of ants can be supremely 'fit' — a fact that rather undercuts the social Darwinists' celebration of strength.","Yet in evolutionary biology, 'fitness' means nothing more than reproductive success in a particular environment.","Few scientific ideas have been as misused as 'survival of the fittest'."],"answer":"4132","explanation":"(4): claim; (1): history of the misuse ('The phrase'); (3): the actual meaning ('Yet'); (2): illustration that undermines the misuse."},{"id":"pj-n48","topic":"pj","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers.","sentences":["Each generation must therefore decide what these phrases demand in circumstances their drafters could not have foreseen.","Seen this way, a constitution is less a final answer than a framework for a continuing argument.","In practice, its most important clauses are written in deliberately open language — 'due process', 'equal protection', 'reasonable restrictions'.","A constitution is often imagined as a fixed text that settles disputes once and for all."],"answer":"4312","explanation":"Common view ((4)), correction ('In practice', (3)), consequence ('therefore', (1)), reconceptualisation ('Seen this way', (2))."},{"id":"pj-n49","topic":"pj","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers.","sentences":["Social media has taken this logic a step further.","Advertising once sold products by describing them.","Users now advertise themselves, curating their lives into a brand while the platforms sell the resulting attention to the highest bidder.","Over the twentieth century, it learned instead to sell identities, attaching soap and cigarettes to visions of the kind of person one might become."],"answer":"2413","explanation":"Historical progression: (2) → (4) ('instead … identities') → (1) ('this logic a step further') → (3) (how)."},{"id":"pj-n50","topic":"pj","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Arrange the sentences into a logical paragraph. Type the correct order of the sentence numbers.","sentences":["Economists have long puzzled over why people tip in restaurants they will never visit again.","One explanation is that tipping is enforced not by future benefit but by present emotion — the discomfort of being seen as ungenerous.","Another holds that social norms, once internalised, operate even when no one is watching.","A purely self-interested diner, after all, gains nothing from rewarding service already received.","Both accounts suggest that the textbook model of the rational consumer leaves out much of what actually drives behaviour."],"answer":"14235","explanation":"(1): the puzzle; (4): why it is puzzling ('after all'); (2): 'One explanation'; (3): 'Another'; (5): 'Both accounts' — must come last."},{"id":"ooo-n1","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit.","sentences":["Many cities are experimenting with congestion pricing, charging drivers a fee to enter busy central zones at peak hours.","Critics argue that the fee falls hardest on lower-income drivers who have few alternatives.","London's black taxis are famous for their drivers' detailed knowledge of the city's streets.","Revenue from the charge was reinvested in public transport, making alternatives to driving more attractive.","London introduced such a scheme in 2003, and traffic in the charging zone fell by around a fifth."],"answer":"3","explanation":"The paragraph is about congestion pricing — its mechanism, effects and criticism. Sentence (3) is about London taxis, sharing only the city."},{"id":"ooo-n2","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit.","sentences":["This resilience comes from their ability to enter a dehydrated state in which their metabolism almost stops.","They have even survived exposure to the vacuum of space.","Tardigrades are microscopic animals that can survive extreme conditions.","They can withstand temperatures close to absolute zero and well above boiling.","Microscopes were first developed in the Netherlands in the late sixteenth century."],"answer":"5","explanation":"The paragraph describes tardigrades' resilience and its cause. (5) is about the history of microscopes, linked only by 'microscopic'."},{"id":"ooo-n3","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit.","sentences":["Sceptics, however, question whether the model can work in sectors such as healthcare that require continuous staffing.","The four-day work week is gaining support among employers in several countries.","Employees report lower stress and better work-life balance.","The five-day week became standard in many countries in the early twentieth century, partly due to the influence of Henry Ford.","Trials suggest that productivity often holds steady even with fewer hours."],"answer":"4","explanation":"The paragraph discusses the current four-day-week movement — support, results and scepticism. (4) is a historical aside about the five-day week, shifting the time frame."},{"id":"ooo-n4","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit.","sentences":["Speakers also use it strategically, to signal identity or to express ideas that one language captures better.","Learning a second language is easier in childhood than in adulthood.","Linguists call this practice code-switching.","Far from indicating confusion, it often follows systematic grammatical rules.","Bilingual speakers frequently switch between languages within a single conversation."],"answer":"2","explanation":"The paragraph is specifically about code-switching. (2) is a general claim about language acquisition, related only to bilingualism broadly."},{"id":"ooo-n5","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit.","sentences":["Mangrove forests grow along tropical coastlines where land meets sea.","Shrimp is one of the most popular seafoods in the world, especially in restaurants.","Yet these forests are being cleared at an alarming rate for shrimp farms and coastal development.","As a result, mangroves protect coastal communities from storm surges and erosion.","Their dense roots trap sediment and break the force of waves."],"answer":"2","explanation":"The paragraph concerns mangroves' protective role and their destruction. (2) shifts to shrimp's popularity as food, picking up only the word 'shrimp'."},{"id":"ooo-n6","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit.","sentences":["The Moon landing in 1969 remains one of the most watched television events in history.","Voyager 1 crossed into interstellar space in 2012, the first human-made object to do so.","The Voyager probes were launched in 1977 to study the outer planets.","Their aging power sources are now steadily weakening, and contact is expected to end within a few years.","Having completed their planetary missions, both spacecraft continued travelling outward."],"answer":"1","explanation":"The paragraph tracks the Voyager probes' journey. (1) concerns the Moon landing, sharing only the broad topic of space exploration."},{"id":"ooo-n7","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit.","sentences":["Commercial landlords in city centres are feeling the effects, with vacancy rates at record highs.","Ergonomic chairs can reduce back pain for people who sit for long periods.","Some have moved to 'hot-desking', where employees no longer have assigned desks.","Many firms have reduced their leases, keeping smaller offices for meetings and collaboration.","Remote work has changed how companies think about office space."],"answer":"2","explanation":"The paragraph is about how remote work reshapes office real estate. (2) is about ergonomic furniture — related to work, but not to the paragraph's angle."},{"id":"ooo-n8","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit.","sentences":["The bacteria produce lactic acid, which gives the bread its characteristic tang.","Sourdough bread is leavened by a culture of wild yeast and bacteria rather than commercial yeast.","The long fermentation also breaks down some of the gluten and other compounds in the flour.","Bread prices have risen sharply in many countries because of rising wheat costs.","Some people who struggle to digest ordinary bread therefore find sourdough easier to tolerate."],"answer":"4","explanation":"The paragraph explains how sourdough's fermentation works and its effects. (4) turns to bread prices — same broad subject, different angle."},{"id":"ooo-n9","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit.","sentences":["Elephants grieve for their dead.","Such behaviour suggests that elephants may have a concept of death.","African elephants are larger than Asian elephants and have bigger ears.","Herds have been observed standing silently over the bodies of deceased companions for hours.","They often return to the bones of relatives years later, touching them gently with their trunks."],"answer":"3","explanation":"The paragraph concerns elephant grief behaviour. (3) is a physical comparison between species — true but irrelevant."},{"id":"ooo-n10","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit.","sentences":["Their long-term effects on human health are not yet well understood.","Scientists have found them in oceans, soil, drinking water and even human blood.","They form when larger plastic items break down, and are also released from synthetic clothing during washing.","Microplastics are fragments of plastic smaller than five millimetres.","Plastic was first mass-produced in the early twentieth century and quickly replaced many traditional materials."],"answer":"5","explanation":"The paragraph discusses microplastics — definition, sources, spread, health uncertainty. (5) is about plastic's history, outside the specific focus."},{"id":"ooo-n11","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit.","sentences":["Ancient libraries, such as the one at Alexandria, collected scrolls from across the known world.","They also position the library as a hub for community sharing rather than a mere repository of texts.","Public libraries in many countries have begun lending items other than books.","These 'libraries of things' aim to reduce waste and make costly items accessible to everyone.","Patrons can now borrow tools, musical instruments, cameras and even seeds."],"answer":"1","explanation":"The paragraph is about modern libraries lending non-book items. (1) shifts to ancient libraries — topically adjacent but off the angle."},{"id":"ooo-n12","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit.","sentences":["Its supporters argue that it reduces energy consumption and encourages outdoor activity.","Yet studies have found that the energy savings are negligible or nonexistent.","Clocks with pendulums were far more accurate than earlier mechanical clocks.","Daylight saving time was introduced to make better use of evening daylight.","Researchers have also linked the spring clock change to a short-term rise in heart attacks and traffic accidents."],"answer":"3","explanation":"The paragraph debates daylight saving time. (3) is about the history of clock accuracy, linked only by 'clocks'."},{"id":"ooo-n13","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit.","sentences":["Some even shift their singing to quieter times of day, such as before dawn.","Many species of birds sing more loudly in cities than in the countryside.","Urban noise from traffic and construction can drown out their songs.","Birdwatching has become an increasingly popular hobby in recent years.","To be heard by mates and rivals, birds raise their volume and pitch."],"answer":"4","explanation":"The paragraph is about birds adapting their song to urban noise. (4) is about a human hobby."},{"id":"ooo-n14","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit.","sentences":["The practice reflects a philosophy that values imperfection and impermanence.","Today, kintsugi is often invoked as a metaphor for resilience and healing.","Kintsugi is the Japanese art of repairing broken pottery with lacquer mixed with gold.","Japan is one of the world's largest exporters of cars and electronics.","Rather than hiding the cracks, it highlights them as part of the object's history."],"answer":"4","explanation":"The paragraph explains kintsugi and its philosophy. (4) is about Japan's exports — sharing only the country."},{"id":"ooo-n15","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit.","sentences":["Desert regions such as the Sahara receive less than 250 millimetres of rain per year.","Residents may rely on convenience stores stocked mainly with processed foods.","Food deserts are areas where residents have little access to affordable, nutritious food.","They are often found in low-income urban neighbourhoods and remote rural regions.","Researchers have linked food deserts to higher rates of obesity and diabetes."],"answer":"1","explanation":"The paragraph defines food deserts and their effects. (1) is about literal deserts — a classic word-association trap."},{"id":"ooo-n16","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit.","sentences":["This is why even identical twins have different fingerprints.","Some smartphones use facial recognition instead of fingerprint sensors.","Fingerprints form in the womb, around the tenth week of pregnancy.","The uniqueness of fingerprints made them a powerful tool for criminal identification from the late nineteenth century onward.","Their patterns are shaped by genes but also by random factors such as pressure in the womb."],"answer":"2","explanation":"The paragraph traces how fingerprints form and why they are unique. (2) is about smartphone security features — a modern tangent."},{"id":"ooo-n17","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit.","sentences":["The Panama Canal shortened the sea route between the Atlantic and Pacific oceans by thousands of kilometres.","Panama hats actually originated in Ecuador, not Panama.","Thousands of workers died during construction, mainly from malaria and yellow fever.","Today, droughts that lower the water level in the canal's lakes are restricting the number of ships that can pass.","Its construction, completed in 1914, was one of the largest engineering projects ever undertaken."],"answer":"2","explanation":"The paragraph concerns the Panama Canal's history and current challenges. (2) is a trivia fact about hats, sharing only the name."},{"id":"ooo-n18","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit.","sentences":["People who experience it attribute their achievements to luck rather than ability.","Impostor syndrome describes the persistent feeling of being a fraud despite evident success.","Many famous impostors throughout history successfully posed as doctors without any training.","It is surprisingly common among high achievers, including doctors, academics and executives.","Psychologists suggest that naming the feeling and discussing it openly can reduce its power."],"answer":"3","explanation":"The paragraph is about the psychological syndrome. (3) is about actual impostors — a word-association trap."},{"id":"ooo-n19","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit.","sentences":["Cities generate enormous amounts of food waste every day.","Some have begun collecting it separately to turn it into compost or biogas.","Many restaurants now offer smaller portions to appeal to health-conscious customers.","Biogas, meanwhile, can power buses or feed into the local energy grid.","The compost can be returned to farms, closing the loop between city and countryside."],"answer":"3","explanation":"The paragraph concerns processing urban food waste. (3) is about restaurant portion sizes — food-related but off the angle of waste processing."},{"id":"ooo-n20","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit.","sentences":["'Green' hydrogen made with renewable electricity remains expensive and scarce.","Hydrogen is the lightest and most abundant element in the universe.","However, most hydrogen today is produced from natural gas, releasing carbon dioxide in the process.","When burned or used in fuel cells, it produces only water.","Hydrogen is often promoted as a clean fuel of the future."],"answer":"2","explanation":"The paragraph discusses hydrogen's promise and the problem of how it is produced. (2) is a true but irrelevant scientific fact."},{"id":"ooo-n21","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit.","sentences":["Critics complain that scooters clutter pavements and cause injuries to riders and pedestrians.","Several cities have responded by capping fleet sizes or banning them altogether.","Supporters argue that they reduce short car journeys and congestion.","Bicycles were first invented in the early nineteenth century.","Shared e-scooters have spread rapidly through cities around the world."],"answer":"4","explanation":"The paragraph debates e-scooters. (4) is a historical fact about bicycles — adjacent transport topic, wrong angle."},{"id":"ooo-n22","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit.","sentences":["Research suggests, however, that apps alone rarely produce conversational ability.","Learners who combine them with real conversation practice make much faster progress.","English is the most widely studied foreign language in the world.","Their gamified design, with streaks and rewards, keeps users returning.","Language apps promise fluency through a few minutes of practice each day."],"answer":"3","explanation":"The paragraph evaluates language apps. (3) is a general statistic about English, unrelated to apps' effectiveness."},{"id":"ooo-n23","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit.","sentences":["Ultraviolet light is also used to sterilise medical equipment.","The Montreal Protocol of 1987 phased out these chemicals worldwide.","The ozone layer protects life on Earth from harmful ultraviolet radiation.","The ozone layer is now slowly recovering and is expected to heal by the middle of this century.","In the 1980s, scientists discovered that chemicals called CFCs were thinning it dramatically."],"answer":"1","explanation":"The paragraph traces the ozone problem and its solution. (1) picks up 'ultraviolet' but shifts to a medical application."},{"id":"ooo-n24","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit.","sentences":["Many people believe that cracking their knuckles causes arthritis.","The popping sound comes from gas bubbles collapsing in the fluid that lubricates the joints.","One doctor even cracked the knuckles of only one hand for sixty years and found no arthritis in either.","Arthritis affects millions of people worldwide and is a leading cause of disability.","Studies comparing habitual knuckle-crackers with non-crackers have found no difference in arthritis rates."],"answer":"4","explanation":"The paragraph debunks the knuckle-cracking myth. (4) is a general statement about arthritis prevalence — off the specific angle."},{"id":"ooo-n25","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"medium","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit.","sentences":["The Great Wall was built over many centuries by successive dynasties.","Though very long, the wall is narrow and built from materials similar in colour to the surrounding landscape.","In reality, astronauts report that it is extremely difficult to see from low Earth orbit.","The Great Wall of China is often said to be visible from space with the naked eye.","Highways and airports, by contrast, are far easier to spot."],"answer":"1","explanation":"The paragraph debunks the visibility myth. (1) is a historical fact about construction — true but off the angle."},{"id":"ooo-n26","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit.","sentences":["The question becomes pressing when applied to persons, since our own cells are continually replaced.","Philosophers have long asked whether a ship whose every plank has been replaced over time remains the same ship.","The puzzle, known as the Ship of Theseus, has no obvious answer.","Ancient Greek ships were typically built from pine and fir, materials prized for their buoyancy.","If the ship's identity lies in its material, it ceased to exist somewhere along the way; if it lies in its form or history, it persists."],"answer":"4","explanation":"The paragraph is about the identity puzzle and its application to persons. (4) gives a historical detail about ship materials — it shares the vocabulary ('ship', 'material') but not the philosophical angle."},{"id":"ooo-n27","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit.","sentences":["Failing to consider such costs leads individuals and firms to systematically misjudge the true price of their decisions.","University tuition fees have risen faster than inflation in many countries over the past two decades.","A student attending university, for example, pays not only tuition but also the wages she could have earned by working instead.","The concept reveals hidden costs that accounting statements ignore.","Economists measure the 'opportunity cost' of a choice as the value of the best alternative forgone."],"answer":"2","explanation":"The paragraph explains opportunity cost. (2) picks up the university example but shifts to the trend in tuition fees — a direct cost, not an opportunity cost — breaking the conceptual thread."},{"id":"ooo-n28","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit.","sentences":["Ostrom was the first woman to receive the Nobel Prize in economics.","The 'tragedy of the commons' describes how shared resources are overused when each individual acts in self-interest.","Every herder gains by adding another animal to a common pasture, while the cost of overgrazing is spread among all.","The political economist Elinor Ostrom showed, however, that many communities have sustainably managed commons through their own locally devised rules.","For decades, the remedy was assumed to be either privatisation or state control."],"answer":"1","explanation":"The paragraph is about the commons problem and Ostrom's alternative remedy. (1) is a biographical fact about Ostrom — true and adjacent, but it doesn't advance the argument."},{"id":"ooo-n29","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit.","sentences":["In the best examples, the reader's growing suspicion mirrors the narrator's own gradual, reluctant self-discovery.","Unreliable narrators force readers to become detectives.","Because the narrator's account cannot be taken at face value, readers must look for inconsistencies and gaps.","The pleasure of such novels lies partly in reconstructing what really happened behind the narrator's distortions.","Detective fiction became enormously popular in the late nineteenth century with the Sherlock Holmes stories."],"answer":"5","explanation":"The paragraph analyses the reader's role with unreliable narrators. (5) takes the 'detective' metaphor literally and shifts to the history of a genre."},{"id":"ooo-n30","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit.","sentences":["Even scientists, trained to test their hypotheses, are not immune.","Confirmation bias is the tendency to seek out and favour information that supports what we already believe.","It operates not only in what evidence we look for but in how we interpret ambiguous evidence.","Some scientists argue that intuition plays a valuable role in forming hypotheses.","Practices such as pre-registering studies and blind analysis were developed partly to guard against it."],"answer":"4","explanation":"The paragraph explains confirmation bias and safeguards against it. (4) introduces a separate claim about intuition's value in science, which doesn't address the bias."},{"id":"ooo-n31","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit.","sentences":["Advocates argue that these objects were acquired through conquest or coercion and belong to the communities that made them.","Critics reply that this argument presumes the originating communities cannot care for their own heritage.","Museums increasingly face demands to return artefacts taken during colonial rule.","Museum attendance has risen steadily as cities invest in cultural tourism.","Some museums counter that they preserve the objects and make them accessible to a global public."],"answer":"4","explanation":"The paragraph presents the repatriation debate. (4) shifts to attendance figures — museum-related but outside the argument."},{"id":"ooo-n32","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit.","sentences":["Chip designers have responded by stacking components vertically and designing specialised processors for particular tasks.","As transistors approach the size of individual atoms, however, further shrinking becomes physically difficult and enormously expensive.","The industry's progress now depends less on raw miniaturisation than on architectural ingenuity.","Moore's Law, the observation that the number of transistors on a chip doubles roughly every two years, held for decades.","Gordon Moore co-founded Intel, which became one of the world's largest chipmakers."],"answer":"5","explanation":"The paragraph traces Moore's Law and the industry's shift away from miniaturisation. (5) is a biographical/corporate fact that doesn't advance this argument."},{"id":"ooo-n33","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit.","sentences":["Over time, the window can shift, so that once-radical ideas become mainstream.","Activists sometimes deliberately advocate extreme positions to make moderate versions of their goals appear reasonable.","Ideas outside the window are dismissed as radical or unthinkable.","The Overton window describes the range of policies considered politically acceptable at a given time.","Opinion polls are often inaccurate because respondents give socially desirable answers."],"answer":"5","explanation":"The paragraph explains the Overton window and how it shifts. (5) is about polling inaccuracy — politically adjacent but off the concept."},{"id":"ooo-n34","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit.","sentences":["A weathered wooden bowl or an asymmetrical tea cup embodies its values better than a flawless object.","Wabi-sabi, a Japanese aesthetic, finds beauty in imperfection, impermanence and incompleteness.","The aesthetic draws on Buddhist teachings about the transience of all things.","It stands in sharp contrast to Western classical ideals that prized symmetry, permanence and perfection.","Japanese tea ceremonies can last up to four hours and follow strict rules of etiquette."],"answer":"5","explanation":"The paragraph defines wabi-sabi and contrasts it with Western ideals. (5) shifts to tea-ceremony etiquette — culturally adjacent, but about ritual rules rather than the aesthetic, and its emphasis on 'strict rules' even pulls against the theme."},{"id":"ooo-n35","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit.","sentences":["Streetlights were first powered by gas in the early nineteenth century.","Policy can be distorted in the same way, as governments target metrics that are convenient rather than meaningful.","The streetlight effect describes the tendency to search for answers where it is easiest to look.","Researchers fall prey to it when they study what is easily measured rather than what matters.","The name comes from a joke about a man searching for lost keys under a streetlight, not because he lost them there but because the light is better."],"answer":"1","explanation":"The paragraph explains a cognitive bias via a metaphor. (1) takes 'streetlight' literally and shifts to history."},{"id":"ooo-n36","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit.","sentences":["Supporters argue that it would reduce poverty and provide security as automation disrupts work.","Critics worry about its cost and about whether it would reduce the incentive to work.","Means-tested welfare programmes often require applicants to prove they are actively seeking work.","Pilot programmes have so far found little evidence of reduced work effort, though none has tested a fully universal scheme.","Universal basic income proposes giving every citizen a regular, unconditional cash payment."],"answer":"3","explanation":"The paragraph debates UBI and its evidence. (3) describes a feature of a different system — it can seem relevant as a contrast, but nothing in the paragraph sets up or uses that contrast, so it breaks the thread."},{"id":"ooo-n37","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit.","sentences":["Its strong form — that language determines thought — has been largely abandoned.","Speakers of languages that mark grammatical gender, for instance, may describe objects in ways that echo the gender assigned to them.","Linguistic relativity holds that the language we speak shapes how we think.","A weaker form, that language influences habits of attention and memory, has found some experimental support.","Mandarin Chinese has more native speakers than any other language."],"answer":"5","explanation":"The paragraph tracks the status of linguistic relativity. (5) is a demographic fact about a language — no bearing on the hypothesis."},{"id":"ooo-n38","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit.","sentences":["Armour, he argued, should go where returning planes showed no damage, since planes hit there had not come back.","During World War II, engineers studied returning bombers to decide where to add armour.","The statistician Abraham Wald pointed out that the holes they saw marked where planes could be hit and still return.","Aircraft manufacturing expanded dramatically during the war, transforming the economies of several countries.","Survivorship bias occurs when we draw conclusions from cases that passed some selection process while ignoring those that did not."],"answer":"4","explanation":"The paragraph explains survivorship bias through the bomber example. (4) shifts to the wartime aircraft industry's economics."},{"id":"ooo-n39","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit.","sentences":["Ironically, total savings may end up no higher than before, since people with lower incomes can save less.","The 'paradox of thrift' holds that what is prudent for an individual can be harmful for an economy as a whole.","Financial advisers typically recommend saving at least ten per cent of one's income.","If all households save more at once, however, spending collapses, businesses cut jobs, and incomes fall.","If one household saves more, it becomes more secure."],"answer":"3","explanation":"The paragraph explains a macroeconomic paradox. (3) is personal-finance advice that reads as relevant because it concerns saving, but it addresses the individual level only and doesn't engage the paradox."},{"id":"ooo-n40","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit.","sentences":["Schools judged by test scores may begin teaching to the test, raising scores without improving learning.","Goodhart's law states that when a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure.","Standardised testing was first introduced in imperial China to select civil servants.","In each case, the effort to hit the number corrupts the very thing the number was meant to reveal.","Hospitals judged by waiting times may reclassify patients to meet the target."],"answer":"3","explanation":"The paragraph explains Goodhart's law with examples. (3) gives a history of standardised testing — it shares the topic of tests but not the argument."},{"id":"ooo-n41","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit.","sentences":["Charles Darwin delayed publishing his theory of natural selection for nearly twenty years.","Natural selection explains how species adapt to their environments over many generations.","He was finally spurred to publish when Alfred Russel Wallace independently arrived at the same idea.","He feared the controversy it would provoke, particularly among religious authorities.","Their findings were presented together in 1858, and Darwin's On the Origin of Species appeared the following year."],"answer":"2","explanation":"The paragraph narrates the history of Darwin's publication. (2) explains the theory's content, shifting from biography to science — a subtle change of angle."},{"id":"ooo-n42","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit.","sentences":["As a result, the least competent lack the means to see their own deficiencies.","Confidence is often rated as one of the most attractive qualities in job interviews.","The skills needed to perform well are often the same skills needed to recognise good performance.","The Dunning-Kruger effect describes how people with limited competence in a domain tend to overestimate their ability.","Experts, by contrast, sometimes underestimate their relative standing, assuming tasks easy for them are easy for others too."],"answer":"2","explanation":"The paragraph explains the effect's mechanism. (2) shifts to how confidence is perceived by others — adjacent but outside the argument."},{"id":"ooo-n43","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit.","sentences":["They also note that astrology and alchemy remained intertwined with the new science for decades.","Many historians now reject the idea of a single 'Scientific Revolution' in the seventeenth century.","The term survives mainly as a convenient label rather than a precise description of a sharp break.","They point out that its supposed founders drew heavily on medieval and Islamic scholarship.","Isaac Newton served as Master of the Royal Mint, where he pursued counterfeiters vigorously."],"answer":"5","explanation":"The paragraph questions the notion of a 'Scientific Revolution'. (5) is a biographical anecdote about Newton, unrelated to the historiographical argument."},{"id":"ooo-n44","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit.","sentences":["Insurance has existed in some form since ancient merchants pooled the risks of sea voyages.","Insured drivers may drive less carefully; banks expecting bailouts may take bigger gambles.","Insurers and regulators try to counter this with deductibles, co-payments and capital requirements.","Such measures keep some of the risk with those who take it, preserving their incentive to be careful.","Moral hazard arises when people protected from the consequences of risk behave more recklessly."],"answer":"1","explanation":"The paragraph explains moral hazard and remedies. (1) is about insurance's origins — topically connected, but historical rather than analytical."},{"id":"ooo-n45","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit.","sentences":["Some interpret it as a purging of emotions, others as a purification or clarification of them.","Greek tragedies were performed at religious festivals in honour of the god Dionysus.","The meaning of catharsis has been debated ever since.","Tragedy, Aristotle argued, arouses pity and fear in its audience and brings about their catharsis.","On the latter view, tragedy does not simply relieve feeling but educates it."],"answer":"2","explanation":"The paragraph discusses interpretations of catharsis. (2) is about the performance context of Greek tragedy — related subject, different angle."},{"id":"ooo-n46","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit.","sentences":["Genetically modified crops have increased yields in several developing countries.","It has shaped environmental law in Europe and international treaties on climate and biodiversity.","Critics argue that, applied strictly, it could block almost any innovation, since nearly every new technology carries some uncertain risk.","The precautionary principle holds that when an activity threatens serious harm, lack of full scientific certainty should not delay protective action.","Defenders reply that it is meant to apply only to serious and irreversible harms, not to every conceivable risk."],"answer":"1","explanation":"The paragraph debates the precautionary principle in general terms. (1) is a specific empirical claim about GM crops that the paragraph never introduces — it may seem like a relevant example, but nothing frames it."},{"id":"ooo-n47","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit.","sentences":["Defenders argue that these challenges have yet to produce a coherent alternative ideology with universal appeal.","The Berlin Wall fell in 1989, and Germany was reunified the following year.","The 'end of history' thesis, proposed after the Cold War, claimed that liberal democracy had triumphed as the final form of human government.","The decades since have tested the claim severely, with the rise of authoritarian capitalism and populist movements within democracies.","Its author did not mean that events would stop, but that no serious ideological rival remained."],"answer":"2","explanation":"The paragraph debates the 'end of history' thesis. (2) is a historical fact from the same era but contributes nothing to the argument."},{"id":"ooo-n48","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit.","sentences":["This raises a puzzle: our choices today determine which people will be born at all.","Some philosophers argue that we have obligations to future generations who do not yet exist.","Population growth is expected to slow and eventually reverse in most countries by the end of the century.","This 'non-identity problem' complicates attempts to ground environmental ethics in harm to specific individuals.","A policy that harms the future may also change who exists, so that no particular future person is worse off than they would otherwise have been."],"answer":"3","explanation":"The paragraph explains the non-identity problem. (3) is a demographic forecast — about future people, but not about the philosophical puzzle."},{"id":"ooo-n49","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit.","sentences":["Governments, too, continue costly programmes partly to avoid admitting that past spending was wasted.","The concept of 'sunk cost' refers to resources already spent that cannot be recovered.","Large infrastructure projects frequently exceed their original budgets by significant margins.","Rational decision-making, economists argue, should ignore sunk costs and consider only future costs and benefits.","Yet people routinely persist with failing projects because of what they have already invested."],"answer":"3","explanation":"The paragraph is about the sunk-cost fallacy — reasoning about past investments. (3) is about cost overruns, a different phenomenon (forecasting error) that merely sounds related."},{"id":"ooo-n50","topic":"ooo","difficulty":"hard","src":"new","type":"tita","stem":"Four of the five sentences form a coherent paragraph. Type the number of the sentence that does not fit.","sentences":["Homer's lengthy description of Achilles' shield in the Iliad is one of its earliest examples.","By trying to capture in words what the eye takes in at once, the ekphrastic poem dramatises the gap between the two arts.","Ekphrasis is the literary description of a work of visual art.","Poets have since used ekphrasis not merely to describe paintings and sculptures but to reflect on the limits of language itself.","Many of the greatest Renaissance paintings depict scenes from classical mythology."],"answer":"5","explanation":"The paragraph defines ekphrasis and its purpose in poetry. (5) is about the subject matter of paintings — it relates to visual art and the classics, but not to the literary device."},{"id":"rc-n1-1","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-np1","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The author's main purpose in the passage is to:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"argue that museum visitors have become less attentive than in the past."},{"k":"B","t":"criticise museums for displaying too many works of art."},{"k":"C","t":"explain why Rembrandt's paintings attract more attention than other works."},{"k":"D","t":"reinterpret short average viewing times as a rational search strategy and consider its implications for museum design."}],"answer":"D","explanation":"The passage reframes the dwell-time statistic (paras 2–3) and draws design implications (para 4). A is the view the author challenges. B is one implication, too narrow. C misreads the Rembrandt reference, which is illustrative.","src":"new"},{"id":"rc-n1-2","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-np1","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The author uses the word 'foraging' (paragraph 3) to suggest that visitors:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"avoid works of art that they consider unfamiliar."},{"k":"B","t":"sample widely and stop when they find something that rewards attention."},{"k":"C","t":"follow a predetermined route through the museum."},{"k":"D","t":"are primarily motivated by hunger and fatigue."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"The metaphor describes skimming until something 'catches them', then stopping. D literalises the metaphor; A and C are not suggested.","src":"new"},{"id":"rc-n1-3","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-np1","difficulty":"medium","stem":"According to the passage, adding more text and interactive screens to galleries may:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"make it harder for visitors to identify works worth close attention."},{"k":"B","t":"be the most effective way to engage younger visitors."},{"k":"C","t":"significantly increase visitor dwell times."},{"k":"D","t":"reduce the number of visitors to museums."}],"answer":"A","explanation":"Para 4 says such additions 'may be making the search problem harder'. C contradicts this; B and D are not mentioned.","src":"new"},{"id":"rc-n1-4","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-np1","difficulty":"medium","stem":"Which of the following, if true, would most weaken the author's interpretation of short dwell times?","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Museums with fewer works on display report higher visitor satisfaction."},{"k":"B","t":"Most visitors spend about two hours in a museum."},{"k":"C","t":"Visitors who browse quickly report finding a few works they remember vividly."},{"k":"D","t":"Dwell times are roughly equal across all works, with almost no paintings receiving extended attention from any visitor."}],"answer":"D","explanation":"The author's reading rests on unequal dwell times — glances for most works, long stops for a few. Uniformly short times would undermine the foraging interpretation. C and A support the author; B is neutral.","src":"new"},{"id":"rc-n1-5","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-np1","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The author's attitude toward the fifteen-second glance can best be described as:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"wholehearted approval."},{"k":"B","t":"indifferent neutrality."},{"k":"C","t":"qualified understanding."},{"k":"D","t":"contemptuous dismissal."}],"answer":"C","explanation":"The author explains why the glance is rational but concedes in the final paragraph that it 'is not ideal' — qualified understanding. A ignores the concession; D ignores the defence; B ignores the clear argument.","src":"new"},{"id":"rc-n2-1","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-np2","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The central concern of the passage is:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"the rising cost of maintaining urban trees."},{"k":"B","t":"the unequal distribution of urban tree cover, its historical roots, and the complications of remedying it."},{"k":"C","t":"the scientific benefits of urban trees for climate and stormwater management."},{"k":"D","t":"the history of redlining in American cities."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"The passage covers inequality, historical cause, resistance to remedies and successful approaches — B. C is only the opening context; D is one cause; A is one concern among several.","src":"new"},{"id":"rc-n2-2","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-np2","difficulty":"medium","stem":"According to the passage, the legacy of redlining persists in tree cover partly because:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"highways were built only in wealthy areas."},{"k":"B","t":"residents of redlined areas oppose all tree planting."},{"k":"C","t":"redlining is still practised in many cities."},{"k":"D","t":"trees take decades to mature, so past planting decisions shape present canopy."}],"answer":"D","explanation":"Para 2: 'The legacy persists because trees take decades to mature'. C is false per the passage; B overstates para 3; A reverses the passage.","src":"new"},{"id":"rc-n2-3","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-np2","difficulty":"medium","stem":"'Green gentrification', as described in the passage, refers to:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"resistance to tree planting due to maintenance costs."},{"k":"B","t":"the conversion of industrial land into parks."},{"k":"C","t":"the displacement of existing residents when greening raises property values and rents."},{"k":"D","t":"the planting of trees in wealthy neighbourhoods."}],"answer":"C","explanation":"Para 3 defines it as greening raising property values and displacing residents. D, A and B describe other phenomena.","src":"new"},{"id":"rc-n2-4","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-np2","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The author would most likely agree that a tree-planting programme in an under-shaded neighbourhood would be most effective if it:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"planted as many trees as possible as quickly as possible."},{"k":"B","t":"left maintenance responsibility entirely to homeowners."},{"k":"C","t":"focused only on reducing summer temperatures."},{"k":"D","t":"involved residents and included measures to prevent displacement."}],"answer":"D","explanation":"Para 4 lists resident involvement, city maintenance and anti-displacement measures. A and C ignore the complications; B contradicts the passage.","src":"new"},{"id":"rc-n2-5","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-np2","difficulty":"medium","stem":"In the final paragraph, the author suggests that shade may increasingly be viewed as:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"a source of conflict between residents and cities."},{"k":"B","t":"a public health necessity and a matter of justice."},{"k":"C","t":"less important than air conditioning."},{"k":"D","t":"an aesthetic luxury."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"The final sentence states this directly. D is the view it moves away from; A and C are not suggested.","src":"new"},{"id":"rc-n3-1","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-np3","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The passage is primarily concerned with:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"proving that all students learn in exactly the same way."},{"k":"B","t":"describing the history of teacher-training programmes."},{"k":"C","t":"arguing that the learning-styles hypothesis lacks support and diverts resources from effective practices."},{"k":"D","t":"explaining how to identify a student's learning style."}],"answer":"C","explanation":"The passage tests the hypothesis, finds it unsupported, explains its persistence and notes its opportunity cost — C. D contradicts; A overstates (the author accepts preferences exist); B is irrelevant.","src":"new"},{"id":"rc-n3-2","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-np3","difficulty":"medium","stem":"According to the passage, the best way to present material depends mainly on:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"the age of the students."},{"k":"B","t":"the learner's stated preference."},{"k":"C","t":"the teacher's own learning style."},{"k":"D","t":"the nature of the material being taught."}],"answer":"D","explanation":"Para 3: 'depends far more on the material than on the learner'.","src":"new"},{"id":"rc-n3-3","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-np3","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The author distinguishes between a 'preference' and an 'aptitude' in order to:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"argue that students' preferences should be ignored entirely."},{"k":"B","t":"suggest that aptitude cannot be measured."},{"k":"C","t":"explain why students' genuine likes may be mistaken for evidence that matching instruction improves learning."},{"k":"D","t":"show that visual learners are more common than auditory learners."}],"answer":"C","explanation":"Para 4 uses the distinction to explain why the myth persists. A overreaches; D and B are not supported.","src":"new"},{"id":"rc-n3-4","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-np3","difficulty":"medium","stem":"Which of the following findings would most strengthen the author's argument?","options":[{"k":"A","t":"A randomised study finds that matching instruction to learning style produces no improvement in test scores."},{"k":"B","t":"Students who prefer diagrams spend more time looking at them."},{"k":"C","t":"Students enjoy lessons tailored to their preferred style."},{"k":"D","t":"Most teachers believe in learning styles."}],"answer":"A","explanation":"The author's case rests on the absence of a matching benefit in properly designed studies; A adds such evidence. B and C concern preference, not learning; D concerns belief, which the author already acknowledges.","src":"new"},{"id":"rc-n3-5","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-np3","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The final sentence, 'The harm of the learning-styles myth lies less in what it does than in what it displaces,' means that:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"the myth has displaced older, worse theories of learning."},{"k":"B","t":"learning-styles instruction actively damages students' ability to learn."},{"k":"C","t":"the main cost of the myth is the opportunity cost of neglecting more effective methods."},{"k":"D","t":"teachers who believe the myth should be replaced."}],"answer":"C","explanation":"The preceding sentence explains that resources spent on learning styles are not spent on evidence-backed practices — opportunity cost. B contradicts 'less in what it does'; A and D misread 'displaces'.","src":"new"},{"id":"rc-n4-1","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-np4","difficulty":"medium","stem":"Which of the following best describes the structure of the passage?","options":[{"k":"A","t":"A historical myth is described and then refuted."},{"k":"B","t":"A problem is described, several solutions are evaluated, and one is recommended."},{"k":"C","t":"Two competing theories are compared and the author sides with one."},{"k":"D","t":"An innovation is introduced, its slow adoption and subsequent effects are traced, and its broader significance is assessed."}],"answer":"D","explanation":"The passage introduces containerisation, explains resistance and standardisation, traces scale effects and assesses impact — D.","src":"new"},{"id":"rc-n4-2","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-np4","difficulty":"medium","stem":"According to the passage, the full advantages of containerisation were realised only after:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"China opened to the world economy."},{"k":"B","t":"international standards for container sizes and fittings were agreed."},{"k":"C","t":"McLean's first voyage in 1956."},{"k":"D","t":"the largest container ships were built."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"Para 2 states this explicitly.","src":"new"},{"id":"rc-n4-3","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-np4","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The passage suggests that ports which could not accommodate very large ships:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"became global hubs."},{"k":"B","t":"were nationalised by governments."},{"k":"C","t":"benefited from reduced competition."},{"k":"D","t":"were bypassed in favour of mega-ports."}],"answer":"D","explanation":"Para 3: such ports 'found themselves bypassed'.","src":"new"},{"id":"rc-n4-4","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-np4","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The author's treatment of the debate over containerisation's effect on trade (paragraph 4) can best be described as:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"balanced, presenting a strong claim alongside a caution about isolating causes."},{"k":"B","t":"strongly supportive of the view that tariffs mattered more."},{"k":"C","t":"dismissive of studies that credit containerisation."},{"k":"D","t":"uninterested in economic evidence."}],"answer":"A","explanation":"The author presents both the strong claim and the methodological caution without resolving it — balanced. C, B and D misrepresent the tone.","src":"new"},{"id":"rc-n4-5","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-np4","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The phrase 'for better and worse' in the final sentence implies that the author:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"believes containerisation should be reversed."},{"k":"B","t":"recognises that the global supply chain has both benefits and drawbacks."},{"k":"C","t":"thinks the container's effects were negligible."},{"k":"D","t":"considers global supply chains entirely beneficial."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"The phrase signals mixed effects. D and A pick one side; C contradicts the passage.","src":"new"},{"id":"rc-n5-1","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-np5","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The passage is mainly concerned with:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"the decline of repair culture, the debate over repairability, and efforts to revive repair."},{"k":"B","t":"the history of the smartphone industry."},{"k":"C","t":"the environmental impact of farming equipment."},{"k":"D","t":"why manufacturers make thinner devices."}],"answer":"A","explanation":"A captures the full arc. B, C and D are narrow details.","src":"new"},{"id":"rc-n5-2","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-np5","difficulty":"medium","stem":"According to the passage, one reason repair declined is that:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"goods became cheap relative to the cost of labour."},{"k":"B","t":"people lost interest in owning durable goods."},{"k":"C","t":"governments banned independent repair shops."},{"k":"D","t":"products became more water-resistant."}],"answer":"A","explanation":"Para 1 states this. C is not mentioned; D is a manufacturers' justification, not a cause of decline; B is unsupported.","src":"new"},{"id":"rc-n5-3","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-np5","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The author mentions farmers primarily to:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"criticise farmers for relying on software."},{"k":"B","t":"argue that tractors are more important than smartphones."},{"k":"C","t":"show that the right-to-repair movement concerns only consumer electronics."},{"k":"D","t":"illustrate how restrictions on repair can have serious practical consequences, making farmers effective advocates."}],"answer":"D","explanation":"The example shows high stakes — a ruined crop — explaining farmers' advocacy. C contradicts; B and A are unsupported.","src":"new"},{"id":"rc-n5-4","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-np5","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The author's view of manufacturers' justifications for repair-resistant design is best described as:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"fully accepting of them."},{"k":"B","t":"indifferent to them."},{"k":"C","t":"dismissing them as entirely dishonest."},{"k":"D","t":"presenting them alongside critics' argument that they often serve commercial interests."}],"answer":"D","explanation":"Para 2 presents both justifications and the critics' counter — D. C overstates ('often mask', not always).","src":"new"},{"id":"rc-n5-5","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-np5","difficulty":"medium","stem":"The final paragraph suggests that:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"repair cafés have replaced professional repair shops."},{"k":"B","t":"right-to-repair laws have fully revived repair culture."},{"k":"C","t":"legal reforms may be insufficient because repair skills have faded and labour remains costly."},{"k":"D","t":"repair is no longer environmentally beneficial."}],"answer":"C","explanation":"The final paragraph states 'laws alone may not revive repair culture' and explains why. B contradicts; A overstates; D contradicts para 4.","src":"new"},{"id":"rc-n6-1","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-np6","difficulty":"hard","stem":"The author's central argument is that:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"sources are shaped by the interests and conditions that produced and preserved them, requiring historians to interrogate their existence as well as their content."},{"k":"B","t":"reading against the grain is the only reliable method for recovering suppressed voices."},{"k":"C","t":"archives are incomplete and should therefore be abandoned in favour of oral sources."},{"k":"D","t":"colonial administrators deliberately falsified their records."}],"answer":"A","explanation":"The final paragraph synthesises the argument: every source bears the imprint of its making, so historians must ask why it exists. C misrepresents the author's view of alternative sources (they share the problem); B ignores the stated hazards of that method; D is not claimed — the distortion is one of angle, not falsification.","src":"new"},{"id":"rc-n6-2","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-np6","difficulty":"hard","stem":"The geologist analogy in the first paragraph serves to:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"show that history is a science like geology."},{"k":"B","t":"emphasise the age of historical documents."},{"k":"C","t":"illustrate a view of archives that the author goes on to reject."},{"k":"D","t":"argue that historians should adopt geological methods."}],"answer":"C","explanation":"The analogy depicts archives as natural deposits; the author immediately counters, 'But archives are not sediment.' A, D and B take the analogy at face value.","src":"new"},{"id":"rc-n6-3","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-np6","difficulty":"hard","stem":"By 'their incompleteness is patterned' (paragraph 2), the author means that:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"archival documents follow a fixed bureaucratic format."},{"k":"B","t":"archives are missing documents at random."},{"k":"C","t":"historians impose patterns on incomplete data."},{"k":"D","t":"the gaps in archives systematically reflect what the recording institutions needed and ignored."}],"answer":"D","explanation":"The colonial example shows that what is recorded follows the state's needs — systematic, not random. B is the opposite; A and C misinterpret 'patterned'.","src":"new"},{"id":"rc-n6-4","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-np6","difficulty":"hard","stem":"Which of the following best captures the author's view of reading 'against the grain'?","options":[{"k":"A","t":"It is the same as turning to oral traditions and material culture."},{"k":"B","t":"It is a useful approach but risks letting the historian's own desires fill the archive's silences."},{"k":"C","t":"It is a flawless method that recovers lost voices."},{"k":"D","t":"It is pointless because silence cannot be interpreted."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"Para 3 credits the method ('can recover traces') and warns of its hazards ('substituting her own desires'). C ignores the warning; D ignores the credit; A conflates it with the 'more radical response' of para 4.","src":"new"},{"id":"rc-n6-5","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-np6","difficulty":"hard","stem":"The final sentence, 'The historian's task is to hear both the voice and the throat,' most nearly means that historians should:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"attend both to what a source says and to the institutional conditions through which it came to say it."},{"k":"B","t":"read documents aloud to detect their tone."},{"k":"C","t":"prefer sources produced by individuals over those produced by institutions."},{"k":"D","t":"focus on oral sources rather than written ones."}],"answer":"A","explanation":"'The archive … is spoken through' — the throat is the medium (institutions, conditions of survival). The historian must grasp both content and conduit. D and B literalise the metaphor; C is not argued.","src":"new"},{"id":"rc-n7-1","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-np7","difficulty":"hard","stem":"The primary purpose of the passage is to:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"show that hybrid systems resolve the conflict between rules and standards."},{"k":"B","t":"analyse the trade-offs between rules and discretion and argue that the choice between them reflects which errors a society prefers to tolerate."},{"k":"C","t":"argue that legal systems should rely exclusively on rules."},{"k":"D","t":"criticise judges for showing prejudice."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"The passage weighs both, rejects the hybrid as a dissolution of the dilemma, and concludes that the choice reflects error tolerance — B. C and A contradict the conclusion; D is a narrow detail.","src":"new"},{"id":"rc-n7-2","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-np7","difficulty":"hard","stem":"The example of the driver rushing a dying patient to hospital illustrates:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"the need to raise speed limits."},{"k":"B","t":"how a rule can capture conduct that falls outside its purpose."},{"k":"C","t":"the superiority of standards over rules in every case."},{"k":"D","t":"why officials cannot be trusted with discretion."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"Para 2 uses it to show that rules are generalisations whose letter can exceed their purpose. C overgeneralises; A and D are not the point.","src":"new"},{"id":"rc-n7-3","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-np7","difficulty":"hard","stem":"According to the passage, the hybrid approach of 'rules for the ordinary case, discretion for the exceptional one' is problematic because:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"the decision about which cases are exceptional is itself discretionary and reintroduces the vices of discretion."},{"k":"B","t":"it has never been tried by any legal system."},{"k":"C","t":"it is too expensive to implement."},{"k":"D","t":"citizens prefer pure rules."}],"answer":"A","explanation":"Para 4 states precisely this. B contradicts 'Many legal systems attempt something like this'; C and D are not mentioned.","src":"new"},{"id":"rc-n7-4","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-np7","difficulty":"hard","stem":"It can be inferred that the author would consider rules more appropriate than discretion in a domain where:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"officials are distrusted and the risk of arbitrary decisions is serious."},{"k":"B","t":"citizens do not need to know requirements in advance."},{"k":"C","t":"circumstances vary widely and officials are highly accountable."},{"k":"D","t":"the stakes of error are trivial."}],"answer":"A","explanation":"The final paragraph: 'Where the stakes of arbitrariness are high and the officials least trusted, rules may be preferable.' C describes where discretion may be better; D and B aren't stated conditions favouring rules.","src":"new"},{"id":"rc-n7-5","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-np7","difficulty":"hard","stem":"Which of the following statements would the author most likely reject?","options":[{"k":"A","t":"A sufficiently well-designed system can eliminate the need to choose between rules and discretion."},{"k":"B","t":"Discretion allows both mercy and prejudice."},{"k":"C","t":"The choice between rules and standards arises outside law as well as within it."},{"k":"D","t":"Rules are cheaper to apply than standards."}],"answer":"A","explanation":"The author insists 'there is … no formula that dissolves the dilemma' and 'What cannot be avoided is the choice itself.' C, B and D are all stated in the passage.","src":"new"},{"id":"rc-n8-1","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-np8","difficulty":"hard","stem":"The author's main argument is that:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"forgeries should be valued as highly as originals."},{"k":"B","t":"Benjamin was entirely wrong about the effects of mechanical reproduction."},{"k":"C","t":"aesthetic experience is purely visual."},{"k":"D","t":"the modern aura of original artworks may be a product of reproduction, grounded in historical narrative rather than visual properties."}],"answer":"D","explanation":"Paras 3–4 develop exactly this. B overstates — the author says the paradox 'may be more apparent than real', reinterpreting rather than flatly refuting Benjamin. A is not argued; C is the view the author challenges.","src":"new"},{"id":"rc-n8-2","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-np8","difficulty":"hard","stem":"The Mona Lisa is mentioned in the passage primarily to:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"show that museums display artworks poorly."},{"k":"B","t":"argue that reproductions are superior to originals."},{"k":"C","t":"demonstrate that Leonardo was the greatest Renaissance painter."},{"k":"D","t":"illustrate the apparent contradiction of Benjamin's prediction and support the author's alternative explanation."}],"answer":"D","explanation":"It appears in para 2 as evidence against Benjamin and in para 3 as the key case for reproduction manufacturing aura. C, A and B misread its function.","src":"new"},{"id":"rc-n8-3","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-np8","difficulty":"hard","stem":"The author's discussion of forgeries suggests that:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"the loss of value when a forgery is revealed shows that value depends on the story attached to an object rather than its appearance."},{"k":"B","t":"forgeries are usually detected because of visible flaws."},{"k":"C","t":"forgeries should be destroyed."},{"k":"D","t":"auction houses deliberately sell forgeries."}],"answer":"A","explanation":"Para 4: 'The forgery … has not changed; what has changed is the story.' B contradicts 'nothing about its appearance has changed'; C and D are unsupported.","src":"new"},{"id":"rc-n8-4","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-np8","difficulty":"hard","stem":"Which of the following, if true, would most directly support the author's claim that reproduction has produced the modern aura?","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Works of art that have rarely been reproduced attract few visitors, while the most reproduced works attract the most."},{"k":"B","t":"Benjamin wrote extensively about film."},{"k":"C","t":"Photographs of artworks have improved dramatically in quality."},{"k":"D","t":"Some visitors to the Louvre prefer the museum's other paintings to the Mona Lisa."}],"answer":"A","explanation":"If fame and pilgrimage track the extent of reproduction, that supports reproduction generating aura. D, C and B don't bear on the causal claim.","src":"new"},{"id":"rc-n8-5","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-np8","difficulty":"hard","stem":"The author's attitude in the final paragraph toward the conclusion that aesthetic experience is shaped by knowledge is best described as:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"despairing, viewing it as the end of genuine aesthetic experience."},{"k":"B","t":"enthusiastic, celebrating the death of the pure encounter."},{"k":"C","t":"dismissive, regarding it as obviously false."},{"k":"D","t":"accepting of its unsettling implications while resisting a cynical interpretation."}],"answer":"D","explanation":"The author calls the conclusion 'unsettling' but says it 'need not lead to cynicism' — a relation to history 'is not nothing'. A and B are extremes; C contradicts the argument.","src":"new"},{"id":"rc-n9-1","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-np9","difficulty":"hard","stem":"Which of the following best expresses the main argument of the passage?","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Practical meritocracy faces problems of measurement and, more fundamentally, of luck, which undermine the claim that its rewards are simply deserved."},{"k":"B","t":"Competitive examinations are the perfect measure of merit."},{"k":"C","t":"Interviews are preferable to examinations because they assess character."},{"k":"D","t":"Meritocracy should be abolished in favour of allocation by lottery."}],"answer":"A","explanation":"The passage moves from measurement problems to the 'deeper difficulty' of luck, concluding that rewards cannot be justified purely by desert. D is explicitly rejected; B contradicts 'crude instrument'; C contradicts para 3.","src":"new"},{"id":"rc-n9-2","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-np9","difficulty":"hard","stem":"The statement 'The examination is impartial at the moment of testing, but the competition it adjudicates began long before' implies that:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"candidates who prepare longer always succeed."},{"k":"B","t":"the impartiality of the test does not neutralise unequal preparation leading up to it."},{"k":"C","t":"examinations should be held earlier in life."},{"k":"D","t":"examiners are secretly biased."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"The sentence contrasts procedural impartiality with prior inequality. D contradicts 'impartial'; C and A are unsupported.","src":"new"},{"id":"rc-n9-3","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-np9","difficulty":"hard","stem":"According to the passage, defenders of examinations argue that holistic assessments are:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"more accurate measures of intelligence."},{"k":"B","t":"impossible to administer at scale."},{"k":"C","t":"less sensitive to character and creativity."},{"k":"D","t":"more vulnerable to advantages of class and connection."}],"answer":"D","explanation":"Para 3: holistic methods are 'more susceptible to precisely the advantages of class and connection'. C reverses the passage.","src":"new"},{"id":"rc-n9-4","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-np9","difficulty":"hard","stem":"The author's point about 'the capacity for effort' (paragraph 4) is that:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"effort cannot be measured by examinations."},{"k":"B","t":"people who work hard always succeed."},{"k":"C","t":"even effort may partly reflect unevenly distributed, unearned capacities, complicating attempts to reward pure desert."},{"k":"D","t":"effort is the only fair basis for reward."}],"answer":"C","explanation":"The author argues that the capacity for effort is 'itself unevenly distributed', so even an effort-based meritocracy wouldn't escape luck. D is the view complicated; B and A are not the point.","src":"new"},{"id":"rc-n9-5","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-np9","difficulty":"hard","stem":"The author would most likely agree with which of the following?","options":[{"k":"A","t":"The privileged deserve their success more than others."},{"k":"B","t":"Surgeons should be chosen by lottery to avoid rewarding luck."},{"k":"C","t":"Meritocracy has no egalitarian origins."},{"k":"D","t":"Skill should determine who holds a position, but the rewards of success should not be justified solely as deserved."}],"answer":"D","explanation":"The final paragraph explicitly endorses choosing by ability while denying that rewards are justified 'simply by appeal to desert'. B is rejected; A and C contradict the passage.","src":"new"},{"id":"rc-n10-1","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-np10","difficulty":"hard","stem":"The author's central claim about 'untranslatable' words is that:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"their meaning can usually be conveyed, but the economy and habitual salience of a single word may be lost."},{"k":"B","t":"they genuinely express concepts that cannot be conveyed in other languages."},{"k":"C","t":"they prove that language determines the limits of thought."},{"k":"D","t":"they are invented by journalists to entertain readers."}],"answer":"A","explanation":"Paras 2–4 argue meaning survives paraphrase but lexicalisation affects economy and what 'tends to be thought'. B and C are views the author rejects; D is an unsupported cynical reading.","src":"new"},{"id":"rc-n10-2","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-np10","difficulty":"hard","stem":"The author raises the question 'how did the list-maker convey its meaning?' in order to:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"argue that English is superior to other languages."},{"k":"B","t":"criticise the quality of the explanations given in such lists."},{"k":"C","t":"suggest that list-makers are poorly informed about foreign languages."},{"k":"D","t":"expose a tension in the claim that such words are untranslatable."}],"answer":"D","explanation":"The rhetorical question highlights that the existence of explanations contradicts strict untranslatability. B, A and C are not the purpose.","src":"new"},{"id":"rc-n10-3","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-np10","difficulty":"hard","stem":"The metaphor 'less a locked door than a well-worn path' (paragraph 4) suggests that an untranslatable word:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"is used by speakers only in literary contexts."},{"k":"B","t":"makes a concept more habitually accessible rather than exclusively available."},{"k":"C","t":"prevents speakers of other languages from understanding a concept."},{"k":"D","t":"will eventually disappear from the language."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"A locked door = impossibility of access (strong relativity); a well-worn path = habitual ease of access. C is the 'locked door' view rejected; A and D are unsupported.","src":"new"},{"id":"rc-n10-4","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-np10","difficulty":"hard","stem":"Which of the following findings would most support the passage's 'modest claim' about language?","options":[{"k":"A","t":"Speakers of a language without a word for a certain emotion are unable to understand it when it is explained."},{"k":"B","t":"Translators prefer to use paraphrase rather than borrowing foreign words."},{"k":"C","t":"Speakers of a language with a word for a certain emotion report noticing and discussing that emotion more frequently than speakers of languages without such a word."},{"k":"D","t":"All languages have roughly the same number of emotion words."}],"answer":"C","explanation":"The modest claim is that lexicalisation affects what 'tends to be thought' — frequency of noticing and discussing. A supports the strong version the author rejects; D and B are irrelevant.","src":"new"},{"id":"rc-n10-5","topic":"rc","type":"mcq","passage":"rc-np10","difficulty":"hard","stem":"According to the final paragraph, a translator who renders saudade with a lengthy paraphrase has:","options":[{"k":"A","t":"failed to convey its meaning."},{"k":"B","t":"made an error that a better translator would avoid."},{"k":"C","t":"preserved its sense while changing its weight and the reader's experience."},{"k":"D","t":"improved upon the original by making it clearer."}],"answer":"C","explanation":"The paragraph says such a translation 'preserved its sense but altered its weight'. A contradicts; D and B impose judgements the author avoids — the point is that translation always involves choosing what to preserve.","src":"new"}]};
